HT_DATA.load("detail", {"BLGFriOKz6U":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as the deepest public look at how iCloud Keychain and Apple's device and cloud security are designed.","q":{"x":"if you haven't watched the talk at Black Hat I'd highly recommend it","i":16228942,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":46736038,"author":"eddyg","month":"2026-01","story":"Microsoft gave FBI set of BitLocker encryption keys to unlock suspects' laptops","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full details on how iCloud is protected.","quote":"You can (and should) watch","text":"You shouldn't include Apple in this. As of macOS Tahoe, the FileVault key you (optionally) escrow with Apple is stored in the iCloud Keychain, which is cryptographically secured by HSM-backed, rate-limited protections. You can (and should) watch [VIDEO] for all the details about how iCloud is protected."},{"id":46744075,"author":"eddyg","month":"2026-01","story":"MS confirms it will give the FBI your Windows PC data encryption key if asked","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full details on iCloud architecture and protection.","quote":"You can (and should) watch","text":"Which is a very good thing. iCloud is much more secure than most people realize because most people don’t take the 30 minutes to learn how it is architected. You can (and should) watch [VIDEO] for all the details about how iCloud is protected, but especially the time-linked section. :)"},{"id":44642506,"author":"jesseendahl","month":"2025-07","story":"UK backing down on Apple encryption backdoor after pressure from US","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive on iCloud Keychain security engineering in second half.","quote":"the second half of this Blackhat talk by Apple's head of Security Engineering & Architecture (SEAR) is really great","text":"This is not just encryption in transit or simplistic client-side encryption. It is end-to-end encryption, where each device's key generation is handled by your phone's Secure Enclave. This article is a decent starting point in terms of what Advanced Data Protection is: [LINK] If you want a deeper dive into the security engineering of iCloud Keychain, the second half of this Blackhat talk by Apple's head of Security Engineering & Architecture (SEAR) is really great: Synchronizing secrets: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39391679,"author":"dilippkumar","month":"2024-02","story":"Apple confirms it's breaking iPhone web apps in the EU on purpose","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced the commenter that Apple knows device security.","quote":"That alone convinced me. Apple knows what it is doing when it comes to device security.","text":"There have been several comments along the lines of: \"Why should I trust apple and their security, but not that of a third-party web browser?\" I don't have an answer for you. But I want to recommend this talk from 7 years ago: [VIDEO] That alone convinced me. Apple knows what it is doing when it comes to device security. Today, I trust them with my most sensitive data and sleep peacefully at night."},{"id":20909573,"author":"Twisell","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: How comfortable do you feel using cloud-based password managers?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced commenter to switch from 1Password to iCloud keychain.","quote":"I tried 1Password but finally resolved to use iCloud keychain after watching this BlackHat 2016 video","text":"I tried 1Password but finally resolved to use iCloud keychain after watching this BlackHat 2016 video [VIDEO] . I mean as far as I already trust their OS nothing can really protect me from being spied by them if they are ill intentioned, so as long as they are serious and patch their security flaw on a timely manner I can live with that. Beside it come as a free plan if you don't need more than 5GB of iCloud storage. I'd figure using an external password manager just add another third party I need to trust and the fact that 1Password offer browser app interface (on top of native) don't reassure me in any way. Of course if I'd ever need to reassess my threat model because I can't trust Apple anymore, I will quit iCloud service at the same time as their OS and go full FOSS."},{"id":16228942,"author":"karlshea","month":"2018-01","story":"Apple, in Sign of Health Ambitions, Adds Medical Records Feature for iPhone","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers device key exchange and iCloud security with SEP keys.","quote":"if you haven't watched the talk at Black Hat I'd highly recommend it","text":"They do all sorts of device key exchange, if you haven't watched the talk at Black Hat I'd highly recommend it: [VIDEO] (link goes to beginning of Secure Enclave content, and later on in the talk he discusses iCloud which uses the SEP keys)"},{"id":16229154,"author":"karlshea","month":"2018-01","story":"Apple, in Sign of Health Ambitions, Adds Medical Records Feature for iPhone","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers SEP, on-device encryption, and iCloud security.","quote":"The Black Hat talk was really great","text":"The Black Hat talk was really great: [VIDEO] (link is to SEP and on-device encryption, 22:30 is iCloud stuff) > I know they said they could have accessed that San Bernardino terrorist’s iCloud backup if only they had one. Not sure if they’ve change the security architecture since then. I actually think they have, the shooting was in 2015 and iOS 10 was released in 2016 and was first to have some of the features he talks about in the video."},{"id":36237169,"author":"jesseendahl","month":"2023-06","story":"Tailscale doesn't want your password","stance":"rec","why":"Security engineering of synchronizing secrets in iCloud Keychain.","quote":"you should watch the \"Synchronizing secrets\" part of this Black Hat talk from 2016","text":">So I don't really understand how this can be said to be a hardware security token. No one claimed it's a hardware security token, but the private keys are stored in iCloud Keychain, which is end-to-end encrypted. Apple cannot access your private keys even if iCloud is compromised by an external attacker or employee. If you're curious about the security engineering, you should watch the \"Synchronizing secrets\" part of this Black Hat talk from 2016: [VIDEO] or you should read the \"Synchronization security\" section of the \"About the security of passkeys\" support doc: [LINK]"},{"id":27843624,"author":"eddyg","month":"2021-07","story":"Luxury Surveillance","stance":"rec","why":"Details iCloud infrastructure security including one-way hash functions.","quote":"You may be interested in watching Ivan Krstić’s BlackHat talk[0], in which he details the security of the iCloud infrastructure.","text":"You may be interested in watching Ivan Krstić’s BlackHat talk[0], in which he details the security of the iCloud infrastructure. The use of physical one-way hash functions is involved. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20662919,"author":"ghostpepper","month":"2019-08","story":"FBI tells lawmakers it can't access Dayton gunman's phone","stance":"rec","why":"Surprisingly deep security talk from a top Apple security person.","quote":"where he goes surprisingly deep","text":"The apple security whitepaper is actually a decent place to start [LINK] There is also a blackhat talk from Ivan Krstic (one of the top security people at apple) where he goes surprisingly deep [VIDEO]"},{"id":16984566,"author":"xmodem","month":"2018-05","story":"Apple iMac Pro and Secure Storage","stance":"rec","why":"Mechanism for securing credentials stored in the cloud.","quote":"where he explains the mechanism they use to secure credentials stored in the cloud","text":"Take a look at this blackhat talk from Apple's head of security Ivan Krstic, where he explains the mechanism they use to secure credentials stored in the cloud: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16455898,"author":"abalone","month":"2018-02","story":"Apple moves to store iCloud keys in China, raising human rights fears","stance":"rec","why":"Cloud Key Vault design for keychain backups in adversarial clouds.","quote":"Check out Cloud Key Vault, it’s a pretty cool solution for keychain (password) backups designed for adversarial clouds.","text":"Passwords are backed up but they are end to end encrypted. Check out Cloud Key Vault, it’s a pretty cool solution for keychain (password) backups designed for adversarial clouds. Unfortunately does not apply to regular iCloud backups or most other data. But a major step forward. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13032407,"author":"arkadiyt","month":"2016-11","story":"The limitations of Android N Encryption","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth dive on iOS class keys from Apple's head of security.","quote":"For a really in-depth dive about the iOS class keys system he mentions I recommend this 2016 blackhat talk","text":"For a really in-depth dive about the iOS class keys system he mentions I recommend this 2016 blackhat talk by Ivan Krstic (head of security at Apple): [VIDEO] (relevant portion starts at 6:56)"},{"id":19656127,"author":"abalone","month":"2019-04","story":"Apple censors song about Tiananmen Square protests","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for iCloud Keychain design for adversarial clouds.","quote":"iCloud Keychain, which they've designed to work securely on \"adversarial clouds\"[1]","text":"Worth noting that Apple \"gets away\" with more in China than most: - iMessage, one of the only end-to-end encrypted messaging systems widely available in China. - iCloud Keychain, which they've designed to work securely on \"adversarial clouds\"[1] (Of course this may only be because not many people actually use these features versus WeChat...) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16489356,"author":"Godel_unicode","month":"2018-03","story":"The Feds Can Now Probably Unlock Every iPhone Model","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's own talk on iPhone hardware security.","quote":"Apple gave a talk specifically about hardware security in the iPhone.","text":"That’s not the apple talk. Apple gave a talk specifically about hardware security in the iPhone. Edit: this one. [VIDEO] Edit 2: the question was asked at 47:20"},{"id":16465510,"author":"bpicolo","month":"2018-02","story":"Apple confirms it uses Google cloud for some of iCloud","stance":"reference","why":"Shows data can be stored safely in adversarial clouds.","quote":"You can store data safely even in adversarial clouds.","text":"[VIDEO] They take security for these things seriously. You can store data safely even in adversarial clouds."},{"id":15780681,"author":"johneth","month":"2017-11","story":"Apple’s Secure Enclave Processor (SEP) Firmware Decrypted","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation by Apple's head of security engineering on iOS security.","quote":"There's a video of a presentation by Ivan Krstic (head of security engineering, Apple) at Black Hat from 2016 about iOS security","text":"There's a video of a presentation by Ivan Krstic (head of security engineering, Apple) at Black Hat from 2016 about iOS security: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15270467,"author":"Shank","month":"2017-09","story":"Our Approach to Privacy","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's public talk on iOS security architecture.","quote":"Talk about iOS security at Black Hat","text":"It all boils down to what you see in the public. The FBI was trying to get Apple to create an iOS variant to extract data from a device, to the point where Tim Cook wrote a letter refusing to do so and was willing to fight it in court. In a similar fashion, Apple has given talks and white papers on iOS security. In tandem with these well documented white papers and talks discussing the internal functions of iOS, we haven't seen any well known or trivial exploits appear to surface that demonstrate flaws in them. In the case of the FBI fiasco, for example, we know that the FBI later paid Cellebrite for an exploit that allowed them to unlock the device. We don't know the details of how that happened, but the FBI had to reach out to an independent company and exploit an older device to do it. To be clear: if they were lying about the lengths they go to with encryption, on device security, and user trust, this story would have been different. The FBI would have already had a backdoor or been able to trivially break the device, or they would have complied and created an iOS variant to break in. All we know is what they stood their ground on, and the effort that was required for the FBI to get in. iOS 10 security white paper: [LINK] Talk about iOS security at Black Hat: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14513879,"author":"IBM","month":"2017-06","story":"Chinese authorities detain Apple employees suspected of selling customer data","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to explain how iCloud security works.","quote":"Given the way iCloud security works I'm not sure iCloud was breached at all [1].","text":"Given the way iCloud security works I'm not sure iCloud was breached at all [1]. Other reports seem to indicate that it was employees at Apple stores and third party resellers who had access to names, phone numbers and Apple IDs [2]. Presumably they would try to phish them later on. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":12448438,"author":"tedmiston","month":"2016-09","story":"iPhone 7","stance":"reference","why":"Black Hat talk on iOS security with slides and abstract.","quote":"I found the talk (though I don't pretend to understand it all)","text":"I found the talk (though I don't pretend to understand it all), video & slides are online if others are interested. Behind the Scenes with iOS Security Abstract - [LINK] Slides - [LINK] Video - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["D1R-jKKp3NA",1],["PE4gwstWhmc",1],["cBDJDZAo41c",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["NdfmMB1E_qk",1],["TS1lpKBMkgg",1]]},"JxAXlJEmNMg":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends the Crockford series as a must-watch history of computing and programming languages, and as a way to learn to think in JavaScript.","q":{"x":"I urge you to watch this. It really is worth it!","i":7150830,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":11820456,"author":"gravypod","month":"2016-06","story":"One Click to Be Pro: my list of the best resources for mastering a subject","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talks that reshaped how commenter thinks about software","quote":"they have reshaped the way I think about software development","text":"I'd strongly recommend putting this link in there: [VIDEO] David, if you see this, I'd like to thank you for these talks as they have reshaped the way I think about software development. I've got more from these then my classes at college have given me do far."},{"id":9844979,"author":"kornakiewicz","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: How to learn about the history of computing?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of computing and web history ending at JavaScript","quote":"is definitely must-watch","text":"The Crockford's lecture about history of web-development (starting far before first computing machines and ending on javascript) is definitely must-watch. It's not so detailed because it's not a book, but gives a good overview. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7422238,"author":"napsterbr","month":"2014-03","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Crockford's JavaScript series is highly recommended","quote":"Crockford series on javascript is just awesome. I highly recommend it.","text":"Hard to say if it's the best, but Crockford series on javascript is just awesome. I highly recommend it. [VIDEO] (Browse the related videos to see the others)"},{"id":7150830,"author":"Dogamondo","month":"2014-01","story":"A Brief And Mostly Wrong History Of Programming Languages","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accurate, digestible history of programming language evolution","quote":"I urge you to watch this. It really is worth it!","text":"For a more accurate, informative and very digestible history of the evolution of programming languages, I urge you to watch this. It really is worth it! [VIDEO]"},{"id":31127213,"author":"gravypod","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn about computing history?","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of web technology history from an influential figure","quote":"A great summary, from someone who was influential in web technologies","text":"A great summary, from someone who was influential in web technologies, can be found in this series \"Crockford on JavaScript\": [VIDEO] (context: [LINK] )"},{"id":17465076,"author":"valw","month":"2018-07","story":"Rise of Kotlin: A Programming Language for the Next Generation","stance":"rec","why":"Retrospective on evolution and adoption of programming languages","quote":"This video is a great retrospective on the evolution and adoption of programming languages / paradigms","text":"The innovation dynamics in the software industry are (have been) really different than in these fields IMHO. But there are some similar historical precedents. Fourier's work was rejected in his time because Lagrange wouldn't adopt his approach to mathematics. This video is a great retrospective on the evolution and adoption of programming languages / paradigms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16801555,"author":"arsalanb","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources for catching up on modern JavaScript for Python/C++ Programmer","stance":"rec","why":"Video series helpful for catching up on modern JavaScript","quote":"(videos, watch all of them)","text":"I'm in the same position as you. I realised I was still writing spaghetti code that relied heavily on DOM manipulation with Jquery. I am currently learning how the language has evolved and the following has been helpful — 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] (videos, watch all of them) 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] Hope this helps :)"},{"id":16839710,"author":"kornakiewicz","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Brief history of computing, solid and fun","quote":"the first two are pretty solid and still fun","text":"I didn't watch the whole serie yet, but the first two are pretty solid and still fun. The first part doest not cover JS at all, but is rather a brief history of computing. Crockford on JavaScript - Volume 1: The Early Years [VIDEO]"},{"id":10462739,"author":"ggurgone","month":"2015-10","story":"Ask HN: What's the best path for learning JavaScript for web development?","stance":"rec","why":"Crockford lectures as JavaScript learning resource","quote":"There are some lectures by Crockford on youtube too","text":"Forget about Bootstrap and jQuery, learn vanilla js. Reading Eloquent JavaScript is a good starting point. Once you are done with that, build something and join #javascript @ FreenodeIRC and ask for help when you get stuck on something. The good parts is nice but keep in mind that it is very opinionated, still it is worth reading. There are some lectures by Crockford on youtube too (tl;dr) [VIDEO] Also this on OOP [LINK] Finally you may want to familiarize with the most common JavaScript design patterns (some are not unique to js) [LINK]"},{"id":8785721,"author":"johnhenry","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: Good JavaScript introductions for experienced programmers?","stance":"rec","why":"Crockford video series for experienced programmers learning JavaScript","quote":"has a number of videos that are very useful.","text":"Here's a really good introduction to inheritance: [LINK] -- a lot of people struggle unless they have a good understanding of how this works. Also, Douglass Crockford (the person who wrote \"JavaScript: The Good Parts\") has a number of videos that are very useful. Here's the first in a series of eight: [VIDEO] -- and if you find that useful, you can check out the rest in the list of related videos."},{"id":7085599,"author":"rtpg","month":"2014-01","story":"Prototypes Are Not Classes","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to thinking in JavaScript rather than Java","quote":"Section 3 is where you really start talking about JS.","text":"This sort of realization is the first step to realizing that you really shouldn't be writing javascript like java/C++. It makes things feel a lot nicer when you start really thinking in JS. I'd recommend checking out Crockford's \"Crockford on Javascript\" series for some good intro on thinking about Javascript ( [VIDEO] ). Section 3 is where you really start talking about JS. For a good counter-example, check out a lot of google's JS libs (in all their giant module goodness)."},{"id":6068553,"author":"jliechti1","month":"2013-07","story":"JavaScript Isn't Scheme","stance":"rec","why":"History of languages leading up to JavaScript","quote":"gives a really nice history of the many programming languages leading up to JavaScript","text":"Part 1 of Douglas Crockford's On JavaScript gives a really nice history of the many programming languages leading up to JavaScript. Chapter 1: The Early Years : [VIDEO] Chapter 2: And Then There Was JavaScript: [LINK]"},{"id":13512758,"author":"gravypod","month":"2017-01","story":"A college student’s individual analysis of productivity of four years (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in debate about CS curriculum","quote":"","text":"> why should a protocl like telnet be relevant today A lot of software uses \"telnet\" as in TCP sockets with \\r\\n and ASCII encoding. That's all telnet is and that's how a lot of data is shifted around all the time. > To learn from its mistakes? Telnet wasn't a mistake. It was an obvious step from terminals via RS232 to terminals via TCP. Also, telnet is everywhere. If you went into research and someone said \"just hook into the telnet stream for $SENSOR\" and you said \"What's telnet\" that wouldn't end well. > No, that's why you take design classes that you seem to dislike. No one in their right minds would call telnet, sending text over TCP, a bad design. It's a bad design for some things, but others it's the simplest way to do things. You can do a lot with it since it is very robust. What can go wrong with a telnet stream? 1) Client/Server drops connection to you 2) ... What else? That's a tenant of fantastic design. > Leaving aside the lack of need to know those ancient things (I say this as a heavy user of vim), you seem to conflate engineering with computer science. I don't conflate it. Everyone in industry does. I'm marketing myself to industry so I will use their terminology. Their terminology associates Computer Science with \"The programmer\". > There's a lot of CS that doesn't have anything to do with network protocols and UNIX programs I and most places you'd go to work in industry aren't mainly concerned with those problems. Different data structures? Great yea I'd lov"},{"id":13216392,"author":"gravypod","month":"2016-12","story":"Why Java Sucks – Jonathan Gardner","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in Java language debate","quote":"","text":"> Yet again, they admitted an important feature was missing. Again, I am not familiar with Java 8 but I suspect their implementation is horribly wrong. Admitting something is broken does not mean it sucks. See this comment: [LINK] Also, please read this before you wave it off: [LINK] > I have. I guess I have just logged more time in Java than others. Java is probably my most used language and that's saying something as I work as a TA for a Python class, use python for most personal projects, and also use many other languages. Java consumes the most time because it has the most polished toolings and standards for developers. Now they may not always be ovserved but if you stay in your own walled garden everything is all honky-dory. You will never make these mistakes if you follow basic practices in Java's naming conventions. All lower case classpaths, all uppercase class names, lower then cammel function names, etc. You'll never be able to mistake Test() for Test.test() as they are instinctivly different names. > Please tell me how to do it properly. IE, if I have a variable of type Foo that is holding a value of type Bar, how do I invoke the function with Bar instead of Foo in the signature? void something(Foo f) { if (f instanceof Bar) { (Bar-Specific commands) } (generic commands) } Or better yet use your magic want: Abstraction . Wave it around and use your super powers to move that functionality into Foo and in anything that needs a specific handling of that function, over"},{"id":9685072,"author":"pluma","month":"2015-06","story":"“Swift will be open source later this year”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for programmers resisting better languages","quote":"","text":"But programmers don't want better languages[0]. Programmers mostly just want what they already have. You can't overcome that kind of resistance just by saying \"look, here's a language that's more fun and modern\". Programmers say they want better languages, but good luck trying to convince them to switch to a new language just by its merit. The only tangible benefit of Swift is that it can be used instead of Objective-C for iOS -- which is important for iOS because Objective-C poses an even bigger hurdle by virtue of its unfamiliar syntax. Swift wins on iOS because it competes with a language nobody wanted to learn to begin with. Even as far as familiarity goes, the only thing Swift looks familiar to (based on first impressions) is Ruby, except it uses a more familiar C-like syntax (i.e. braces). While Ruby programmers are extremely visible (especially in startup/valley crowds) there aren't that many of them -- not to mention that some have already moved on to Rust or JS. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7302843,"author":"kachhalimbu","month":"2014-02","story":"Difference between JavaScript and Java?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on JavaScript's early years","quote":"watch Douglas Crocford talk about JavaScript early years in this talk","text":"or if you prefer watching instead of reading, watch Douglas Crocford talk about JavaScript early years in this talk [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1],["wMFPe-DwULM",1],["7erJ1DV_Tlo",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["oFeoS41xe7w",1],["f84n5oFoZBc",1]]},"ajGX7odA87k":{"wy":"HN considers it a classic, hilarious and sobering talk on ML inscrutability and tech hubris, often rewatched.","q":{"x":"His keynote at USENIX Security 2018 is by far the best summation of the dangers of ML that I've ever seen.","i":21198451,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46818920,"author":"vizzier","month":"2026-01","story":"Was Vibe Coding a Ruse?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Decade-old talk still applies to current tech trends","quote":"I re-watch it fairly often","text":"James Micken's talk from almost a decade ago now covers this stuff perfectly... [VIDEO] I re-watch it fairly often, and we just keep getting worse."},{"id":40860920,"author":"tomxor","month":"2024-07","story":"Explainability is not a game","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brilliant cathartic humor about inscrutable ML","quote":"well worth the watch, even if you already agree it's brilliant cathartic humour","text":"> ML models is most often inscrutable, with the consequence that decisions taken by ML models cannot be fathomed by human decision makers Yup, James Mickens planted this seed 6 years ago, well worth the watch, even if you already agree it's brilliant cathartic humour (the subject isn't really \"security\"). [VIDEO]"},{"id":38836107,"author":"jamesblonde","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: What talks do you consider real gems?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic funny and technically excellent talk","quote":"Nearly all talks by James Mickens are a laugh a minute.","text":"Nearly all talks by James Mickens are a laugh a minute. The guy is the only computer science standup comedian I have ever encountered. He is also technically excellent. This one is a classic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21198451,"author":"_wldu","month":"2019-10","story":"James Mickens’ Tenure Announcement","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious summation of the dangers of ML","quote":"His keynote at USENIX Security 2018 is by far the best summation of the dangers of ML that I've ever seen.","text":"I've always been a fan of his talks. His keynote at USENIX Security 2018 is by far the best summation of the dangers of ML that I've ever seen. It's hilarious too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34498558,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2023-01","story":"Why the infosec community is ahead of the curve and rationalists and nihilists","stance":"rec","why":"Cautionary tale on AI, IoT and technological manifest destiny","quote":"A cautionary tale about AI, Technological Manifest Destiny, IoT, and why you shouldn't trust it, ever.","text":"The actual video referenced is a hoot[1] A cautionary tale about AI, Technological Manifest Destiny, IoT, and why you shouldn't trust it, ever. His take down of IoT devices is hilarious. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31089803,"author":"hallway_monitor","month":"2022-04","story":"Planting Undetectable Backdoors in Machine Learning Models","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful, funny talk on trusting neural nets with real decisions","quote":"It is insightful and hilarious but also terrifying.","text":"I believe this talk [0] by James Mickens is very applicable. He touches on trusting neural nets with decisions that have real-world consequences. It is insightful and hilarious but also terrifying. [VIDEO] \"Why do keynote speakers keep suggesting that improving security is possible?\""},{"id":28507864,"author":"bawolff","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Have you found it difficult to get quality technical content on YouTube?","stance":"rec","why":"A favorite conference talk among good technical video content","quote":"one of my faves","text":"I generally dislike consuming computer content in video form, but there is plenty of interesting content out there. Lots of conferences video tape sessions. Plenty of noise but some good things too. E.g. one of my faves: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23485283,"author":"specialist","month":"2020-06","story":"Police have been spying on black reporters and activists for years","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous, logical argument that impact matters more than intent","quote":"James Mickens, using logic and humor, makes this point in a way that even geeks might understand.","text":"\"What matters now is impact, not intent.\" James Mickens, using logic and humor, makes this point in a way that even geeks might understand. \"Why Do Keynote Speakers Keep Suggesting That Improving Security Is Possible?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":20769477,"author":"w8rbt","month":"2019-08","story":"How to Start with Machine Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk arguing ML is inscrutable black magic","quote":"This is long, but worth watching","text":"I think James Mickens (Harvard CS) has it right. ML is inscrutable, black-magic that no one really understands. This is long, but worth watching: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18345245,"author":"tempodox","month":"2018-10","story":"Google Home (in)Security","stance":"rec","why":"James Mickens humorous security keynote","quote":"You might be interested in this keynote by James Mickens","text":"You might be interested in this keynote by James Mickens: [VIDEO] titled “Why Do Keynote Speakers Keep Suggesting That Improving Security Is Possible?”."},{"id":38494052,"author":"norir","month":"2023-12","story":"The Inside Story of Microsoft's Partnership with OpenAI","stance":"reference","why":"Warns against deploying technology we do not understand","quote":"He warns quite effectively against widely deploying technology that we don't understand.","text":"Reading this article, I kept think about James Micken's 2018 usenix security talk [VIDEO] . He warns quite effectively against widely deploying technology that we don't understand. Kevin Scott, on the other hand, seems to have exactly the opposite take. I suppose there is a reason though why one of them is a successful businessman and the other is an academic."},{"id":36940498,"author":"southerntofu","month":"2023-07","story":"Hugging Face, GitHub and more unite to defend open source in EU AI legislation","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a long argument about AI regulation","quote":"","text":"> If it doesn't work as I expect it so it should be banned. Well yes that's a very reasonable expectation. In most fields, when you sell something that doesn't work, it's called fraud. But in fields where it actually matter for safety, regulations are most stringent, such as in mechanics or medicine. If a car had 1% chance of blowing up killing everyone inside, would it be legal to sell it? Why is it ok for OpenAI to sell a chatbot that has 1% chance to convince you of utter bullshit when you ask it a serious question? I believe education and communication to be critical to society's wellbeing, so i would hold companies in this field to greater standards. > Also NLP can be used to protect vulnerable groups from online abuse. Automated filters do not understand sarcasm, quotation, criticism... Sure that avoids the scunthorpe problem, but for example, auto-moderation bots trying to prevent racist abuse often censor black people calling one another the n word, which is completely ok. Nothing can replace moderation. > It helps people find related content (which they enjoy). Maybe, but i doubt it. I've yet to see advertisement/recommendations for content i actually enjoy, or to meet people who have such experiences. I've only ever heard that from advertisers, but maybe you have better experience than i do? > Don't blame technology for the bad actions of companies. Their intentions would have corrupted whatever technology is available. Good point! But technology that makes it easie"},{"id":34698993,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-02","story":"Microsoft Adds ChatGPT AI Technology to Bing Search Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on earlier AI put on the internet","quote":"","text":">> internal people probably won't ask it as awful things or try as hard to break it - I doubt my coworkers would risk asking it super racist things for example. >> 4chan has no such restrictions, and over time what escapes or issues people do find can be mitigated. Hopefully better than previous attempts to put AI on the Internet: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34900447,"author":"dralley","month":"2023-02","story":"AI Companion Users Are in Crisis, Reporting Sudden Sexual Rejection","stance":"reference","why":"Mickens' satirical take on synthetic companions for lonely people","quote":"But clearly, a much better solution is to direct all that lonely energy to synthetic personalities running on data center VMs.","text":"\"Here's the pool of socially well connected people talking to each other, and here's the pool of lonely people that basically talk to nobody. Now, we could create new network edges between the lonely and non-lonely people, so that both groups of people can expand their social network and share their rich sets of deeply personal experiences with each other. But clearly, a much better solution is to direct all that lonely energy to synthetic personalities running on data center VMs. So the people who are happy can continue to be happy, and the people who are lonely can shout into availability zones on the West Coast and Northern Europe. This is clearly the humanist solution.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34550517,"author":"bitwize","month":"2023-01","story":"Just know stuff. (Or, how to achieve success in a machine learning PhD)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of a funny line about ML practice","quote":"The stuff is what the stuff is, brother.","text":"\"The stuff is what the stuff is, brother.\" --from a James Mickens talk on machine learning, [VIDEO] Can confirm, the way ML is used in many businesses is like an egg drop: You open up Jupyter, load in some data, and play around with various models until you find one that fits the data, then use it to try to predict future data. If the future results comport with the model, congratulations: your egg is safe, at least for drops of that height."},{"id":18898349,"author":"RodericDay","month":"2019-01","story":"The media are unwittingly selling us an AI fantasy","stance":"reference","why":"Mickens makes similar arguments about AI hype","quote":"James Mickens is obviously aware of","text":"James Mickens is obviously aware of \"how this stuff works\" and his arguments are very similar: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19044460,"author":"perfmode","month":"2019-01","story":"Opinion: A.I. Could Worsen Health Disparities","stance":"reference","why":"Mickens' talk abstract on questioning technology","quote":"James Mickens on this topic","text":"James Mickens on this topic: [VIDEO] > Some people enter the technology industry to build newer, more exciting kinds of technology as quickly as possible. My keynote will savage these people and will burn important professional bridges, likely forcing me to join a monastery or another penance-focused organization. In my keynote, I will explain why the proliferation of ubiquitous technology is good in the same sense that ubiquitous Venus weather would be good, i.e., not good at all. > Using case studies involving machine learning and other hastily-executed figments of Silicon Valley’s imagination, I will explain why computer security (and larger notions of ethical computing) are difficult to achieve if developers insist on literally not questioning anything that they do since even brief introspection would reduce the frequency of git commits. At some point, my microphone will be cut off, possibly by hotel management, but possibly by myself, because microphones are technology and we need to reclaim the stark purity that emerges from amplifying our voices using rams’ horns and sheets of papyrus rolled into cone shapes. I will explain why papyrus cones are not vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks, and then I will conclude by observing that my new start-up papyr.us is looking for talented full-stack developers who are comfortable executing computational tasks on an abacus or several nearby sticks."},{"id":17979843,"author":"sunyataishere","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: Do you know who this person is?","stance":"reference","why":"Funny keynote including the TayTweets racist bot example","quote":"","text":"Wow... this was worth the watch - thank you! awesome lol at [VIDEO] (tldr - feeding gratuitously racist input to ML twitter bot 'TayTweets'"},{"id":33655958,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-11","story":"Show HN: Get answers for shell commands from GPT3 from your terminal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I recommend a stiff dose of Mickens","text":"ORT (Only Read Title, sorry) What I'm hearing is \"put a non-deterministic black box between you and the man command.\" Nah. I recommend a stiff dose of Mickens: \"Why Do Keynote Speakers...\" [VIDEO] ) and a good lie down."},{"id":23528520,"author":"a1369209993","month":"2020-06","story":"An understanding of AI’s limitations is starting to sink in","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> the possible MNIST digits are 0-9 Except - and this rather ties into your point - those are not the only possible digits; your network also has to deal with (ie reject) other possible digits such as \"P\", \"E\", \"3̸̶\", or \"[Forlorn Sigil of Amon-Gül redacted]\"[0], which look like, but are not, decimal digits. 0: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PaypcVFPs48",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["Th4VlqQyVr4",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["BBwepkVurCI",1],["KLjgBLwH3Wc",1],["9kaIXkImCAM",1],["mdKst8zeh-U",1],["LmpuerlbJu0",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1]]},"bmSAYlu0NcY":{"wy":"HN treats it as the standard one-hour teaser for the book and recommends it for its deep-modules thinking before buying or reading the book.","q":{"x":"This google talk is a condensed version of the (already small - 150 pags IIRC) book. I highly recommend both.","i":41019916,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46961829,"author":"pcwelder","month":"2026-02","story":"Parse, Don't Validate (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ousterhout's talk on deep interfaces.","quote":"John Ousterhout's talk [1] on deep interfaces was transformational for me.","text":"Each repost is worth it. This, along with John Ousterhout's talk [1] on deep interfaces was transformational for me. And this is coming from a guy who codes in python, so lots of transferable learnings. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41019916,"author":"devnonymous","month":"2024-07","story":"A brief interview with Tcl creator John Ousterhout (2023)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Condensed version of a short book that beats Clean Code.","quote":"This google talk is a condensed version of the (already small - 150 pags IIRC) book. I highly recommend both.","text":"This google talk [VIDEO] is a condensed version of the (already small - 150 pags IIRC) book. I highly recommend both. To the point made by the GP (and others here) about how useful the book is, I must add that I got a whole lot more from this small book than some other well known and oft suggested titles (like Clean Code, POSA etc). My most recent peeve about the matter is that Kent Beck\"s \"Tidy, Fisrt?\" was apparently motivated partly in response to this book and yet it falls way too short to matching up. Half of thst book is way too simplistic and almost patronizing and the other is handwavy abstract notions too scared to touch reality lest it spoil the narrative."},{"id":34375806,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2023-01","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great teaser for the book on simplicity and deep modules.","quote":"I highly recommend A Philosophy of Software Design by John Ousterhout. His talk is a great teaser for the book","text":"> I'd rather get to value now by making something that just works(and is adequately tested) than engineer something thats future proof but takes longer to get out. If you haven't read it, I highly recommend A Philosophy of Software Design by John Ousterhout. His talk is a great teaser for the book: [VIDEO] Here's the thing: a program properly written for the future does not take longer to get out. Not much, at least. What we instinctively think of as \"future proof\" is generally anything but: by anticipating some particular kind of change that may happen some time in the future, but in practice almost never does, we end up with more complex programs that are harder to modify when an actual (typically unforeseen ) change comes our way. The best way to future proof your program is to make it simpler , according to the requirement you are currently aware of. If you expect a particular change, sure, plan for it. But for the unexpected, just keep it simple. Because simple is easier to adapt, add to, or rewrite. That said, the simplest solution is rarely the most obvious. Making thing simple does take a bit more time than rushing through the first thing that comes to mind. But it also pays off in a matter of weeks, so it's quite worth it. And if you have a hard time assessing what \"simple\" means, SLoC is a surprisingly good metric (we have research that shows this). And mostly your modules should be deep: small APIs that hide significant implementations. I think of it as encapsulati"},{"id":19228349,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2019-02","story":"Programming Books You Wish You Read Earlier","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One-hour talk on the book's ideas, worth rewatching.","quote":"I have watched his Talks at Google talk on the topic [1] a couple of times (1 hour), highly recommended.","text":"I haven't read the book yet, but I have watched his Talks at Google talk on the topic [1] a couple of times (1 hour), highly recommended. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44472014,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-07","story":"A 37-year-old wanting to learn computer science","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video overview of the book.","quote":"interesting video overview at","text":"I've been re-learning CS (programmed as a kid, then missed getting a minor in CS in college after the service by one 300-level course which wasn't being re-offered when I needed it) and then just did (La)TeX and AppleScript for my day job, but am now trying to create a tool for CNC which is quite different from those which have existed previously, and one thing which has helped a lot is MIT OCW: - the Python courses got me up-to-speed on the basics of that language: [LINK] and [LINK] - Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs served as a disciplined review of a book which I wasn't patient enough to do the exercises of when I first read it: [LINK] - Mathematics for Computer Science helped make up for my spotty math: [LINK] I've also found the recent book: [LINK] very helpful (first reading I did one chapter at a time, re-writing my current project applying the principles of that chapter) --- interesting video overview at: [VIDEO] If one is fortunate, there are videos on specific subjects/algorithms which one needs, e.g., [LINK]"},{"id":38009366,"author":"fzil","month":"2023-10","story":"A Philosophy of Software Design","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture by the author as a quick overview of the book.","quote":"There’s a lecture by the author[1] as a quick overview of what the book is about.","text":"Probably the best book I’ve read on this subject. There’s a lecture by the author[1] as a quick overview of what the book is about. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36121390,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-05","story":"Death by design patterns, or On the cognitive load of abstractions in the code","stance":"rec","why":"Video primer for Ousterhout's book as mandatory homework.","quote":"Video primer","text":"Ah, a Java developer. Try a normal language and when faced with the need to split code up, use... functions. You will be enlightened. Mandatory homework: - [LINK] - \"A philosophy of software design\" by John Ousterhout. Video primer: [VIDEO] - \"Functional Core, Imperative Shell\": [LINK]"},{"id":31386387,"author":"bear8642","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How did you learn good program design?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk as a taste of the book before buying.","quote":"He's done a talk[0] about it if you want a taste before buying.","text":">\"A Philosophy of Software Design\" by John Ousterhout Indeed, very interesting little book! He's done a talk[0] about it if you want a taste before buying. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23133711,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-05","story":"In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm (2014) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Ousterhout's presentation on his software design book.","quote":"this presentation from professor John Ousterhout on the book was pretty good","text":"I haven't read the book, but this presentation from professor John Ousterhout on the book was pretty good: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23366115,"author":"code_biologist","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Best books under 200 pages for developers?","stance":"rec","why":"Ousterhout describes the Stanford course that inspired the book.","quote":"in the talk he gave at Google about the book [1] he describes the course he teaches that was the inspiration for the book","text":"I came into this thread hoping to find the suggestion near the top. Beyond his credibility as the creator of Tcl, in the talk he gave at Google about the book [1] he describes the course he teaches that was the inspiration for the book. The course is to write a word processor with a team and extended it with features over the semester, swapping codebases between teams so occasionally you have to work with other's code. Essentially the largest possible code project you can fit in one semester. Along the way, Ousterhout will give a deep code review of the each project. I think it must be very insightful to see the same project implemented so many ways, and to see what works in terms of simplicity and extensibility. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22189028,"author":"bch","month":"2020-01","story":"Who Says Tcl Rules?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk associated with Ousterhout's software design book.","quote":"you might dig his “Philosophy of Software Design”[0] book, or talks associated with it[1]","text":"You’re right re: it’s early internals, and we probably both know people who still (mistakenly) complain about that. Conceptually the “everything is a string”(EIAS) model still holds though, even though it’s supported by dual-ported “objects” w native representation, and the running is often byte-compiled instead of string parsing. We’ve got early smart fans who saw the vision and possibilities like Karl Lehenbauer, Don Libes, Richard Hipp, and the current core team like Kevin Kenny, Don Porter, Donal Fellows, etc who deeply understand What Tcl Is, and strictly maintain it, even while pushing it ahead (TclObj, finally an in-core blessed object system, native database framework, Miguel Sopher’s(RIP) work on the stackless non-recursive engine (NRE) and coroutines that fell out of that work... Back to Ousterhout though, you might dig his “Philosophy of Software Design”[0] book, or talks associated with it[1]. Lots to like in this space... [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46423679,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-12","story":"You can't design software you don't work on","stance":"reference","why":"Video overview of Ousterhout's book.","quote":"Video overview at","text":"John Ousterhout has been addressing this one class at a time at Stanford for a while now, and has scaled up to a book: [LINK] Video overview at: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42362427,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2024-12","story":"The Rules of Programming (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Ousterhout's principles, among other links.","quote":"and there's a video: (which has also been discussed)","text":"Sounds quite a bit like the principles put forward in Ousterhout's _A Philosophy of Software Design_ [LINK] [LINK] which has seen a fair bit of discussion here: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] and there's a video: [VIDEO] (which has also been discussed) I read it when I got stuck on a recent project, and paused after each chapter to review _all_ of the code (fortunately, it's a fairly small project) and to apply the principles from that chapter to the code, and it was a big help and got me over the hurdle to a working version."},{"id":38158663,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2023-11","story":"Building a high performance JSON parser","stance":"reference","why":"Ousterhout's idea of defining errors out of existence.","quote":"his reference to John Ousterhout, Define errors out of existence.","text":"My favorite bit about this is his reference to John Ousterhout, Define errors out of existence. [VIDEO] the distinct lack of: if err != nil {"},{"id":36806293,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-07","story":"Ten Years of “Go: The Good, the Bad, and the Meh","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm not sure why you think that quoting random HN users proves anything except personal opinions of specific people? These are fine, but other people have other opinions. They support my argument so I included them. I don't see a practical difference between a \"random HN user\" and a random blog post or anything else. A good idea is a good idea, regardless of the medium. > I don't oppose the \"principles\" you've quoted, but they would just as easily apply to e.g. Haskell Possibly, I threw it together pretty quickly, so it's not very thought-out. > (maybe except for the \"there's only one way to do it\" which however has never been true of any language, including Python). It's not binary, but a spectrum. No language has just literally one way to express each concept. However, Python is definitely further towards the \"one way to do it\" end of the scale. Perhaps not as much these days as it used to, but still far more than for example Ruby or Perl. > I feel like you missed my larger point. It's not terribly difficult to write code that you can understand line by line. It's incredibly hard to write a huge code base in a way that you can reason about many code paths simultaneously, however. That's where the abstractions start to make sense. I thought I adressed that; everything I mentioned - explicit error returns, pure functions, no global state, and no metaprogramming - all show their true worth in large and/or unfamiliar codebases. > I don't understand why people think that those"},{"id":31120397,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2022-04","story":"The Birth of Tcl","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Ousterhout's remark on hiring in the talk.","quote":"In his google talk on the philosophy of software design, he said that the hires who worked out best for him","text":"In his google talk on the philosophy of software design, he said that the hires who worked out best for him were the ones where he just really enjoyed the conversations with them during the interviews [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30553372,"author":"wasabigeek","month":"2022-03","story":"What does a 1972 paper have to do with the Single Responsibility Principle?","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter recalls watching the talk and plans to revisit it.","quote":"I remember watching a video on the philosophy of software design","text":"Thanks for this! I remember watching a video on the philosophy of software design ( [VIDEO] ) but I'll definitely need to re-watch/read, as well as check out the rest of his work. Do you have a suggestion on how the article title could be better? I came across Robert Martin's post while trying to build a better intuition for SRP, and it referenced the Parnas paper. So the title was really me thinking \"What does this paper have to do with the SRP?\", though the '1972' was both for convenience (e.g. \"What does 'On the Criteria to be Used in Decomposing Systems' have to do with the single responsibility principle\"? is too long) and intrigue. Perhaps \"How a 1972 paper relates to the Single Responsibility Principle\"? The Parnas paper wasn't one that Martin initially credited for the SRP as well (see [LINK] ), so I didn't feel comfortable claiming that it had direct influence (e.g. \"The paper that influenced the Single Responsibility Principle\")."},{"id":19012287,"author":"svat","month":"2019-01","story":"Confessions of an Abstraction Hater","stance":"reference","why":"Ousterhout's talk on thick classes and deep modules.","quote":"I'm also reminded of Ousterhout's advice in A Philosophy of Software Design that “classes should be ‘thick’","text":"Relevant recent discussion: • [LINK] (John Carmack on Inlined Code (2014)) • This comment, which contains a useful example: [LINK] Please let me know of any further discussion on the topic. (I'm also reminded of Ousterhout's advice in A Philosophy of Software Design that “classes should be ‘thick’” — see talk [VIDEO] or (unrelated) slides: [LINK] ) One thing I see people miss in this discussion is that there's a tradeoff between having an individual function be understandable (shorter functions are easier to understand) having the entire codebase or interrelationships between functions be easier to understand (fewer functions are easier to understand). When there's an example presented like in the post, it's often presented as an example with a single function, and of course four short functions may each be easier to understand than a single function, but you've also added three nodes to the “graph” (of functions and calls between them) and now you need to study whether these functions are called from anywhere. To make up for this one may start introducing classes/objects to indicate “private”/“public” functions, and so on. (Some languages allow nested functions, which can help.) Most of all I'd say reading very different styles of code, and seeing how people achieve great things in unusual ways (e.g. with lots of abstraction, or with very little abstraction), can be an illuminating experience (see my comment in this thread about reading Knuth's programs)."},{"id":18332038,"author":"quaunaut","month":"2018-10","story":"Book Review: A Philosophy of Software Design","stance":"reference","why":"Linked talk on A Philosophy of Software Design; comment is about the book.","quote":"","text":"This book has been my own personal technical revolution over the past few months. I've been programming professionally for 7 years now, so while I'm not the most experienced I'm certainly no one new either. Some takeaways from how it changed my flow: 1. It gave me specific goals in terms of my own improvement, and the chapter titles did a good job of being a stand-in for challenging my own intuition. 2. In mentoring other developers, those I shared some of the lessons in the book with saw huge improvement literally week over week. I saw, personally, one of my mentees go from producing code that was hard for me to step through without bringing up half a dozen issues, to producing code where my largest criticisms were ecosystem dependent. 3. It created common language for discussing with teammates why certain code felt wrong. 4. It significantly reduced time spent refactoring later. The subtleties in some of the points make a re-read valuable as well. One of the better examples of this, was \"Make code just a little generic\"- often, we attempt to code things as generic as possible, and end up making what is effectively code soup that doesn't help accomplish the problem. Instead, building specifically for this problem, but trying to spot places it can be made just a little bit more generic, has altogether eliminated times I know I would've had to refactor again later if I hadn't been thinking of it. 5. The book knows what it isn't. He even says right in the forward and on the rel"},{"id":18334056,"author":"bch","month":"2018-10","story":"Book Review: A Philosophy of Software Design","stance":"reference","why":"Ousterhout's talk linked as a source in a note about his work.","quote":"","text":"JO (John Ousterhout) is so thoughtful and lucid. My experience with him is firstly through Tcl, which I like more than he does now (time for a TIP: \"-jo\" switch to [unset] which removes complaint to unset non-existent variables)[0], and his book on it[1], and various papers. His work is at once both powerful and humble. Note in [0] how he without much emotion he suggests how Tcl came to be less prominent, and how even at Tcl's height, he explained without ego why it was there, and how it would leave[2]. His papers on threads[3] and comments on performance and testing[4] are also lovely reads. I encourage any developer or architect to read or re-read them. [0] [VIDEO] (thx `quaunaut) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] (This is The Law, and it is good.) [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["PUv66718DII",2],["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["ZR0ujwlvbkQ",1],["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1],["BnSUk0je6oo",1],["hV41QEKiMlM",1],["D9H9qTdserM",1],["agw-wlHGi0E",1]]},"EY6q5dv_B-o":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for Thompson's funny, first-hand Unix history stories, some of which even Kernighan had not heard.","q":{"x":"Also I found these fascinating","i":21589163,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21737960,"author":"kick","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: What are the best computing-related videos you've watched recently?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interesting anecdotes, like a chess machine seized by government","quote":"lots of interesting anecdotes, like getting a chess machine seized by the US government","text":"Two of the most interesting ones I've seen this year: Brian Kernighan (of K&R fame, and much more) interviewing Ken Thompson (initial author of UNIX, though you probably already knew both of these): lots of interesting anecdotes, like getting a chess machine seized by the US government: [VIDEO] Drew DeVault, (sway/sr.ht/much more author) hacking on his Wayland VR desktop inside of his Wayland VR desktop (don't believe the people who say you can't do programming inside of VR yet!): [LINK] NB. the second video is on a PeerTube instance. PeerTube is a federated and decentralized YouTube alternative."},{"id":21589163,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-11","story":"A Mystery of Unix History","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Very funny, entertaining stories even Kernighan hadn't heard","quote":"They're both very funny, a very entertaining video, some stories I (and even Brian) hadn't heard.","text":"Also I found these fascinating: The Unix-Haters Handbook (1994) has a lot of history and takes you back in time.. [LINK] Brian Kernighan 'interviews' Ken Thompson (2019) - Ken telling stories. They're both very funny, a very entertaining video, some stories I (and even Brian) hadn't heard. [VIDEO] UNIX: Making Computers Easier To Use (1982) [LINK] Also the other day I read in the AWK book that it was mainly inspired by SNOBOL4, so looked at some SNOBOL4 books from archive.org, from 1970-2 and just before, slightly earlier than Unix history takes you back. One tells you how to organize your punch cards to feed in the program.. Another had a bibliography with many dozens of SNOBOL books. It was widely used by people in the humanities apparently; like AWK it was at home dealing with words and text."},{"id":21378020,"author":"jen_h","month":"2019-10","story":"Unix: A History and a Memoir, by Brian Kernighan","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kernighan and Thompson speaking at VCF East","quote":"totally worth a watch","text":"Looking forward to reading this! Had the pleasure of seeing Brian Kernighan and Ken Thompson speak at VCF East earlier this year, totally worth a watch: [VIDEO] I highly recommend hitting up a Vintage Computer Festival if you've got the opportunity ( [LINK] ). Not only did Kernighan and Thompson speak, but also Joe Decuir of Atari & Amiga fame."},{"id":45088363,"author":"bentley","month":"2025-09","story":"Unix co-creator Brian Kernighan on Rust, distros and NixOS","stance":"rec","why":"Kernighan interviews Thompson at the same conference","quote":"Another worthwhile video from the same conference (in a different year) is Kernighan’s interview of Ken Thompson","text":"The article provides ancillary hyperlinks to nearly every topic mentioned, but fails to link the actual video of Kernighan speaking. Here it is: [LINK] In reality the subject of the talk is Kernighan’s perspective of the history of Unix at Bell Labs. The questions covered in the article are the least interesting part, and make up about six minutes at the very end—a small fraction not just of the 70-minute video, but even of the 30-minute Q&A session. In short, the headline and article are disappointing clickbait, but the video itself is worth watching for Kernighan’s relating of history and his theories about what confluence of factors made Unix successful (collegial environment, good management, overall mission…). Another worthwhile video from the same conference (in a different year) is Kernighan’s interview of Ken Thompson: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43112344,"author":"cjs_ac","month":"2025-02","story":"1972 Unix V2 \"Beta\" Resurrected","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long interview with many entertaining Thompson stories","quote":"One hour long, and Thompson tells a lot of entertaining stories.","text":"The interview is here: [VIDEO] One hour long, and Thompson tells a lot of entertaining stories. Kernighan does a good job of just letting Thompson speak."},{"id":33697781,"author":"caned","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: What story from the PDP-8 era would make a good script?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining stories of Bell Labs hiring Thompson and McIlroy's paper compiler","quote":"the story of Bell Labs going to extraordinary lengths to hire Ken Thompson is a hoot","text":"This isn't specifically PDP-8-related, but the story of Bell Labs going to extraordinary lengths to hire Ken Thompson is a hoot: [VIDEO] ...not to mention Ken's story of Doug McIlroy writing a compiler for TCG on a sheet of paper, then using it to compile itself - by hand - thus \"feeding his sheet of paper his sheet of paper.\""},{"id":30501983,"author":"tuckerpo","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Single-person creations that have stood the test of time?","stance":"rec","why":"Ken Thompson recounts creating Unix in about three weeks","quote":"Ken Thompson's monologue about creating Unix in 3 weeks","text":"See Ken Thompson's monologue about creating Unix in 3 weeks: [VIDEO] Programmers used to be pretty damn productive."},{"id":29911447,"author":"BenoitEssiambre","month":"2022-01","story":"Why don't I have a blog?","stance":"rec","why":"Short version of Kernighan's Unix book","quote":"this talk is quite something","text":"I found Kernighan's book quite riveting. If you want the short version (this might spoil some of the book) this talk is quite something: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25177720,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"rec","why":"Thompson tells how he got hired at Bell Labs","quote":"One of my favorite moments from the birth of UNIX","text":"One of my favorite moments from the birth of UNIX is Ken Thompson's telling (in this interview with Brian Kernighan) of how he got hired to work at Bell Labs: [VIDEO] (starting at about 11 minutes in to the video)"},{"id":25182238,"author":"dwcnnnghm","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"rec","why":"Ken describes McIlroy's remarkable TMG self-compilation and origin of B","quote":"in a very interesting conversation with Brian Kernighan about the history of Unix","text":"> Brian is super modest and claims to be a horrible programmer but he is comparing himself to Ken Thompson, who he thinks is just incredible. Ken once wrote a disassembler, assembler and B interpreter for a mini-computer that ran a printer they were struggling with, in a couple of days, so that they could get it printing again. This blew Brian's mind. For the next level up here's Ken (in a very interesting conversation with Brian Kernighan about the history of Unix) describing McIlroy: > McIlroy keeps coming up. He's the smartest of all of us and the least remembered (or written down)... McIlroy sat there and wrote ---on a piece of paper, now, not on a computer--- TMG [a proprietary yacc-like program] written in TMG... And then! He now has TMG written in TMG, he decided to give his piece of paper to his piece of paper and write down what came out (the code). Which he did. And then he came over to my editor and he typed in his code, assembled it, and (I won't say without error, but with so few errors you'd be astonished) he came up with a TMG compiler, on the PDP-7, written in TMG. And it's the most basic, bare, impressive self-compilation I've ever seen in my life. This story, by the way, leads into how Ken created B (and how that was eventually improved by Dennis Ritchie into C). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19896217,"author":"alister","month":"2019-05","story":"Brian Kernighan (Coined Word “Unix”) Interviews Ken Thompson (Co-Invented Unix)","stance":"rec","why":"Funny anecdotes on writing the Unix kernel in three weeks","quote":"Full of funny anecdotes like needing 3 weeks to write the kernel","text":"The interview starts at [VIDEO] Brian Kernighan coined the word “Unix” and is the “K” in K&R (co-author of the The C Programming Language book). Ken Thompson co-invented Unix. Full of funny anecdotes like needing 3 weeks to write the kernel and serendipitously his wife was going on vacation for 3 weeks."},{"id":41549183,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-09","story":"One in five genetics papers contains errors thanks to Excel (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Thompson interview covering pipes and dc's role in Unix","quote":"it goes on to pipes and dc makes an appearance there again","text":"The question that I'm curious about is \"why does POSIX require unary negation to have a higher precedence?\" Was it a \"this is the way that bc worked in the 70s because it was easier to write a parser for it?\" or was there some more underlying reason for the \"this problem gets really icky if unary negation has lower precedence than the binary operators and makes for other expressions that become less reasonable?\" It's like the Logical XOR issue ( [LINK] ) ... and I'm of the opinion that there's a real reason why this design choice was made. (Aside: Some other historical \"why things work that way\" touching on dc's place in history: Ken Thompson interviewed by Brian Kernighan at VCF East 2019 [VIDEO] - it goes on to pipes and dc makes an appearance there again)"},{"id":40774188,"author":"peoplefromibiza","month":"2024-06","story":"Ladybird browser spreads its wings","stance":"reference","why":"Thompson's story of writing an OS in three weeks","quote":"Ken Thompson's \"three weeks away from an OS","text":"> Has the rise of these memory safe languages caused any shift in the proficiencies of the average developer? That's a good question, but I have no answer for it. AFAIK the data is missing or is inconsistent. But I can link to you the obligatory Ken Thompson's \"three weeks away from an OS\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34666700,"author":"Egoist","month":"2023-02","story":"C Port of Ken Thompson's Space Travel","stance":"reference","why":"Thompson mentions a multiplayer game played with colleagues at Bell Labs","quote":"","text":"Not sure if it's based on this, but Ken also talked about a similar, but multiplayer game, to play with random people at Bell Labs which sounded fun. [VIDEO] I wonder if the source code for that exists too."},{"id":33218304,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2022-10","story":"TMG (language): A self-hosted compiler from scratch on paper","stance":"reference","why":"Thompson describes McIlroy hand-compiling a self-written compiler for TMG","quote":"Ken Thompson says it's one of the most impressive things he's ever seen in his life.","text":"Ken Thompson interviewed by Brian Kernighan at VCF East 2019 (min 39, sec 46) [VIDEO] Describes how Douglas McIlroy designed a language, wrote a compiler in the language that he designed that didn't exist yet and hand converted this compiler to assembly. Ken Thompson says it's one of the most impressive things he's ever seen in his life."},{"id":30355027,"author":"ghoward","month":"2022-02","story":"Lorinda Cherry, author of dc, bc, eqn has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As one of few authors of an implementation of `dc` and `bc`, but one who never actually met Lorinda Cherry, perhaps I have a slightly different perspective of her work. I've read the closest thing we have to the original sources of `dc` and `bc`: the source bundled with Plan 9. It didn't take me long to read the entirety of the source because they were simple and concise. That immensely impressed me. However, I hate to say that my youth (compared to Ms. Cherry, at least) caused me to look at the source with disdain, mostly from a lack of handling errors caused by user mistakes. It took several days for me to think more carefully about the context. She was writing for herself and other programmers, who would probably be able to recognize when they made a mistake and fix it. The code has a simple elegance that mine will never have. Sure, you might call mine \"industrial strength,\" but I think a quote by ** Gabriel sums up the difference between Ms. Cherry's code and mine: \"I'm always delighted by the light touch and stillness of early programming languages. Not much text; a lot gets done. Old programs read like quiet conversations between a well-spoken research worker and a well-studied mechanical colleague, not as a debate with a compiler. Who'd have guessed sophistication bought such noise?\" [1] And that says nothing of the design of her software. `dc` was, and in many ways still is, the simplest calculator that could ever exist. It was the simplest shell too, with the `!` com"},{"id":24855852,"author":"tpaschalis","month":"2020-10","story":"/* You Are Not Expected to Understand This */ (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Thompson wears a t-shirt of the famous comment","quote":"In [1] Brian Kernighan interviews Ken Thompson, who's wearing a t-shirt","text":"This comment has produced one of the geekiest t-shirts of all time. In [1] Brian Kernighan interviews Ken Thompson, who's wearing a t-shirt which contains the relevant snippet along with a comment \"ΕΠΙΤΕΛΟΥΣ ΤΟ ΚΑΤΑΛΑΒΑ!\" (greek for 'I finally understood it!'). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21316721,"author":"fjarlq","month":"2019-10","story":"Unix: A History and a Memoir, by Brian Kernighan","stance":"reference","why":"Ken Thompson telling Unix history stories","quote":"Videos of Ken Thompson and Rob Pike telling Unix history stories","text":"Videos of Ken Thompson and Rob Pike telling Unix history stories: Ken Thompson (interviewed by Brian Kernighan, 2019, starts after 7m38s): [VIDEO] Rob Pike (Unix History presentation, 2018, starts after 3m40s): [LINK]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["hSrGfElyfVE",1]]},"uxykI30fS54":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as the entry point to a channel on urban planning and car dependency in North America.","q":{"x":"I watched an excellent video about Houston","i":31799513,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36491585,"author":"merek","month":"2023-06","story":"U.S. pedestrian deaths reach a 40-year high","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short walk in Houston shows pedestrian conditions.","quote":"I highly recommend the Not Just Bikes youtube channel.","text":"For those interested in urban planning, including contrasts between the US and places that do it well, I highly recommend the Not Just Bikes youtube channel. The intro video details a short walk in Houston, which is pretty alarming: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31799513,"author":"rendall","month":"2022-06","story":"Construction is life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains pushback against sprawl and poor urban design.","quote":"I watched an excellent video about Houston","text":"> The reality is the western world is falling behind in essential infrastructure in every capacity. Could you expand on this? What do you mean? > Its hard to build roads, highways, sewers these days without some pushback. We need to start changing our views and start seeing constriction as an essential human need and return to the old view of infrastructure as something we take pride in For me, it's not \"construction\" by itself, but I suspect it's pushback against urban sprawl and poor urban design. I watched an excellent video about Houston: [VIDEO] It takes awhile to get to the point, so you might want to skip to 3:42."},{"id":27903835,"author":"benrbray","month":"2021-07","story":"Twike","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed comparisons of US cities with the Netherlands.","quote":"I recommend the Not Just Bikes channel.","text":"For anyone who has trouble understanding this perspective because they've lived in car-dependent hell their entire lives, I recommend the Not Just Bikes channel. He does extremely detailed comparisons of US cities vs Netherlands and other places. Here is a recent one [1]. For me, I always felt that something was wrong about American cities / suburbs but I couldn't quite put my finger on it. Then, I spent a few months in Tokyo and something \"clicked\". It's an entirely different paradigm that forced me to reevaluate what I thought was possible. When I returned to the US, on the ride from the airport my first thought was: Everyone in this traffic jam is going the same direction, and would fit in a handful of train cars. Why are we OK with this?? People say trains are expensive, but they don't realize how much money we spend on cars. Americans spent $1.1 TRILLION on personal vehicles in 2017 [2], not including the hundreds of billions spent yearly on road maintenance. Redirecting a fraction of that money would more than pay for high-speed rail. Let's do a crude estimate. Using the chart here [3], we can expect to pay somewhere between $3-163 million USD per mile of high-speed rail. The distance from NY to LA is 2789.9 miles. At $100M/mile, which is excessive, it would still only cost $279 billion. We could build THREE transcontinental high-speed rail lines every year with the money we spend on personal automobiles. If we're as cost-effective as Japan at $2.6 M / mile, we could af"},{"id":28016024,"author":"tux1968","month":"2021-07","story":"Pedestrian fatalities are rising in the US","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows why walking is dangerous in North American cities.","quote":"Can't recommend this guys entire channel of videos enough","text":"It also has a lot with the car-centric nature of urban planning. Can't recommend this guys entire channel of videos enough: [LINK] He delves into a movement known as \"Strong Towns\" that look to undo a lot of the mistakes city planners have been making for the last half century. Edit: Here's a link to another of his videos where he shows why it is dangerous to walk in many North American cities: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37110317,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-08","story":"Texas could get a bullet train between Houston and Dallas","stance":"rec","why":"Houston walkability, among transit talking points.","quote":"There are several talking points mentioned in this thread that are explored more on the NotJustBikes YouTube Channel.","text":"There are several talking points mentioned in this thread that are explored more on the NotJustBikes YouTube Channel. - When Canada built a relatively recent urban rail to Toronto, they built it so you have to drive to the train station , and used the high value land right around the station for ... surface car parking. This is absurdly wasteful of valuable real estate and makes the expensive train add much less value to the people. Also the train only runs around rush hours, making it useless for almost anyone who isn't driving to the train for a commute to work. \"The Trains that Subsidize Suburbia - GO Transit Commuter Rail\" - [LINK] - \"Why city design is import (and why I hate Houston)\" [it's not walkable] - [VIDEO] - How American urban transit is built misunderstanding the way transit needs to work to be successful, notably trying to build transit as a bandaid over car problems, but not making it regular, frequent, reliable, fast, convenient, instead plonking an isolated transit connection from nowhere to nowhere which runs once every couple of hours and then pointing to it as an example of how transit doesn't work in America - \"America Always Gets This Wrong (when building transit)\" - [LINK] - Addressing the \"America is too big and spread out for rail\" arguments, notably that USA and Canada were built by trains, and streetcar suburbs, and that most journes Americans take every day are less than 5 miles. - \"The Dumbest Excuse for Bad Cities\" - [LINK]"},{"id":33547518,"author":"dementis","month":"2022-11","story":"The Deadliest Road in America","stance":"rec","why":"Car-dependent city design in the US and Canada.","quote":"This vid has a good take on the problem of car dependent city designs","text":"Deadliest road for pedestrians(in the US). This vid has a good take on the problem of car dependent city designs that is very predominant in the US and Canada. Why City Design is Important (and Why I Hate Houston) [VIDEO]"},{"id":33028135,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2022-09","story":"KittyHawk air taxi startup is winding down","stance":"rec","why":"Argues planners destroyed walkability, favoring big box stores.","quote":"For a detailed argument see the YouTube channel Not Just Bikes","text":"Do you have a source for this claim? All the urbanists will tell you that small grocery stores are a joy to use in Europe due to transit oriented development that makes quick trips by bike to grab groceries fast and easy. But in North America we’ve built out larger and larger car-centric developments which have become so hostile to anyone not in a car that people are forced to drive to the grocery store. This makes the experience way less enjoyable than a quick trip by bike to a small store, the urbanists will say. So your claim is that small stores were essentially selected out of existence by customers, but it may in fact be the case that city planners destroyed walkability in a way that left big box stores as the only viable option. Those two things are not the same, as the theoretical “we could have more smaller stores accessible by bike and foot traffic” would be true in the latter case. For a detailed argument see the YouTube channel Not Just Bikes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32188450,"author":"xputer","month":"2022-07","story":"Why we ignore thousands of daily car crashes","stance":"rec","why":"US lacks safe road infrastructure.","quote":"Watch some videos from this channel","text":"Watch some videos from this channel and you'll be able to see how the US definitely does not do enough in terms of creating safe road infrastructure for all: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30165505,"author":"marto1","month":"2022-02","story":"Can American Cars Be Made Safer for Pedestrians?","stance":"rec","why":"American infrastructure hostile to walking and biking.","quote":"I'd recommend the NotJustBikes youtube channel that really drives the point home","text":"Oh, the rabbit hole goes much much deeper. American infrastructure is actively hostile to people walking/biking/doing anything else than driving and even then not very safe. I'd recommend the NotJustBikes youtube channel that really drives the point home, e.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28848431,"author":"tdeck","month":"2021-10","story":"Show HN: Identify car crash editorial anti-patterns using NLP","stance":"rec","why":"Typical US pedestrian journey in Houston.","quote":"watch this video from about 4:25 on showing a typical urban pedestrian journey the US","text":"People in the US have developed an aversion to walking because our built environment makes it terrible. For example, watch this video from about 4:25 on showing a typical urban pedestrian journey the US: [VIDEO] (One thing the narrator mentions is shade: Keep in mind that Houston is a hot city, with temperatures often above 30c during the summer.) But that's not unusual at all. In fact, where I grew up in Pennsylvania many roads had literally no sidewalk. Let's say I want to walk into town where my parents live. I'd be going down this road (you can follow along on street view): [LINK] That's a narrow road, but don't let it fool you. People there drive fast and they're in a hurry. They certainly do not expect to see someone trudging along the berm of the road. Or more likely in a muddy ditch beside the road, because Pennsylvania is wet and that's the safest way to not get hit by a car. If you walk along into North Wales, PA, pay attention to the pedestrian infrastructure. Where are there sidewalks? Are there crossing signals? How would you know when it's safe to cross? Now imagine doing this in the evening (note: there are no streetlights)."},{"id":28625906,"author":"shafyy","month":"2021-09","story":"Airbus reveals the next generation of CityAirbus","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Houston city planning to other cities.","quote":"Here's a good video that compares the city planning of Houston to other cities, and explains why it sucks.","text":"I'm not going to argue with you about the different definitions of public transportation, but for sure \"taxi\" isn't what comes to mind when someone mentions public transportation. The point you're making is exactly the problem. The reason bus lines aren't used more is because they suck in a lot of cities. Improving the public transportation infrastucture (and no, not taxis), would solve this problem - as demonstrated in many European cities. Here's a good video that compares the city planning of Houston to other cities, and explains why it sucks. It focuses more on cars vs. bikes, but the same basic point also applies to public transportation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44774730,"author":"masklinn","month":"2025-08","story":"Helsinki records zero traffic deaths for full year","stance":"reference","why":"Critique of Houston's city design.","quote":"","text":"> well-designed human-oriented city like Houston Said no urban planner in the history of urban planning. Or NJB ( [VIDEO] ) > FASTER commutes than ANY similar-sized city in Europe. Houston ranks 7th worst traffic in the US. The internet tells me you’re boasting of 30mn for an “average 6 miles commute”. That’s bicycle distance and speed that you need to drive due to a broken city."},{"id":44774839,"author":"cyberax","month":"2025-08","story":"Helsinki records zero traffic deaths for full year","stance":"reference","why":"Cited by another commenter as critic of Houston.","quote":"","text":"> Said no urban planner in the history of urban planning. Or NJB ( [VIDEO] ) Wrong. Houston is a great example for planners who care about housing availability and the quality of life for the people. And not bike lanes and road diets. > Houston ranks 7th worst traffic in the US. Yes. And the 7th worst traffic in the US is STILL BETTER than any large European city's oh-so-great transit. Tells you volumes, doesn't it?"},{"id":43442424,"author":"Mawr","month":"2025-03","story":"Congestion Pricing Is a Policy Miracle","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch streets as example of better environments.","quote":"","text":"> So make cars pollute less Physically impossible to have them pollute less than methods of mass transportation. Mathematically, even. Just divide the number of people carried per pollution caused by cars vs trains, bikes, or even buses. There's nothing you can do to make cars competitive. > Figure out how to filter microplastics from our food and water \"Just figure it out\", ok. But even then, an ounce of prevention is worth the pound of a cure. > Figure out what's causing asthma in children A little random but ok. I'm guessing grinding up rubber and metal particles into the air right next to where said children live isn't the best for their lungs. > Not sure how obesity is related. Maybe improve the food supply? 1. Wake up 2. Walk 30 feet to your car 3. Sit as you drive to work 4. Spend 8 hours at your desk 5. Walk 30 feet to your car 6. Sleep 7. Why am I fat?! Maybe improve the food supply?! > A lot of these are solved with electric cars which are increasingly more competitive. Virtually none are, an electric car is still a car, see point 1. > Why force people to regress towards a worse product rather than address these technical issues? Highly dependent on your definition of worse; I'd argue the sort of streets and environments you can find in the Netherlands [1][2][3] are way better than in the US. Not everything is a technical issue that can simply be solved by better engineering. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38045276,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-10","story":"Cruise pauses driverless operations across all cities","stance":"reference","why":"Cost of adding highway lanes.","quote":"","text":"You're comparing the cost of only self driving tech vs the cost of the entire rail infrastructure. • Underground parking for 600 cars? 35 million euro - [LINK] • Rebuilding a highway? Half of the transportation budget gone - [LINK] • Adding just one more lane bro to a freeway? $2.2 billion - [VIDEO] Take into account the money saved from scaling back car infrastructure: • lower obvious direct building and maintenance costs • reduced healthcare spending - increased activity levels [1], lower local pollution [2] • lower stress thanks to reduced noise levels • less of a need to spend money on counteracting the negative effects of climate change • freeing up a significant chunk of the valuable ~30% of space in cities taken up by oversized roads and parking lots -> more housing, denser cities -> less travel needed -> lower costs Sum all that up and then tell me self-driving is cheaper than rail. [1]: \"If every Londoner walked or cycled for 20 minutes a day we would prevent 1 in 6 early deaths and save the NHS 1.7bn pounds in treatment costs over the next 25 years.\" ( [LINK] ) [2]: \"Each year air pollution causes thousands of Londoners to die prematurely and is associated with an increased risk of Londoners developing life-changing illnesses like cancer, lung disease, dementia and asthma. [...] So far, the ULEZ has contributed to over 1 million hospital admissions averted by 2050.\" ( [LINK] )"},{"id":35048630,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-03","story":"Pickup Trucks: From Workhorse to Joyride","stance":"reference","why":"Backstory of the creator's experience in many cities.","quote":"He used to consult all over the world and has visited many cities around the world","text":"> This guy makes some good points, but jeez he is so condescending and pretentious. It's really easy for a guy who makes YouTube videos and lives in a crowded European city to pass judgement on people who live lives completely different than his. He has lived in those \"completely different\" places. He grew up in one of those areas: he moved to a crowded European city. He grew up in London, Ontario (\"fake London\"), lived in Toronto, lived all over the world. He used to consult all over the world and has visited many cities around the world: see his video \"Why City Design is Important (and Why I Hate Houston)\" for some of the backstory: * [VIDEO] After seeing all the different options, and initially trying to settle in Toronto—see his video \"Suburbs that don't Suck - Streetcar Suburbs (Riverdale, Toronto)\": * [LINK] he and his wife decided to move to Amsterdam to raise their kids. It wasn't random chance that he ended in Europe, but a conscious choice . He looked at the options and chose the one that he though was best. And he has been asked about the 'tone' of this videos: his answer was/is: he doesn't care about how he comes off because (in his opinion) trying to be \"nice\" to the dumbasses that try to justify the stupidity of car-centric society that is destroying our planet is pointless. Either you have a brain and can see how bad this way of thinking/designing is, or you don't have a brain. He's tired of pussy footing around the (in his view) stupidity."},{"id":32360800,"author":"novok","month":"2022-08","story":"Marc Andreessen says he’s for new housing, but records tell a different story","stance":"reference","why":"Car-mandatory suburban design experience in Houston.","quote":"Look at pretty much an identical experience in Houston","text":"He was talking about the car-mandatory suburban design of the USA, which is pretty much everywhere by law outside of a few grandfathered places that were built before the car was invented and some commercial down towns. They also tend to be very expensive since they are in short supply and high demand. It has nothing to do with the people or culture per say and his experience exists everywhere! Look at pretty much an identical experience in Houston: [VIDEO] . The channel also has few examples of the same thing in london, ontario, canada. In Europe, small town life is not necessarily suburban life, there are a lot of of walk-able villages connected via a train line that you could take to commute to your job in city, and they're wayyyyyyyyyy better than the sadness that is US & Canadian suburbia."},{"id":32178719,"author":"hourago","month":"2022-07","story":"Congress' push to regulate Big Tech is fizzling out","stance":"reference","why":"Cities designed around cars.","quote":"Cities were designed to push for cars.","text":"Not just bikes: [VIDEO] It's an example of what I'm referring to. Cities were designed to push for cars. People wanted cars because cities were designed to exclude people that didn't have one."},{"id":29050771,"author":"novok","month":"2021-10","story":"U.S. house prices are rising exponentially faster than income","stance":"reference","why":"Why American suburbs are car dependent.","quote":"More about it","text":"When people say cities, they talk about them in the global context with functioning metro systems, not the sprawling monster american 'suburities' that require you to drive everywhere. If you want less suburities with their shit traffic, you'd support getting rid of zoning laws that force this form of city. I don't like US 'cities'. I do like cities elsewhere in the world. More about it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kYHTzqHIngk",2],["bnKIVX968PQ",2],["ORzNZUeUHAM",2],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1]]},"d2lJUOv0hLA":{"wy":"HN commenters strongly recommend it as an audiobook-length, deeply informative introduction to Sumer and early Mesopotamian civilization.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the _Fall of Civilizations Podcast_ episode 8.","i":33298127,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33578055,"author":"Strom","month":"2022-11","story":"Ur","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Episode covering Ur and Sumer","quote":"Fall of Civilizations is absolutely amazing stuff, I double recommend it.","text":"Fall of Civilizations is absolutely amazing stuff, I double recommend it. It has an episode that covers this specific topic as well - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33298127,"author":"wanderingstan","month":"2022-10","story":"Drone photos reveal an early Mesopotamian city made of marsh islands","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Audiobook-length episode teaching a ton about Sumerian civilization","quote":"I highly recommend the _Fall of Civilizations Podcast_ episode 8.","text":"If you’re interested in the history of Lagash and the wider Sumerian civilization, I highly recommend the _Fall of Civilizations Podcast_ episode 8. [VIDEO] At 2.5 hours it’s more like an audiobook. During the pandemic this was my gateway into learning a ton about this ancient civilization, when humanity first began to write and thus be able to communicate with us thousands of years later."},{"id":34861824,"author":"wanderingstan","month":"2023-02","story":"Discovery of 4,500-year-old palace in Iraq may hold key to ancient civilisation","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent introduction to Sumerian history","quote":"I was introduced to Sumerian history by this excellent episode","text":"Related: I was introduced to Sumerian history by this excellent episode of the “Fall of Civilizations Podcast”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33578105,"author":"Strom","month":"2022-11","story":"Ur","stance":"rec","why":"Visual version of the episode","quote":"It's a great episode and I also recommend it.","text":"It's a great episode and I also recommend it. For those who wish to have something visual the better link would be [VIDEO]"},{"id":31458455,"author":"davedx","month":"2022-05","story":"Ancient civilisation under eastern Turkey estimated to be 11k-13k years old","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into Sumerian history and language","quote":"This YouTube series is a great deep dive into it all","text":"Yeah, the sea level rise thing is one explanation for why Sumerian is a language isolate. This YouTube series is a great deep dive into it all: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29450394,"author":"miltondts","month":"2021-12","story":"The sea people: Alexander the Great trod in the footsteps of forgotten Greeks","stance":"rec","why":"First cities were advanced and lasted centuries","quote":"I especially enjoyed","text":"And one more resource with the history of many civilizations: [LINK] I especially enjoyed [VIDEO] , as I had no idea the first cities were already so advanced and lasted for so many centuries. EDIT: fixed youtube link."},{"id":29677098,"author":"miltondts","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: What is the best podcast you listened to in 2021?","stance":"rec","why":"First cities were advanced and lasted centuries","quote":"I especially enjoyed","text":"Fall of civilizations has the history of many: [LINK] I especially enjoyed [VIDEO] , as I had no idea the first cities were already so advanced and lasted for so many centuries."},{"id":28571766,"author":"mannerheim","month":"2021-09","story":"Life-size camel sculptures in Saudi Arabia are older than Stonehenge, pyramids","stance":"rec","why":"Covers collapse of Mesopotamian civilizations","quote":"I recommend the Fall of Civilizations podcast for more on this and other societies","text":"Arizona is very hot, very arid, and also very favourable for agriculture, with significant production of lettuce and cotton. As long as you can make sure your crops get water through irrigation (which ancient Mesopotamians were very skilled at), your crops will thrive. In fact, the heat and sunlight actually provide a bit of an advantage. However, a number of factors eventually led to the collapse of the civilisations in the fertile crescent. One factor has been the region getting warmer and drier, which poses a challenge even with irrigation. As an example of how different the climate is now from what it was then, Uruk used to be on a channel of the Euphrates, but now that riverbed is completely dried up. Meanwhile, Ur was once a coastal city, but is now ten miles inland. Another factor for the collapse of these civilisations is that the process of irrigation accumulated salt in the soil. The very process that made complex civilisation possible in this part of the world eventually contributed to its end. I recommend the Fall of Civilizations podcast for more on this and other societies: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28173672,"author":"andyxor","month":"2021-08","story":"Archaeologists discover 4k-year-old ancient city in Iraqi desert","stance":"rec","why":"Series on Sumer and what preceded it","quote":"btw this is great series on that time (and what preceded it)","text":"this is just 30km from Ur, I thought Akkad capital was more to the north , in the traditionally Akkadian territory [LINK] btw this is great series on that time (and what preceded it) \"The Sumerians - Fall of the First Cities\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":26011016,"author":"wanderingstan","month":"2021-02","story":"How to write cuneiform","stance":"rec","why":"Sparked deeper interest in cuneiform","quote":"after listening to the excellent “Fall of Civilizations Podcast” episode about Sumer","text":"I went down the cuneiform rabbit hole last summer after listening to the excellent “Fall of Civilizations Podcast” episode about Sumer: [VIDEO] This led to many of Finkel’s videos and the book mentioned in this post. (So far I’m not too impressed with the book, TBH.)"},{"id":25210080,"author":"oblio","month":"2020-11","story":"1% of farms operate 70% of world's farmland","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-plus podcast on Fertile Crescent civilization","quote":"If you have 1 hour, hear/listen to this podcast/video","text":"> The producer of \"civilization and culture\"? In what sense? Farms produce the goods we eat, how does that lead to culture and civilization? I'm not that knowledgeable at history - if that is the case, then I'd really like to read more about it. Well, I recommend any history book about the Fertile Crescent and Ancient Egypt. If you have 1 hour, hear/listen to this podcast/video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30982926,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2022-04","story":"Why are there 360° in a circle?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of a clip on why 360 degrees","quote":"Full show","text":"I clipped this from Fall of Civilizations - The Sumerians - Fall of the First Cities. I'm not sure if they got it right, but it sounds good. (not monetizing, so not promoting) [LINK] Full show [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["bAxRYrpbnuA",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["za56H2BGamQ",1],["wOmjnioNulo",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1]]},"ZUvGfuLlZus":{"wy":"HN commenters call it an absorbing, entertaining deep dive on elevator security and the go-to link whenever elevators come up.","q":{"x":"it was so interesting to me that I ended up watching the entire 2 hours in one sitting.","i":27777999,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":49129276,"author":"nhhvhy","month":"2026-07","story":"Elevators","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Personal favorite among Deviant Ollam's elevator talks.","quote":"My personal favorite","text":"If you feel like going down an elevator rabbithole, Deviant Ollam has quite a few talks/videos about all sorts of niche elevator stuff. My personal favorite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27777017,"author":"royjacobs","month":"2021-07","story":"List of Special Elevator Modes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highly recommended talk on elevator hacking.","quote":"I would also highly recommend this talk","text":"If you're interested in elevators and the hacking thereof I would also highly recommend this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27777999,"author":"mpd","month":"2021-07","story":"List of Special Elevator Modes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Absorbing two-hour talk watched in one sitting.","quote":"it was so interesting to me that I ended up watching the entire 2 hours in one sitting.","text":"I ran into [VIDEO] (Elevator Hacking: From the Pit to the Penthouse) awhile back, and it was so interesting to me that I ended up watching the entire 2 hours in one sitting."},{"id":20187952,"author":"thejenk","month":"2019-06","story":"I'll Let Myself In: Tactics of Physical Pen Testers (2017) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive on elevator security with consultant Howard Payne.","quote":"just about elevator security which is fantastic.","text":"Deviant Ollam does another talk with elevator consultant Howard Payne just about elevator security which is fantastic. It's very similar to this one, but goes deeper into the single subject. There are a few versions on YouTube of various lengths, but here's the 2 hour version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17477982,"author":"unixhero","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What talks or videos do you suggest to watch on a long flight?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full two-hour version with all the secrets.","quote":"I recommend the full 2 hour version, with all the secrets","text":"Yes. That's the shorter censored versoion. I recommend the full 2 hour version, with all the secrets: [VIDEO] My second favourite talk: You spent all that money and your still got owned: [LINK] I even watch these again and again."},{"id":11575019,"author":"_asummers","month":"2016-04","story":"Mosh: the mobile shell","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining talk explaining how elevator safety measures work.","quote":"watch this[0] entire (super entertaining) talk from Hope X about elevator security.","text":"And watch this[0] entire (super entertaining) talk from Hope X about elevator security. You get a pretty good feal for how the safety measures work after watching it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27781389,"author":"ben_bai","month":"2021-07","story":"List of Special Elevator Modes","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on elevators.","quote":"this is a great talk if you want to know more about elevators","text":"SimTower started as elevator simulator, and was then turned into a game. Also, this is a great talk if you want to know more about elevators: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27489331,"author":"wolrah","month":"2021-06","story":"Elevator Saga – An elevator programming game","stance":"rec","why":"Related elevator video by the same creators.","quote":"I have to also mention another video of theirs","text":"Relevant video discussing fire keys and other common keys: [LINK] And since this thread is about elevators, I have to also mention another video of theirs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18741370,"author":"lathiat","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Two-hour elevator talk with Howard Payne.","quote":"his elevator with Howard Payne talk","text":"Deviant Ollam - The Hotel Room Gourmet [LINK] Also not 2018 but his elevator with Howard Payne talk: [LINK] (1h version) [VIDEO] (2h version) And the search for the perfect door: [LINK]"},{"id":18695414,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2018-12","story":"Can Repelling Magnets Replace the Spring in a Pogo Stick?","stance":"reference","why":"Covers elevator buffers at the bottom of the shaft.","quote":"There was an elevator hacking talk that went into the buffers at the bottom of the elevator.","text":"There was an elevator hacking talk that went into the buffers at the bottom of the elevator. You may have seen them in elevators that have glass on the outside of the shaft like in malls and hotels. The elevator never rests on those. Those are to take the impact of an elevator that crashes. Inspectors will often test the buffers as well as part of routine maintenance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11267179,"author":"eatkinson","month":"2016-03","story":"In one single image, mainstream media show their utter ignorance of security","stance":"reference","why":"Shows elevators are easy to hack.","quote":"elevators should never be a part of a building's security because they're so easy to hack[1].","text":"I don't know about subways, but elevators should never be a part of a building's security because they're so easy to hack[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8530710,"author":"maqr","month":"2014-10","story":"Rappelling Off a Roof","stance":"reference","why":"Elevator access-control card restrictions and bypasses.","quote":"","text":"> After a week of recon I found out that the 4th and 5th floors are only accessible with a very specific card via the elevator. I didn’t have that card. Even if I get to the elevator I could not go to the 5th floor See [VIDEO]"},{"id":20348018,"author":"alanfalcon","month":"2019-07","story":"Why did moving the mouse cursor cause Windows 95 to run more quickly?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the button frequently does nothing because you frequently lack the proper permissions...","text":"It is perhaps more appropriate to say the button frequently does nothing because you frequently lack the proper permissions... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["a9b9IYqsb_U",2],["J1q4Ir2J8P8",1],["FnEWBtCnFs8",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1],["eECjjLNAOd4",1]]},"Tb823aqgX_0":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as the talk that made data-oriented, REPL-driven bottom-up design click.","q":{"x":"is the talk that really made it clear to me how to design programs this way.","i":26943836,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26943836,"author":"robto","month":"2021-04","story":"Making a Lego Domino Machine [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made clear how to design programs bottom-up with a REPL.","quote":"is the talk that really made it clear to me how to design programs this way.","text":"This strikes me as a perfect visual example of repl-driven development. I like how he starts very simple and slowly makes it more sophisticated, testing every step of the way. If you've never tried it before, I highly recommend giving it a shot - it's extremely satisfying. I use Clojure, but other lisps also have fantastic repl environments that facilitate this style of development. \"Bottom Up vs Top Down Design in Clojure\"[0] is the talk that really made it clear to me how to design programs this way. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19709538,"author":"beders","month":"2019-04","story":"Show HN: Tetris Implemented in ClojureScript","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highlights how top-down design creates needless complexity.","quote":"Here's a good video highlighting the difference of approach. Well worth watching.","text":"I picked on needless complexity due to top-down design not on static typing per se. Here's a good video highlighting the difference of approach. Well worth watching. Applies to how many Java architects would design a solution. I see the same kind of top-down approach happening in the Typescript area as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16471190,"author":"lewisl9029","month":"2018-02","story":"How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the State Machine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Practical demonstration of data-oriented modeling versus OOP for a board game.","quote":"I highly recommend this talk from Mark Bastian in Clojure/conj 2015","text":"This is exactly my impression after taking a quick glance through these articles as well. If anyone is curious and would like to see a practical demonstration of this data-oriented approach of modelling problems, I highly recommend this talk from Mark Bastian in Clojure/conj 2015: [VIDEO] In it, he contrasts the data-oriented modelling approach with a traditional OOP approach for implementing a board game, and was able to come up with a complete implementation of the game with the former approach that was shorter than even the structural boilerplate for the OOP version. Another related talk that I loved was Chris Granger's 2013 talk on Light Table: [LINK] Where he walks through his process of building a game in ClojureScript using an Entity-Component-System architecture, which is very well suited to this data oriented modeling approach."},{"id":11269126,"author":"lewisl9029","month":"2016-03","story":"A Database Model for Simple Board Games","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Contrasts data-oriented and OO modeling of a board game with big simplicity gains.","quote":"One of my favorite videos from Clojure Conj last year","text":"I personally prefer a more data-oriented approach to modeling, as opposed to modeling in terms of objects and relations. One of my favorite videos from Clojure Conj last year talks about the approach in more detail and contrasts it with traditional OO modeling in the context of a board game: [VIDEO] In summary, modeling the board game with data (state) and pure functions (state transitions) had significant benefits over the alternative OO style model in terms of the overall simplicity of the model. The OO modeling approach resulted in a model that consisted of 22 classes, 11 interfaces, 1 enum, and 271 lines of boilerplate code just to implement the classes and relationships alone, with no game logic whatsoever. On the other hand, the data-oriented approach produced an entire working implementation of the game logic in 144 lines using 31 functions (small and highly composable functions, longest of which was 7 lines). My takeaway was that modeling in the traditional OO style can be a helpful process in getting a good feel for your problem domain at a high level, but trying to immediately translate the resulting relationship model into code is not necessarily a good idea, because the classical OO relationship model is inherently highly complected, and will bring that complexity straight into your codebase if you try to translate it word-for-word. Taking the insights from your relational model and using it as a guide to help model your problem domain as data and functions usuall"},{"id":13493885,"author":"abc_lisper","month":"2017-01","story":"Making the move from Scala to Go","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Clojure as a good tool for bottom-up design.","quote":"It is however a very good tool to do bottom up design.","text":"I see what you are trying to say. You are saying Clojure is not the right tool to do top-down design. I agree with it. It is however a very good tool to do bottom up design. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":12720335,"author":"cygned","month":"2016-10","story":"Modern Software Over-Engineering Mistakes","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on data-driven development making implementations easier.","quote":"I think, a \"Data Driven Development\" approach would make a lot of implementations easier.","text":"I think, a \"Data Driven Development\" approach would make a lot of implementations easier. Something as described in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11036912,"author":"lewisl9029","month":"2016-02","story":"Ask HN: Why don't more web projects start with a JVM stack?","stance":"rec","why":"Board game as 22 classes in OOP versus 144 lines of Clojure.","quote":"Here's a more realistic comparison between classic Java OOP design vs the more data-oriented approach to design encouraged in functional languages like Clojure","text":"Thanks for the link. That made my day. =) Here's a more realistic comparison between classic Java OOP design vs the more data-oriented approach to design encouraged in functional languages like Clojure for a simple board game: [VIDEO] TL;DW: The classic OOP design of the board game ended up with 22 classes, 11 interfaces, 1 enum in total, with 271 lines of pure skeleton code with no implementation of any game logic whatsoever. The class diagram can be seen here for a better sense of the complexity involved: [VIDEO] The data-oriented design had 31 functions, longest of which is 7 lines, with a grand total of 144 lines of code that implements the entirety of the game logic."}],"r":[["WxK11RsLqp4",1],["yCcWpzY8dIA",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["a1rC79DHpmY",1],["f84n5oFoZBc",1],["Uj6skZIxPuI",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1]]},"nm1TxQj9IsQ":{"wy":"HN users repeatedly credit it with fixing their sleep, especially its advice on morning light and dimming lights in the evening.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend it. The key is light.","i":29354445,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30286969,"author":"moshferatu","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Sleep supplements that worked for you (excluding melatonin)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"This episode started fixing the commenter's sleep problems","quote":"This episode in particular is what started me down the path to correcting my sleep","text":"I essentially solved all of my sleep problems by doing the following: Getting 15-30 minutes of bright light exposure first thing in the morning. Ideally this would be sunlight, but I don't have that option this time of year based on how early I wake up (I'm in the PNW and wake up at 6am), so I use a sun lamp I bought on Amazon. Likewise, minimizing blue / bright light after the sun goes down. If I need to use my computer, I have flux installed and set to the lowest brightness setting. I do something similar for my phone as well. Supplementing glycine, magnesium glycinate (which also contains glycine), l-theanine, and ashwaghanda in the evening before bed. Glycine has had the most impact on my sleep. It's as if I had been exiled from dream-land before, but now I've been allowed back! My sleep is so much deeper. Note: I bought the BulkSupplements powdered version on Amazon and take 3-5x the recommended dose in my evening smoothie. I've found megadosing magnesium in this way also helps. The recommended doses seem to be way too low, especially if you happen to be deficient. As someone who spends a significant portion of the day in front of a screen, I can't emphasize enough how important managing light exposure throughout the day is. Check out the Huberman Lab podcast if you haven't already. This episode in particular is what started me down the path to correcting my sleep: [VIDEO] After watching that episode, I used my sun lamp to dial in my circadian rhythm over the course of a"},{"id":30001844,"author":"musesum","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Burned Out. What Now?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Huberman sleep protocol sped burnout recovery","quote":"I followed the Huberman protocol for sleep [1] and daily tools [2], with profound results.","text":"I've gone through two burnouts in my career. The first time around, I tried CBT (Cognitive Behavior Therapy), with limited results. Recently, I followed the Huberman protocol for sleep [1] and daily tools [2], with profound results. Recovery time was respectively 12 months (CBT) and 3 months (Huberman). YMMV [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29354445,"author":"yamrzou","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How did you correct your sleep patterns?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fixed sleep via light timing from this episode","quote":"I highly recommend it. The key is light.","text":"I was the same as you, and I was recently able to correct my sleep patterns. Now, I naturally wake up without using an alarm. What helped me was watching Huberman Lab's episode on sleep: [VIDEO] I highly recommend it. The key is light. Light controls your circadian rythm (biological clock), so make sure you get enough sunlight in the morning, and make sure to avoid light exposure after 10pm. For more: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":27473999,"author":"makison","month":"2021-06","story":"Why Can’t I Sleep?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed brain explanations, several episodes devoted to sleep","quote":"The whole podcast is great.","text":"I have found Huberman podcast incredibly helpful - in detail explanation(too much sometimes) of how brain works. There are 4 episodes(1.5h each) dedicated just to sleep. The whole podcast is great. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31073131,"author":"moshferatu","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: What did you do that got you to wake up earlier?","stance":"rec","why":"Bright light on waking shifted the biological clock","quote":"I learned of this from the Huberman Lab Podcast","text":"I managed to wake up earlier by ensuring that I got 15-30 minutes of bright light exposure immediately upon waking. Sunlight is ideal, but I'm in the PNW and it was winter at the time, so that wasn't an option for me. Instead, I bought a sun lamp on Amazon. The particular model is no longer available, but it was a 10,000 lux lamp that I placed right below my monitor. The incredible thing is that it took less than a week before I was waking up before 6am without the use of an alarm clock. I learned of this from the Huberman Lab Podcast, specifically the part where he touches on advancing your biological clock here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28817540,"author":"mabil","month":"2021-10","story":"Blue light from monitors no danger to eyes or sleep","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman explains how blue light affects sleep","quote":"Andrew Huberman goes to great extent explaining the effects of pretty much everything related to neurobiology","text":"Andrew Huberman goes to great extent explaining the effects of pretty much everything related to neurobiology, here he talks about blue light and how it affects sleep. [VIDEO] Also a great science based podcast. edit: context"},{"id":27472769,"author":"slothtrop","month":"2021-06","story":"Why Can’t I Sleep?","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on sleep","quote":"Andrew Huberman's video on sleep is also quite good","text":"This prattles on too much about things that don't answer the titled question. The list of problems is fine, but also light on helpful solutions. This guide is more comprehensive - [LINK] , and Andrew Huberman's video on sleep is also quite good - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25872613,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: How To Overcome Information Addiction?","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman has good tips on sleep and body needs","quote":"Prof Andrew Huberman has some good tips[2].","text":"A. Meet your basic biological needs. Your brain is a part of your body. A person with an iron deficiency can feel compelled to chew ice[1]. Ice does not cures anemia, brains are weird parts of the body and do weird things when the body is not getting what it needs. So, get better sleep, food, and exercise. Prof Andrew Huberman has some good tips[2]. B. Decide what few things you really value and allocate your time to those on purpose. If you find yourself repeatedly drawn to the news, maybe you are genuinely give a fuck about civic engagement. Your personality has a need for it. Trying to meet that need by scrolling twitter is like eating 3 bags of tortilla chips for dinner -- quick-access, but not healthy. If you be better off deciding that you value Mexican food, opening your calendar and honestly deciding to spend the time to learn to cook Mexican food. Look for the virtue underneath your vice -- What information are you most drawn to and what healthy activities does it point towards? Pick 3 and pursue those deeply. Why 3? You can only honestly give a fuck about a few things[3], so that requires deciding to not do other reasonable fun activities. If shallow engagement with your interests has been your primary mode of procrastination, then honest and deep engagement might deceptively feel like a waste of time. That makes this hard. Why arbitrarily avoid those other things? You are choosing to be the arbiter of your time. Take pride in grappling with the fear of procrastinat"},{"id":32140198,"author":"hukuuu","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: How to get to sleep after an evening workout?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among other sleep resources","quote":"Some other resources that might be helpful","text":"Hi, two suggestions that work for me: 1) The Wim Hof method. How to do it: [LINK] Guided session: [LINK] 2) Reveri - self hypnosis app: [LINK] Also, do you get enough sunlight during the day? They say it balances your circadian rythm. Some other resources that might be helpful: [VIDEO] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30624473,"author":"jaoa","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Any weird tips for weight loss?","stance":"reference","why":"Passing link in a long weight loss and hormones comment","quote":"","text":"This is so so right. I've went from someone who was skinnyfat until my early 20s to maintaining an 8-pack (around 7-8% bf) which has its own problems..but i digress it is generally great, simply by controlling hormones and eating to sleep well. I usually eat whatever I want, but I consider the effects a certain food has on insulin. By the way, this change coincided with me studying nutrition/biochemistry and undergoing a master in dietetics at a tertiary institution so it isn't based on pure anecdata. REMEMBER THIS - CONTROL YOUR HORMONES Your body won't put on fat without insulin, the downside is that you probably wont put on a lot of muscle either. This is a heuristic I employ however is more complicated in reality. We can go into this if you want. In order to blunt the insulin response of say a sugary snack, you can combine a fat source (pistachios) etc. so there is a balance of fats, carbohydrates and protein. This lowers the insulin response, and leads to lower fat accumulation - remember fat not weight - you still gonna put on weight. Other tricks you can employ include timing these treats or taking magnesium which helps your insulin sensitivity. What I do: Usually perform some fasted training - usually 20-40 minutes of loaded carries in the morning, something like suitcase carries, farmers walks that requires some form of resistance - not everyday maybe every other day. morning green tea usually boosts my energy levels - and has some l-theanine and caffeine for body co"},{"id":29485380,"author":"swader999","month":"2021-12","story":"Lack of sunlight makes our cells store more fat (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Timing light correctly maintains circadian rhythm","quote":"See the Huberman podcast on this","text":"True but those wavelengths will maintain your circadian rhythm if you time it correctly. See the Huberman podcast on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27586080,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: How to learn proper systems programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Support for viewing outside light in the morning","quote":"","text":"Make sure your body and mind are calm and alert. Turn off overhead lights at 9pm. Rise at a consistent time in the morning and view the outside world[1]. Eat nutritious food. Make sure your desk is clean. Get yourself a nice big pad of dotted paper. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26933028,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: What has been the biggest contributor in improving your sleep?","stance":"reference","why":"Morning light and evening dim lights habits from Huberman","quote":"Both habits inspired by Prof. Andrew Huberman.","text":"In order to accomplish this, I’ve had great success by: 1. Looking at natural light first thing in the morning. 2. At 9-10pm, turning off all overhead lights and using a usb-charging lantern. I usually call family at this hour. Both habits inspired by Prof. Andrew Huberman. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40684798,"author":"pseudalopex","month":"2024-06","story":"Sleep deprivation disrupts memory","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the transcript had no matches for insomnia or camping.","text":"Episode 2 was about sleep. But the transcript had no matches for insomnia or camping.[1] It sounds like a distorted memory of a small study where subjects without sleep disorders slept earlier but not longer or better when camping with natural light and fire only.[2] How long the effect lasted was not studied. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["s254IPHXgVA",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1],["aXvDEmo6uS4",1],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["Yocja_N5s1I",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["QmOF0crdyRU",1]]},"wfm2xCKOCNk":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the go-to explanation of Japanese zoning in housing and urbanism debates.","q":{"x":"The best videos on this (in my opinion)","i":46647886,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46647886,"author":"jonpurdy","month":"2026-01","story":"America could have $4 lunch bowls like Japan–but our zoning laws [prevent that]","stance":"rec","why":"Among the best videos on Japanese zoning","quote":"The best videos on this (in my opinion)","text":"The best videos on this (in my opinion) which compliment urbankchoze's post: ‣ Not Just Bikes: [LINK] ‣ Life Where I'm from: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44357099,"author":"jonpurdy","month":"2025-06","story":"Backyard Coffee and Jazz in Kyoto","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on Japanese zoning","quote":"There are two great videos specifically on Japanese zoning and narrow streets","text":"There are two great videos specifically on Japanese zoning and narrow streets: Life Where I'm From, on zoning: [VIDEO] Not Just Bikes, narrow streets: [LINK]"},{"id":36486561,"author":"com2kid","month":"2023-06","story":"Why Britain Doesn’t Build","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on Japan's housing policy","quote":"For anyone not familiar, here is a primer","text":"The developed world has one great example of functional housing policy: Japan. A single policy that allows for unique neighborhoods, affordable houses, supports small businesses, and enables walkable cities. Just.... do what we know already works. For anyone not familiar, here is a primer [VIDEO]"},{"id":34216294,"author":"marsantwo","month":"2023-01","story":"Why Neighborhoods and Small Businesses Thrive in Tokyo","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Tokyo urban design works","quote":"If you are interested in why the Japanese/Tokyo urban design works then look at these 2 links","text":"If you are interested in why the Japanese/Tokyo urban design works then look at these 2 links from the \"Life where I am from\" Youtube channel Why Japan Looks the Way it Does: Zoning [VIDEO] What a Typical Tokyo Neighbourhood is Like [LINK]"},{"id":33748909,"author":"alexchantavy","month":"2022-11","story":"Can Tokyo’s charms be replicated elsewhere?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how lax zoning enables mixed use and walkability","quote":"This was a fascinating video on it","text":"The lax zoning laws (at least compared to the US) allow mixed use so you have more things within walking distance. This was a fascinating video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32451239,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Anyone else feels the commoditization of real estate is unethical?","stance":"rec","why":"Tokyo zoning and living in industrial zones being cheaper","quote":"Take a look at this[0] video on Tokyo zoning and how he talks about living in an industrial zoned area.","text":"Yes, land use is one factor. What is interesting is that the dynamic actually flips once there is adequate zoning. Take a look at this[0] video on Tokyo zoning and how he talks about living in an industrial zoned area. He talks about how it's cheaper to do so, and if you want to move to a single family zoned area, it's actually more expensive! Under the current system, landowners want their own land to be zoned as high as possible, while they want everyone else's land to be zoned as low as possible. Since 1 < everyone else, when people vote on zoning it is zoned as low as possible. With a LVT the system flips, everyone wants their land to be zoned as low as they desire, and they want everyone else's land to be zoned as high as possible. They would only accept land zoned restrictively if those people would pay higher taxes for the exclusive use of it. This is a system with proper incentives. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31886464,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2022-06","story":"Abolish Zoning","stance":"rec","why":"Useful comparison point on Japanese zoning","quote":"The _Life Where I'm From_ episode on zoning in Japan is useful, I think, as a point of comparison.","text":"The _Life Where I'm From_ episode on zoning in Japan is useful, I think, as a point of comparison. Zoning in Japan can still be pretty complex (limits on floorspace, setback, and angles), but allows for environments that many people like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31052869,"author":"mikem170","month":"2022-04","story":"Tax the Land","stance":"rec","why":"More information on how Japan handles zoning","quote":"Here's some more information","text":"I like the way that Japan handles zoning. It's based on population densities. Localities can designate industrial and commercial areas, but can't deviate from federal guidelines to prevent growth. Instead of single family homes they allow triplexes where a percentage of space can be used for a low-impact business, like a barber shop or something. They have setbacks geared towards making sure that you don't build three stories on top of your neighbor's house. They have neighborhoods. They don't have a housing shortage. They don't have a system that doesn't empower developers who want to clear-cut cheap land on the outskirts of town and build sprawling anti-social subdivisions of oversized identical houses. Here's some more information: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28940725,"author":"7373737373","month":"2021-10","story":"Why can't we build nice neighborhoods anymore?","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating explanation of Japanese zoning","quote":"which was very illuminating","text":"I found this video yesterday: \"Why Japan Looks the Way it Does: Zoning\" which was very illuminating [VIDEO]"},{"id":47833144,"author":"throwaway2037","month":"2026-04","story":"Why Japan has such good railways","stance":"reference","why":"Source for residential homes in light industry zones","quote":"I learned this surprising fact here","text":"A huge amount of residential homes are actually in light industry zoned areas. I learned this surprising fact here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32359370,"author":"mijustin","month":"2022-08","story":"Marc Andreessen says he’s for new housing, but records tell a different story","stance":"reference","why":"Japan's mixed-use zoning approach","quote":"","text":"Exactly this. This is a cultural issue, more than anything else. Countries like Japan have taken a profoundly different approach to urban development, and have more mixed-use zones (single-family, multi-family, high-rise, etc). [VIDEO]"},{"id":30893784,"author":"mikem170","month":"2022-04","story":"Alright, amigo, let's build some affordable housing","stance":"reference","why":"Source on Japan's flexible zoning","quote":"There are other countries that are much more flexible, like Japan [0].","text":"> Define “significantly impose”. That’s the basic problem, I agree. > existing near someone can have effects that are easy to justify in court I had the impression this was a \"mob rule\" problem, that local voters were voting for zoning laws that limit the quality and quantity of new housing. I admit to not understanding the legal or moral justification. It just doesn't seem right to micromanage others to the extent that occurs today. There are other countries that are much more flexible, like Japan [0]. Instead of single family single use zoning they start with three story multi-use housing. Some places in the U.S. are moving in this direction. > decreasing or limiting increase on future land values That can be flipped around. Someone who opposes new housing is taking value from the people who could have made use of that new housing, had it been allowed. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30367406,"author":"mikem170","month":"2022-02","story":"How much do construction costs matter to the price of housing?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for how Japanese zoning works","quote":"zoning in Japan [0] makes a lot of sense.","text":"This is true, zoning in Japan [0] makes a lot of sense. Instead of single family homes they allow triplexes, even a percentage of space dedicated to a low-impact business (like hair stylist, etc). They have setback rules so that you can't put second/third floor up against your neighbors property line blocking the sun all day. They dedicate nearby spaces for commercial and hi-rise buildings, making it possible to live near where you work. And there is dedicated space for factories and other industrial buildings. Single-use single family zoning is being eliminated in some areas of the U.S., allowing accessory dwelling units to be built, etc. Even California just passed a low changed R1 to R3 for all cities in the state (pending court cases, I assume). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29232664,"author":"mikem170","month":"2021-11","story":"Will real estate ever be normal again?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for Japanese-style zoning","quote":"If I had a magic wand I think that the way the Japanese do zoning is much better than the way it has evolved in the U.S.","text":"Your note above contains a lot of assumptions about what I think, and vitriol, words like steal, destroy, trash, etc. This seems to be something that you are emotional about. Lots of people think it's immoral for neighbors to be able to micromanage the living spaces of other neighbors outside of basic safety issues. I'm not sure how you'd defend imposing your will on other people just because you can at any given moment. Who are you to hijack the decades and centuries that goes into a city, like you mentioned, and freeze it in time for your convenience, at the expense of others who were born into the same society you were. We're all here together, you can't morally just marginalize people you're afraid of. I stand by my original statement that I think it's wrong for you to characterize everyone who doesn't agree with the way zoning has evolved in this country over the last 100 years as an interloper. We live in a democracy. These are fellow citizens, people who are just as important as you and me. I'm not in favor of big developers or corporations or profits. I don't want to build a high rise or a factory next to your house. I am in favor of lots more real neighborhoods, as opposed to suburban developer big-box retail car centric sprawl. I am opposed to single use zoning. If I had a magic wand I think that the way the Japanese do zoning is much better than the way it has evolved in the U.S. [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28971648,"author":"mlindner","month":"2021-10","story":"Long Beach has temporarily suspended container stacking limitations","stance":"reference","why":"Japan's tiered zoning system as a model","quote":"","text":"Why blame the rich people rather than the system that allows them to abuse it? Rich people will always exist. The solution is to make it so that abuse is not possible. One possibility: create a state system for zoning and override every local zoning system. Let local zoning boards decide how to zone but make zoning be based on a tiered zoning system. Namely every zoning is a superset of the previous zoning. So industrial areas also allow every other type of zoning. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28971664,"author":"mlindner","month":"2021-10","story":"Long Beach has temporarily suspended container stacking limitations","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Japan's permissive mixed-use zoning","quote":"Example Japan","text":"Why? If people choose to live next to the incinerator that's their choice. You can also have \"industrial only\" zones for stuff that's pollution emitting, but most things don't need that zoning. Example Japan: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CcAJ3_-Hou8",1],["5RKbSi87J5Q",1],["586Zn1ct-QA",1],["dCSIXLIzhzk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["CiHN0ZWE5bk",1],["1rCrfQUcXDI",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["RjD1aLm4Thg",1],["FzRP1qdwPKw",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["wROQUZDCIMI",1],["1bO8zEaSuWg",1],["Z1PCtIaM_GQ",1],["wwmOkaKh3-s",1]]},"hX2aZUav-54":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it in gas stove and induction threads for exposing how the idea that gas is better for cooking was a marketing ploy.","q":{"x":"Here's a great video on the topic by Climate Town, an awesome educator on climate change","i":34333893,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34333893,"author":"willio58","month":"2023-01","story":"A US federal agency is considering a ban on gas stoves","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great educational video on gas dangers","quote":"Here's a great video on the topic by Climate Town, an awesome educator on climate change","text":"Recently I've had an experience with a minor gas leak that's left me afraid to even use my furnace. I have a gas furnace, and I live in a rental. The furnace works, but for the first hour of it being on I can smell gas. It's enough that a carbon monoxide detector will go off nearby. One day I told my partner that I've been feeling super groggy and cloudy-headed for a few months, and we narrowed down the time frame to when I moved into my place. Sure enough, I switch to using a small space heater and two weeks in I'm feeling better than ever before. I 100% attribute the fogginess in my brain to the gas leak. It wasn't enough to cause an explosion, but it doesn't take much to cause serious health problems. Here's a great video on the topic by Climate Town, an awesome educator on climate change: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30657418,"author":"Zenbit_UX","month":"2022-03","story":"The case for induction cooking","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes gas lobbyists and health effects, with citations","quote":"The YouTuber Climate Town did an excellent video on gas cooking","text":"Before anyone reads this and decides, \"oh ya the NYT is lying to me and gas is fine\", it's not. Gas in your home is objectively bad for your health, the environment and is overall worse than induction when it comes to the cooking experience in just about all ways. That being said, It might not be as bad as living with a smoker (as the NYT asserted) but it is bad for you. The YouTuber Climate Town did an excellent video on gas cooking which exposed some very nasty truths of the gas lobbyists and the negative health effects it has on the population. Citations are in the description: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35760738,"author":"kentiko","month":"2023-04","story":"New York to Ban Natural Gas, Including Stoves, in New Buildings","stance":"rec","why":"Why induction adoption lags in the USA","quote":"Some elements of answer about why the adoption of induction is behind in the USA","text":"Gaz stove have almost no upside compared to induction stove in most cases. Some elements of answer about why the adoption of induction is behind in the USA: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35764742,"author":"danny_codes","month":"2023-04","story":"New York to Ban Natural Gas, Including Stoves, in New Buildings","stance":"rec","why":"Education on gas industry lobbying and marketing","quote":"For your education","text":"Induction stoves are cheaper, more energy efficient, safer, and better for your health. There aren't any advantages to gas at all. The gas industry has, however, put many billions into lobbying and marketing to convince you otherwise. And it seems they have succeeded. For your education [VIDEO]"},{"id":35764803,"author":"danny_codes","month":"2023-04","story":"New York to Ban Natural Gas, Including Stoves, in New Buildings","stance":"rec","why":"Education on electricity versus gas","quote":"Some education for you","text":"Dumbest take I've heard all day. Banning gas would increase quality of life. Why subject yourself to pollution, gas leaks and expensive secondary energy infrastructure when you can just not do that. I understand the gas lobby has spent billions convincing you otherwise, but the fact is that direct natural gas is far inferior to electricity at this point. Some education for you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34283555,"author":"LesZedCB","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What $500-2500 product improved your 2022","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video on natural gas propaganda","quote":"climate town has a fun video about it","text":"natural gas has run a pretty effective propaganda campaign against induction. we are switching soon and i'm very excited! climate town has a fun video about it, he's a climate policy and science grad [VIDEO]"},{"id":33502100,"author":"diordiderot","month":"2022-11","story":"Barilla passive cooker","stance":"rec","why":"Gas cooking superiority was a marketing ploy","quote":"Watch this video about how the idea that gas was better for cooking was a marketing ploy","text":"Watch this video about how the idea that gas was better for cooking was a marketing ploy with no basis in reality and how they used lobbyist to force has connections into building codes [VIDEO]"},{"id":32425005,"author":"aiisjustanif","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla of Luxury Campers Will Make Airstreams Blush","stance":"rec","why":"Case against gas stoves","quote":"Induction stoves are awesome, normal electric stoves suck.","text":"Induction stoves are awesome, normal electric stoves suck. You know what sucks? Gas stoves. [0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31617349,"author":"bradlys","month":"2022-06","story":"What a gas stove ban means for restaurants","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on getting rid of gas stoves","quote":"Climate Town has a good video on this subject.","text":"The thing is - when you get rid of gas stoves you can get rid of almost all other gas appliances. That’s literally the only thing keeping gas in most homes in the USA and continued in new construction. When you add in gas water heaters, heating, etc. then gas becomes a more significant contributor to GHG. Whereas switching all of those with heatpumps and induction cuts off the gas lobby from fucking with everyone. Best way to get gas lobby to stop winning? Get rid of gas stoves. Climate Town has a good video on this subject. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30658810,"author":"katbyte","month":"2022-03","story":"The case for induction cooking","stance":"rec","why":"Eloquent case for induction over gas","quote":"What the video for someone putting it far more eloquently then I ever could.","text":"By what measure? Induction wins hands down for control, responsiveness, efficiency, and heat output: [VIDEO] Doing a super low temp simmer on gas is hard. Double boilers are only something you need with gas. Induction imho and experience is just better all around. What the video for someone putting it far more eloquently then I ever could."},{"id":29688001,"author":"UFOFlyer","month":"2021-12","story":"New York City bans natural gas in new buildings","stance":"rec","why":"Gas industry marketing and lobbying against legislation","quote":"the decades of marketing and lobbying the natural gas industry has paid for","text":"The big problem with piping natural gas to buildings is that the miles of pipes that deliver the gas leak. Gas leaking is a real issue since an equal amount of un-burnt methane has 25x the climate change potential as CO2. Even if the power plants supplying electric heat pumps/radiators is natural gas, the shorter supply chain (only to power plant and not to every building in the city) go some way to offsetting extra emissions. Then you have the obvious benefit that once the NYC grid transitions to renewables there won't be as many natural gas ovens and heaters that need to be replaced. The commenters pointing out that natural gas stoves don't have a significant effect on emissions are right, but once a building gets a gas hookup installing natural gas heaters and water heaters becomes more attractive and economical. That's something we don't want to be incentivizing Climate town recently released a new video talking about this issue as well as the decades of marketing and lobbying the natural gas industry has paid for to neuter or outright kill legislation like this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43255913,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-03","story":"How the U.K. broke its own economy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It isn't completely irrelevant; they didn't mention wild birds, or any wider context - the way they commented was as a shock number, the intent of that is \"wind turbines kill $bignum, stop wind turbines\". The relevance of the much bigger shock number is to put it in context - human activity slaughters a lot of animals. Okay, something else which affects wild birds: \" Bird corpses were counted - and, where possible, identified - at 166 locations throughout Britain during the summer of 1985. The survey covered all roads except motorways. It is the most recent such study, and the most comprehensive. The results were depressing. A minimum of 30 million birds are killed on Britain's roads every year. Depending on the assumptions made in the statistical analysis, the death rate could be even higher: 70 million a year is not impossible. \"[1] And that's as well as wild rabbits, hedgehogs, badgers, foxes, deer, and so on killed by cars which wind turbines don't affect. So the wildlife in the UK would be much better off if we built more viable alternatives to driving and road trucking than if we scrapped wind turbines. We can also note that people are working on wind turbines that don't kill birds, e.g. [2]. It's not a fixed fact that all wind turbine designs do the same damage. In the context of a world where fossil fuel propaganda really does exist[3] and Trump's recent executive order on unleashing energy generation where he tells government departments to look for all kinds of ener"},{"id":36821432,"author":"LesZedCB","month":"2023-07","story":"Cooking Air Quality","stance":"reference","why":"Gas industry influencer marketing","quote":"UNLIKE the gas industry which has been pushing this shit through influencers and marketing for decades","text":"I switched to induction holy shit it's so much better! if you have compatible cookware, definitely try it out, you can get an external induction cooktop from Ikea for like $60. if you don't, get some and switch because the cooking process is so much more enjoyable. you know how when you're sauteing something and you wanna get a good sniff because it's smells amazing, only to get your eyebrows singed and a lungfull of half combusted propane? with induction, you get nothing but the smell of your food, without the excess wasted heat. also, it's fast on the big eye I can boil water insanely fast, I honestly didn't know it was possible to boil water that fast. and I can say this all only as a happy customer who paid my own money, not as a paid advertisement, UNLIKE the gas industry which has been pushing this shit through influencers and marketing for decades. [1] [1] [VIDEO] [Climate Town - It's Time to Break Up with Our Gas Stoves 21:27]"},{"id":36407022,"author":"jeffbee","month":"2023-06","story":"Benzene Exposure Alters Endocrine Activity","stance":"reference","why":"Pro-gas astroturf campaign","quote":"they created this entire astroturf campaign for the pro-gas side","text":"They must have perceived some threats because they created this entire astroturf campaign for the pro-gas side. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35762459,"author":"kibwen","month":"2023-04","story":"Why Are Lithium Prices Collapsing?","stance":"reference","why":"Gas industry propaganda spending","quote":"the gas industry spends heavily on propaganda to convince people otherwise","text":"Gas stoves need to go away because getting rid of gas infrastructure in the long term will be a huge win, but the gas industry spends heavily on propaganda to convince people otherwise: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34317429,"author":"screye","month":"2023-01","story":"“I measured the pollution from my gas stove. It was bad.”","stance":"reference","why":"Additional reasons to switch from gas","quote":"There are additional good reasons to switch as seen from this[1] video.","text":"I would be all for moving off gas-stoves, but can we acknowledge that the alternatives really suck if you like cooking ? I want to be part of the solution, so here are few things that alternatives need to figure out before I move off gas: Easy problems : 1. No more cycling-induction/electric stoves. I do not want my food alternating between burning & sitting around. 2. A power setting where low-to-medium aren't only kind of burning your food. 3. Off means off. I don't want my food cooking for 5 more minutes after I turn the stove off. 4. Kitchen ranges having high quality broilers to make up for the lack of a charring mechanism on alternative ranges. Hard problems : 1. Being able to change the shape of the surface element to trace the shape of the pan. I cook with a wok, and all non-gas-ranges only heat the very center. If I can use a wok to fix the shape of the coil into a bowl, then I can ensure even heating even in a wok. (It's a spring coil, the shape should be mechanically adjustable). 2. Can the good ones be competitive on prices with top gas ranges ? I really dislike electric stoves, they have the worst of all worlds. Induction stoves: I could live with, but the good ones are too expensive to be a reasonable alternative for most people. There are additional good reasons to switch as seen from this[1] video. [1] [VIDEO] ______________ Final caveat for tropical countries. Here, windows are always open. Houses often lack a gas connection, and instead have a gas-cylinder s"},{"id":34367347,"author":"alamortsubite","month":"2023-01","story":"The Gas Industry Is Paying Instagram Influencers to Gush over Gas Stoves","stance":"reference","why":"Features the Rappin with Gas video around 2:45","quote":"The \"Rappin' with Gas!\" video is featured in this Climate Town at about the 2:45 mark","text":"The \"Rappin' with Gas!\" video is featured in this Climate Town at about the 2:45 mark: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31613510,"author":"inerte","month":"2022-06","story":"What a gas stove ban means for restaurants","stance":"reference","why":"Gas industry paying for cookingwithgas videos","quote":"the author talks _exactly_ about that","text":"> By the way, this is why the gas company is paying for articles like this. That's such huge coincidence! During a break I started to watch [VIDEO] (still on the first few minutes) and the author talks _exactly_ about that, where a gas company (industry?) is paying people to post #cookingwithgas videos."},{"id":31838026,"author":"otikik","month":"2022-06","story":"Chasing utopian energy: How I wasted 20 years of my life","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for methane leaks from natural gas","quote":"My sources: * (around 16:11)","text":"> I used to think solar and wind power were the best ways to reduce CO2 emissions. But the biggest reduction in CO2 emissionsduring the past 15 years (over 60%) has come from switching from coal to natural gas. That one needs a citation to back it up. It might be true, but natural gas leaks a lot of Methane (CH4) into the atmosphere, which way stronger greenhouse gas effect than CO2. So it might be worse than coal actually. My sources: * [VIDEO] (around 16:11) * [LINK]"},{"id":30735916,"author":"bradlys","month":"2022-03","story":"Why America can’t build quickly anymore","stance":"reference","why":"Corporate origin of cooking-with-gas culture","quote":"A lot of “cultural” things are corporations at work.","text":"I wonder - do you think that people always loved cooking with gas as much as they do now? [VIDEO] A lot of “cultural” things are corporations at work. This is a very small example…"}],"r":[["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1],["n3Xv_g3g-mA",1],["31CcclqEiZw",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"2KSahAoOLdU":{"wy":"HN people recommend the whole AGC restoration series as a deeply nerdy look at real Apollo-era hardware, including core rope memory.","q":{"x":"My new favorite YouTuber, CuriousMarc, has a whole series of videos about restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC).","i":22815735,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22815735,"author":"cpascal","month":"2020-04","story":"A circuit board from the Saturn V rocket, reverse-engineered and explained","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full series restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer.","quote":"My new favorite YouTuber, CuriousMarc, has a whole series of videos about restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC).","text":"My new favorite YouTuber, CuriousMarc, has a whole series of videos about restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC). His AGC Playlist: [VIDEO] His channel is full of fascinating retro-computing and EE videos."},{"id":22417007,"author":"crmrc114","month":"2020-02","story":"Recovering Fossil Data on IBM 8” Floppies [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Apollo AGC video series by CuriousMarc.","quote":"If you are new to his channel I cannot recommend enough the Apollo AGC videos","text":"I love his videos he has a webpage with merch if you want to support him [LINK] If you are new to his channel I cannot recommend enough the Apollo AGC videos [VIDEO]"},{"id":42100854,"author":"CableNinja","month":"2024-11","story":"Somebody moved the UK's oldest satellite in the mid 1970s, but no one knows who","stance":"rec","why":"Restoring Apollo systems and talking to them with lab equipment.","quote":"Its a long one but very worth the watch even if you arent into everything they cover.","text":"CuriousMarc on Youtube has a very large playlist of him and a few others restoring the apollo systems they have been able to get their hands on, and youre absolutely correct on the hacky possibilities. There was no authentication, just lock on and issue commands. They were not only able to restore pretty much everything to a working apollo comms and control system, they were even able to use lab equipment to communicate with apollo outright. I linked the first part of the series. Its a long one but very worth the watch even if you arent into everything they cover. Many sats were and are similar, theres not much security. Newer ones with specific uses are most definitely encrypted. The only real saving grace is that the size of dish/antenna and power you need to do anything with stuff actually in space at long distances is beyond what most people could do (not saying a very dedicated nerd couldnt, look at SaveItForParts for example). [VIDEO]"},{"id":32382945,"author":"bmitc","month":"2022-08","story":"The Apollo On-Board Computers","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent video of restoring original AGC hardware.","quote":"there is an excellent video of him working on restoring original AGC hardware","text":"The main person behind that effort, as far as I know, is Mike Stewart, and there is an excellent video of him working on restoring original AGC hardware. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":27624293,"author":"ArcticCelt","month":"2021-06","story":"NASA can't figure out what's causing computer issues on the Hubble telescope","stance":"rec","why":"Group restores an AGC rescued from the trash, recreating original software.","quote":"follow a group that restored an Apollo Guidance Computer that a collector basically pulled from the trash","text":"This youtube series of video, follow a group that restored an Apollo Guidance Computer that a collector basically pulled from the trash. [VIDEO] My favorite part is when they needed the version of the software that was used for the moon landing but they only had the source code for a previous version (scanned from giant binder) and the hash value of the version of the landing. By a series of educated guesses, by reading memos and by analysis of the source code they modified the old code the exact way so it gave them the correct hash, confirming that they correctly and exactly recreated the original code. It's being a while and I go from memory, I might have some details wrong. See this video for this story. [LINK]"},{"id":22417492,"author":"CaptArmchair","month":"2020-02","story":"Recovering Fossil Data on IBM 8” Floppies [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Awesome series on restoring an original Apollo Guidance Computer.","quote":"CuriousMarc has also done this awesome series on the restoration of an original AGC","text":"If you haven't seen this already, CuriousMarc has also done this awesome series on the restoration of an original AGC - Apollo Guidance Computer last year. [VIDEO] And then there are real treats such as the restoration of a Teletype 33 ASR: [LINK] Or them trying to get Fortran to compile on an IBM 1401 Mainframe dated 1959. [LINK]"},{"id":20329724,"author":"acomjean","month":"2019-07","story":"Former NASA Flight Director Gene Kranz Restores Mission Control in Houston","stance":"rec","why":"Channel rebuilding the Apollo computer, with core rope memory.","quote":"Very nerdy.","text":"there is a youtube channel trying to rebuild the apollo launch computer. Very nerdy. The memory is rings of magnets. It is pretty fun though: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20449514,"author":"LeonM","month":"2019-07","story":"Apollo 11 50th Anniversary","stance":"rec","why":"CuriousMarc's restoration series of an actual AGC.","quote":"If you are into space stuff, and haven't seen it yet","text":"Not so much an article about the 50th anniversary, but just the main article about Apollo 11. If you are into space stuff, and haven't seen it yet, there were some pretty cool projects going on on Youtube in preparation for the 50th anniversary: - CuriousMarc's restoration series of an actual ACG [0] - Applied Science made a replica DSKY electroluminescent glass panel display [1] - Project Egress: A maker collaboration on building a replica of the Apollo 11 door (Adam Savage, Jimmy Diresta, This old tony, Blondihacks, etc) [2] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22353702,"author":"thewonderidiot","month":"2020-02","story":"Weird-Looking Freak Saves Apollo 14 (1971)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of schematics used in the AGC restoration.","quote":"","text":"Don is still around, and is still awesome! His memory for events around that time is surprisingly sharp, for it being 50 years ago. He's been an invaluable resource for us at the VirtualAGC project [1]. He provided over half (!) of the AGC programs we've made available, including LM software for Apollo 5, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, and 15-17, plus several other ground development programs. He also was the source of a majority of the schematics that we used in the AGC restoration [2] -- I can say with 100% confidence that it wouldn't have been possible to get that computer working again without his help. He's got a book out now, Sunburst and Luminary [3], that is very good and goes into a lot of technical detail on the LM software, without getting too much into the weeds. I highly recommend it if you're interested in that sort of thing! [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["-J_xL4IGhJA",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["pOmXiapfCs8",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["U4FNBMZsqrY",1],["KFNxJVTJleE",1],["uFQ3sajIdaM",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["Z2o_Sp2-aBo",1]]},"1qSTcxt2t74":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining and inspiring Blender talk, and often links it in threads about learning Blender.","q":{"x":"this is a must watch for inspiration and laughs","i":33273890,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33273890,"author":"lathiat","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Recommend me a Blender course","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring and funny Blender conference talk.","quote":"this is a must watch for inspiration and laughs","text":"I can’t give you a good tutorial but this is a must watch for inspiration and laughs: “Captain Disillusion: World’s Greatest Blenderer - Live at the Blender Conference 2018” [VIDEO] Note for after: The UI has apparently been fixed"},{"id":25217293,"author":"lathiat","month":"2020-11","story":"Blender 2.91","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious talk that critiqued Blender's interface.","quote":"The entire presentation is hilarious","text":"Some would say this amazing diss by Captain Disillusion at BlenderCon had something to do with the major interface changes in Blender 2.8. The entire presentation is hilarious but for the interface related bit I've linked to 21m15s here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20447375,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2019-07","story":"Epic Games Supports Blender Foundation with $1.2M","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential Blender conference presentation.","quote":"An essential Blender video to watch is Captain Disillusion's presentation at Blender Conf","text":"Great post. An essential Blender video to watch is Captain Disillusion's presentation at Blender Conf: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29437292,"author":"xmprt","month":"2021-12","story":"Blender 3.0","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable talk by Captain Disillusion at Blender Conference.","quote":"I really enjoyed this talk by Captain Disillusion at the Blender Conference 2018","text":"I really enjoyed this talk by Captain Disillusion at the Blender Conference 2018 where he goes over some of that - [VIDEO]"},{"id":21371691,"author":"theclaw","month":"2019-10","story":"Show HN: Make a video of any word forming out of candy","stance":"rec","why":"Captain Disillusion's great presentation at the Blender conference.","quote":"did a great presentation at the Blender conference last year","text":"For those not aware Captain Disillusion is an impressively well produced youtube series mimicking the vibe of 90's kids TV, but for an adult audience. The Captain uses Blender for the 3D effects in his videos, and did a great presentation at the Blender conference last year [0]. The type of effect used to create these candy words is covered in the \"Marble Sorting Machine\" video [LINK] [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["qTBAW-Eh0tM",1],["lvTqbM5Dq4Q",1],["ett_8wLJ87U",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["DoA4Txr4GUs",1],["LZPKTaVB1IU",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["XpDsk374LDE",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1]]},"qp0HIF3SfI4":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it to explain why customers buy into a company's beliefs rather than its specs, especially in threads about Apple, marketing and product messaging.","q":{"x":"I think this video by Simon Sinek explains very well why Apple is successful.","i":4127046,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":16975284,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-05","story":"Amazon threatens to suspend Signal's AWS account over censorship circumvention","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Companies thinking only about money fail long term","quote":"I urge you to watch this Simon Sinek video","text":"You can apply this idea to everything, though. \"How is it the interest of Github stockholders to not censor certain projects when China starts DDoSing the whole site?\" or even \"How is it in the interest of Cloudflare to raise the prices for all of its customers, just to protect a site Russia doesn't like?\" It can't all be about money. Companies that think only about money fail in the long term. If you don't believe that, then I urge you to watch this Simon Sinek video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25421918,"author":"viktorcode","month":"2020-12","story":"Technical Copywriting:What Engineers Can Learn from Apple’s M1 MacBook Marketing","stance":"rec","why":"Broader take on why marketing works, still relevant","quote":"I recommend watching this take instead. 10 years old, and still clicks","text":"I find this a rather simple take on Apple marketing. Not saying it is wrong, but surely it can not apply to other products. I recommend watching this take instead. 10 years old, and still clicks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18889986,"author":"peteforde","month":"2019-01","story":"Re-decentralizing the Web, for good this time","stance":"rec","why":"Reframe a message as a call to action","quote":"I recommend that you check out this video and reconsider how you might reframe your message as a call to action","text":"This essay is in radical need of a TL;DR. If something is this important, you owe it to the subject matter not to bury the lede under a mountain of history and flowery exposition. Ask yourself: who is this for? People who are not already deeply passionate will stop reading unless they are engaged in a minute of reading. Note that a minute is being extremely generous; on a commercial consumer site, it's apparently an average of 7 seconds before someone will click away. I recommend that you check out this video and reconsider how you might reframe your message as a call to action that speaks to a better future we can create together. [VIDEO] I even jumped you to the good part."},{"id":4127046,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-06","story":"Apple to become most profitable company ever","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Apple's success through motivation behind its products","quote":"I think this video by Simon Sinek explains very well why Apple is successful.","text":"I think this video by Simon Sinek explains very well why Apple is successful. It's the motivation behind doing the things they do that matters, not just what they make. This tends to attract a lot of customers to think the same way, and believe in Apple's goals and philosophy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3422918,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-01","story":"Windows Phone is failing because the seeds were planted elsewhere","stance":"rec","why":"Companies succeed when customers share their beliefs","quote":"This is very relevant (How great leaders inspire action)","text":"This is very relevant (How great leaders inspire action): [VIDEO] Hint: It's all about the beliefs of a company and how consumers associate their beliefs with a company's beliefs. We know what Apple stands for (higher quality and more beautiful products). We know what Google stands for (more openess, more user freedom). What does Microsoft stand for? Can you answer it quickly? The only answer that comes fast enough is \"money\". I don't think Microsoft has an inbred culture inside the company for much else, at least not anymore. I also agree with the ending conclusion of the post, about Microsoft trying to cater to the \"people in the middle\", like not wanting to be close enough like Apple to deliver a great user experience, but not open enough like Android, too. In fact WP7 is even much stricter than desktop Windows. So they aren't addressing neither the Apple users, nor the Android users with this \"mediocrity\" strategy. Seth Godin likes to talk about how mediocrity is a bad place to be in, too: [LINK]"},{"id":31309930,"author":"peteforde","month":"2022-05","story":"Dashy: Homepage for Your Homelab","stance":"reference","why":"Explaining why a thing exists matters","quote":"Your assertion that there's nothing wrong with not explaining why a thing exists is not backed by reality.","text":"Your assertion that there's nothing wrong with not explaining why a thing exists is not backed by reality. [VIDEO] Of course, it's their choice . But don't conflate that with being the best thing for the project or the visitor. Presumably the site exists to convince people to use it, no?"},{"id":15496302,"author":"tarheeljason","month":"2017-10","story":"Introducing Surface Book 2","stance":"reference","why":"Start with why framing","quote":"They're 'starting with why'","text":"> \"When you think about Microsoft and you hear our mission – to empower every person and every organization on the planet to achieve more – it’s powerful.\" What? They're 'starting with why' - [VIDEO] TL;DW: a marketing consultant suggested replacing \"We make computers to empower people\" with \"We empower people; we just happen to make computers\""},{"id":4773605,"author":"trendspotter","month":"2012-11","story":"Show HN: Simon.Says.do – easy way to dispatch tasks to 1 to many","stance":"reference","why":"Explain why a product exists","quote":"The WHY is missing","text":"I was confused. There should be a clearly visible video explaining it in 30 seconds. Explaining why it exists, how it works and what it does; the problem, the solution. The WHY is missing - WHY do you offer it? Why does it exist? - WHY should I care? - WHY should I be a part of what you do? - WHY do I need it? Related: \"Start With Why\" [VIDEO] Besides that I wish your project all the best. It looks very interesting!"},{"id":4317137,"author":"ehosca","month":"2012-07","story":"Why Apple's new ads look like Microsoft made them.","stance":"reference","why":"Selling why you should have a product","quote":"Its not about the product itself or its specs but why I should have it.","text":"Apple ads have always emphasized \"why\" I should be using that product. Its not about the product itself or its specs but why I should have it. They are not straying away from that principle here so I'm not really sure where your beef is.. relevant link : [VIDEO]"},{"id":2855339,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2011-08","story":"Windows Phones Down 38% Since '7' Launch","stance":"reference","why":"Customers buy into a company's beliefs","quote":"it quickly clicked with me that the supporters of Apple are also people who above all want a quality product","text":"When I saw the Simon Sinek video on leadership ( [VIDEO] ), it quickly clicked with me that the supporters of Apple are also people who above all want a quality product, that has beautiful design and is easy to use. Also, people who support Google believe in what Google believes - freedom, openness, personalization, and not getting overcharged and arm and a leg for the service they provide. But when I thought of Microsoft, I couldn't get neither a quick answer, nor a good one. What are Microsoft's beliefs? What is their mantra? Why do they exist? I couldn't really come up with an answer - except \"making money\". 15 years ago Microsoft's mantra was about putting a computer on every desk. They've pretty much accomplished that mission. But Microsoft doesn't inspire that in me right now - what they inspire and makes me think about them is that above else, they want to make money. Every company wants to make money, but we \"believe\" in the companies we love for different reasons, not because they want to make money. We love the companies that share our beliefs. What exactly are Microsoft's beliefs nowadays? To kill Google? Is that what makes whatever fans they have left to still love them? I don't think that's enough to win over a larger crowd in the future, as they start losing their monopoly on \"computing machines\", and all the negative sentiment they are gathering with the patents right now, will come back to haunt them later and create a negative halo around all their products. "},{"id":2740250,"author":"danudey","month":"2011-07","story":"Poll: would you buy a service that bills only in €?","stance":"reference","why":"Impulse buys driven by feelings rather than thoughts","quote":"Impulse buys are feelings, not thoughts","text":"Just had a discussion with some coworkers, and we settled on a conclusion, which may be beneficial or not. Impulse buys are feelings, not thoughts (see Simon Sinek's TED talk ), so it really depends on your market and why they're buying your product. If it's the sort of thing that they'll do a ton of research on, compare other products, and then settle on a conclusion, then you should be fine either way. If it's the sort of thing people will either impulse buy or forget about, then you may want to consider the issues involved with making a currency conversion. 1. When a user encounters a currency they're not familiar with (for many Americans, this is any non-USD currency), there's an immediate break in their purchase flow as they suddenly have to try to figure out what it's going to cost them. This requires math or research (most people don't know Google will do currency conversion), which gets in the way of their quick-purchase-then-off-to-facebook workflow. 2. When a user sees your product at (for example) €20, they see the number, they see that it's twenty, and they decide in a split second whether twenty is a reasonable price. When they realize that it's twenty Euro* and the price is actually thirty of their local currency, then, despite their conscious awareness of how currency works, the price has effectively increased by 50%. This can hurt purchasing depending on how devoted they are to the service. Even if '30 of something' is a fair price, their brain got used to the"}],"r":[["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["wq5D43qAsVg",1],["LfascZSTU4o",1],["7Q25-S7jzgs",1],["nBiJB8YuDBQ",1],["QWveXdj6oZU",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["i1vbvikDiI8",1],["ALc8CBYOfkw",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1]]},"f7MzFfuNOtY":{"wy":"HN links it in hydrogen threads as a clear breakdown of why compounded inefficiencies make hydrogen a poor fit for cars, though one commenter calls it outdated.","q":{"x":"This video gives a good breakdown on how the inefficiencies in hydrogen fuel cell based cars add up.","i":26327467,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26327467,"author":"rdedev","month":"2021-03","story":"Toyota Develops Packaged Fuel Cell System Module for Hydrogen Utilization","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of how hydrogen fuel cell car inefficiencies add up","quote":"This video gives a good breakdown on how the inefficiencies in hydrogen fuel cell based cars add up.","text":"[VIDEO] This video gives a good breakdown on how the inefficiencies in hydrogen fuel cell based cars add up. The best case scenario for hydrogen based land transport I can think of is probably in the trucking industry where engine weight, range and refuelling time are important factors"},{"id":24368517,"author":"nuccy","month":"2020-09","story":"Small nuclear reactors: tiny NuScale reactor gets safety approval","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of hydrogen as car fuel","quote":"Nice summary as for cars fuel application here","text":"Hydrogen is not a good (at the moment at least) source of energy or fuel for cars or industry (chemically speaking, obviously much better together with Deuterium and Tritium for fission, though we are still from such a technology). Steam reforming, which is currently the most common way of hydrogen production, uses natural gas and water and produces plenty of CO2 [1]. Nice summary as for cars fuel application here [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23734017,"author":"dmix","month":"2020-07","story":"After many false starts, hydrogen power might now bear fruit","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of hydrogen production methods including on-site","quote":"this video had a good overview of the various production methods, including on-site at the fuel station","text":"Now I'm curious how Hyundai or the stations actually produce the hydrogen fuel. Edit: this video had a good overview of the various production methods, including on-site at the fuel station [VIDEO]"},{"id":22180404,"author":"mcot2","month":"2020-01","story":"Nuclear power is the only green solution (2004)","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on hydrogen being poor for cars","quote":"This is a good video on the subject","text":"I am sorry you are so misinformed. Lithium is a common abundant resource and battery packs are easily recycled but more importantly there is a financial incentive to do so. Manufacturing technology for cells is improving rapidly. Much faster than the pace of innovation in Hydrogen fuel cells. Most are focusing on reducing Cobalt and other techniques to reduce or eliminate chemicals and energy used in wet electrode processes. Hydrogen is pretty much DOA for cars. This is a good video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18722377,"author":"nayuki","month":"2018-12","story":"Water-stabilized hydrogen promises twice the range of gasoline at half the price","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why hydrogen storage is less efficient than batteries","quote":"Storing energy as hydrogen is much less efficient than storing energy in batteries.","text":"Storing energy as hydrogen is much less efficient than storing energy in batteries. The channel Real Engineering explains: [VIDEO] ; [LINK]"},{"id":28727798,"author":"JProthero","month":"2021-10","story":"Rivian S-1","stance":"reference","why":"Compares hydrogen fuel cell and battery vehicle efficiency","quote":"about 25%, compared to about 65% for typical existing battery vehicles","text":"> Finally, electric cars charge at ~96% efficiency, while hydrogen is something like 40%. >> This claim has little substance and credibility. The efficiency of hydrogen fuel cell vehicles versus battery vehicles is considered in some detail in this video [1]. The conclusion [2] is that, in the best case, the energy efficiency from power generation to road for existing or near future hydrogen fuel cell vehicles is about 25%, compared to about 65% for typical existing battery vehicles. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24730014,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2020-10","story":"Separating Hype from Hydrogen: EU's 6GW Electrolyzer Plan","stance":"reference","why":"Says hydrogen is more complex than all-electric","quote":"it's really more complex than all electric it seems","text":"This vid [VIDEO] kinda says the same I like the H2 idea but it's really more complex than all electric it seems. And also late to the game. ps: to be fair, electric batteries and cars required a lot of faith to evolve and progress... who knows if someone won't come with a few innovations for H2 storage and distribution."},{"id":22188448,"author":"Hypx","month":"2020-01","story":"Nuclear power is the only green solution (2004)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's a nonsense youtube video, citing claims that are a decade out of date.","text":"[LINK] Li-ion batteries are very poorly recycled if at all. There is poor financial incentive to recycle batteries, and in fact you lose more money than you get back for many components. Pace of hydrogen fuel cell innovation is moving at warp speed: [LINK] We could see FCEVs no more expensive than ICEVs. > Hydrogen is pretty much DOA for cars. This is a good video on the subject: [VIDEO] It's a nonsense youtube video, citing claims that are a decade out of date."}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["AnaASTBn_K4",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1]]},"2gFsOoKAHZg":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an entertaining and thoughtful look at flat earthers that frames the belief as a distrust of failing institutions.","q":{"x":"I strongly recommend watching hbomberguy's video about flat earthers","i":22449006,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22449006,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-02","story":"If your cipher were secure, this image wouldn't have repeating patterns (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Frames flat earth as rejection of institutions failing people.","quote":"I strongly recommend watching hbomberguy's video about flat earthers","text":"I strongly recommend watching hbomberguy's video about flat earthers, \"Flat Earth: A Measured Response\" [VIDEO] The short summary is that flat earthers are probably seen as a rejection of established institutions (like capitalism/neoliberalism) that the (correctly) see as a major source of the problems in their life. Unfortunately, lacking the social/political background required to actually understand these problem, they work with what they can understand. \"The government/etc has been consistently lying to us for decades while our jobs were shipped overseas and our quality of life keeps sliding lower and lower. If they are consistent liars, why should I believe anything they've said?\""},{"id":39667315,"author":"smcl","month":"2024-03","story":"Latam Airlines SYD to AKL flight: 50 people treated after mid-air incident","stance":"rec","why":"Light entertainment debunking flat earth.","quote":"In case you want a bit of light entertainment, the video where I learned about this is here","text":"Yeah I've known someone (a family, actually) who did the same. In case you want a bit of light entertainment, the video where I learned about this is here - don't worry it's debunking flat earth, not in favour it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20470647,"author":"dejawu","month":"2019-07","story":"How YouTube converted people to flat Earth","stance":"rec","why":"Look at the present-day flat earth community.","quote":"is also an interesting look into the present-day flat earth \"community\".","text":"HBomberguy's \"Flat Earth: A Measured Response\" [0] is also an interesting look into the present-day flat earth \"community\". [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19061248,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-02","story":"Forget privacy: you're terrible at targeting anyway","stance":"rec","why":"Investigation of flat earth belief and why people hold it.","quote":"I recommend hbomberguy's recent investigation into recent flat earth belief","text":"> A flat earth makes sense if you only spent 5 minutes thinking about it and have no background in or exposure to basic science. Not that I particularly want to jump to the defense of people that believe in a flat earth, but they are not people that \"only spent 5 minutes thinking about it\". A lot of time has been spent trying to back up their beliefs and to form a coherent theory. They're wrong , of course, for many reasons, but it's incorrect and dismissive to call them intellectually lazy. The core of flat earth and similar beliefs is often a legitimate reaction against the large established systems in society that, from their perspective, appear to be failing in obvious ways while lying to them regularly that everything is fine. The are recognizing a problem, and attempt to apply something resembling science to try and find answers. I recommend hbomberguy's recent investigation into recent flat earth belief: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42181165,"author":"dijksterhuis","month":"2024-11","story":"Scientific American's departing editor and the politicization of science","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a YouTuber refuting flat earth claims.","quote":"most of it is so easy to refute any random youtuber with a spare hour can do it","text":"Scientist 1: If we put a sugar cube into water whose temperature is exactly 74.7373 degrees centigrade, the water will likely turn pink. here is our evidence for this. Scientist 2: we tried this and found that if the water is cooling that it doesn’t work, it has to remain at a constant temperature. Scientist 3: we tried it with refined and unrefined sugar. unrefined sugar did not work. scientist 1: we took another look - it seems there was some weird additive in the refined sugar, when this additive added to water at 74.7373 degrees centigrade the water turns pink. that’s a very silly and stupid example of “challenging” other scientist’s work. you precisely explain what you tried and how it differed, in the hope it leads to a more specific and accurate picture down the line. flat earthers et. al just “say stuff” they think is right, where the evidence does not actually challenge any hypothesis or existing evidence because the claims are just … bad. this is not “challenging” science. it is stubborn ignorance. pure and simple. most of it is so easy to refute any random youtuber with a spare hour can do it (read: 6-12 months [0]) - [VIDEO] however, your point about platforming is important, because people who wouldn’t have had a soapbox 15 years ago, now have a soapbox anyone in the world can find them on. if you’re looking for something to confirm your world view, there’s something on the internet for you. rule 1 of the internet should be spammed in front of everyone’s eyes for"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"Bkm2Vfj42FY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a concise, chart-backed explanation of the economics behind anti-establishment politics.","q":{"x":"which concisely outlines the economics behind the decline of western middle classes","i":13253112,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":20792602,"author":"einhverfr","month":"2019-08","story":"Blame Economists for the Mess We’re In","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Political economist on Keynesianism, neoliberalism and the global class revolt","quote":"I strongly recommend listening to Mark Blyth's 2016 lecture entitled \"Global Trumpism","text":"If you have the time and patience to listen to it I strongly recommend listening to Mark Blyth's 2016 lecture entitled \"Global Trumpism\" where as a political economist he discusses the implications of Keynsian economics, Neoliberalism and the class revolt we now see around the world. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13253112,"author":"wycx","month":"2016-12","story":"All Hollowed Out  the lonely poverty of America’s white working class","stance":"rec","why":"Economics behind decline of western middle classes and anti-establishment sentiment","quote":"which concisely outlines the economics behind the decline of western middle classes","text":"Thomas Frank on Listen Liberal: [LINK] I also suggest the following talk by Mark Blyth: [VIDEO] which concisely outlines the economics behind the decline of western middle classes, and the link to sentiment against mainstream political parties. If you are interested in how bad things look for europe (from 2013, but the fundamentals have not changed): [LINK]"},{"id":12909272,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-11","story":"Donald Trump is the president-elect of the U.S.","stance":"rec","why":"Discontent with establishment and revolt against technocracy and globalism","quote":"Mark Blyth's discussion of \"Global Trumpism\" - the discontent with the establishment and general revolt against technocracy and globalism.","text":"This was ignored when I tried to post it recently, but maybe now is the time for Brown U. Prof. of Political Economy Mark Blyth's discussion of \"Global Trumpism\" - the discontent with the establishment and general revolt against technocracy and globalism. Full lecture: [VIDEO] TL;DW 4min summary: [LINK]"},{"id":12666709,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-10","story":"Hillary Clinton's Wall St Speeches Published by Wikileaks","stance":"rec","why":"Explains rise of populism with charts","quote":"For a good explanation of this (with charts!), see Mark Blyth's recent lecture","text":"There is a rapidly growing group of people that are tired of the status quo. They are reacting strongly against the neoliberal and technocratic establishment. To the very large masses of blue collar workers, globalism means watching their jobs move to cheaper markets. This is the environment where populist movements arise, which are not tied to traditional right/left politics. Hence the rise of populists like Trump, anti-establishment reactions like brexit, and a long list of similar movements that have been popping up around the world. For a good explanation of this (with charts!), see Mark Blyth's recent lecture[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"KPUlgSRn6e0":{"wy":"HN recommends it whenever fitness, aging or cycling comes up, as the clearest statement that city design is the best public health investment.","q":{"x":"The best public investment to promote physical activity is designing cities to enable \"the gym of life\"!","i":37636085,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":32506672,"author":"Doxin","month":"2022-08","story":"Cycle for One Hour and You’ll Live an Hour Longer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows that dislike of cycling is mostly about the city, not cycling.","quote":"I can heartily recommend anyone who thinks they dislike cycling to watch Not Just Bikes on youtube","text":"I can heartily recommend anyone who thinks they dislike cycling to watch Not Just Bikes on youtube[0]. Most of the problem is the city you live in, not the concept of cycling. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39294691,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Best Preventative for Aging Parents?","stance":"rec","why":"Daily exercise through walking in urban areas.","quote":"See \"The Gym of Life\"","text":"> Daily exercise This can simply be walking a lot, especially if you're in a more urban area, where you don't have to drive everywhere (either figuratively or literally). See \"The Gym of Life\": * [VIDEO]"},{"id":37636085,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2023-09","story":"Workers who cut countertops are dying of silicosis","stance":"rec","why":"Designing cities for physical activity as the best public health investment.","quote":"The best public investment to promote physical activity is designing cities to enable \"the gym of life\"!","text":"If not sugar, then other causes should be taxed if the evidence supports that such taxes would have an impact. I'm not a policy expert myself. The best public investment to promote physical activity is designing cities to enable \"the gym of life\"! [VIDEO]"},{"id":34691827,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: I hate gym. How to stay in shape?","stance":"rec","why":"Describes how walkable and bikeable cities replace the gym.","quote":"this video describes the idea of \"The Gym of Life\" well","text":"Live somewhere you can bike and/or walk to work and for your daily needs. Admittedly this can be weather-dependent, so it might not suit your requirement (though even living in Ireland I find rain only rarely causes an issue). I lost about 100 pounds this way. Moving to a new country would be one of the more extreme options but this video describes the idea of \"The Gym of Life\" well [VIDEO]"},{"id":47140688,"author":"Herring","month":"2026-02","story":"Cardiorespiratory fitness is associated with lower anger and anxiety","stance":"reference","why":"Argues cities should build physical activity into daily life rather than rely on gyms.","quote":"it literally has to be built into the design of the cities, so people get enough exercise while walking to work or grabbing groceries.","text":"Exercise is good, everyone knows. The problem is advising people to exercise doesn't work and doesn't scale. Gyms are for people who have plenty of intrinsic motivation and money and time. To improve physical activity at the population scale and over a lifetime, it literally has to be built into the design of the cities, so people get enough exercise while walking to work or grabbing groceries. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44996352,"author":"Herring","month":"2025-08","story":"The ROI of Exercise","stance":"reference","why":"Argues cities should build physical activity into daily life rather than rely on gyms.","quote":"it literally has to be built into the design of the cities, so people get enough exercise while walking to work or grabbing groceries.","text":"Advising people to exercise doesn't work and doesn't scale. Gyms are for people who have plenty of intrinsic motivation and money and time. To improve physical activity at the population scale and over a lifetime, it literally has to be built into the design of the cities, so people get enough exercise while walking to work or grabbing groceries. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":44570642,"author":"Herring","month":"2025-07","story":"LLM Inevitabilism","stance":"reference","why":"Cars damaged human fitness; walkable cities are the response.","quote":"","text":"Cars were/are inevitable. But they did massive damage to human fitness, which we still haven't recovered from. I intentionally don't own one, and at least some places in the world are starting to wake up and restrict them and build walkable cities. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41106979,"author":"LeChuck","month":"2024-07","story":"If we want a shift to walking, we need to prioritize dignity","stance":"reference","why":"Case for walking and biking for health and environment.","quote":"","text":"Because walking is good for you and good for the environment. Additionally, if everyone walked/biked when possible the roads would be more pleasant for you to drive on. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36735803,"author":"oblio","month":"2023-07","story":"S.F. says incidents by Cruise, Waymo driverless taxis are ‘skyrocketing.’","stance":"reference","why":"Shows daily errands without a car.","quote":"","text":"Because you see no other way to grocery shop than what you're doing right now. [VIDEO] In my case I didn't go by bike, I walked to the local grocery store 500m or so from home. You'd just pop in on your way home from work or from whatever you'd be doing in town. 5 minutes in a small grocery store, back home with a bag weighting max 10kg, usually 2-5. This is close to impossible in many places. Big business killed small living."},{"id":34408065,"author":"greggman3","month":"2023-01","story":"Quitting the Rat Race","stance":"reference","why":"Walking built into commutes as exercise.","quote":"","text":"Yes, I did consider that. That's exactly my point. He stated he hates (A) and likes (B). I suggested that hating (A) is a poor POV. It would be better to like both (A) and (B) even if you prefer (B). Let's put it another way. If someone says the France sucks, Germany rules. And someone replies \"The France doesn't suck, here's a few reasons why\". Why do you feel the need to jump in and defend the POV that \"France sucks\"? I also listed spending time with people as a plus to the city. It's interesting that you left that out. I'd guess if the author had a job they loved with people they loved their attitude about everything else would change. The fact they're in a job they hate arguably taints everything else about their life. Commuting of course sucks if it's to a place you don't want to go in the first place. To me, commuting by public transport rocks because I got, on average, 50+ minutes of walking (25 each way) for free (added to the 20 minutes of standing on the train) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["Ot4qdCs54ZE",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1],["0lvUByM-fZk",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["5q-BH-tvxEg",1],["saRir95iIWk",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["zT58pzY41wA",1],["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["MejbOFk7H6c",1],["qWKz3psejb0",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["QvW61K2s0tA",1]]},"YbZ3zDzDnrw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a clearer introduction to Raft than the paper and as part of a well-designed understandability study.","q":{"x":"The 2 videos do an excellent job of explaining both the Paxos and Raft algorithms and the key differences between them.","i":8529187,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8529187,"author":"krat0sprakhar","month":"2014-10","story":"The Raft Consensus Algorithm","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear lecture explaining Raft and Paxos and how they differ.","quote":"The 2 videos do an excellent job of explaining both the Paxos and Raft algorithms and the key differences between them.","text":"One of the most lucid explanations of the Raft Consensus Algorithm that I've ever seen is the one in John Ousterhout and Diego Ongaro's Raft User Study[0]. The 2 videos do an excellent job of explaining both the Paxos and Raft algorithms and the key differences between them. John's narration is extremely clear and the slides are very informative. Definitely recommended! Raft is a consensus algorithm designed to be easier to understand than Paxos. To measure Raft's understandability, we conducted an experimental study using CS students at two universities. We recorded a video lecture of Raft and another of Paxos, and created corresponding quizzes. This page makes our materials available for anyone interested. We think these are valuable resources for anyone learning consensus (whether Raft or Paxos or both). [0] - [LINK] Raft Video - [VIDEO] Paxos Video - [LINK]"},{"id":44928657,"author":"sathish316","month":"2025-08","story":"The Raft Consensus Algorithm (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Solid explanation of Raft internals by its creators.","quote":"gives a solid explanation of Raft internals","text":"This YouTube video by the Raft creators (linked at the bottom of the page) gives a solid explanation of Raft internals - [VIDEO]"},{"id":6662497,"author":"pkj","month":"2013-11","story":"The Writings of Leslie Lamport","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to Raft with clear practical examples.","quote":"A really good intro to Raft","text":"A really good intro to Raft --> [VIDEO] Very useful if you are into distributed systems and would like to see a practical implementation with clear examples."},{"id":6367282,"author":"tomjohnson3","month":"2013-09","story":"ZooKeeper vs. Doozer vs. Etcd","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on Paxos from the Raft user study.","quote":"i found the videos from the raft user study to be very well done (and easier to understand than even their paper)","text":"one other thing to keep in mind is that the underlying algorithms (zab and raft) provide different guarantees. for example, zookeeper/zab allows reading directly from followers with a guarantee to get at least a past value that won't be rolled back. this was one reason zookeer didn't use paxos: [LINK] in my understanding, raft doesn't allow reading directly from followers, because the follower logs may get repaired/rolled-back when a new leader is elected. (though i'm sure an implementation can tweak the protocol to provide this support.) that said, raft has a lot of interesting applications, and, in my opinion, is definitely more understandable than the many versions of paxos. (implementing zab yourself, at this point, would be a futile exercise.) i found the videos from the raft user study to be very well done (and easier to understand than even their paper): raft: [LINK] paxos: [VIDEO] ...however, i think they did paxos a disadvantage by not just focusing on multi-paxos (which is probably the most common implementation). but, it's certainly fair to say that info about paxos is spread out far and wide...with perhaps too many knobs to turn and implementation-related details to fill in yourself. as a side note: i've just started implementing raft in a set of libraries (multiple languages) that will be open source - along with other protocols."},{"id":27860020,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-07","story":"Paxos vs. Raft: Have we reached consensus on distributed consensus?","stance":"reference","why":"Part of user studies comparing understandability of Raft and Paxos.","quote":"Raft is very similar to Multi-Paxos.","text":"Raft is very similar to Multi-Paxos. These Raft and Paxos user studies comparing the \"understandability\" of the two protocols might be helpful: [LINK] (Paxos), [VIDEO] (Raft). See also, comparison of various \"Paxos\" implementations: [LINK]"},{"id":26888068,"author":"throwawaybbq1","month":"2021-04","story":"“They introduce kernel bugs on purpose”","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a well-designed user study comparing Paxos and Raft.","quote":"One of the most impressive ways I have seen this being done by grad students was a user study by John Ousterhout and others on Paxos vs. Raft.","text":"This is a good question. You would recruit actual maintainers, [edit: or whoever is your intended subject pool] (who would provide consent, perhaps be compensated for their time). You could then give them a series of patches to approve (some being bug free and others having vulnerabilities). [edit: specifying the population of a study is pretty important. Getting random students from the University to approve your security patch doesn't make sense. Picking students who successfully completed a computer security course and got a high grade is better than that but again, may not generalize to the real world. One of the most impressive ways I have seen this being done by grad students was a user study by John Ousterhout and others on Paxos vs. Raft. IIRC, they wanted to claim that Raft was more understandable or led to fewer bugs. Their study design was excellent. See here for an example: [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":8272500,"author":"deathanatos","month":"2014-09","story":"Raft: Understandable Distributed Consensus","stance":"reference","why":"Talk accompanying slides explaining how Raft handles elections and conflicts.","quote":"there's also a YouTube talk here","text":"> and does not address the situation of two nodes having conflicting sets of data (for instance from network partition). I believe it does address this. Each log entry is either committed or not; an entry can only be committed if it has been replicated to a majority of nodes. Any node that lacks a committed entry cannot be elected master because of the election rules: a node will not vote for another node less complete than itself. Since a committed entry has been replicated to a majority, a node lacking that entry cannot receive a majority of the votes. (Thus the committed log entries will always be the same on all nodes (though some may be behind, and may only have a subset), which is the purpose of the protocol.) > Considering it basically relies on random chance (I.E. who receives the message first) to elect a master, has basically no real way of resolving a conflict in election This is mostly true. The PDF slides I link to below recommend that the election timeout be much greater than the broadcast time, the idea being that things should work out in the long run. Highly recommend the PDF slides here, as they explain it better than I can: [LINK] — there's also a YouTube talk here: [VIDEO] > Considering all that, this protocol doesn't seem very interesting (from a use-case point of view). I'd love to hear of alternatives."},{"id":8380003,"author":"nieksand","month":"2014-09","story":"Ask HN: Final year project in CS","stance":"reference","why":"Raft video as an understandable protocol to implement.","quote":"implementing the Raft consensus protocol could be neat too. It was designed to be understandable and implementable.","text":"Consider doing some language processing using the excellent NLTK library: [LINK] It wasn't on the lists of \"wants\", but implementing the Raft consensus protocol could be neat too. It was designed to be understandable and implementable. The video is here: [VIDEO] . The research paper is here: [LINK]"},{"id":6373887,"author":"tomjohnson3","month":"2013-09","story":"ZooKeeper vs. Doozer vs. Etcd","stance":"reference","why":"Explains log repair during leader election.","quote":"log entries on a follower may get rolled back - and thrown out - since they were not accepted on a majority of followers.","text":"i don't think this is the case. here is a link to a video by one of the authors about log repair during leader election: [VIDEO] log entries on a follower may get rolled back - and thrown out - since they were not accepted on a majority of followers."}],"r":[["JEpsBg0AO6o",2]]},"gAk7aX5hksU":{"wy":"In early pandemic threads, HN commenters pointed to it as an uninterrupted, practical expert view from Korea on masks, testing and airborne spread.","q":{"x":"The videos are excellent because the doctors are not interrupted with questions.","i":23025601,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23136769,"author":"dkfjdkfjda","month":"2020-05","story":"Take the Shutdown Skeptics Seriously","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interviews with Korean expert saying masks are effective.","quote":"I highly recommend these interviews with one of their leading experts, what he says makes a lot of sense","text":"I agree there's a lot of misconceptions and myths about masks. It clearly is not a panacea, it's really difficult (or even impossible) to quantify the impact, especially when combined with other precautions. Plus there's very little research about how masks affect this particular virus, because it's always somewhat specific. IMO the best thing we can do is look at experience from countries that managed to get the infection under control, like SK. I highly recommend these interviews with one of their leading experts, what he says makes a lot of sense: [LINK] [VIDEO] When he says face masks are one of the effective measures, I'd probably trust him ... The question is what exactly should be the rules. Our country is in a lockdown for ~2 months now, and most of the time face masks were required when leaving the house. We're probably going to relax the rules a bit soon, only requiring them when closed spaced, etc. Which probably makes sense, it's pointless to wear a mask when jogging alone in the woods, or something like that (and people were not following that perfectly anyway). My personal opinion is that face masks do help, partly because they limit how far your droplets reach, partly because it limits how frequently you touch your face. Even a simple home-made mask or scarf will help with that. I think a lot of the \"do not wear masks\" recommendations in many countries is due to concerns that a recommendation to wear a face mask would make the shortage even worse."},{"id":23025601,"author":"dba7dba","month":"2020-04","story":"Recovered virus patients retest positive due to 'dead' virus fragments: experts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Uninterrupted long-form interviews with Korean coronavirus experts.","quote":"The videos are excellent because the doctors are not interrupted with questions.","text":"To learn more about coronavirus, treatment and vaccine development from the perspective of doctors in S. Korea, try watching these interviews in order. Each is about 30 - 50 min long. I posted the links and the dates they were uploaded. The videos are excellent because the doctors are not interrupted with questions. 1) 2020.03.27 1st interview with Professor Kim, leading expert on coronavirus in Korea. In Korean with excellent English subtitles. [VIDEO] 2) 2020.04.14 2nd interview with Professor Kim, leading expert on coronavirus in Korea. In Korean with excellent English subtitles. [LINK] 3) 2020.04.25 Interview with the Director General of the International Vaccine Institute (IVI) about COVID-19 Vaccine. It's in English. [LINK] 4) Note in the interview with Prof Kim uploaded on 2020.04.14, they were still not sure if a recovered patient testing positive again was due to reactivation/reinfection or not. They were still trying to confirm it as of 2020.04.14. But it seems the yna.co.kr article posted on 2020.04.29 confirms recovered coronavirus patients may have tested positive again due to traces of virus fragments that have been inactivated. 5) I'm hoping the Youtube channel will upload another interview with Prof Kim about covid-19 soon."},{"id":22726946,"author":"bkor","month":"2020-03","story":"The origin story of the N95 mask","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Korean expert on masks, testing and spread.","quote":"The entire video is great, so suggest to watch the whole thing.","text":"According to the Corona expert in South Korea, masks help a great deal. His assumption on why the WHO only recommends masks in Asia and not anywhere else is due to the limited amount of supply anywhere but in Asia. In Asia people like already own masks. Anywhere else people would first need to stock up. Leading to shortages. As such, it's better not to recommend them so medical professionals are prioritized. It's also interesting that in in Europe/US they focus on a person not getting a disease. In Asia they often wear a masks if the person isn't feeling well. Meaning, the mask is to prevent the person who is wearing it from infecting others. This bit is often glossed over. The video is available at [VIDEO] . I don't know exactly where they discussed the masks. The entire video is great, so suggest to watch the whole thing. Note: It also talks about spreading by touching your nose and then someone or some surface."},{"id":22734442,"author":"JimTheMan","month":"2020-03","story":"On “Armchair Epidemiology”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Korean perspective on masks, testing and pandemic preparedness.","quote":"I highly recommend this video from Korea","text":"I highly recommend this video from Korea (Professor Kim Woo-joo from Korea University Guro Hospital) that gives a Korean perspective on the disease and the western response to it: [VIDEO] A few points of interest: (He is a very respectful fellow so apologies if the summary seems a bit to the point) - Masks help and he is slightly puzzled why the west hasn't adopted that culture - Their testing and tracing helps and they doubt the US would allow their level of privacy invasion - Korea is doing well because they have had to respond to pandemics and were prepared (as are Taiwan, Singapore etc)"},{"id":22751397,"author":"balladeer","month":"2020-04","story":"Bill Gates calls for nationwide shutdown","stance":"reference","why":"Supports Korea's test, isolate and mask approach.","quote":"","text":"> The numbers from Korea, Italy and the Diamond princess are not good but they are not world-ending either. You lumped Korea and Italy together which is strange. Then apparently (seeing your comment chain) you tried to transpose it onto US and rest of the world. Korea was ready (experience with previous outbreaks). They started early. They tested like there's no tomorrow and are still doing that. Test - isolate, test - isolate, test - isolate. Everybody wearing masks - negative, positive, or untested. Everybody [1] And their health infra is impeccable. They are people with a great civic sense (I have lived there for months). 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22838101,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-04","story":"South Korea reports recovered Covid-19 patients testing positive again","stance":"reference","why":"Covers recovered patients retesting positive at 7:59.","quote":"The issue is talked about at 7:59 in this video","text":"The issue is talked about at 7:59 in this video [VIDEO] (Leading COVID-19 Expert Professor Kim Woo-joo from Korea University Guro Hospital)"},{"id":22845456,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-04","story":"S.Korea says recovered Covid-19 patients re-testing positive","stance":"reference","why":"Covers recovered patients retesting positive at 7:59.","quote":"The issue is talked about at 7:59 in this video","text":"The issue is talked about at 7:59 in this video [VIDEO] (Leading COVID-19 Expert Professor Kim Woo-joo from Korea University Guro Hospital) made a couple of weeks ago."},{"id":22728905,"author":"gameswithgo","month":"2020-03","story":"A choir decided to go ahead with rehearsal, now dozens have Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Korean expert says aerosolization possible in crowded singing settings.","quote":"at least one expert, in South Korea, states that it is possible to become aerosolized and actually airborne","text":"This is an important distinction for experts who know the terms of art, but for regular people it is maybe misleading to keep insisting that \"it isn't airborne\". It is traveling through the air, you can get it, through the air, if you are near another person. I have seen this misunderstanding in real life. People who think an outdoor festival is safe because \"it isn't airborne\" As well, at least one expert, in South Korea, states that it is possible to become aerosolized and actually airborne in the right conditions (many people, singing, shouting, in a small area). Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22732342,"author":"bkdbkd","month":"2020-03","story":"A choir decided to go ahead with rehearsal, now dozens have Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Professor Kim on droplets becoming aerosols and Korean choir outbreak.","quote":"Professor Kim Woo-joo describes a similar incident in Korea, where a choir contracted the virus.","text":"In this interview Professor Kim Woo-joo describes a similar incident in Korea, where a choir contracted the virus. He adds that 5um is the min for 'droplet' infection, and that if the droplets remain airborne they can desiccate, and shrink , and then move from 'droplet' to 'aerosol'. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sBzRwzY7G-k",1]]},"lG4VkPoG3ko":{"wy":"HN's go-to link whenever Bayesian reasoning, base rates or imperfect tests come up.","q":{"x":"is a brilliant introduction to the concept","i":25758943,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25544087,"author":"drewmate","month":"2020-12","story":"1937 film explains how a car differential works","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engaging explanation of the medical test paradox and Bayes' rule.","quote":"All his videos are absolutely phenomenal - the best educational content I've seen on YouTube.","text":"I agree that this is definitely a trend, so I feel like highlighting a great example of educational content that is very dense, concise, and with just a touch of whimsy: 3Blue1Brown on Youtube[0]. He has been doing math education videos on YouTube for years. I just watched his latest video on the medical test paradox and redesigning the Bayes rule to address it[1]. All his videos are absolutely phenomenal - the best educational content I've seen on YouTube. I cannot recommend them highly enough. In my experience it is more akin to reading a (very engaging) textbook than watching a video. I often find myself pausing to think things through and can _feel_ myself grasping at new ideas and learning while watching. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44982810,"author":"V__","month":"2025-08","story":"Everything is correlated (2014–23)","stance":"rec","why":"Thinking of significance as a probability update.","quote":"I really like this video [1] from 3blue1brown","text":"I really like this video [1] from 3blue1brown, where he proposes to think about significance as a way to update the probability. One positive test (or in this analog a study) updates the probability by X % and thus you nearly always need more tests (or studies) for a 'meaningful' judgment. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39174037,"author":"andersrs","month":"2024-01","story":"The cancer that some doctors don't want to call cancer","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why rare conditions make positives mostly false.","quote":"3Blue1Brown explains it well.","text":"3Blue1Brown explains it well. [VIDEO] If the population who do _not_ actually have serious cancer is orders of magnitudes larger than those who do then there will be far more people in the false positive group than the true positive group and thus the vast majority of operations will be unnecessary."},{"id":25758943,"author":"kilotaras","month":"2021-01","story":"U.S. to require negative Covid-19 tests for international air passengers","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to updating odds from imperfect tests.","quote":"is a brilliant introduction to the concept","text":"Almost no tests in existence can confirm or deny particular infection. Think of them (and symptoms) more as odds updater. If you have 20 minutes to spare this video [0] by 3 Blue 1 Brown is a brilliant introduction to the concept. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46088800,"author":"conartist6","month":"2025-11","story":"Major AI conference flooded with peer reviews written by AI","stance":"reference","why":"Bayesian reasoning applied to imperfect detectors.","quote":"","text":"Usually the detectors are only called in once a basic \"smell test\" has failed. Those tests are imperfect, yes, but Bayesian probability tells us how to work out the rest. I have 0 trouble believing that the prior probability of an unscrupulous individual offloading an unpleasant and perceived-as-just-ceremonial duty to the \"thinking machine\" is around 20%. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37822905,"author":"lovecg","month":"2023-10","story":"The Prosecutor's Fallacy (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Bayesian odds update explained.","quote":"obligatory 3blue1brown video","text":"For me, taking the logarithm makes it even more intuitive somehow - the test becomes something like a modifier in an RPG-style game. In your example, the test gives +2 to the possibility of having a disease, a more powerful test could be +3, etc. Adding all the different +/- modifiers works just as well, and all this math is easy to do in one’s head. Edit: also, obligatory 3blue1brown video [VIDEO]"},{"id":37823226,"author":"mananaysiempre","month":"2023-10","story":"The Prosecutor's Fallacy (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Odds formulation of Bayes' rule.","quote":"See also 3Blue1Brown","text":"The advantage of the odds formulation is that you can compute posterior odds in your head, whereas computing the posterior probabilities via the standard form of the theorem (that you wrote down here) involves an unpleasant normalization factor. E.g. for the example in [LINK] : prior odds of infection = 2 : 98 × likelihood ratio = 95 : 5 = posterior odds ≈ 200 : 500 and thus the probabilities (if you actually need them at this point) are ≈ (2/7, 5/7). See also 3Blue1Brown’s exposition: [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["2SuvO4Gi7uY",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["Y5K9HBiJpuk",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1],["SGJ5cZnoodY",1],["PJnJ8mK3Q3g",1],["t52LB2fYhoY",1],["FvNRlE4E9QQ",1]]},"uknEhXyZgsg":{"wy":"Recommended as a vivid look at live, interactive programming environments and what Xerox PARC built on very limited hardware.","q":{"x":"have really been enjoying this two hour long interview / demo with Dan Ingalls on Smalltalk and the Alto","i":30871579,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33271783,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2022-10","story":"Stop Writing Dead Programs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Smalltalk-76 on a restored Xerox Alto, impressive on limited hardware.","quote":"here's another great related video that is well worth watching","text":"Since the talk was already posted yesterday, here's another great related video that is well worth watching: Dan Ingalls demonstrating Smalltalk 76 on a restored Xerox Alto. [VIDEO] This is where it all started and it's truly amazing what they achieved on such limited hardware."},{"id":30871579,"author":"MaxLeiter","month":"2022-03","story":"The wild world of non-C operating systems","stance":"rec","why":"Two hour interview and demo with Dan Ingalls on Smalltalk.","quote":"have really been enjoying this two hour long interview / demo with Dan Ingalls on Smalltalk and the Alto","text":"I’ve been on a Smalltalk binge the last few days and have really been enjoying this two hour long interview / demo with Dan Ingalls on Smalltalk and the Alto: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25622823,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-01","story":"On Repl-Driven Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Demos of Xerox PARC and similar early environments.","quote":"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing","text":"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing, while Bell Labs was busy pushing for UNIX. \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Emulating a Xerox Star (8010) Information System Running the Xerox Development Environment (XDE) 5.0\" [LINK] \"Documents as User Interfaces Video Demo\" [LINK] \"SYMBOLICS S-PACKAGES 3D GRAPHICS AND ANIMATION DEMO\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [VIDEO] \"Action!, the worlds first dynamic interface builder - 1988\" (Interface Builder percursor, written in Lisp) [LINK] \"The Interlisp programming environment\" [LINK] And the cherry, how Lucid used Lisp ideas for their Energize C++ IDE, including an image based format for AST storage [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23078814,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-05","story":"Advanced Programming Languages (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk on the Alto.","quote":"Here are some video sessions.","text":"Anything based on the workstation concepts explored at Xerox PARC, and further pursued at ETHZ, TI and Genera. Bitsavers has all the necessary papers and manuals, and computer museum as well. Here are some video sessions. \"Yesterday's Computer of Tomorrow: The Xerox Alto │Smalltalk-76 Demo\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [VIDEO] \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer\" [LINK] The end of the road from Plan 9, Inferno. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":45944769,"author":"cben","month":"2025-11","story":"The Mac calculator's original design came from letting Steve play with menus","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"PARC showing Steve Jobs how the Smalltalk environment allowing modifying GUI behavior at run-time","text":"I'm not sure of the timing (and whether you're sarcastic) but yeah, I found it funny compared to PARC showing Steve Jobs how the Smalltalk environment allowing modifying GUI behavior at run-time let them iterate quickly, sometimes in front of users ( [VIDEO] ). I guess [LINK] is the closest you get with a compiled language :-) P.S. [LINK] has good historical context on that visit(s)"},{"id":43080380,"author":"morphle","month":"2025-02","story":"Homemade polarimetric synthetic aperture radar drone","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You won't get any reliable numbers during wartime. I heard Ukrainian government recently mention they ordered a million. Hundreds of small companies (around 10 employees each) in Ukraine are making and assembling drone's right now (according to journalists who filmed them in Ukraine last month). I counted at least two dozen companies in the Netherlands, probably a few hundred all over Europe. Besides the frame that holds the 4 or six rotors together all other parts are off the shelf, mostly ordered from Shenzen. You can CNC mill a carbon fiber or aluminum frame from a single plate on an x-y flatbed cnc. I researched all this last night because I need $20K-$200k investment right now to start my own startup making unjammable autonomous kamikaze drones with 20K payload on top of 20km optical fiber spools unreeling. I think it is possible to make them lighter without fibers if you put a few thousand microprocessors in with a wafer scale integration for $500. Of course you could just make cheap drones that kill every warm body in sight[4] indiscriminately. To disable a tank you need these fiber tethered drones with bigger payloads[3][5][6]. You cab see two fiber strands still in the air in a few frames of the video. What you need is a Paul MacCready style development cycle for drones in the field. You need to be able to make 10 crashes per hour, fix the drone by replacing with off-the shelf components and try again. The programming should be live-coding like Squeak Smalltalk, no r"},{"id":33256706,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2022-10","story":"Stop Writing Dead Programs [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"They date back to Smalltalk, which was created at Xerox PARC in the 1970s","text":"The ideas he's promoting aren't new, and in fact predate even the NES. They date back to Smalltalk, which was created at Xerox PARC in the 1970s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27459085,"author":"Rochus","month":"2021-06","story":"Building the First GUIs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I assume you know these interesting presentations","text":"Bravo was modal, Gypsy was non-modal, Smalltalk-74 onwards had multi-windows (including pop-up menus) and a metaphorical desktop. I assume you know these interesting presentations: [LINK] , [VIDEO] EDIT: see also e.g. [LINK]"},{"id":24509116,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-09","story":"Nova by Panic","stance":"reference","why":"One of several demos of early integrated environments.","quote":"You can start by having a look at,","text":"It is a bit hard to explain in an HN comment something that requires live experience, You can start by having a look at, [LINK] \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [VIDEO] \"SYMBOLICS CONCORDIA ONLINE DOCUMENTATION HYPER TEXT MARKUP 1985\" [LINK] You can see how NeXT builds on many of these concepts on the famous \"NeXT vs Sun\" marketing piece, [LINK] Sun also had some ideas along these lines with NeWS, \"NeWS: A Networked and Extensible Window System,\" [LINK] Naturally OS/2, BeOS, Windows, macOS, iOS, and even Android share some of the ideas. Now, before I proceed note that actually modern Linux distributions have all the tooling to make these concepts happen, but it fails short to have everyone agree on a proper stack. So basically, the main idea is to have a proper full stack in place for developing a Workstation computer as one single experience, from bottom all the way to the top. On Xerox's case, they used bytecode with in-CPU execution via programmable microcode loaded on boot, and later on just a thin native glue on top of host OS. The environments had frameworks / modules for the whole OS stack, supported distributed computing, embedded of data structures across applications (OLE can trace its roots back to these ideas), REPLs that not only could interact with the whole OS (commands, modules, running applications), it was also possible to break into the debugger, change the code and redo the failed instructions."}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["lgivCGdmFrw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["8LGDM9exlZw",1]]},"3uiEyEKji0M":{"wy":"Recommended as the clearest explanation of Halide's ideas and of scheduling for performance.","q":{"x":"The best way to understand it is probably to watch the video, which is relatively long but explains things clearly","i":7543596,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":27055261,"author":"T-R","month":"2021-05","story":"Apache Arrow 4.0","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates locality and vectorization concepts behind column-oriented layouts.","quote":"I feel like this talk on Halide[0] is really illustrative of the general concepts.","text":"This is what I was trying to get at - using column vectors gives good cache locality and lets you use SIMD for \"multiply all of these by this scalar\" for each column, and then for \"sum all of these\" for the resulting rows. I'd imagine it could also let you optimize multiplications into things like bit-shifts with minimal overhead as well, though I have no idea if that's done in practice. Maybe only tangentially related, but I feel like this talk on Halide[0] is really illustrative of the general concepts. As others have mentioned, for some operations it can also save you from loading whole columns that aren't relevant for your transformation. The compression point in the sibling comment is definitely also relevant, especially for serialization. A whole lot of reasons to use column vectors. Using \"column-major\" here might've been terminology abuse; sorry for the confusion. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18769780,"author":"all2","month":"2018-12","story":"Halide: a language for fast, portable computation on images and tensors","stance":"rec","why":"Talk from the creator with illustrations explaining how it works.","quote":"Here is a relevant video from the creator. It has some fun illustrations","text":"[VIDEO] Here is a relevant video from the creator. It has some fun illustrations to help with what its going on. /edit - letters"},{"id":17167683,"author":"whos3424","month":"2018-05","story":"How to write efficient matrix multiplication","stance":"rec","why":"Opens new ground in performance work balancing parallelism, locality, redundancy.","quote":"they seem to open new ground in the area you are prusuing","text":"please look at [LINK] and especially [VIDEO] they seem to open new ground in the area you are prusuing edit: if you know any other groups/ppl who practice a similar approach send them my way anyhow, it seems like the hand written assembly vs compiled code goes in to a new skirmish the age where the whole spectrum from generalcpu > asic becomes available to programmers all around bottle necks of parallelism, locality and redundancy seems to be the guiding lights hand written we'll be probably lead by the magnificent [LINK] compiler generated by halide and the like and once we got the ball rolling and more public knowledge is available probably the locked down techniques of the major supercomputers will start to emerge ( i just assume pps from the supercomputers area have been dealing with this stuff for the last couple of decades ) can i spit some more, dear hacker news? focus, you gotta focus, forget about the compiler generating good code, it's all about the ability for us humans to explore the domain even in halide, they mention the point that you still need to sit down and code different approaches, but their tool makes it a breeze compared to exploring those different approaches using hand written assembly"},{"id":17168632,"author":"tehsauce","month":"2018-05","story":"How to write efficient matrix multiplication","stance":"rec","why":"Animations of scheduling approaches for linear algebra algorithms.","quote":"A really nice video with animations of various scheduling approaches for these types of linear algebra algorithms","text":"A really nice video with animations of various scheduling approaches for these types of linear algebra algorithms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7543596,"author":"modeless","month":"2014-04","story":"Speed memory access by arranging data to take advantage of CPU caching","stance":"rec","why":"Clear, long explanation of Halide's algorithm/schedule separation.","quote":"The best way to understand it is probably to watch the video, which is relatively long but explains things clearly","text":"You can see it a little bit in the blur example on the front page: the blur algorithm is described in two lines and the optimizations are described separately in the \"schedule\" below. In this case the schedule specifies that intermediate results should be computed in tiles of a certain size, and the whole thing is both vectorized and parallelized. The best way to understand it is probably to watch the video, which is relatively long but explains things clearly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18567488,"author":"Groxx","month":"2018-11","story":"Go 2, here we come","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies Halide as the technique they were trying to remember.","quote":"I'd love to find out if this could be a more generally-applicable technique, but it's fascinating regardless.","text":"Halide! That's the one I was thinking of: [VIDEO] I'd love to find out if this could be a more generally-applicable technique, but it's fascinating regardless."},{"id":13148909,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2016-12","story":"Fast Fourier transform in x86 assembly","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as video resource alongside website and paper for Halide.","quote":"","text":"Halide is an interesting direction for generating optimized code. By isolating algorithm from schedule, it is better able to optimize for vectorization, locality, pipelining, threading, tiling, and recomputation vs. storage. It then uses LLVM to do all the ordinary optimization. Example code: // The algorithm - no storage or order blur_x(x, y) = (input(x-1, y) + input(x, y) + input(x+1, y))/3; blur_y(x, y) = (blur_x(x, y-1) + blur_x(x, y) + blur_x(x, y+1))/3; // The schedule - defines order, locality; implies storage blur_y.tile(x, y, xi, yi, 256, 32) .vectorize(xi, 8).parallel(y); blur_x.compute_at(blur_y, x).vectorize(x, 8); Halide can auto-optimize to create schedules for specific architectures, which can match or exceed performance of C code written by experts. It's intended for image processing, and has an unnatural affinity for rectangles, but it's an interesting direction for optimization. Darkroom does similar optimization for FPGAs Website: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] Paper: [LINK]"}],"r":[["jHP942Livy0",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["hM_h0UA7upI",1],["5QwOpRh-IfI",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["t0mhvd3-60Y",1]]},"90NsjKvz9Ns":{"wy":"HN links it whenever menu hover problems or nearest-to-pointer selection come up, and as proof algorithms matter in front-end work.","q":{"x":"Algorithms do play an important role as you can see in this great presentation","i":9945491,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9945491,"author":"jsteenkamp","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: The Case for the “Front End Engineer” Role","stance":"rec","why":"Shows algorithms playing an important role in front-end work.","quote":"Algorithms do play an important role as you can see in this great presentation","text":"I think you make an excellent observation that FE is a system engineering challenge. Algorithms do play an important role as you can see in this great presentation by Marc de Marco ( [VIDEO] ) However I think it's fair to say most FE engineers apply existing solutions and the system design is a bigger part of their role. I differentiate between UI Engineering and Front-End Engineering. The former emphasises design integration (pixels) and performance (rendering). The latter is broader scope and emphasises the systems engineering. Both have to deal with system design. UI is about components and messaging across the components with the front-end application being the \"system\". FE is about components that integrate the front-end application with what have traditionally been back-end APIs and services. In many cases when people say FE they mean both FE and UI. I would not get hung up on these but they help to clarify what you are talking about and who you are trying to hire. In the case of design and pixels there are specific challenges around dealing with layout and CSS and achieving 60fps. Creating UI components is an exciting area. For example see Pure UI ( [LINK] ) by @rauchg. Mess in Web Components ( [LINK] ). Challenge of style encapsulation... So there are a lot of unique challenges and skills and experience required in UI engineering. There are also exciting developments that start to push more overlap between UI and FE. For example GraphQL - the UI is \"language\" that \"pr"},{"id":8701422,"author":"cipher0","month":"2014-12","story":"Show HN: Piste.io","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses selecting nearest target to the mouse position.","quote":"There's also a great talk that discusses this from JSconf2014 [1]","text":"Great work! It might be cool if you could use voronoi diagrams to select the closest path to the mouse's position like the NYT did [0] because without really zooming in, the lines on some terrains appear to be very thin which makes hovering over a specific one a little harder. There's also a great talk that discusses this from JSconf2014 [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8191280,"author":"dkannan","month":"2014-08","story":"Mapping Migration in the United States","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant section at 4:13; rest of talk is good too.","quote":"relevant section starts at 4:13 rest of the talk is good as well","text":"Mark DiMarco gave a talk at JSConf 2014 called User Interface Algorithms which touches upon this [VIDEO] relevant section starts at 4:13 rest of the talk is good as well"},{"id":7993618,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2014-07","story":"How Does One Create A Gtk+ Application?","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating talk linked from a bug report.","quote":"This video was linked from it, fascinating stuff","text":"Am I correct thinking you are talking about this bug? [LINK] If so, then yes they are aware of it and treating it as critical. Edit: Reading into this bug is so cool. This video was linked from it, fascinating stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17401051,"author":"currysausage","month":"2018-06","story":"Show HN: Mimicking the Bloomberg menu widget without JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the diagonal-hover menu problem that Bloomberg's menu solves.","quote":"solving the famous problem that when you accidentally hover another menu item while moving the cursor to the right","text":"The original implementation at bloomberg.com is really well-executed, even solving the famous problem that when you accidentally hover another menu item while moving the cursor to the right, you might activate the adjacent menu item. [1] It took VLC ages to get rid of this issue, but it is solved as of VLC 3.0.3 (relevant Qt bug: [2]). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8583606,"author":"gorhill","month":"2014-11","story":"Pointer Pointer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows serious applications of finding the closest item to the pointer.","quote":"Same solution is also useful for other more serious applications","text":"Look beyond the funny app. Think of the challenge of finding an efficient solution to the original problem regardless of its \"unseriousness\": finding the optimal picture according to pointer position. Same solution is also useful for other more serious applications: [VIDEO] Edit: Sorry, I missed the \"me\" in your \"entertain me\"."}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1]]},"H6ZFzEW7_Q4":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it in threads on homelessness and drug policy as a short, honest firsthand account from an articulate addict.","q":{"x":"Extremely powerful 5 minute interview with an obviously intelligent homeless person addicted to heroin: Terrifying","i":28994813,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28994813,"author":"blackandsqueaky","month":"2021-10","story":"My experience of losing a friend to heroin (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Powerful short interview with an intelligent homeless heroin addict.","quote":"Extremely powerful 5 minute interview with an obviously intelligent homeless person addicted to heroin: Terrifying","text":"Extremely powerful 5 minute interview with an obviously intelligent homeless person addicted to heroin: [VIDEO] Terrifying"},{"id":26787434,"author":"Grieving","month":"2021-04","story":"Heroin-Using Professor Wants to Change How We Think About Drugs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Self-aware, honest interview with a homeless heroin addict.","quote":"this interview ( ) with a homeless heroin adict made a great impression on me. He comes off as both extremely self-aware and honest.","text":"Can't read with the paywall, and this is probably contrary to the message of the article, but this interview ( [VIDEO] ) with a homeless heroin adict made a great impression on me. He comes off as both extremely self-aware and honest."},{"id":27953870,"author":"nate_meurer","month":"2021-07","story":"Finland provides housing and counseling to the homeless (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Hear firsthand from addicts on YouTube.","quote":"Youtube is actually a fantastic resource for the average Joe (like me) who wants to hear how it is straight from the source","text":"> doing drugs to forget your life has been a mess for a long time is not a lifestyle choice decision. That's a bold statement. Choice and compulsion occupy the same place in hell. Youtube is actually a fantastic resource for the average Joe (like me) who wants to hear how it is straight from the source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25906528,"author":"itake","month":"2021-01","story":"Finland has slashed homelessness; the rest of Europe is failing","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with a homeless person, offered as firsthand account of addiction.","quote":"Checkout these interviews on Youtube: LA -","text":"> Have you actually talked to them? yes. Everyone has their own story. Checkout these interviews on Youtube: LA - [VIDEO] Seattle - [LINK] The homeless I talked with in Boulder romanticize camping forever along the creeks and being free from society, while trying to leach off of public services. > Do you know why they prefer their lifestyle? Drug addiction, mental health, and in some cases lack of family support. > that drug addiction is a lifestyle choice Drug addiction is a choice. Based on my very limited personal experiences, you gotta commit to making a change (see above videos). Many addicts just aren't ready to leave their vices for a better life. No amount of rehab will help someone if they aren't ready to commit to a change. > society failed them. Sorry if this sounds conservative, but trying to blame other people for your problems doesn't really get you any where. Society throws problems at everyone, some more than others. Laying around complaining or rejecting it doesn't improve anyone's situation. People need to apply critical thinking skills and tackle their own issues (hence the need for an addict needing to commit to the idea of solving the problem of their addiction). Ex-homeless/addicts getting together to correct \"society failed\" them problems via AA meetings is an excellent example of people that apply critical thinking skills and try to help."}],"r":[["xe-f4gokRBs",1]]},"ZDR433b0HJY":{"wy":"HN commenters keep recommending it as the best video for newcomers who want to understand how Git works technically.","q":{"x":"This is the video that I keep recommending to new people who want to know how it works on a technical level.","i":13229253,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13229253,"author":"kwe","month":"2016-12","story":"A Git catastrophe cleaned up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended to newcomers wanting a technical grasp of Git.","quote":"This is the video that I keep recommending to new people who want to know how it works on a technical level.","text":"This is the video that I keep recommending to new people who want to know how it works on a technical level. I've always had amazing/euphoric feedback to that recommendation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13073585,"author":"jzl","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: Is there a “ground-up” explanation of PGP/GnuPG?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how git works internally in simplest terms.","quote":"Scott Chacon's videos are priceless.","text":"Scott Chacon's videos are priceless. (He's the co-author of the git book.) They explain exactly how git works internally in the simplest terms possible. Either or both of these: Introduction to Git - talk by Scott Chacon [LINK] Introduction to Git with Scott Chacon of GitHub [VIDEO] The latter is newer but a bit longer. Don't be fooled by the video names, these are introductions to the internals not just the interface."},{"id":4203534,"author":"grannyg00se","month":"2012-07","story":"Got 15 minutes and want to learn Git?","stance":"rec","why":"Long, detailed talk on how Git works.","quote":"It's over an hour long and very detailed.","text":"A lot of these Git tutorials seem to walk you through a series of commands as if knowing the commands is knowing Git. I think they should focus on scenarios instead. Short stories like: \"Bob's code is working well but he wants to try something that might be completely misguided. He knows better than to mess with his working code.....\" Then at the end of the story a command is introduced. Also, some kind of indication of what Git is actually doing when you issue that command. When you tell Git to branch, what actually happens to the file system? When you say git status, what is it actually looking at? And please, don't start a tutorial on Git without introducing the three area paradigm (working, staging/index, commit) Here's a talk by Scott Chacon of GitHub. [VIDEO] It's over an hour long and very detailed."}],"r":[["hpTCZ-hO6iI",1],["YEBfamv-_do",1]]},"fOGdb1CTu5c":{"wy":"It is the go-to accessible explanation of zero-knowledge proofs, linked whenever a thread needs an intuitive introduction.","q":{"x":"This is the best explanation I have seen","i":31283169,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48762205,"author":"mohsen1","month":"2026-07","story":"Opening up 'Zero-Knowledge Proof' technology to promote privacy in age assurance","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautifully explains zero-knowledge proofs.","quote":"This video is very beautifully explains it","text":"Computer Scientist Explains One Concept in 5 Levels of Difficult [VIDEO] This video is very beautifully explains it"},{"id":31283169,"author":"jimmySixDOF","month":"2022-05","story":"Zero knowledge proofs in RISC-V","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Zero-knowledge proofs explained at five levels, child to expert.","quote":"This is the best explanation I have seen","text":"This is the best explanation I have seen [1] : \"Computer scientist Amit Sahai, PhD, is asked to explain the concept of zero-knowledge proofs to 5 different people; a child, a teen, a college student, a grad student, and an expert.\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44458289,"author":"abhv","month":"2025-07","story":"Opening up ‘Zero-Knowledge Proof’ technology","stance":"rec","why":"Simple introductory video explanation of zero-knowledge proofs.","quote":"That doesn't explain the way this scheme works, but it's a nice start.","text":"My colleague Amit made a simple video explanation about zkp with Wired. [VIDEO] That doesn't explain the way this scheme works, but it's a nice start."},{"id":47305958,"author":"ghywertelling","month":"2026-03","story":"We should revisit literate programming in the agent era","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> With agents, does it become practical to have large codebases that can be read like a narrative, whose prose is kept in sync with changes to the code by tireless machines? I think this is true. Your point supports it. If either the explanation / intention or the code changes, the other can be brought into sync. Beautiful post. I always hated the fact that research papers don't read like novels, eg \"ohk, we tried this which was unsuccessful but then we found another adjacent approach and it helped.\" Computer Scientist Explains One Concept in 5 Levels of Difficulty | WIRED [VIDEO] Computer scientist Amit Sahai, PhD, is asked to explain the concept of zero-knowledge proofs to 5 different people; a child, a teen, a college student, a grad student, and an expert. Using a variety of techniques, Amit breaks down what zero-knowledge proofs are and why it's so exciting in the world of cryptography."},{"id":32981979,"author":"jimmySixDOF","month":"2022-09","story":"How to explain zero-knowledge protocols to your children (1998) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Computer scientist Amit Sahai, PhD, is asked to explain the concept of zero-knowledge proofs to 5 different people;","text":"On YouTube (23 min) Computer scientist Amit Sahai, PhD, is asked to explain the concept of zero-knowledge proofs to 5 different people; a child, a teen, a college student, a grad student, and an expert. Using a variety of techniques, Amit breaks down what zero-knowledge proofs are and why it's so exciting in the world of cryptography. Amit Sahai, PhD, is a professor of computer science at UCLA Samueli School of Engineering. From S1 E16 of Computer Scientist Explains One Concept in 5 Levels of Difficulty | WIRED [VIDEO]"},{"id":30045362,"author":"ricochet11","month":"2022-01","story":"Why should you care about Ethereum Layer-2?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"agree, ZKProofs are really interesting and i'm sure they will come to be used across lots of areas of the web and not just blockchain, though their use and development is most active in the blockchain space for how useful they are for privacy+scalability. Proving knowledge without revealing information allows us to prove computations (validating them is a lot quicker than repeating the computation) and \"use things\" without sacrificing privacy. You can combine the two and have private transactions which are then rolled up in a computation, and then post the proof of the computation to mainnet. You get both cheap and private transactions and infra on top of the base chain. But this goes beyond blockchain: we can hand code to other people to run and then have proof they haven't altered what we agreed upon running, so we can trust the results of someone else running something. That is useful in all sorts of research for replicability in science/engineering. WIRED Computer Scientist Explains One Concept in 5 Levels of Difficulty : [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["_gXiVOmaVSo",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1]]},"tU3rGFyN5uQ":{"wy":"HN commenters link it in threads on greedflation and corporate pricing power as an accessible exchange of opposing views.","q":{"x":"It's a real good exchange of viewpoints on the matter -- better than you'd get with most other commentary.","i":36036588,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37957460,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2023-10","story":"Jon Stewart's Show on Apple Is Ending","stance":"rec","why":"Example of Stewart speaking truth to power.","quote":"I think he's the only person doing serious journalism.","text":"I dunno. I think he's the only person doing serious journalism. He had access and was willing to speak truth to power, publicly. [VIDEO] [LINK] It's not surprising that the show was finally shuttered."},{"id":36485016,"author":"batmaniam","month":"2023-06","story":"IMF: Largest driver of EU inflation is corporate profits","stance":"rec","why":"Conversation on inflation solutions favoring corporations over people.","quote":"Jon Stewart touches on this issue (but for the US)","text":"Jon Stewart touches on this issue (but for the US): [VIDEO] It's rather funny (and sad) to watch the entire conversation reflecting how inflation solutions are geared towards benefiting corporations over the people."},{"id":36036588,"author":"bb88","month":"2023-05","story":"Once a fringe theory, “greedflation” gets its due","stance":"rec","why":"Good exchange of viewpoints on corporate profits and inflation.","quote":"It's a real good exchange of viewpoints on the matter -- better than you'd get with most other commentary.","text":"There's an episode of The Problem With Jon Stewart about two months ago where he interviews Larry Summers about inflation in general and corporate profits being part of the issue. It's a real good exchange of viewpoints on the matter -- better than you'd get with most other commentary. Relevant clip is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35257597,"author":"NotACop182","month":"2023-03","story":"America’s banks are missing hundreds of billions of dollars","stance":"rec","why":"Jon Stewart interviews Larry Summers on inflation causes.","quote":"Great interview with Jon Stewart ad fed chair here","text":"Lots point to Covid-19 ppp and spending to be the cause of inflation. When the majority is supply chain and corporate opportunist. Great interview with Jon Stewart ad fed chair here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39587749,"author":"benreesman","month":"2024-03","story":"PG&E claims no connection between rate increases and $2.2B jump in earnings","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’m not sure whether you will agree wholly or partly with this, your comment can be read more than one way. But several child commenters seem to be reading it as “Government is not the solution to our problems, government is the problem”. So I’m going to address the ever more psychotic Reaganomics that still haven’t “trickled down” 40 years later and how that ties into the energy situation in California. He won his second term the year I was born, in California, so I watched all of this since like, 1989 and was hearing about it in the womb. Enron is arguably the nastiest collapse of any large company due to sanctioned crime, there’s just not another word, a bunch of people went to jail. They lobbied to get energy business de-regulated, and it was so extreme that they had the pull to get something like half the base load generation turned off, spiking the price per kilowatt hour over 4k USD at one point, fire departments were rescuing people from elevators in blackouts. Capitalism sounds awesome: I hope I live to see it. But it’s not practiced in the United States in any credible way: you’re always hearing “it not what you know, it’s who you know”, “you get jobs through your network”, “the value of an Ivy university degree is the people you meet”. “The YC network is an advantage no startup should turn down”. That’s not capitalism, that’s how to get a decent pair of shoes in East Berlin in the 1970s. Or so I’ve been told. Our system is a capture kleptocracy with a core economic"},{"id":37856715,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2023-10","story":"Millions of Homes Still Being Kept Vacant as Housing Costs Surge, Report Finds","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Aka profit inflation, instead of wage inflation.","text":"> Asset inflation at this point is a runaway process caused by companies raising prices to cover increased costs and adding a margin for safety because of volatility Aka profit inflation, instead of wage inflation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37871922,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2023-10","story":"You've Never Had It So Good. Which Is Why You're Stuck","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Greedflation.","text":"This is the classic WSJ defending the Fed protecting the upper class wealth and telling the lower classes to suck it up, imo. Greedflation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35246593,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2023-03","story":"Unchecked corporate pricing power is a factor in US inflation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And for some reason corporate profiteering is fine but workers getting paid more isn't [3].","text":"This is why corporate taxation is so important as well as the real solution to rising inflation. We have the additional factor that the banking sector may not be able to withstand further interest rate hikes due to bad risk management. Protecting the banking system is pretty much the only thing that could prompt lawmakers into action (eg a windfall profits tax) but these aren't ordinary times. But this will never happen. It's not even a partisan issue. Particularly since the awful \"money = speech\" decision [1], both sides of politics are bought and paid for by unchecked corporate power (eg [2]). Corporate consolidation makes this worse. Terms that might otherwise be illegal like \"price gouging\" and \"price fixing\" have been replaced by sanitized legal versions like \"price leadership\", which is basically the same thing. One company raises prices. The other follows suit. And for some reason corporate profiteering is fine but workers getting paid more isn't [3]. Weird . [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35261788,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2023-03","story":"Banks as Collateral Damage in a Class War","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You get the same sentiments as the Bank of England governor from Larry Summers [1].","text":"I wouldn't call this \"class war\". It's more accurate to say \"neofeudalism\". Real wages have been relatively stagnant for 40+ years while we've seen a massive concentration of wealth at the top. Trickle-down economics and deregulation have repeatedly shown themselves to be nothing more than tools for increasing income and wealth inequality. Every aspect of your life is increasingly financialized. College, once free or cheap, now saddles you with lifelong debt. Mortgage debt and rent as a ratio to income have massively increased. Medical debt can wipe you out in the blink of an eye. All of this is by design. It's an inevitable consequence of hyper-capitalist neoliberalism. All of this debt without a real increase in wages is designed to keep you a compliant worker. After all, with any kind of real freedom or financial independence, you're absolutely showing up for work tomorrow. Additionally, inflation could be tackled way more effectively with a windfall profits tax without indiscriminately hitting all businesses as well as individual borrowers. Even if it did it would fill government coffers so the most in need could be helped. You get the same sentiments as the Bank of England governor from Larry Summers [1]. All of this is built on the lie that wages are the real threat to inflation rather than boundles corporate greed and profiteering. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36319177,"author":"sitkack","month":"2023-06","story":"Do High Interest Rates Fix High Inflation?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It does if you force everyone in to abject poverty for long enough.","text":"It does if you force everyone in to abject poverty for long enough. Interview with Larry Summers, Former Secretary of the Treasury | The Problem with Jon Stewart [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UBX2QQHlQ_I",1],["mH0oCDa74tE",1],["D5in5EdjhD0",1]]},"vGAqYNFQdZ4":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in political threads as a classic warning about divisive agitators and fascism, and suggest it be remade for each generation.","q":{"x":"long, but worth a watch sometime for anyone who hasn't seen it","i":46653471,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46653471,"author":"neilv","month":"2026-01","story":"East Germany balloon escape","stance":"rec","why":"1945 film warning against fascism and divisive demagogues","quote":"long, but worth a watch sometime for anyone who hasn't seen it","text":"Yes, IIUC, that was one of the things that mobilized a lot of IMHO positive education/propaganda. It may not have always been for the most noble of reasons (e.g., a very wealthy person not wanting to be disrupted), but the fascism-is-bad messages are still great messages. For example, \"Don't Be a Sucker\" (long, but worth a watch sometime for anyone who hasn't seen it): [VIDEO]"},{"id":28695185,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2021-09","story":"Private censorship is not the best way to fight hate or defend democracy (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"1945 anti-bigotry film worth remaking for each generation","quote":"Perhaps we need to continuously keep making versions of this?","text":"I'm not sure people can't grasp it. But it certainly can't be taken for granted. Perhaps we need to continuously keep making versions of this? [VIDEO] (\"Don't be a sucker\" 1945)"},{"id":24333267,"author":"jenkinsj","month":"2020-08","story":"Don't Be a Sucker","stance":"rec","why":"1945 film showing how demagogues divide people","quote":"A classic.","text":"A classic. The part when the agitator-clown-carnival barker yells \"Folks I'm just an average American but I'm an American American! {{insert-ad-libbed-racist-tirade}}\". Good thing we defeated nonsense like this long ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23674859,"author":"sergeykish","month":"2020-06","story":"Zero-sum thinking on immigration will make America poorer","stance":"rec","why":"1943-45 US war film on prejudice and liberty","quote":"We must guard everyone's liberty or we can lose our own.","text":"I've recently stumbled on United States Department of War film from 1943 > We must guard everyone's liberty or we can lose our own. If we allow any minority to lose its freedom by persecution or by prejudice we are threatening our own freedom [VIDEO]"},{"id":43210392,"author":"adfm","month":"2025-02","story":"Zelensky leaves White House after angry meeting","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ask yourself how many people will die as a result of this show of incompetence. If you're interested in how a democracy should handle itself in such a situation, take a look at Justin Trudeau's recent speech pledging support for Ukraine. Don't be a sucker. [^1] [^1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42281876,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-11","story":"US War Department 1945 // Army Talk Orientation Fact Sheet #64 – Fascism","stance":"reference","why":"1945 US War Department film on fascism","quote":"","text":"> \"Don't Be a Sucker\", 1945 [VIDEO] I like how the opening scene is 'performance wrestling', which is still around (WWE): * [LINK]"},{"id":35999518,"author":"areoform","month":"2023-05","story":"The proto-fascism of Elon Musk","stance":"reference","why":"1945 film about how hate speech spreads","quote":"The US Department of War produced this wonderful film in 1945","text":"I don't usually reply to such statements, but there are more things in heaven and earth, Pseudo0, than are dreamt of in your philosophy. > we live in a society where men can give birth Biology doesn't deal in absolutes. Chromosome anomalies are extremely common. Here's an example of an XY woman who is/was fertile and had a child, \"Report of Fertility in a Woman with a Predominantly 46,XY Karyotype in a Family with Multiple Disorders of Sexual Development\" [LINK] Biology isn't a switch that we flick, it's a messy process with results that are often outside of human expectations; [LINK] Somewhere around 1% to 2% of the human population has one of these conditions. That's quite common. > Mexicans can be white supremacists I'm going to let other people comment on the recent incident, but how about Jewish Nazis? [LINK] They existed. As have many other horrifying example. There's nothing unique here. Hate and idiocy knows no boundaries. > speech is violence The US Department of War produced this wonderful film in 1945, [VIDEO] I think it applies to our world. The world is complicated and we want simple solutions. Can speech be violence? Yes. If I incite a mob and use that to murder someone, then the speech I used to commit that act was a form of violence. Speech is powerful. It can be thoughtful. It can also cause people to view one another as cockroaches and then go around slaughtering their neighbors until all is a pile of rubble. [LINK]"},{"id":33951835,"author":"1MachineElf","month":"2022-12","story":"Dreams Change Under Authoritarianism (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"US Army 1945 propaganda about German society","quote":"","text":"The various tradesman here remind me of the Germans seen in Don't Be a Sucker (1945), a piece of war propaganda produced by the US Army: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28695384,"author":"wutbrodo","month":"2021-09","story":"Private censorship is not the best way to fight hate or defend democracy (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yea, I hear you, that's certainly possible. But I'm not reasoning top-down from liberalism's unpopularity, but rather bottom-up from the inability most people have to grasp the components I mentioned. Some of them are much simpler concepts than liberalism, and are applicable in many cases that people have more direct stakes in. And yet, in my experience, vanishingly few people are capable of grasping them to any reasonable degree. > Perhaps we need to continuously keep making versions of this? [VIDEO] (\"Don't be a sucker\" 1945) We're drowning in anti-racist and anti-fascist material. I don't think it's particularly relevant: clearly it's not difficult to nominally support these beliefs while still being extremely illiberal. That's what's so insidious about illiberalism. It can (and does) poison any ideology, even \"good ones\", because once a sufficient number of people are bought into it, it hardens into dogma and leads easily to \"why should we protect those that disagree with our holy belief system?\". To use an example that we have no trouble recognizing as illiberal, from our modern perspective: Christianity on paper is a very liberal tradition, full of exhortations to love thy enemy and spread peace and love. I don't doubt that this deeply resonated with many early converts. But after a thousand years of spreading to the masses and ossifying into institutions, the medieval Church resembled every other illiberal institution: here's the dogma, and if you don't like it, well t"},{"id":27666540,"author":"PaulHoule","month":"2021-06","story":"An announcement about the comment section","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The gangs who are promoting coronavirus comment spam create two kinds of victims. (1) are the people whose free speech is impaired by relentless toxic comments (2) are the posters themselves who will be increasingly isolated by their behavior and feeling persecuted. It is like the cult member who is driven into evangelism not to recruit new members but to become convinced that the outside world is corrupt. These will become increasingly radicalized and over the next few years will be recruited into more violent attacks and other forms of exploitation such as spending their income on scam alt-health supplements. The most difficult problem in all of this is the the type (2) victims and many observers will insist that their disingenuous communications be taken at face value which is absolutely exhausting for the the type (1) victims who really ought so say that we don't want to hear about their dog pills. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24142323,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-08","story":"Unraveling the Mindset of Victimhood","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"We agree that the Nazi plan was intended only to benefit (a subset of) themselves. We also agree that Nazis intended to treat slavs as prior empires had treated their Worthy Oriental Gentlemen (wogs, in the vernacular). I'd like to make the distinction on the first point that they marketed[1] their intentions differently. I should have said \"sold\" instead of \"viewed\", sorry. And my \"benevolently\" had not been meant to be taken in a positive light. (If someone tells you they are benevolent, keep a hand on your wallet.) To me it is clear that Goebbels said that the Nazis must protect 2'000 years of Western Civilisation from the Communists (who, you correctly note, were severely conflated with jews in that speech), and that not only was everyone who was not For Them implicitly Against Them, but that it didn't matter anyway, because they were totes going to protect even countries whose governments/intellectuals didn't wish such protection[2]. Would it help if I translate the parts I left in german into english? (I hadn't because I think the original translation pretty much got it right, but will try if you'd like fresh phrasing.) As to sheep, dogs, and pigs, they're from Animal Farm [3], repurposed to fit one of PG's recent essays[4]: Concrete groups: pigs - animals who seizing power for animals from humans. (aggressive unconventionals / Nazi leadership) Somehow the benefits from this change of power don't extend much beyond pigs. At the end of the book, the pigs and the neighbou"},{"id":24333800,"author":"IncRnd","month":"2020-08","story":"Don't Be a Sucker","stance":"reference","why":"1945 film on scapegoating agitators","quote":"","text":"It wasn't a racist tirade. It was a tirade against everyone who the fictional person felt was different from him as a method of identifying who he believed to be taking away his opportunities - just by those people existing. See the consequent side-conversation here, [VIDEO] . The crowd rejected him, the more he spoke. Today's news is full of exactly the same attitude displayed by this person. Why are people not rejecting this today?"}],"r":[["agZ0xISi40E",1],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["ovJcsL7vyrk",1],["EYPapE-3FRw",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["oSmqbnhHp1c",1],["GWQh_DmDLKQ",1]]},"wSdV1M7n4gQ":{"wy":"It is the standard reference for why modern compilers and language servers differ from textbook designs, cited in rust-analyzer and parsing threads.","q":{"x":"I really recommend this video from Anders Hejlsberg (creator of C# and TypeScript)","i":18700021,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":25390399,"author":"vector_spaces","month":"2020-12","story":"Advanced Compilers: Self-Guided Online Course","stance":"rec","why":"How modern compiler construction differs from traditional university courses.","quote":"I saw this great interview recently with Anders Hejlsberg at MSFT","text":"I saw this great interview recently with Anders Hejlsberg at MSFT on how modern compiler construction differs from what is taught in traditional University courses. Is this what you're alluding to? After watching that interview, it's strange to read a comment like yours. While the architecture is completely different, it doesn't frankly seem like that big a leap to go from \"senior engineer with X years working on thing that's tangentially related to compilers\" to being able to be productive in a reasonable amount of time working with the new architecture. What am I missing? [VIDEO]"},{"id":23001032,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-04","story":"Rust-analyzer – an IDE backend for Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Covers traditional compilers, then IDE-focused compiler design.","quote":"Anders Hejlsberg explains explains how “the IDE” way compiler works.","text":"Anders Hejlsberg explains explains how “the IDE” way compiler works. First ~10 minutes is traditional compiler background. Then about ~8 minutes before he starts talking about how an IDE focused compiler works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22215520,"author":"atombender","month":"2020-02","story":"Rust Moving Towards an IDE-Friendly Compiler with Rust Analyzer","stance":"rec","why":"Short lecture on incremental IDE-friendly compiler design.","quote":"Anders Heljsberg has a great little lecture [2] about it.","text":"Microsoft's Roslyn [1] design (now called .NET Compiler Platform) is what I think any language should strive for, turning the classical black box of the compiler into an incremental pipeline where each stage is annotated/augmented/refined by different mechanisms such as type checkers. If each step is incremental, it follows that it's easier to also let each step \"integrate\" deltas; for example, adding a comment shouldn't need to recompile anything at all, merely modify the AST, because it doesn't lead to any actual code artifacts. The second benefit is that all the information needed to inspect the program — from syntax highlighting to type information — is already there. If you can keep the entire high-level compilation state in memory, then it's easy to query it for all sorts of IDE features. This same design was used for the TypeScript compiler. Anders Heljsberg has a great little lecture [2] about it. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18700021,"author":"ceronman","month":"2018-12","story":"Rust Analyzer in 2018 and 2019","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how modern IDE-oriented compilers are built.","quote":"I really recommend this video from Anders Hejlsberg (creator of C# and TypeScript)","text":"To better understand why this project is trying to re-implement the compiler frontend, I really recommend this video from Anders Hejlsberg (creator of C# and TypeScript) explaining how modern compiler construction oriented towards IDE works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36315457,"author":"sarosh","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"A (now 6 year old) discussion that might be helpful","text":"A (now 6 year old) discussion that might be helpful: Anders Hejlsberg on Modern Compiler Construction: [VIDEO] Also [LINK]"},{"id":24484187,"author":"DylanSp","month":"2020-09","story":"Which Parsing Approach?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"To your first point, I've got a couple of bookmarks: - Query-based compiler architecture: [LINK] - Lezer [a parsing system used by CodeMirror]: [LINK] - Anders Hejlsberg on Modern Compiler Construction: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24130222,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-08","story":"Emacs 27.1","stance":"reference","why":"Hejlsberg explains AST-aware, incremental compilation with IDE.","quote":"Anders explains it better than me","text":"Anders explains it better than me, \"Anders Hejlsberg on Modern Compiler Construction\" [VIDEO] Basically ability to see the language AST at all time, do time checking with broken code, incremental compilation, REPL and IDE editing experience is fused together. Here is another short examples, \"7 minutes of Pharo Smalltalk for Rubyists\" [LINK] \"MountainWest RubyConf 2014 - But Really, You Should Learn Smalltalk\" [LINK]"},{"id":23858659,"author":"fanf2","month":"2020-07","story":"Don't Panic! Better, Fewer, Syntax Errors for LR Parsers","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion with Hejlsberg on compiler design.","quote":"this discussion with Anders Hejlsberg","text":"In a query-oriented compiler, you can give it a source location and ask for type infomation, or where is the definition of the symbol at that location, and the compiler will do just enough work to give you the answer, using laziness and memoization to make it reasonably efficient. As far as I know, LISP systems don't work that way: they parse and evaluate (and maybe compile) as soon as an expression is entered, without the kind of laziness you see in a query-oriented compiler. This recent link was pretty good, I think [LINK] and this discussion with Anders Hejlsberg [VIDEO]"},{"id":20972772,"author":"fanf2","month":"2019-09","story":"Parallel GCC: a research project aiming to parallelize a real-world compiler","stance":"reference","why":"Roslyn and TypeScript compilers designed for incremental IDE support.","quote":"he talks about how the Roslyn and TypeScript compilers are designed to support IDEs","text":"There is an interview with Anders Hejlsborg [VIDEO] where he talks about how the Roslyn and TypeScript compilers are designed to support IDEs, with a structure that allows the compiler’s model of the program to be incrementally updated as the code is modified. So there has been some fairly prominent work along the lines you want..."},{"id":17575145,"author":"wenc","month":"2018-07","story":"Introducing the Python Language Server","stance":"reference","why":"Anders explains motivation for compiler as a service.","quote":"Anders talks about the motivation for doing that here [2].","text":"From what I've seen (MS folks can correct me here), language services are part of a much larger effort at Microsoft led by Anders Heijsberg (Delphi, C#) called Roslyn [1], which decouples code analysis from IDEs and provides things like refactoring, autocomplete, find-all-references, Intellisense, etc. as services. This means the compiler is now an abstracted platform that editors can get services from (CompilerAndCodeAnalysis-as-a-service?). Anders talks about the motivation for doing that here [2]. It's a pretty exciting idea. This might mean I would some day be able to use Vim and get IDE-like functionality. [1] Roslyn [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["rmueBVrLKcY",1],["9q3uNcJh4pc",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["DXvAlwMAxiA",1]]},"3qln2u1Vr2E":{"wy":"Considered a favorite deep dive into how AWS does virtual networking at L3 and why VPC hosts cannot snoop each other.","q":{"x":"Here's the ever excellent Eric Brandwine explaining what AWS does at L3 wrt VPC","i":19363043,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19363043,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-03","story":"Nginx to Be Acquired by F5 Networks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eric Brandwine on how AWS does L3 for VPC.","quote":"Here's the ever excellent Eric Brandwine explaining what AWS does at L3 wrt VPC","text":"Here's the ever excellent Eric Brandwine explaining what AWS does at L3 wrt VPC: [VIDEO] Here's the super versatile Colm Mac explaining what AWS does at L4: [LINK] --- Google has been very open about their network infrastructure, here's a nice summary from 2015: [LINK] and not mentioned in that blog... their NetworkLoadBalancer, Maglev: [LINK] (AWS equivalent of which would be HyperPlane: [LINK] allegedly based on S3's load balancer)."},{"id":23079522,"author":"thinkharderdev","month":"2020-05","story":"Redis 6.0 GA","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why hosts in a VPC cannot snoop each other's traffic.","quote":"Here is a good video explaining why","text":"You can't snoop on traffic between hosts in the same VPC. Here is a good video explaining why [VIDEO] . The tl;dr; is that your guest OS (the EC2 instance) is not connected to the physical layer 2 network. The host OS hypervisor is and when it receives a packet from the physical NIC, if that packet is not directed to the guest OS then it won't be passed to it. So the NIC on the guest OS (your EC2 instance) will never even see the packets that are not intended for it. Of course this gets slightly more complicated because AWS added some tools for traffic mirroring. So theoretically someone with the right access could setup a mirror to a host they control in the VPC and sniff the traffic that way. But if someone were able to pull that off then you're likely f'ed either way."},{"id":15801241,"author":"colmmacc","month":"2017-11","story":"VPC Endpoints for Your Own Applications and Services","stance":"rec","why":"AWS VPC networking talk, a favorite.","quote":"talks on youtube ( is a fav)","text":"There's lots on [LINK] , and talks on youtube ( [VIDEO] is a fav). But the TLDR is this: VPC stands for \"Virtual Private Cloud\" and it gives you your own virtual data center on AWS. You get a private network that uses whatever IP range you'd like, and you can connect to it privately via VPN or Direct Connect (physical links). You can also connect it to the internet with Elastic IP Addresses. VPCs are free and you can create and edit them programmatically (add subnets, change routes, manage ACLs and security groups) - so they're a useful way to manage an environment of things like EC2 instances, containers, Elastic Load Balancers, RDS databases, Lambda functions, and more, without needing to worry as much about them being exposed to things you don't control. With this latest PrivateLink feature, it's now possible to offer services between VPCs. So you can build a service in its own VPC, and map into other ones, securely."}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1]]},"ALc8CBYOfkw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, accessible layperson explanation of the real-versus-complex quantum theory debate.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend video by Sabine Hossenfelder [1] on this topic.","i":30286406,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30286406,"author":"viktorcode","month":"2022-02","story":"How the modern world arose from imaginary numbers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Layman explanation of whether complex numbers physically exist","quote":"I highly recommend video by Sabine Hossenfelder [1] on this topic.","text":"I highly recommend video by Sabine Hossenfelder [1] on this topic. She's explaining the topic in layman terms, starting from what does it mean to exist (or be real) in physical sense. Complex numbers, as useful as they are, just an abstraction, a tool which can be replaced with different forms of calculations. That makes the question of \"reality\" rather complex (pardon the pun). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35690750,"author":"ars","month":"2023-04","story":"Quantum Physics Falls Apart Without Imaginary Numbers","stance":"rec","why":"More informative discussion of the paper","quote":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder is probably more informative on this topic","text":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder is probably more informative on this topic: [VIDEO] (it discusses this paper)."},{"id":29650375,"author":"johnp271","month":"2021-12","story":"Quantum theory based on real numbers can be experimentally falsified","stance":"rec","why":"Ten-minute discussion of the real-number quantum theory paper","quote":"There is a nice 10 minute discussion about this paper by physicist Sabine Hossenfelder","text":"There is a nice 10 minute discussion about this paper by physicist Sabine Hossenfelder on her \"Science without the gobbelydgook\" youtube series (which I recommend). She considers the existential questions regarding necessity of complex numbers a \"super-niche nerd fight\". You can find the video here [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["i1vbvikDiI8",1],["5nW3nJhBHL0",1],["05WS0WN7zMQ",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1]]},"IJiHDmyhE1A":{"wy":"HN commenters hold it up as the greatest video game song, evoking wonder and pride in humanity.","q":{"x":"What about the greatest video game song ever produced (Baba Yetu)","i":23261486,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23261486,"author":"jointpdf","month":"2020-05","story":"Sid Meier’s Civilization VI free until May 28th","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Considered the greatest video game song.","quote":"What about the greatest video game song ever produced (Baba Yetu)","text":"> I honestly can’t remember anything special about Civ4/5/6 Aww c’mon, not even one thing? What about the greatest video game song ever produced (Baba Yetu): [VIDEO] Agree on Alpha Centauri—definitely recommend picking up a copy on GOG for anyone in the mood for a classic strategy game timesink."},{"id":31831288,"author":"watersb","month":"2022-06","story":"‘The War on Music’ Review: Songs Without Listeners","stance":"rec","why":"Christopher Tin's song cycles, loved by commenter.","quote":"holy cow I love his work","text":"ABSOLUTELY. I am not plugged into a community of musicians these days, but in college and the decade afterwards we were hearing, performing music written by contemporary composers. Choral music, mostly. But cello concertos, crazy harpsichord binges, and banging on drums were there, too. A \"Classical vernacular\" these days would be blockbuster movie soundtracks! Are you (the article, its author) telling me that doesn't matter? To say nothing of new venues and art forms. I learned about Christopher Tin's song cycles via a soundtrack bundle marketed to video gamers. I am not a gamer, but I think I was shopping for a gift for my kids and holy cow I love his work. [VIDEO] Eric Whitacre! Arvo Pärt! These are not obscure artists. Symphonies and opera houses perform fun and popular works during the summer season in the US. So now would be a good time to find stuff you might not expect."},{"id":12812893,"author":"redthrowaway","month":"2016-10","story":"What Sid Meier’s Video Game Empire Got Right and Wrong About ‘Civilization’","stance":"rec","why":"Evokes wonder and pride in humanity.","quote":"Watching the Baba Yetu intro from Civ IV[1] gives me the same sense of wonder and pride in humanity","text":"Complaints about Civ's omission of the dark side of history ring hollow to me. Civ is, at its heart, a deeply aspirational and optimistic game. Watching the Baba Yetu intro from Civ IV[1] gives me the same sense of wonder and pride in humanity that Chris Hadfield's rendition of Space Oddity[2] does. Dwelling on the darkness in our history would be so discordant for a game like Civ. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30565837,"author":"dmichulke","month":"2022-03","story":"How Swahili became Africa's most spoken language","stance":"reference","why":"Swahili song used by the UN as a hymn.","quote":"Baba Yetu, the Swahili language song that the UN uses sometimes as kind of hymn","text":"Olbigatory reference to Baba Yetu, the Swahili language song that the UN uses sometimes as kind of hymn [VIDEO] (Original from Civ 4) [LINK] (my preferred version)"}],"r":[["u404SLJj7ig",1],["KaOC9danxNo",1],["jErqdRE5zpQ",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["drv3BP0Fdi8",1],["-suTmUX4Vbk",1]]},"QWveXdj6oZU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible explanation of rhyme craft that works even for people who aren't hip hop fans.","q":{"x":"Please consider this required watching, even if you have no interest in hip-hop music","i":21668330,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21668330,"author":"peteforde","month":"2019-11","story":"Visualizing Hip-Hop Melodies (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Educational explainer on rhyme craft in rap, even for non-fans.","quote":"Please consider this required watching, even if you have no interest in hip-hop music","text":"One of the most educational and interesting explainer videos I've ever seen is Vox's \"Rapping, Deconstructed\". Please consider this required watching, even if you have no interest in hip-hop music: [VIDEO] After you watch that, I suspect you'll hear this Kendrick Lamar track with new ears: [LINK] I know that while I am still very much a jazz/rock person, those videos expanded my perception of what was musically possible. No hyperbole on my end."},{"id":33458881,"author":"m463","month":"2022-11","story":"Bob Dylan takes us on a wide-ranging tour of songs he admires","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting deconstruction showing YouTube music discovery journeys.","quote":"you can get really interesting journeys like this deconstruction","text":"I remember back before the internet when \"music discovery\" rarely involved listening to music (except the radio). I would be so interested in music, but READING about music was a horrible hit-or-mostly-miss experience. I could read something like rolling stone and then be disappointed when the music didn't capture the emotion the description portrayed. Even word of mouth was hit-or-miss unless someone had a tape in the car. anyway... now we have infinite discoverability, and with youtube, you can get really interesting journeys like this deconstruction: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25599575,"author":"freddie_mercury","month":"2021-01","story":"MF DOOM Has Died","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to why MF DOOM was technically brilliant.","quote":"this video is a pretty good introduction to why he was considered to be technically brilliant.","text":"For those unfamiliar with MF DOOM, this video is a pretty good introduction to why he was considered to be technically brilliant. [VIDEO] (Skip to 9:31 if the link doesn't take you there automatically)"},{"id":34996636,"author":"basch","month":"2023-03","story":"Show HN: Beatbot.fm – Make Songs with AI","stance":"reference","why":"Example of rhyme analysis that generative AI should understand.","quote":"This video is something I would like generative ai writing to understand.","text":"There will be a middle ground where the technology augments professional tools. If you can pull open an audio editor, start with 20 tracks and have the ai pull things I like from other songs AND modify each track to match as elements get added, you’ll start to see pros make some amazing things. Similar to how people with a photography background have a huge head start with midjourney. What you are describing will be more powerful when I can make a playlist of my 10 favorite songs AND write a sentence about why I chose each song, and what features of the song are worth preserving. Then it can be used to match me with existing or generated songs. Current gen streaming radio doesn’t have the context for why I like each song I like. Use the ai to take songs apart and understand their elements. This video is something I would like generative ai writing to understand. [VIDEO] . This tools current rhyming isn’t any better than Kurtis Blow."}],"r":[["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Zk11vI-7czE",1],["tnBQmEqBCY0",1]]},"an5abNFba4Q":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an exciting look at how a browser can be rebuilt for performance and safety, including the WebRender ideas that fed into Firefox Quantum.","q":{"x":"This video is one of the most exciting I have seen with regards to web browser performance, and it's Mozilla","i":13504773,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13504773,"author":"yoasif_","month":"2017-01","story":"I cannot continue working on my add-ons","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exciting talk on web browser performance from Mozilla.","quote":"This video is one of the most exciting I have seen with regards to web browser performance, and it's Mozilla","text":"There seem to be a lot of people that are saying \"oh, without XUL based extensions, Firefox is just a (worse) Chrome clone, so I might as well just use Chrome.\" I think what they might be missing is that Mozilla's conclusion is a lot like the people seem to be thinking here -- even though Chrome doesn't have the fancy extensions that are possible in Firefox, many many more people are using Chrome than Firefox, because they see it as a better Firefox. Clearly, that has something to do with either security or performance, where Firefox has a perceived lag. Mozilla is actively working to improve performance, and you may not want to switch to Chrome after all, because Chrome may instead be the poor man's Firefox (I personally think this is already the case, simply because with my 679 open tabs, Chrome would simply kill itself [44 loaded]). This video is one of the most exciting I have seen with regards to web browser performance, and it's Mozilla: [VIDEO] More info on what this means for Firefox: [LINK] TL;DR: What if even with the missing XUL extensions, you wouldn't want to switch to Chrome, because Firefox was just plain faster?"},{"id":15445824,"author":"lkbm","month":"2017-10","story":"The web at maximum FPS: How WebRender gets rid of jank","stance":"rec","why":"Metajack's talk on WebRender in Servo.","quote":"I also recommend Metajack's talk on WebRender in Servo","text":"This is a great explanation. I also recommend Metajack's talk on WebRender in Servo: [VIDEO] Also go read everything else Lin Clark has done. They're pretty great."},{"id":14165303,"author":"NiLSPACE","month":"2017-04","story":"Mozilla and Microsoft are rebuilding their browsers’ foundations","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk including a bit on Quantum around 25:30.","quote":"Here is another great talk","text":"If you have the time there is a nice talk about WebRender: [LINK] Here is another great talk: [VIDEO] At ~25:30 he'll also talk a bit about Quantum. In a nutshell WebRender is used to send a web page to the GPU which will paint all divs, borders, shadows, images etc. Currently WebRender is the only component I'm aware of that is inside Firefox Nightly. It's still behind a config though."}],"r":[["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["4s9IjkMAmns",1],["BTURkjYJ_uk",1]]},"lIW5jBrrsS0":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a thoughtful mini-doc on reading time and frequency in the age of social media.","q":{"x":"it's an interesting exploration of modern bookstores and the dynamics of reading as a pursuit in the age of social media","i":39857876,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":34254312,"author":"chrisandchips","month":"2023-01","story":"Lex Fridman's Reading Plan in Numbers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mini-doc on reading time and frequency","quote":"Phenomenal and related mini-doc on the subject of reading time and frequency","text":"Agreed. He is presenting the list as \"too be finished by X date\", which within itself sort of implies trying to do it quickly, or at least that he is taking time into account while reading. But it's not unreasonable that he make it through this list and enjoy everything. Half an hour a day really isn't much. Phenomenal and related mini-doc on the subject of reading time and frequency: [VIDEO] I should also add, page count is very much so dependant on font size."},{"id":47198754,"author":"j1elo","month":"2026-02","story":"Cognitive Debt: When Velocity Exceeds Comprehension","stance":"rec","why":"Speed reading technique explained at 20:15","quote":"this old gem of a video (minute 20:15)","text":"I know the topic won't probably be about this -and I'll be reading the article next-, just wanted to share that this title perfectly reminded me the feeling of attempting the speed reading technique explained in this old gem of a video (minute 20:15) BOOKSTORES: How to Read More Books in the Golden Age of Content [VIDEO]"},{"id":39857876,"author":"underlipton","month":"2024-03","story":"How does an independent bookstore survive for 90 years?","stance":"rec","why":"Explores modern bookstores and reading in the social media age","quote":"it's an interesting exploration of modern bookstores and the dynamics of reading as a pursuit in the age of social media","text":"I know some might chafe at me recommending a Youtube video about reading, but I came across this one a few years ago, and it's an interesting exploration of modern bookstores and the dynamics of reading as a pursuit in the age of social media: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["M3BM9TB-8yA",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1]]},"nU8DcBF-qo4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clear explanation of why Google's statistical, data-first approach works.","q":{"x":"You should definitely watch it if you haven't seen it before.","i":150849,"y":"2008"},"m":[{"id":150849,"author":"henning","month":"2008-03","story":"More data usually beats better algorithms","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Norvig on why more data beats better algorithms.","quote":"You should definitely watch it if you haven't seen it before.","text":"One talk in particular in which he focuses on this idea, including with facts and figures from research literature, is a talk he gave a while ago called \"Theorizing from Data\": [VIDEO] . You should definitely watch it if you haven't seen it before. Norvig states his opinion slightly differently: of course being smart about algorithms is good. But until you get a lot of it, you often can't even fairly evaluate different algorithms. It's only when you're no longer getting significant gains from more data that you should then start thinking about being an algorithm smartypants."},{"id":780556,"author":"timcederman","month":"2009-08","story":"Ask HN: Access to the Corpus - What Would You Do?","stance":"rec","why":"Norvig explains the subtle sophistication of Google's data work.","quote":"Peter Norvig does a better job explaining some of it than I ever could.","text":"If you really think that's all they're doing then you have very little understanding in just how subtly awesome some of the stuff they're doing is. Peter Norvig does a better job explaining some of it than I ever could. [VIDEO]"},{"id":90353,"author":"dood","month":"2007-12","story":"Google Is All About Large Amounts of Data","stance":"rec","why":"Norvig talk on theorizing from massive amounts of data.","quote":"Its a good talk","text":"[ [VIDEO] ] Its a good talk, though maybe should be called \"theorizing from massive amounts of data\". Makes you wonder what Google are keeping under wraps for now, and how the powerset approach can compete, except maybe for domain-specific stuff."},{"id":640481,"author":"dood","month":"2009-06","story":"Google Squared is Live","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig talk from 2007 on data-driven approaches.","quote":"","text":"I like that they show you the source pages, confidence, and alternative sources for the data. However, it suffers the classic Google app problem; it does interesting stuff, but just looks ugly, and is generally unappealing. Hard for me to explain why, but despite my interest in the technology I just didn't enjoy using it at all. But it is interesting stuff, I'd been wondering when something like this would emerge since watching this Norving talk from 2007 [ [VIDEO] ]."},{"id":126880,"author":"ntoshev","month":"2008-03","story":"Awaiting the Day When Everyone Writes Software ","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig explains Google's statistical approach over precise models.","quote":"","text":"Humans resolve a lot of the complexity intuitively and unconsciously. It is not an inherently bad way to handle it, just incompatible with current programming paradigms. This is not to discount the value of clear explicit thinking in humans, it is just to say that programming would benefit a lot from ability to apply common sense to mundane things. For some dramatic evidence that imprecise is better and it really works, look at the success of Google and the non-delivery of the Semantic Web. Google's approach is probably best explained by Peter Norvig in his \"Theorizing from Data\" lecture: [VIDEO] State of the art in natural language processing is achieved with statistical inference, not building precise linguistic models. See e.g. Marti Hearst's text here: [LINK] Back to programming languages, I believe that a lot of the Ruby/Rails success is due to mimicking natural language in DSLs. Rails goes as far as using pluralization. DSLs bring convenience at the price of less understanding: people tend to try using them intuitively, without understanding precisely what happens, much more so than they do with libraries/frameworks. Perl, similarly, was created with linguistic principles in mind: [LINK] It is by no means conclusive evidence, but I believe there is an underlying trend."}],"r":[["j02b8Fuz73A",1],["kZRE7HIO3vk",1]]},"qE7dYhpI_bI":{"wy":"Recommended as a thoughtful, enjoyable 45-minute dive into measurement and the appreciation of precision.","q":{"x":"I think you will thoroughly enjoy Adam Savage's (of Mythbusters fame) recent video on measurement.","i":29826605,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31116916,"author":"waynesonfire","month":"2022-04","story":"I can't let go of “The Dunning-Kruger Effect is Autocorrelation”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the idea of developing new eyes to appreciate novelty.","quote":"one of my favorite Adam Savage videos","text":"Your post reminded me of one of my favorite Adam Savage videos where he touches upon this idea you're exploring. I encourage folks to see it, he articulates it so well. I linked to the start of the video where he begins to build the idea. TLDR is he mentions Monet painting Impression Sunrise and how it was something that people have never seen before and it took a bit of time for it to blow people away--they needed to develop \"new eyes\" to see the genius. Adam then dives into this idea of \"new eyes\". I'm sure many of us have experienced this in our life and it was so nice to hear Adam unpack it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38454775,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-11","story":"'Nuts and Bolts' Review: The Levers That Move the World","stance":"rec","why":"Philosophical 45-minute talk on accuracy and precision via gauge blocks.","quote":"Adam Savage has an interesting (45m!) video entitled","text":"Adam Savage has an interesting (45m!) video entitled \"Epiphany on the Science of Measurement!\" where he gets a bit philosophical on accuracy and precision (using gauge/Johansson blocks as jumping off point): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29826605,"author":"gibolt","month":"2022-01","story":"Longform.org is shutting down its article recommendations service","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Savage video on measurement.","quote":"I think you will thoroughly enjoy Adam Savage's (of Mythbusters fame) recent video on measurement.","text":"I think you will thoroughly enjoy Adam Savage's (of Mythbusters fame) recent video on measurement. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36658302,"author":"gizmo686","month":"2023-07","story":"Amateurs obsess over tools, pros over mastery","stance":"reference","why":"Savage geeking out over gauge blocks.","quote":"he geeks outs over his new measurement tool: guage blocks (and corresponding guage)","text":"I'd say there are two different types of \"obsession\" here. One is the attitude that that you need to use the best tools possible and by doing that you will get a good result. The other type is old fashion geakery. If you look at Adam, he is two things: a maker, and a tool geek. These aspects certainly intersect, but when he is making things, you do not see him reach for the most sophisticated tool in his toolbox. A great example of this is this video [0] where he geeks outs over his new measurement tool: guage blocks (and corresponding guage). The thing is, I don't think I have ever seen him do a build where he uses them, because they are simply beyond what his work demands. I'm a similar way with programming languages. I'm a PL geek, and enjoy geeking out about the fine details of various programming languages, and pointing out flaws in langauges. But, at the end of the day, when it comes time to pick a language for a project, I typically reach for a combination of Java/C++/python/m4. There is nothing to geek out about those languages (except for how bad they are), but pragmatically speaking, they are typically the best tool available for very boring reasons. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UiS27feX8o0",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["QWWgr2rN45o",1],["LMxate9gegg",1],["HNkLcD3PKyk",1],["YcVSgYz5SJ8",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1]]},"21j_OCNLuYg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thoughtful counterpoint to the pursuit of complicated, consumption-driven lives.","q":{"x":"He self-reports being much much happier, whether you believe it or not.","i":42991359,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42991359,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-02","story":"Obscure islands I find interesting","stance":"rec","why":"Jon Jandai's account of leaving Bangkok for subsistence farming and happiness.","quote":"He self-reports being much much happier, whether you believe it or not.","text":"> And no, I don’t believe in the happy-life-in-the-forest. Jon Jandai moved from a village in rural Thailand to Bangkok, and took up a fulltime labouring job. He struggled to afford enough food to eat, slept in a shared room which was too hot, saved for a month to buy a pair of jeans to wear and realised he was still the same person and still unhappy, and he saw house prices out of reach with educated high-earners working for 30 years to pay a mortgage. He didn't like it, and moved back to his village. He spends a month planting rice and a month harvesting rice and gets enough rice to feed his family of six for a year, and several times more than that leftover to sell at the market. 2 months a year work, ten months free time. He spends fifteen minutes a day tending a half-acre garden and gets enough vegetables for six people to eat, and more to sell at the market. And he fishes. He built a house with earthen building in 3 months. He self-reports being much much happier, whether you believe it or not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28256332,"author":"movedx","month":"2021-08","story":"The thing standing between procrastination and daily progress is ritual","stance":"rec","why":"Thai farmer questions why we make our lives so complicated.","quote":"who didn't understand why we make our lives so complicated","text":"That's an interest point! I'm going to find and read that. It reminded me of the guy from Thailand (TED Talk - linked below) who didn't understand why we make our lives so complicated when he can work for 2-3 months of the year and spend the rest of it wondering which of his six houses, which he built, he's going to live in - [VIDEO]"},{"id":9430184,"author":"pharke","month":"2015-04","story":"The Slow Death of the University","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on living simply and growing your own food.","quote":"Reminds me of this talk","text":"Reminds me of this talk [VIDEO] and also happens to be my current mid-range goal. I've worked on a farm, gardened, and raised chickens growing up and I'm dead set on doing it again, the amount of work that goes into it vs. the amount of food and enjoyment that you get out of it makes it a no brainer if you can take the leap and buy a plot. Construction begins this summer and I couldn't be happier."}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"7ZVWIELHQQY":{"wy":"HN users share it in threads on drinking and addiction as an honest account of alcoholism and sobriety.","q":{"x":"Craig Ferguson's comments on alcoholism are worth listening to","i":11723294,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11723294,"author":"klodolph","month":"2016-05","story":"Do startups have a drinking problem?","stance":"rec","why":"Ferguson's comments on alcoholism are worth hearing.","quote":"Craig Ferguson's comments on alcoholism are worth listening to","text":"This comment is profoundly unsympathetic to people who have recovered from alcoholism and decided that for their own health they must abstain. This comment is profoundly unsympathetic to people with sensitivity to alcohol. I'm deeply grateful that my friends and the people I work with respect each others' boundaries with alcohol and other substances. So what if you do \"have a problem\"? Is it right for someone to push alcohol on you, knowing that you have a problem? Perhaps I should offer discounts at the liquor store to people who turn in their AA coins, because alcoholism is \"their problem\" not mine? Craig Ferguson's comments on alcoholism are worth listening to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8085335,"author":"sp332","month":"2014-07","story":"6 Things I Learned From Not Drinking For 1 Year","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful for explaining alcoholism to others.","quote":"I thought it might be helpful for explaining to people.","text":"Have you seen Craig Ferguson talking about alcoholism? I thought it might be helpful for explaining to people. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7487641,"author":"goatforce5","month":"2014-03","story":"I am a successful software dev but I have a serious drinking problem","stance":"rec","why":"Ferguson on his alcoholism and sobriety.","quote":"It's really good, and there might be something in it for you","text":"Craig Ferguson did a good piece about his alcoholism, a year or two that went missing from his life, 15 years of being sober, etc. It's really good, and there might be something in it for you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48567516,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-06","story":"Has AI already killed self-help nonfiction books?","stance":"reference","why":"Ferguson describes bad versus good rehabs.","quote":"how Craig Ferguson described bad rehabs VS good rehabs.","text":"I’m not going to defend the self-help industry, but your dismissal made me think of how Craig Ferguson described bad rehabs VS good rehabs. [VIDEO] Yes, of course you need to put in work to enact change. The bigger the change, the more work you need to put in. That desire for a quick and easy solution to hard problems is exactly what lead to the proliferation of scams."}],"r":[["En_2T7KH6RA",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1],["7w4rg3UcsgI",1],["R37zkizucPU",1],["tISy7EJQPzI",1]]},"Bea4FS-zDzc":{"wy":"Recommended whenever door handles or car reliability engineering come up, as an enjoyable look at design iteration.","q":{"x":"This is a great video by SuperfastMatt on the engineering behind and evolution of the Tesla door handle.","i":46915820,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46915820,"author":"lylejantzi3rd","month":"2026-02","story":"The overlooked evolution of the humble car door handle","stance":"rec","why":"Engineering and evolution of the Tesla door handle.","quote":"This is a great video by SuperfastMatt on the engineering behind and evolution of the Tesla door handle.","text":"This is a great video by SuperfastMatt on the engineering behind and evolution of the Tesla door handle. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43014678,"author":"gloxkiqcza","month":"2025-02","story":"Nvidia's RTX 5090 power connectors are melting","stance":"rec","why":"Ex-Tesla engineer on door handle design over time.","quote":"this is something HN might enjoy","text":"A bit off topic, but this is something HN might enjoy. It’s a video by a mechanical engineer that worked for Tesla, Apple and a NASCAR team about Tesla door handles over time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33624927,"author":"duncans","month":"2022-11","story":"EVs are the least reliable vehicle type: Consumer Reports","stance":"rec","why":"Evolution and reliability engineering of Tesla Model S door handles.","quote":"this is a fascinating video on how the Tesla Model S door handles design has been refined and made more reliable","text":"Regarding the pop-out door handles - this is a fascinating video on how the Tesla Model S door handles design has been refined and made more reliable by someone who used to work at Tesla (SuperfastMatt) [VIDEO]"},{"id":46163270,"author":"torginus","month":"2025-12","story":"BMW PHEV: Safety fuse replacement is extremely expensive","stance":"reference","why":"Door handle redesign from many parts to motor plus sensor.","quote":"where its explained how they redesigned the retracting door handles of the Model S","text":"This reminded me of the video about the Tesla door handles, where its explained how they redesigned the retracting door handles of the Model S from having a bunch of switches and mechanical parts to just a motor + a position sensor: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TgFdics3uKo",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1]]},"OLLwG_SN8oo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best talk from Sonic Pi's creator, with live-coding examples and technical and teaching insights.","q":{"x":"There's also a _great_ talk from the creator (Sam Aaron) on Sonic Pi","i":23078997,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23078997,"author":"Leszek","month":"2020-05","story":"Sonic Pi is a code-based music creation and performance tool","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Live-coding examples and insights on teaching computer science","quote":"There's also a _great_ talk from the creator (Sam Aaron) on Sonic Pi","text":"There's also a _great_ talk from the creator (Sam Aaron) on Sonic Pi, including live-coding examples and some wonderful insights on how computer science is taught in schools: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22192456,"author":"baldgeek","month":"2020-01","story":"The live coding language that lets you be an actual rock star","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sam Aaron's talk touching on Sonic Pi's technical details","quote":"My favorite talk where he scratches the surface of the tech details was a 2018 talk at Goto","text":"Sam Aaron has done several talks on Sonic Pi. It's an amazing piece of work. My favorite talk where he scratches the surface of the tech details was a 2018 talk at Goto : [VIDEO] He also has saved several of his sessions on youtube."},{"id":34418284,"author":"wglb","month":"2023-01","story":"Common Lisp and Music Composition","stance":"reference","why":"Explanation of Sonic Pi","quote":"","text":"Don't think this is at a haskel conference, but this looks like fun: [LINK] . Their web site is [LINK] . An explanation of sonic [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FTKP0Y9MVus",1]]},"XJ9ckqCMiKk":{"wy":"Pointed to as the best source on Phoenix Presence internals and for combining Phoenix with Elm.","q":{"x":"My Erlang Factory keynote also goes into the design considerations and PubSub layer optimizations that sits underneath Phoenix.Presence","i":11361872,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13026373,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2016-11","story":"Elm for the Front End, Right Now","stance":"rec","why":"Half Phoenix, half Elm intro with nice debugging and error messages.","quote":"I liked this video from Erlang Factory about using Phoenix and Elm together.","text":"Speaking of Elm I liked this video from Erlang Factory about using Phoenix and Elm together. They both have a functional flavors so I think it might appeal to same people: [VIDEO] The first half is about Phoenix, the second about Elm. I don't know much about front-end stuff but I did like the Elm bit as an intro, especially the debugging and nice error messages part."},{"id":11669533,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2016-05","story":"A Farewell to FRP","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on using Phoenix and Elm together.","quote":"There is a nice talk from Erlang Factory 2016 called \"Making the Web Functional with Phoenix and Elm","text":"There is a nice talk from Erlang Factory 2016 called \"Making the Web Functional with Phoenix and Elm\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11361872,"author":"chrismccord","month":"2016-03","story":"What makes Phoenix Presence special, and a sneak peek","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote covering Phoenix.Presence design and PubSub optimizations.","quote":"My Erlang Factory keynote also goes into the design considerations and PubSub layer optimizations that sits underneath Phoenix.Presence","text":"My Erlang Factory keynote also goes into the design considerations and PubSub layer optimizations that sits underneath Phoenix.Presence: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30986342,"author":"andy_ppp","month":"2022-04","story":"Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types (CRDT)","stance":"reference","why":"Chris McCord implements a CRDT in Phoenix.Presence.","quote":"Chris McCord had fun implementing a CRDT in Phoenix.Presence","text":"Chris McCord had fun implementing a CRDT in Phoenix.Presence: [VIDEO] (from about 17 mins by the looks of things)."},{"id":11852672,"author":"qohen","month":"2016-06","story":"Integrating Elm and Phoenix Channels via Elm-Phoenix-socket","stance":"reference","why":"Shared keynote by creators of Phoenix and Elm.","quote":"the keynote at 2016's Erlang Factory conference was a shared talk between Chris McCord (creator of Phoenix) and Evan Czaplicki (creator of Elm)","text":"I wonder if the same might happen to Elm, should Phoenix adopt it as their browser language of choice. There's definitely a connection there -- for what it's worth, the keynote at 2016's Erlang Factory conference was a shared talk between Chris McCord (creator of Phoenix) and Evan Czaplicki (creator of Elm), \"Making the Web Functional\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11281627,"author":"yelnatz","month":"2016-03","story":"Managing two million web servers","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Phoenix.Presence, CRDTs and heartbeats solving distributed presence.","quote":"They used a combination of CRDTs with heartbeats to implement it.","text":"The Phoenix Framework (web framework for Elixir) already solves this for you.[1] It's called Phoenix.Presence. They used a combination of CRDTs with heartbeats to implement it. You are right, it is a hard problem because of the distributed nature of Erlang/Elixir. That's why Chris provided a framework level solution for it. [1] [VIDEO] (Erlang Factory SF 2016 Keynote Phoenix and Elm – Making the Web Functional)"}],"r":[["h3gwyHNo7MI",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1]]},"jSo5v5t4OQM":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as supporting material in arguments about IQ and the g-factor view of intelligence.","q":{"x":"IQ has an amazingly high correlation with academic success and work success","i":15888599,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":41540877,"author":"zahlman","month":"2024-09","story":"The data on extreme human ageing is rotten from the inside out","stance":"rec","why":"Additional viewing supporting the g-factor view of intelligence.","quote":"Additional viewing that largely covers my points below","text":"The link demonstrates that there is a well-reproduced phenomenon in real science whereby, e.g., test scores in various academic subjects correlate positively with each other, and that this can be explained by a common psychometric factor that is reasonable to refer to as \"intelligence\". The IQ or \"intelligence quotient\" is an attempt to quantify that which is known to exist, and it's actually one of the best understood ideas in the science of the brain. Additional viewing that largely covers my points below: [VIDEO] You started off saying: > There a lot of pseudoscience around IQ too, probably starting with the very concept of IQ for measuring \"intelligence\" (for which we would need a strong definition anyway) The point is that we do, in fact, have all the necessary scientific research to argue that the concept of \"intelligence\" exists - i.e., that we can identify a single-factor quantity that can be fairly described with a single number - and that anything calling itself IQ is definitionally a measurement of that single quantity. In particular: problem-solving capability is a real thing, and some people very obviously have more of it than others. Also, we notably don't have data to support more than one factor anywhere near as strong as Spearman's g . (That is to say: we see correlations between academic performance in all subjects - rather than strong positive correlations within certain groups but weak or negative correlations between those groups). The fact that specific "},{"id":18804084,"author":"akira2501","month":"2019-01","story":"IQ is largely a pseudoscientific swindle","stance":"rec","why":"Good classroom segment on problems with using IQ data for policy.","quote":"I think Jordan B Peterson has a good classroom video segment[1] on this problem.","text":"> but \"intelligence\" less so I don't think there's any problem measuring intelligence, where we've traditionally run into issues is where we start to make policy or social decisions using this data without understanding it's subtleties. I think Jordan B Peterson has a good classroom video segment[1] on this problem. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15888599,"author":"lucio","month":"2017-12","story":"Superior IQs associated with mental and physical disorders, research suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Explains IQ's correlation with academic and work success.","quote":"IQ has an amazingly high correlation with academic success and work success","text":">Yet, also, IQ tests are strongly influenced by cultural knowledge Raven Progressive Matrices, I believe the most used IQ test today is not influenced by cultural knowledge. IQ has an amazingly high correlation with academic success and work success, see [VIDEO] it is worthwhile"},{"id":43733588,"author":"ninetyninenine","month":"2025-04","story":"America underestimates the difficulty of bringing manufacturing back","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as supporting material in an argument about IQ by country.","quote":"this can help","text":">I didn't say anything about the validity of IQ. I said that Richard Lynn's numbers, which this site cites, are fraudulent. \"IIT 2024\" appears to be results from a website survey. You said IQ by country doesn't exist. And i said, IQ is so pervasive it fucking does. You also referenced something completely off topic. Some random book claiming that because that random book is invalid the whole concept of IQ by country doesn't exist which is absolutely wrong. >Respectfully, I think it's you that needs to do a bit more reading. I might be wrong about any of this stuff; I'm not an expert. But I'm pretty sure the first Google search result you find for \"IQ by country\" isn't going to rebut me effectively. I'm well versed enough in IQ to know that even the first link on google is good enough to refute you. You don't have to believe me, but you can always do your own research to find out I'm right. [VIDEO] this can help"}],"r":[["jHP942Livy0",1],["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["o9pEzgHorH0",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1]]},"lI7T650RTT8":{"wy":"Pointed to as an authoritative answer, from a former Abrams commander and tank historian, to claims that tanks are obsolete.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend this video by a former Abrams commander and tank historian who presents this argument far better than I can.","i":31610032,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33519694,"author":"mellavora","month":"2022-11","story":"Dragon’s teeth – Stopping tanks in their tracks","stance":"rec","why":"Expert disagrees that tanks are overmatched by anti-tank weapons","quote":"The Chieftan disagrees with you.","text":"> currently completely overmatched by anti-tank weapons The Chieftan disagrees with you. [VIDEO] I suspect he has more practical and theoretical knowledge on the topic than the sum total of all posters on this thread."},{"id":33524125,"author":"scottLobster","month":"2022-11","story":"Dragon’s teeth – Stopping tanks in their tracks","stance":"rec","why":"Former tanker covers why tanks need crews and combined arms","quote":"The Chieften, a former Tanker, has a good video on the subject","text":"Against an enemy with signal jamming capability remote operation is not a given. Plus tanks communicate in real time with infantry/other elements. The lag introduced by remote operation/communication would be a serious hindrance even if everything was working, which it often isn't. Tanks have to respond in real time, they don't have the luxury of seconds of delay like say an orbiting drone. There's also the case of not wanting disabled tanks to fall into the hands of the enemy. Short of rigging the tanks with a self-destruct system (which would likely just make them explode more easily when hit), you need soldiers to do that. Keep in mind that anything operating in battlefield conditions must be resilient. The entire situation is a constant stream of edge cases. Also, don't look to Russia in Ukraine for an example of how tanks are used. Tanks are generally not meant to act alone. Either they're operating with a lot of other tanks, substantial supporting infantry, or both. There are tons of other reasons as well, The Chieften, a former Tanker, has a good video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31610032,"author":"some_random","month":"2022-06","story":"Classified specs leaked on War Thunder forum for third time","stance":"rec","why":"Former Abrams commander argues tanks are not obsolete","quote":"I'd recommend this video by a former Abrams commander and tank historian who presents this argument far better than I can.","text":"I really don't think that's a good comparison, cavalry didn't just fall from grace because they became easily slaughtered by infantry, they stopped being used because their role became irrelevant and were replaced. The role tanks play has not become irrelevant and while they aren't as survivable as they used to be they aren't nearly as vulnerable as clips from UA seem to imply. I'd recommend this video by a former Abrams commander and tank historian who presents this argument far better than I can. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31755571,"author":"breadloaf","month":"2022-06","story":"Does the Tank Have a Future?","stance":"rec","why":"Tank's battlefield task persists even though it is easily killed","quote":"Yes it does. See \"The Chieftain: No, The Tank Is Not Dead","text":"Yes it does. See \"The Chieftain: No, The Tank Is Not Dead\" [VIDEO] TLDW: Tank has a task on a battlefield and fact that you can easily kill it won't take that task away. Same goes for infantry and they are not going away either. On the other hand we don't see battleships anymore, because big guns were replaced by precise missiles with much more range."},{"id":31298203,"author":"chiph","month":"2022-05","story":"When is a ‘tank’ not a tank?","stance":"rec","why":"Armor expert answers whether tanks are obsolete","quote":"An armor officer can answer this better than I can","text":"An armor officer can answer this better than I can: [VIDEO] If you're watching the videos from Ukraine - the Russians are pretty much doing it all wrong. You do not send tanks into cities unless they're following infantry who are clearing houses & rooftops. You do not leave your tanks parked in a nice neat line on a country road. When your unit comes under fire, you do not just sit there. Why are the Russians doing these things when they know it's bad for their health? They don't have much of an NCO cadre. In a modern military, the NCOs have the experience and the authority to make sure stuff happens rapidly and correctly. They will have made sure the junior enlisted know how to operate and maintain their weapon & gear, and are able to do the mission without fucking up."},{"id":31758405,"author":"ckozlowski","month":"2022-06","story":"Does the Tank Have a Future?","stance":"reference","why":"Tank value lies in capabilities nothing else yet provides","quote":"it's not about the tank's vulnerabilities, but the capabilities it provides that have yet to be provided elsewhere.","text":"To paraphrase The Chieftan (who's video is linked elsewhere in this thread but I'll included here: [VIDEO] ), it's not about the tank's vulnerabilities, but the capabilities it provides that have yet to be provided elsewhere. ATGMs, RPGs, guided mortar rounds and switchblade drones are not offensive weapons that can take and hold ground. But infantry, supported by a tank which they can knock on the hatch of and tell to obliterate the machine gun nest pinning them down with instant 120mm fire, can. When armies find something that can replace the mobile, protected, and relatively instant firepower a tank can provide to the infantry, the tank will be dead. But nothing yet has quite come along to combine those things, and so regardless of the tank's vulnerabilities, it will remain."},{"id":31758760,"author":"ckozlowski","month":"2022-06","story":"Does the Tank Have a Future?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why direct-fire guns are not replaced by drones","quote":"A large-caliber, direct-fire gun provides long range, precise, and instant effects.","text":"They don't offer the same level of firepower. A portable drone may be faster employed than a helicopter, large drone, or close support aircraft, but it suffers from lack of payload. It also takes longer to get on target, and has it's own set of vulnerabilities. (The vulnerability argument being that even if it is expendable, a drone disrupted or shot down does not help your unit eliminate the threat it was facing.) A large-caliber, direct-fire gun provides long range, precise, and instant effects. As The Chieftan explains: [VIDEO] (timecode link provided.)"},{"id":31082245,"author":"csours","month":"2022-04","story":"The tank is dead: Long live the javelin, the switchblade, the ... ?","stance":"reference","why":"Chieftain's view that tanks remain relevant, listed among sources","quote":"Perun Chieftain Bernard","text":"Perun [LINK] Chieftain [VIDEO] Bernard [LINK] Extreme summary, blame me if I missed something obviously important - unsupported tanks will die to infantry or artillery and have done so since shortly after the inception of the tank. Tanks are actually pretty cheap compared to replacing a human, it costs quite a lot to raise a modern human. The tank role still exists, but ratios of tanks to other fighting vehicles may change."}],"r":[["8AHCfZTRGiI",1],["MesrrYyuoa4",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["Hhy7JUinlu0",1],["DnPmg0R1M04",1],["N_4R4X7AWtU",1]]},"5hUT8VZNvm8":{"wy":"HN cites it in Lambda School threads as a revealing interview that lends credibility to Woo's reporting.","q":{"x":"It's a great watch.","i":40086847,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40086847,"author":"adroitboss","month":"2024-04","story":"CFPB Takes Action Against Coding Boot Camp BloomTech and CEO Austen Allred","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Confident, well-spoken debate on Lambda School.","quote":"It's a great watch.","text":"I remember seeing you debate Jason Calacanis from the all in podcast on this week in startups on this exact topic. You are probably one of the most confident, well spoken people I have ever heard on a podcast. You also don't derive your comfort from the people around you, so you didn't feel the need to laugh away awkward moments. It's a great watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28990344,"author":"digianarchist","month":"2021-10","story":"Lambda School leaked documents show poor performance over the last two years","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting interview on Lambda School despite host's hostile questioning.","quote":"Vincent's interview with This Week in Startups on Lambda School is also very interesting","text":"Vincent's interview with This Week in Startups on Lambda School is also very interesting despite the weird McCarthyist line of questioning the host goes down. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26949628,"author":"eachro","month":"2021-04","story":"Lambda School agrees to end deceptive educational financing practices","stance":"reference","why":"Interview makes the author's claims more credible.","quote":"I'm more inclined to believe what Woo wrote.","text":"Note that the author of this piece was the founder of Coderpad, not some industry entrenched journalist. After watching his interview with Jason Calacanis( [VIDEO] ), where they discuss the content of his piece, I'm more inclined to believe what Woo wrote."}]},"tvos4nORf_Y":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as an inspiring reflection on what to optimize in life beyond career, especially in threads on burnout and meaning.","q":{"x":"This is a very inspiring presentation, especially towards the end.","i":12336551,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12336551,"author":"khaledh","month":"2016-08","story":"How to be mediocre and be happy with yourself","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring talk on measuring life by impact on others.","quote":"This is a very inspiring presentation, especially towards the end.","text":"[VIDEO] How Will You Measure Your Life? Clay Christensen at TEDxBoston This is a very inspiring presentation, especially towards the end. The take away is that it's not important how high you went up a certain hierarchy or how much money you have in the bank. The important question is what did you do with your life to make other people's lives better? When you're put in certain situations that touch other people's lives, it's for a reason, and your life will be measured by how you did in those situations."},{"id":7892180,"author":"lateguy","month":"2014-06","story":"Founder Depression","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on measuring a meaningful life.","quote":"A good youtube link","text":"What a good response, I first read sam article and agreed to him but with good response changed a lot. I still feel there is a problem with life that is tradeoff. Lets see right from the start when we are born in this world we don't have any thoughts or goals nothing. The world start giving us reality. Suppose most of the people here on HN will want to be a better programmer and improve themselves. In Programming they find something good. Ideally as they were born with nothing so how they can say this is actually they want, so when people say this is what they really want I think they still say what the world them to say. Now the next point come when people make decision and then repent, its a tradeoff. Suppose Right now someone have option of doing a PHD in Physics( which is my passion) and other to roam across world in some job( which also he wants). He choose PHD and after 10 years he say he didn't satisfied with PHD he must have took the job, I feel we human always do that because we enjoy the first think and now we will say we didn't enjoy it much and want other think. Yes, I agree people say that enjoy their work and is in resonance with it because they don't have some equal tradeoff to make their work for them is given to world by them as a best thing. A good youtube link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41970485,"author":"khaledh","month":"2024-10","story":"Care Doesn't Scale","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts hierarchy-based success with positive impact on others.","quote":"He compares how most people define success by how high up the hierarchy they get","text":"This reminds me of Clay Christensen's talk: How Will You Measure Your Life?[0] He compares how most people define success by how high up the hierarchy they get, against the individual effort of making a positive impact on the people around you. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26870577,"author":"nuisance-bear","month":"2021-04","story":"Harvard Diploma for Sale","stance":"reference","why":"Christensen's story about HBS reunion lessons.","quote":"Clay Christensen used to tell a story about the atmosphere at HBS reunions over time.","text":"Clay Christensen used to tell a story about the atmosphere at HBS reunions over time. It sounds like this person learned early on the lesson that HBS graduates learn by their 25th reunion. Edit: [VIDEO]"}]},"udJJ7n_Ryjg":{"wy":"Cited in nuclear-versus-renewables debates to support the argument that cheap renewables undercut new nuclear.","q":{"x":"Eye-opening video","i":38301667,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38301667,"author":"natmaka","month":"2023-11","story":"Portugal just ran on 100% renewables for six days in a row","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening argument that renewables undercut new nuclear","quote":"Eye-opening video","text":"> Nuclear AND Renewable energy It cannot be done because it financially condemns new nuclear plants. \"The rates at which conventional power plants are utilized will continue to decrease as competitive pressure from near-zero marginal cost solar photovoltaic and onshore wind power, and battery energy storage continue to grow exponentially worldwide.\" Eye-opening video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35215198,"author":"enslavedrobot","month":"2023-03","story":"Nuclear power plant leaked 1.5M litres of radioactive water in Minnesota","stance":"rec","why":"Outlines future of distributed energy and centralized generation","quote":"I'll leave you with two YouTube videos that outline the future of distributed energy and the fate of centralized generation.","text":"I don't think there is much point is continuing this discussion, though, as a PhD materials chemist with an MBA, I could literally write dissertations on the future costs of solar and storage. Instead, I'll leave you with two YouTube videos that outline the future of distributed energy and the fate of centralized generation. [LINK] [VIDEO] Peace"},{"id":45196328,"author":"natmaka","month":"2025-09","story":"Microsoft doubles down on small modular reactors and fusion energy","stance":"reference","why":"Supports renewables-dominant grid argument","quote":"","text":"Indeed! Experts (International Energy Agency, McKinsey, etc.) are clear: renewables will produce the majority of electricity by 2050. [LINK] [LINK] This is inevitable (no other possibility is realistic), and this applies to France, where RTE (EDF's subsidiary that manages the electricity grid) has emphasized that 100% nuclear power is out of the question, and that more than 60% nuclear power would be utopian. This trend is already clearly visible and welcomed by investors. [LINK] The cost of producing renewable electricity is increasingly lower than that of nuclear power. [LINK] During their operation, they do not consume fuel, produce waste, or expose people to a major accident, and are therefore and will be preferred. [LINK] The surplus electricity sometimes produced by renewables will be leveraged by increasing storage capacity (electric vehicles, hydro pumped storage, etc.) as well as green hydrogen. It will gradually replace nuclear electricity, which is supposed to compensate for their variability, especially since the latter cannot adjust its production quickly and frequently enough ( [LINK] ). All of this will reduce the nuclear load factor and therefore increase its production cost (it is only profitable with a high load factor), with strong feedback (the higher the load factor, the more likely it will be). This will increasingly favor the deployment of a system based primarily on renewables, meaning that nuclear electricity will become less and less useful and more "},{"id":45259992,"author":"natmaka","month":"2025-09","story":"EU court rules nuclear energy is clean energy","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in debate on nuclear versus renewables","quote":"","text":"> China currently builds more renewables than nuclear. I said it is irrelevant. Those are different things No: nuclear and renewables are electricity-generating equipment types, and all the debate is about the proportion of renewables and nuclear in the final system. Seeing them as disconnected (in different universes) is not even funny. [VIDEO] > unless you don't understand the irrelevance of nameplate capacity with intermittent renewables. This perspective dates back a time when transporting electricity was expensive (lines, losses...), storing it also was expensive ( ), fossil fuels and nuclear were the only way to obtain gridpower... all this is obsolete. Explanations: [LINK] > China is also currently seeing the bottom drop out of their renewables industry Source? (I lived in China from mid-2017 to mid-2025) The renewables industry there is, as in nearly every nation, in much better state than nearly any other one. > The EPR2 projects could not even have started in 2022, because he law that prohibits increasing nuclear capacity beyond the currently installed 63.2GW was only repealed in March 2023 Nope. This law stated about active production capacity, and never forbade any reactor-building project. The very first EPR (Flamanville-3) project was running while this law was instated (2015) and did not stop. It simply forbade it to start without other reactor with at least a total equivalent powerplate value to be shutdown. Recent news: [LINK] > they still have to deal with a"},{"id":42876639,"author":"natmaka","month":"2025-01","story":"Nuclear fusion: it's time for a reality check","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for renewables eroding nuclear baseload","quote":"","text":"> Renewables are outpacing nuclear at such a pace that you could legitimately wonder why to bother with such projects at all. Indeed, especially as renewable will more and more eat into nuclear 'baseload', lowering its load factor and therefore raising its total production cost. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41776790,"author":"natmaka","month":"2024-10","story":"Fukushima Reactor: TEPCO robot aims to extract nuclear fuel","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim renewables will kill nuclear economics","quote":"","text":"'Advanced nuclear' neglects the last developments (the Vogtle nuclear plant project hugely over-budget and late [LINK] , cancelled plans (Montagorda, V. C. Summer, River Band, Callaway...). Even the newest pipe dream in town (SMR) already had its first rebuff ( [LINK] ). Last but not least... there is no running project. Therefore writing \"US nuclear capacity has the potential to triple from ~100 GW in 2024 to ~300 GW by 2050.\" in a title is for sure easy, however there is now sign of such potential to become anything else. As long as the source is not consuming any fuel nor producing much waste any EROI greater than one seems OK to me. > why they assume 4h storage? AFAIK because they consider that electric vehicles' batteries will be useful (through V2G). > What you'll do in case both solar&wind will be low? AFAIK the idea is to interconnect at continental scale, as this is useful whatever the type of sources (even if it is mainly nuclear), then to benefit from diverses wind (or even solar) regimes. > overcapacity will need to be subsidized heavily because excess solar capacity will be unused a lot of the time Not with an electric fleet of vehicles, to begin with. Green hydrogen will also absorb part of it (for industrial applications, electric backup...). > if it'll be used less and less, youll need subsidies This will kill nuclear (see [VIDEO] )."},{"id":41776926,"author":"Moldoteck","month":"2024-10","story":"Fukushima Reactor: TEPCO robot aims to extract nuclear fuel","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted in a rebuttal about overcapacity costs","quote":"","text":"you sure it neglects vogtle? > AFAIK because they consider that electric vehicles' batteries will be useful (through V2G). - lol, kinda interesting assumptions, especially considering that it'll still imply additional costs > AFAIK the idea is to interconnect at continental scale - lol, at such scales that sounds as a bigger pipedream than cheap h2 emission free generation > if it'll be used less and less, youll need subsidies > This will kill nuclear (see [VIDEO] ). This will kill basically any peaker plant be that fossil, hydro or nuclear. That's kinda the point. With a renewable grid you'll need huge overcapacity of both production, peaker and storage that'll be rarely used. No matter the technology - any of it will get extremely expensive the higher the renewable share will get. Even Norway starts facing similar problems since they import cheap renewable in peak production, meaning their hydro is earning less"},{"id":41375886,"author":"natmaka","month":"2024-08","story":"Nuclear reactors a mile underground promise safe, cheap power","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for effect of renewables on nuclear load factor","quote":"","text":"The production cost of 'renewable' electricity (RE) is increasingly lower than that of nuclear. So when they produce, they are and will be preferred. This will reduce the effective load factor of nuclear power and therefore increase its production cost (it is only profitable with a high load factor), with a strong feedback. It gets worse because, on the technical level and despite some progress, nuclear power cannot (due to technical limitation and for safety-related reasons) adjust its production (\"load following\") to always exactly match demand. This will promote the deployment of an RE system based on a continental a mix (wind, solar, etc.), smart grid, clean backup (green hydrogen), storage, etc. which will reduce the variability of its production (intermittency effect) so nuclear power will be less and less useful and more and more expensive. The effect on nuclear is easy to predict. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39411023,"author":"enslavedrobot","month":"2024-02","story":"Solar and battery to make up 81% of new US electric-generating capacity in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim nuclear costs rise as renewables undercut price","quote":"","text":"The LCOE of nuclear is very sensitive to its capacity factor. Nuclear costs are set to skyrocket because for a larger and larger part of the day they will not be profitable as renewables undercut the price of power. With a lower and lower capacity factor, new nuclear plants will never pay for themselves. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30661056,"author":"natmaka","month":"2022-03","story":"Finland starts much-delayed nuclear plant, brings respite to power market","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument nuclear is condemned by renewables","quote":"","text":"With such a (realistic) approach nuclear is condemned because its production costs (LCOE: [LINK] ) are higher than renewables' (and the gap is enlarging). Therefore renewables will be preferably used. Nuclear gets more expensive as it is more sparsely used: this feedback loop will widen the gap. [VIDEO] With time renewables production will get less an less intermittent thanks to a more and more adequate spreading, to a mix (wind, solar...), to storage (hydrogen...) and an overly-expensive nuclear will become useless. [LINK]"}],"r":[["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1]]},"x_q50tvbQm4":{"wy":"Praised as a lesson in explaining technology to non-technical people and as a window into early Mac design.","q":{"x":"I think some of us (me included) would do well to keep this video in mind when explaining technology to non-technical people.","i":27409836,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27409836,"author":"dorkwood","month":"2021-06","story":"The Woman Who Gave the Macintosh a Smile (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kare slowly introduces the Mac interface; a lesson in explaining tech to novices.","quote":"I think some of us (me included) would do well to keep this video in mind when explaining technology to non-technical people.","text":"I love this video of Kare giving an introduction to the Mac interface on \"Computer Chronicles\" back in 1984 [1]. At first it seems quaint to see how slowly and carefully she explains what we now consider to be basic UI features. But then you realize that for many people in the world, a desktop computer is just as strange and confusing to them now as it was to most people back then. I think some of us (me included) would do well to keep this video in mind when explaining technology to non-technical people. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32016440,"author":"aasasd","month":"2022-07","story":"Ed is the standard text editor (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Astounding Mac control panel by modern standards, among favorite old tech videos.","quote":"the control panel is just astounding by modern standards","text":"Yeah, I'm kinda getting into these vids from the seventies and eighties. “Introducing The Amazing Compact Disc, 1982” [LINK] — “You can pick it up and move around, even shake, and nothing happens”, yeah sure bud. “Introduction to Microsoft Excel, 1992” [LINK] “Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics on the Computer Chronicles, 1984” [VIDEO] — the control panel is just astounding by modern standards, especially seeing as for me the control panel is a litmus test of an OS interface. Also one of my favorite music-making tutorials is the three-part, three-hour series ‘Intro to Synthesis’ from sometime in the early 80s: [LINK] — instead of going through all the terminology in ten minutes and one second, the guy takes his time twiddling knobs and showing what each one does."},{"id":31319015,"author":"neilv","month":"2022-05","story":"The Making Of Grand Theft Auto (1996) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Kare demonstrating something new; compares to evolution of software job interviews.","quote":"She's demonstrating something pretty new and significant to a lot of people.","text":"\"Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics on the Computer Chronicles (1984)\" [VIDEO] She's demonstrating something pretty new and significant to a lot of people. Maybe software job interviews have followed a related arc, to amped-up performance art rituals and shibboleths, from genuine meaningful down-to-earth conversation."},{"id":29385028,"author":"dang","month":"2021-11","story":"Susan Kare Explains Macintosh UI Ergonomics (1984) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Moderator notes the submission URL was changed to this video at the Kare interview timestamp.","quote":"I changed the timestamp to where they start interviewing Susan Kare.","text":"Ok, we've changed the URL to that from [VIDEO] above. Thanks! (I changed the timestamp to where they start interviewing Susan Kare.)"},{"id":24054392,"author":"jonny_eh","month":"2020-08","story":"Greg Joswiak replaces Phil Schiller as head of Apple marketing","stance":"reference","why":"Susan Kare explaining Macintosh UI ergonomics.","quote":"Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics","text":"> The person missing from this list who I'd like to see added is Susan Kare. She defined the look of the original Mac and iPod, designing its original bitmap fonts and icons. Hugely influential. Absolutely! Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8CAafjodkyE",1],["i9MXYZh1jcs",1],["atvtBE6t48M",1],["b6URa-PTqfA",1],["8PwwRR8deHk",1]]},"-ZXrJRpA0Jw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a realistic demonstration of E-Ink monitor refresh rates and how such displays feel in use.","q":{"x":"I do think the videos do a a good job of showing how the monitors feel.","i":40921890,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40921890,"author":"jrmg","month":"2024-07","story":"Daylight eInk Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how Dasung E-Ink monitors feel in use.","quote":"I do think the videos do a a good job of showing how the monitors feel.","text":"‘Quite high’ is still not what you’d think of as ‘quite high’ if you’d never experienced earlier versions of eink though. LTT on YouTube has some video reviews of Dasung’s displays. I know tastes vary when it comes to LTT - and they focus on some ridiculous things in the reviews (Can you game on them? Obviously, no.) but if you get past that I do think the videos do a a good job of showing how the monitors feel. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":37652900,"author":"pests","month":"2023-09","story":"Show HN: E-Ink Day Schedule","stance":"rec","why":"Review of large 25 inch E-Ink monitor running at 15Hz.","quote":"It worked way better than I thought it would and is a pretty decent size.","text":"LTT just did a video[1] on an 25inch E-Ink display[2] that runs at 15Hz. It worked way better than I thought it would and is a pretty decent size. Little pricey. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":37736813,"author":"c0balt","month":"2023-10","story":"Eye comfort a priority for all-in-one desktop PC with color E Ink display","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates the E-Ink display refresh rate.","quote":"The refresh rate of this display was somewhat demonstrated in this[0] LTT video","text":"The refresh rate of this display was somewhat demonstrated in this[0] LTT video on the KickStarter display. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UJ7Qc3bsxjI",1],["yXbJe-rUNP8",1],["dXdoim96v5A",1]]},"160F8F8mXlo":{"wy":"HN treats it as the origin of the full-wine-glass meme and an illustration of how models lack concepts missing from their training data.","q":{"x":"this video is where the \"AI can't draw a full glass of wine\" meme got traction","i":43479027,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45363902,"author":"jimbo808","month":"2025-09","story":"Getting AI to work in complex codebases","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates that models lack world models for concepts missing from training data.","quote":"illustrates it pretty well","text":"I think you're looking at it too abstractly. An LLM isn't representing anything, it has a bag of numbers that some other algorithm produced for it. When you give it some numbers, it takes them and does matrix operations with them in order to randomly select a token from a softmax distribution, one at a time, until the EOS token is generated. If they don't have any training data that covers a particular concept, they can't map it onto a world model and make predictions about that concept based on an understanding of the world and how it works. [This video]( [VIDEO] ) illustrates it pretty well. These things may or may not end up being fixed in the models, but that's only because they've been further trained with the specific examples. Brains have world models. Cats see a cup of water, and they know exactly what will happen when you tip it over (and you can bet they're gonna do it)."},{"id":43155938,"author":"proc0","month":"2025-02","story":"AI Is Already Obsolete – The Open Challenge No One Will Answer","stance":"rec","why":"Shows ChatGPT failing to draw a full wine glass, illustrating AI limits.","quote":"There is also a great video by Alex O'Connor on a simple prompt to generate a full glass of wine, which illustrates this really well","text":"I completely agree. You can see this limitation if you try to create anything complex with the current state of the art. There is also a great video by Alex O'Connor on a simple prompt to generate a full glass of wine, which illustrates this really well - [VIDEO] This wave of AI is amazing. It's a great tool for many applications, and it will even revolutionize many of them, but it is still far from AGI, a term that has been hijacked by the tech industry to hype up their products. In some ways AI has advanced a lot, but in other ways it has been set back by the hype and muddying of the terms."},{"id":43788094,"author":"EGreg","month":"2025-04","story":"Ask HN: Share your AI prompt that stumps every model","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the example of a prompt that stumps image models.","quote":"","text":"Draw a clock that shows [time other than 10:10] Draw a wine glass that's totally full to the brim etc. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43430195,"author":"wavemode","month":"2025-03","story":"Appeals court rules that Constitution protects possession of AI-generated CSAM","stance":"reference","why":"Example of diffusion models failing to generalize to a full wine glass.","quote":"","text":"Well, maybe. Maybe not. See \"Why can't ChatGPT draw a full glass of wine?\" [VIDEO] Diffusion models do posses some capability to synthesize ideas, but that capability does not necessarily generalize to every possible use case. So it's impossible to say for certain that that is what is happening."},{"id":43479027,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2025-03","story":"4o Image Generation","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the full wine glass AI meme, with explicit prompts failing.","quote":"this video is where the \"AI can't draw a full glass of wine\" meme got traction","text":"I think you're missing the context everyone else has - this video is where the \"AI can't draw a full glass of wine\" meme got traction [VIDEO] The prompts (some generated by ChatGPT itself, since it's instructing DALL-E behind the scenes) include phrases like \"full to the brim\" and \"almost spilling over\" that are not up to interpretation at all."}]},"5BIP_N9qQm4":{"wy":"Pointed to as the standard introduction to SILE as a modern TeX alternative, though possibly dated.","q":{"x":"was nice introduction, although I don't know how up to date it is","i":27303963,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27303963,"author":"lahcim8","month":"2021-05","story":"Finl is not LaTeX","stance":"rec","why":"Nice introduction to SILE as TeX alternative.","quote":"was nice introduction, although I don't know how up to date it is","text":"Recently I have been also thinking about TeX's internals, also because I feel the pain points you describe in at [LINK] . I have too thought about experimenting with TeX rewrites/alternatives and particularly in Rust. I don't know if you are aware, but finl has been also submitted to Lobsters ( [LINK] ). One of the comments mentions SILE ( [LINK] ). I looked into it ( [VIDEO] was nice introduction, although I don't know how up to date it is). It seems to me that SILE replaces many pain points in TeX, while being somewhat close to LuaTeX (and TeX in general), due to use of similiar components (Lua, alogorithms, hyphenation patters, libraries). It even supports \"LaTeX\" like input syntax, but frankly I don't think that is the part of LaTeX I would keep :). Can you share your opinion on SILE?"},{"id":11473277,"author":"akavel","month":"2016-04","story":"Brian Kernighan on the Typesetting of “The Go Programming Language” Book","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing deep dive from SILE's author at FOSDEM 2015.","quote":"see the author's presentation at FOSDEM 2015 - I found it really amusing","text":"Of recent attempts at dethroning LaTeX, there's also SILE, which actually reuses some LaTeX internals. For some examples of the output (hard to find, the author seems not great at marketing), see e.g.: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] For a deeper dive, see the author's presentation at FOSDEM 2015 - I found it really amusing - from the starting line of: \"I wrote a typesetting system - by mistake.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33452156,"author":"svat","month":"2022-11","story":"Sile: A Modern Rewrite of TeX","stance":"reference","why":"SILE author's talk from FOSDEM 2015.","quote":"A couple of the author's talks are also online","text":"A couple of the author's talks are also online: • [VIDEO] [FOSDEM 2015, \"Introducing SILE: A New Typesetting System\"] • [LINK] [GRANSHAN Conference 2015, \"Global typesetting with SILE\"]"},{"id":29715646,"author":"svat","month":"2021-12","story":"LuaTeX Comes of Age (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by SILE's author on its Lua design.","quote":"You can see this talk by its author about it","text":"> Furthermore, the effort to rewrite as much of the TeX implementation as possible in Lua is perplexing. Very little of the TeX implementation has been rewritten in Lua. (Assuming you're talking of LuaTeX itself and not the \"TeX without TeX\" wiki page, which is just for demonstration.) LuaTeX starts with the WEB (Pascal) code of TeX, manually translated into C (see e.g. [LINK] ), and, additionally, hooks are added: callbacks that can be written in Lua, and which have access to the internal data structures. (By the way, SILE is a separate typesetting system (unrelated to TeX) that is actually written in Lua, and re-implements some of the TeX algorithms in Lua, I think. You can see this talk by its author about it: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":13682056,"author":"gkya","month":"2017-02","story":"SILE Typesetting System","stance":"reference","why":"FOSDEM 2015 talk introducing SILE.","quote":"I first saw it in FOSDEM '15 talks [1].","text":"Yes, it made me recall this, though I first saw it in FOSDEM '15 talks [1]. I thought it'd be nice to see what people think about this as I'm in the looks for a TeX alternative. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dE-vOscpiNc",1]]},"8XS5q9xhaMc":{"wy":"HN links it in Imba threads as a quick demonstration of how the language combines code, HTML and CSS.","q":{"x":"It demonstrates how CSS styling, code, and HTML works together in this language","i":31498408,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31498408,"author":"knight17","month":"2022-05","story":"Show HN: My free course for learning Imba","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a simple counter showing CSS, code and HTML together","quote":"It demonstrates how CSS styling, code, and HTML works together in this language","text":"This looks really nice. This is a video of author creating a simple counter with two buttons using imba. It demonstrates how CSS styling, code, and HTML works together in this language: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28196160,"author":"somebee","month":"2021-08","story":"Show HN: Imba 2.0 – The JavaScript Alternative by Scrimba (YC S20)","stance":"rec","why":"Build a counter in Imba in under a minute","quote":"Check out this video ( ) building a counter in less than 1 minute.","text":"I'm Sindre, CTO at Scrimba (S20). We're about to launch a _major_ overhaul of Imba, the programming language we use for everything here (both frontend and backend). Imho the best frameworks are the ones that are extracted from real projects trying to solve real problems. Imba is like that, but at a language-level. It has been through countless iterations over the past 7 years, striving to be the perfect language for developing web applications. We have added tons of cool things like touch modifiers ( [LINK] ), inline styles ( [LINK] ), optional types and great tooling that integrates deeply with TypeScript. With this version we feel that we are very close to our vision for what Imba should be. In other words; it is finally ready for public consumption. I'd wholeheartedly advice you to look into it and give it a whirl if you are interested in web development :) Check out this video ( [VIDEO] ) building a counter in less than 1 minute. - Compiles to Javascript, works with node + npm - DOM tags & styles as first-class citizens - Optional typing and deep TypeScript integration - Blazing-fast dev/build tools based on esbuild - Advanced tooling with refactoring++ across js,ts, and imba files"},{"id":37624898,"author":"trafnar","month":"2023-09","story":"Imba – The friendly full-stack language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Building a counter in 49 seconds","text":"I’ve been building all my projects with Imba for the past few years, I love it. - TaskTXT - Plain text todos and tasks with timers [LINK] - ReadyRunner - GPT chat for desktop [LINK] - Chonky Menu Re-Creation - Breakdown of a fancy menu UI [LINK] I’ve made several videos about Imba too: - Building a counter in 49 seconds: [VIDEO] - Video timeline UI: [LINK] - All of my Imba videos here: [LINK] I made the (free) official course on Scrimba: [LINK] Which is also on YouTube: [LINK] I understand that Imba is not appropriate for everyone and every project but I encourage you not to dismiss it. One are where it is clearly valuable is in building prototypes and side projects."},{"id":28207938,"author":"somebee","month":"2021-08","story":"Show HN: Imba – I have spent 7 years creating a programming language for the web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Relevant links for those who wish to look into it: Repo: [LINK] Docs: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] Article: [LINK] Site: [LINK] Discord: [LINK] Twitter: [LINK]"}]},"CnMRePtHMZY":{"wy":"It is the standard link whenever a new Peltier or thermoelectric cooling breakthrough is posted.","q":{"x":"they are _massively_ less efficient.","i":44435551,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44435551,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2025-07","story":"Scientists identify culprit behind biggest-ever U.S. honey bee die-off","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Peltier devices are far less efficient than compressors.","quote":"they are _massively_ less efficient.","text":"> though less efficient than other types IIRC they are _massively_ less efficient. Relevant Technology Connections video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44633060,"author":"otikik","month":"2025-07","story":"Staying cool without refrigerants: Next-generation Peltier cooling","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why thermoelectric cooling is inefficient.","quote":"Mandatory Technology Connections video about Peltier cooling","text":"Mandatory Technology Connections video about Peltier cooling, \"Why Thermoelectric Cooling Is Inefficient\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":42907131,"author":"tommiegannert","month":"2025-02","story":"New thermogalvanic tech paves way for more efficient fridges","stance":"rec","why":"Relaxed explanation of why Peltier cooling is inefficient.","quote":"there's a 50x difference in volume to cool.","text":"> Did peltiers ever get efficient? Not compared to gas compression. For a relaxed take on it: [VIDEO] (Thermoelectric cooling: it's not great) Though part of the complaints about the mini fridge is due to lack of insulation. But he compares it to his usual fridge, which has a similar power draw as this 60W mini fridge, and there's a 50x difference in volume to cool."},{"id":45323469,"author":"vintermann","month":"2025-09","story":"New thermoelectric cooling breakthrough nearly doubles efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Shows thermoelectric cooling efficiency started out poor.","quote":"the efficiency was pretty lousy to start with","text":"Doubles efficiency? From what I understand, the efficiency was pretty lousy to start with: [VIDEO] Aren't there theoretical limits to this sort of cooling too? But, if this innovation causes Technology Connections to make yet another heat pumps video, I'm all for it."},{"id":44451650,"author":"minimaxir","month":"2025-07","story":"Nano-engineered thermoelectrics enable scalable, compressor-free cooling","stance":"reference","why":"Standard video on Peltier cooling inefficiency.","quote":"Obligatory video on the inefficiencies of thermoelectric cooling/Peltier elements","text":"Obligatory video on the inefficiencies of thermoelectric cooling/Peltier elements from Techonology Connections: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["oOttvpjJvAo",1],["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["si9iqF5uTFk",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["xcwJt4bcnXs",1]]},"EeYvFl7li9E":{"wy":"Cited as the primary source for why Node exists and why JavaScript was chosen, in debates about async models and language choice.","q":{"x":"it's kind of refreshing to see the original node.js presentation, where using javascript is sort of a side-note","i":41766721,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41766721,"author":"karel-3d","month":"2024-10","story":"The Rise of Worse Is Better (1991)","stance":"rec","why":"Original Node presentation where JavaScript is a side-note","quote":"it's kind of refreshing to see the original node.js presentation, where using javascript is sort of a side-note","text":"And it keeps being polished and improved to the point it's almost not a turd, and now has types sort of, and much better engines, and now there are ARM machines that are literally designed to run it faster, and now most of your actual PC applications are written in it. But honestly it's kind of refreshing to see the original node.js presentation, where using javascript is sort of a side-note. He wanted to use callback-heavy language and JS fit the bill [VIDEO]"},{"id":37436037,"author":"mrkline","month":"2023-09","story":"Async Rust Is A Bad Language","stance":"rec","why":"Dahl's original Node pitch, ambivalent about JavaScript, focused on event loop","quote":"it's really interesting to go back and watch Ryan Dahl's talk introducing Node.js to the world","text":"> Async/await was a terrible idea for fixing JavaScript's lack of proper blocked threading that is currently being bolted onto every language. As fun as it is to hate on JavaScript, it's really interesting to go back and watch Ryan Dahl's talk introducing Node.js to the world ( [VIDEO] ). He's pretty ambivalent about it being JavaScript. His main goal was to find an abstraction around the epoll() I/O event loop that didn't make him want to tear his eyes out, and he tried a bunch of other stuff first."},{"id":41162998,"author":"throwitaway1123","month":"2024-08","story":"Learn PHP the Right Way","stance":"reference","why":"Shows JS was chosen for its asynchronous nature","quote":"If you watch Ryan Dahl's original presentation introducing Node, you'll see that JS was explicitly chosen because of its asynchronous nature","text":"> One of those environments is a GUI which is naturally a good application for async. The other is not. Backend web development is still largely input -> process -> output. Node is just shoehorning JS in, and pretending that JavaScript's weaknesses are strengths. Until you need to perform two or more IO operations at the same time (e.g. more than one of: database query, Redis query, HTTP request, file operation, etc). Then it becomes: input -> Promise.all (or Promise.race, or Promise.allSettled, or Promise.any) -> output. If you watch Ryan Dahl's original presentation introducing Node, you'll see that JS was explicitly chosen because of its asynchronous nature — it wasn't shoehorned in: [VIDEO] > Not sure what you mean. There are a bunch of ways to run PHP Node doesn't require an upstream CGI server at all. There's a server built-in to the Node binary: [LINK]"}]},"JBgG_VSP7f8":{"wy":"HN treats it as the classic reference for evolutionary simulation and genetic programming, an inspiration for many projects, with notes on evolution exploiting simulation bugs.","q":{"x":"I remember being obsessed with that paper","i":27475323,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27475323,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2021-06","story":"ALiEn – a GPU-accelerated artificial life simulation program","stance":"rec","why":"Classic evolving virtual creatures work, an inspiring paper.","quote":"I remember being obsessed with that paper","text":"Also relevant is Karl Sims' Evolving Virtual Creatures: [VIDEO] Not sure how they're connected, but I remember being obsessed with that paper and actually recreating it in 2D as a grad project."},{"id":7921292,"author":"jal278","month":"2014-06","story":"Maverick forerunner of artificial life and animation remains largely unknown","stance":"rec","why":"Karl Sims' evolved virtual creatures as an idea to add to a project.","quote":"Something like evolving virtual creatures would be a fun (although pretty ambitious) addition","text":"Something like evolving virtual creatures would be a fun (although pretty ambitious) addition [1]. There's a processing 2D virutal creatures model called sticky feet [2] (source available) that perhaps could be ported to js pretty quickly. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30820348,"author":"HaukeHi","month":"2022-03","story":"It looks like you’re trying to take over the world","stance":"reference","why":"Example of evolutionary optimization producing creatures.","quote":"Also see Karl Sims 'Creatures' from the 90s","text":"I think evolution is not an undirected process in that sense because it's an optimization process, that optimizes to create more copies of itself. Superintelligence will likely use some Evolutionary Computation (see en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evolutionary_computation ). Also see Karl Sims 'Creatures' from the 90s: [VIDEO] or OpenAI's Multi-Agent Hide and Seek: youtube.com/watch?v=kopoLzvh5jY"},{"id":16601773,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-03","story":"Surprising Creativity: Anecdotes from Evolutionary Computation","stance":"reference","why":"Evolved creatures exploited simulation bugs.","quote":"He noted at the time that the evolution process was great at exploiting bugs in the simulation.","text":"The article's philosophical discussion of \"surprise\" feels a little klunky and academic to me, and I think conflates some different kinds of surprise, but it's still really fun to think about. Many of the projects here are all inspired by Karl Sims' work in the early 90's. [LINK] Sims evolved virtual creates by specifying goals to achieve and then running a physics simulation. He noted at the time that the evolution process was great at exploiting bugs in the simulation. [VIDEO] I was insipred enough by Sims' genetic images ( [LINK] ) that I spent a few years trying to get surprising and beautiful results of my own, with some limited success ( [LINK] )."},{"id":15635634,"author":"dahart","month":"2017-11","story":"Self-Replicating Functions","stance":"reference","why":"Karl Sims' work as the main inspiration for the commenter's project.","quote":"Karl Sims, which is the main source of inspiration behind my project","text":"I hadn't before, thanks for the tip! It's been a while since I played with this stuff, but I might have to get a copy and explore. I have heard of MetaSynth in audio circles. I'm not sure, but the images and videos on the ArtMatic site look (to me) very inspired and influenced by the work of Karl Sims, which is the main source of inspiration behind my project... [LINK] You can find a bunch of his videos on YouTube too. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13153202,"author":"informatimago","month":"2016-12","story":"‘I am utterly disgusted’: Legendary animator Hayao Miyazaki takes AI down a peg","stance":"reference","why":"Classic genetic programming creatures learning movement, used to explain a misunderstanding.","quote":"This is a classical application of genetic programming.","text":"It seems to me that there are several levels of misunderstanding here. And the most interesting part is when Suzuki asks \"So, what is your goal?\", and the engineer answers \"Well, we would like to build a machine that can draw pictures like humans do.\". This is certainly a commendable goal. Then we cut to another sequence, where Miyazaki is drawing, and reflecting: \"I feel like we are nearing the end of times. We humans are losing faith in ourselves...\" It may or may not be so, but this is an interesting point to discuss. I can certainly understand how it could feel like it, but you'd have to study the motive for wanting to \"replace\" humans by machines, or just have machine perform like humans. One key misunderstanding in this exchange, and foremost on the part of the journalists reporting it (damn dumb journalists!), is that \"This is a presentation of an artificial intelligence model which learned certain movements.\" Obviously, they didn't teach it to move, they had a certain model of the body, and let the AI loose, trying to learn by itself how to move. This is a classical application of genetic programming. See how some of those creatures look \"creepy\" too: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] They obtained some creepy result, added a creepy texture and made it into a quick & dirty demo for \"animating zombies\". Clearly, the people who assisted to this demo didn't understand what it was. And the answer of the artist was the worst. Imagine assisting to Wright brothers' demo, and comi"},{"id":13153262,"author":"informatimago","month":"2016-12","story":"“An insult to life itself”: Miyazaki critiques artificial intelligence animation","stance":"reference","why":"Classic genetic programming creatures learning movement, used to explain a misunderstanding.","quote":"This is a classical application of genetic programming.","text":"It seems to me that there are several levels of misunderstanding here. And the most interesting part is when Suzuki asks \"So, what is your goal?\", and the engineer answers \"Well, we would like to build a machine that can draw pictures like humans do.\". This is certainly a commendable goal. Then we cut to another sequence, where Miyazaki is drawing, and reflecting: \"I feel like we are nearing the end of times. We humans are losing faith in ourselves...\" It may or may not be so, but this is an interesting point to discuss. I can certainly understand how it could feel like it, but you'd have to study the motive for wanting to \"replace\" humans by machines, or just have machine perform like humans. One key misunderstanding in this exchange, and foremost on the part of the journalists reporting it (damn dumb journalists!), is that \"This is a presentation of an artificial intelligence model which learned certain movements.\" Obviously, they didn't teach it to move, they had a certain model of the body, and let the AI loose, trying to learn by itself how to move. This is a classical application of genetic programming. See how some of those creatures look \"creepy\" too: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] They obtained some creepy result, added a creepy texture and made it into a quick & dirty demo for \"animating zombies\". Clearly, the people who assisted to this demo didn't understand what it was. And the answer of the artist was the worst. Imagine assisting to Wright brothers' demo, and comi"},{"id":10114393,"author":"fragsworth","month":"2015-08","story":"Robots learn to evolve and improve","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think they just took a concept figured out in 1994","text":"I think they just took a concept figured out in 1994 ( [VIDEO] ) and applied it to robots, in an attempt to get an easy publication written up. Really, though, we know we can make robot parts. The hard part is the software. What these people did by making it robotic was completely pointless (because making the robots is a huge amount of unnecessary added work) that doesn't add to our knowledge until we can solve all the software problems in simulations first."}],"r":[["X-iSQQgOd1A",1],["iphcyNWFD10",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["pkCLMl0e_0k",1],["0M7IINwTFVw",1]]},"KkMDCCdjyW8":{"wy":"HN suggests it as a free lecture for beginners that eases them into writing readable, reusable code.","q":{"x":"the lecturer eases you into the most important concepts with an understanding of how to engineer readable, reusable code","i":659327,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":46664741,"author":"TZubiri","month":"2026-01","story":"MIT's Computer Systems Security (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Old Java lecture with historical value and solid basics","quote":"it still has historical value","text":"Yeah, there were 2 golden ages: 1- When this internet thing came out 2- When this covid thing came out On the first era, here's a Java lecture from Stanford, if it's too basic for you, it still has historical value, iirc it's something like Java 6. And it also reinforces the basics. [VIDEO] It's a bit harder to follow along with online materials since you have to use the Internet Archive, and download older compilers or use options to target older versions, but it's all the more fun for it."},{"id":659327,"author":"jamesk2","month":"2009-06","story":"Ask HN: Where to begin programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Free Stanford lecture easing beginners into readable, reusable code design","quote":"the lecturer eases you into the most important concepts with an understanding of how to engineer readable, reusable code","text":"Python is fantastic but Learning Python might be a bit steep for someone who's not familiar with programming at all. Strangely, I think this youtube lecture series from Stanford is fantastic. [VIDEO] It's java but the lecturer eases you into the most important concepts with an understanding of how to engineer readable, reusable code. So watch it free. Then buy Learning Python. If there is an equivalent python learning video on youtube, I haven't found it."},{"id":20131722,"author":"world32","month":"2019-06","story":"Ask HN: How to deal with impostor syndrome when you don't have tech degree?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of university lectures available free on YouTube","quote":"many university film and publish their lectures on youtube","text":"Ok I see what you're saying. But if you have learned something through practice, can't you validate it by researching / googling it? See what people are saying on stackoverflow, in blog posts, in books etc.? Also many university film and publish their lectures on youtube, i.e. Stanford Software Engineering course: [VIDEO] > But after years of good performance reviews, successful projects, and promotions, I started to finally allow myself to believe that I knew what I was doing. Back to your original post, this kind of learning is FAR more valuable than anything you will learn in university. You have learned through practice and experience. I have probably interviewed at at ~20 different companies in the last 6 years and never once have I been asked a single question about my university degree, literally never. Nobody cares about them. They know that its the experience that counts, not the degree (though I'm not saying degrees are worthless either). In my experience I would say around 40% of the software developers I have worked with either do not have a degree at all or have a degree in a completed unrelated subject (i.e. Philosophy). The idea that one developer would discount another developer's idea because he doesn't have a degree in computer science is actually laughable. Though I'm only saying all of that in order to tell you that a degree is not the answer to your imposter syndrome. The answer is to educate yourself, read books, experiment with different technologies an"}]},"Ws4kbgfpKCw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for its fluid dynamics of detonation waves and its high-speed imaging of rotating pulses.","q":{"x":"you can still enjoy the pretty damn cool high speed imaging of the detonation waves in this type of engine","i":33047329,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33047329,"author":"tofof","month":"2022-10","story":"Japan tests explosion-powered rocket for the first time in space (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Researcher-level science of RDEs with striking high-speed imaging of detonation waves.","quote":"you can still enjoy the pretty damn cool high speed imaging of the detonation waves in this type of engine","text":"Here's a video that presents the science behind rotating detonation engines; the article was pretty light on detail. This video is at the other end of the spectrum, clearly intended for other researchers in this domain. Or, if it's over your head (it was for me, at least while trying to wake up), you can still enjoy the pretty damn cool high speed imaging of the detonation waves in this type of engine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31072013,"author":"flubloppoma","month":"2022-04","story":"No one knows why the most used spacecraft propulsion system works","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on fluid dynamics behind detonation and thruster effects.","quote":"it is just a great presentation on the fluid dynamics behind these effects","text":"I think a better title would be \"Hall Thrusters Understood So Well Better Instruments Needed to Measure the Friction of Quanta\" - If something has momentum, it has mass. - If something isn't leaving an engine now, it's condensing. - Electromagnetic fields can recruit and organize a conductors' permissively and permeability. Hall Thrusters use electromagnetic confinement. - If there is a delay in exhaust, there is condensation. If there is condensation without a holding tank then there is a pulse holding the condensation. - Pulse waves require detonating fuel at the right time to feed the pulse and never feed the lull. Also, detonating is... different. Careful control of timing is needed to maintain a pulse. Finer detection is needed for tuning the pulse. It will take work to understand where this tiny detonating mass has friction in its electromagnetic confinement. - Hall Thrusters once understand more can possible be tuned to run with, dipole, quadrupole... tuned pulse waves. A Hall Thruster is a \"smaller\" version of this [VIDEO] however, the fluid dynamics of the Hall Thruster have interactions at the Quanta maybe so those will be that hardest to detect and analyze. But, once recorded and analyzed the knowledge should help fine tune these engines. Let me link this again because it is just a great presentation on the fluid dynamics behind these effects [VIDEO]"},{"id":28341361,"author":"chrispeel","month":"2021-08","story":"Japan tests a rotary detonation engine in space, big increase in fuel efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Shows multiple rotating pulses in an RDE at 5:50.","quote":"I see that one can have multiple rotating pulses in an RDE [1]","text":"Would an toroidal aerospike work with rotary detonation? :-) What sort of nozzles can one use with an RDE? I see that one can have multiple rotating pulses in an RDE [1]; I guess with some work the engine designer can control the number of pulses, is that right? Are there performance tradeoffs when deciding how many pulses to employ? What are they? If employing multiple pulses, does one want them rotating the same direction, or opposite directions? BTW, Scott Manley has an introductory video about RDEs [2]. [1] 5:50 in [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"aMnn0Jq0J-E":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as programming inspiration and as a source of Bentley's anecdotes about adding function by deleting code.","q":{"x":"If you're looking for programming inspiration I love these videos","i":37248336,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37248336,"author":"tonym128","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Where do I find good code to read?","stance":"rec","why":"Programming inspiration through beautiful quicksort implementations","quote":"If you're looking for programming inspiration I love these videos","text":"I'm sorry these are videos, but your question made me think of these. If you're looking for programming inspiration I love these videos Jon Bentley - Three Beautiful Quicksorts [VIDEO] Bret Victor - Inventing on Principle [LINK]"},{"id":5682484,"author":"erre","month":"2013-05","story":"A regular expression matcher By Rob Pike and Brian Kernighan (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Bentley's Google Tech Talk on quicksort","quote":"there's a (also very interesting) Google Tech Talk","text":"Ah, and Jon Bentley's chapter about quicksort, along whose lines there's a (also very interesting) Google Tech Talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1114356,"author":"ehsanul","month":"2010-02","story":"-2000 Lines Of Code","stance":"reference","why":"Bentley quoting praise for adding function by deleting code","quote":"I'm reminded of this Google Tech Talk, where the speaker (Jon Bentley) refers to a colleague's praise of someone who","text":"I'm reminded of this Google Tech Talk, where the speaker (Jon Bentley) refers to a colleague's praise of someone who \"adds function by deleting code\": [VIDEO] Edit: Fixed quote"}]},"i_qE1iAmjFg":{"wy":"A classic recommendation among JavaScript talks and for reading real library code to learn patterns.","q":{"x":"There is also a great presentation by Paul Irish","i":2466172,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2466172,"author":"jarek-foksa","month":"2011-04","story":"Code which every programmer must read before dying","stance":"rec","why":"Great presentation on patterns from the jQuery source","quote":"There is also a great presentation by Paul Irish","text":"If you are fronted web developer then you should defenitely read jQuery sources. There is a lot of patterns that you could borrow and reuse in your own JS libraries. A good place to start is this interactive code viewer: [LINK] There is also a great presentation by Paul Irish (\"10 Things I Learned from the jQuery Source\"): [VIDEO]"},{"id":2476220,"author":"geuis","month":"2011-04","story":"Tell HN: Will answer your javascript questions for the next few hours","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of interesting techniques in jQuery","quote":"This is Paul Irish giving a breakdown of interesting bits about jQuery.","text":"Take a look at this. [VIDEO] This is Paul Irish giving a breakdown of interesting bits about jQuery. He talks about this technique roughly 50 minutes in."},{"id":2164495,"author":"niyazpk","month":"2011-02","story":"JQuery Deconstructed","stance":"rec","why":"Paul Irish's talk of jQuery source lessons","quote":"I found these videos by Paul Irish very interesting too","text":"I found these videos by Paul Irish very interesting too: 10 Things I Learned from the jQuery Source : [VIDEO] 11 more Things I Learned from the jQuery Source : [LINK]"},{"id":7014188,"author":"tangue","month":"2014-01","story":"Wonderful Javascript Talks?","stance":"reference","why":"Classic talk listed among JavaScript talks","quote":"Paul Irish : 10 things I learned from the Jquery source","text":"Classics John Resig : The Dom is a mess [LINK] Javascript, The Good Parts [LINK] Speed up your Javascript [LINK] Brendan Eich : Fluent 2013: Brendan Eich, \"JavaScript at 18: Legal to Gamble\" [LINK] Paul Irish : 10 things I learned from the Jquery source [VIDEO] Less known but very interesting Building High-Performing JavaScript for Modern Engines [LINK]"}]},"rTawFwUvnLE":{"wy":"Recommended as Hinton's own explanation of capsule networks and his critique of pooling in CNNs.","q":{"x":"Hinton explains the concept of capsules in this video","i":15609898,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15609898,"author":"dr_zoidberg","month":"2017-11","story":"A New Twist on Neural Networks","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton explains capsules in detail","quote":"Hinton explains the concept of capsules in this video","text":"Hinton explains the concept of capsules in this video: [VIDEO] Which is a lot better than reading someone tell you about this new idea that is called \"capsules\" but doesn't go into detail. The only thing is that, when this presentation was given, it seems they hadn't worked much more than MNIST (so the new thing now would be the toys-recognition net). Better source, with date: [LINK] (December 2014, for the lazy)."},{"id":15370542,"author":"bra-ket","month":"2017-09","story":"Is AI Riding a One-Trick Pony?","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton on capsule model based on shape perception psychology","quote":"Prof. Hinton has an interesting talk about his new 'capsule' model based on psychology of shape perception","text":"Prof. Hinton has an interesting talk about his new 'capsule' model based on psychology of shape perception: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16612779,"author":"nabla9","month":"2018-03","story":"Deciphering China’s AI Dream","stance":"reference","why":"Hinton's earlier critique of convolutional nets","quote":"Hinton made his \"What is wrong with convolutional neural nets\" speech years ago","text":"I'm certain that AI business will plateau and there will be yet another AI winter without new big breakthroughs in fundamental research. We are currently living in a era where the results from Hinton & Canadian Mafia are still begin adopted and refined. It's just alternating layers of affine transformations and nonlinearity with lots of tricks and improved routing. There needs to be fundamental theoretical breakthrough in every decade or so, and not just refinements and new applications. Hinton made his \"What is wrong with convolutional neural nets\" speech years ago \" [VIDEO] I believe that Hinton's capsule networks type attempts are what we need more. They may not deliver immediately impressive results because fundamental research involves lots of setbacks."},{"id":15630483,"author":"giardini","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite Machine Learning topic?","stance":"reference","why":"Hinton's capsule networks talk","quote":"Hinton's \"capsule networks","text":"Hinton's \"capsule networks\": [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] They sound a bit like \"grandmother neurons/cells\": [LINK] I would very much appreciate an ELI5 of capsules and some prognostications about how capsules may affect the state of the art in neural networks and the current flood of papers and graduate students in the pipeline of ML."},{"id":15525735,"author":"uoaei","month":"2017-10","story":"BadNets: Identifying vulnerabilities in the machine learning model supply chain","stance":"reference","why":"Hinton's ideas on pooling problems in CNNs","quote":"G. Hinton has some ideas though they aren't very fleshed out yet.","text":"I have a hypothesis that these errors in interpretation stem mainly from the max pooling layers that occurs in nearly all CNNs today. Simply cutting out a large fraction (at least 75%!) of the information passing through each pooling layer is making the models quite brittle. That, and obviously it would be a big help to be able to interpret meaningful symbols (e.g. words) from the images rather than just recognize a certain arbitrary class of pattern activations. That is pretty hard, of course, but IMO essential for the upcoming self-driving car revolution, if it is to succeed. G. Hinton has some ideas though they aren't very fleshed out yet.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"swQbA4zub20":{"wy":"It is the go-to reference whenever HN discusses cell-based architecture and outage isolation, as an explanation straight from AWS engineers.","q":{"x":"This YouTube video[1] of a re:invent presentation does a great job of explaining it.","i":25237836,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25237836,"author":"talawahtech","month":"2020-11","story":"Summary of the Amazon Kinesis Event in the Northern Virginia (US-East-1) Region","stance":"rec","why":"Explains cell-based architecture; cell section starts around minute 20.","quote":"This YouTube video[1] of a re:invent presentation does a great job of explaining it.","text":"Multi-AZ doesn't protect against a software/OS issue like this, Multi-AZ would be relevant if it was an infrastructure failure (e.g. underlying EC2 instances or networking). The relevant resiliency pattern in this case would be what they refer to as cell-based architecture, where within an AZ services are broken down into smaller independent cells to minimize the blast radius. They specifically mention in the write-up that this was a gap they plan to address, the \"backend\" portion of Kinesis was already cellularized but that step had not yet been completed on the \"frontend\". Celluarization in combination with workload partitioning would have helped, e.g. don't run Cloudwatch, Cognito and Customer workloads on the same set of cells. It is also important to note that celluarization only helps in this case if they limit code deployment to a limited number of cells at a time. This YouTube video[1] of a re:invent presentation does a great job of explaining it. The cell-based stuff, starts around minute 20. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19291163,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-03","story":"An introduction to distributed systems (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Vosshall presents cell-based design in use at AWS.","quote":"Peter Vosshall (co-creator of Dynamo [5]) presenting \"cell-based\" design in-use at AWS.","text":"This is a great outline. Martin Kleppmann's Designing Data Intensive Applications [0] is very much in the same domain as what's in these notes. Speaking about scale and distributed systems: AWS specific resources I find interesting from their EdgeEngineering/NetEng teams (I haven't seen many other service teams in AWS openly share as much about design as them): - [LINK] series of articles on Route53's 100% data-plane availability architecture [1]. - [LINK] colmmacc [2] (seems to be the eng behind AWS HyperPlane [3][4]?) on 10 design patterns for building resilient systems. - [VIDEO] Peter Vosshall (co-creator of Dynamo [5]) presenting \"cell-based\" design in-use at AWS. ---- [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] Decandia, G.; Hastorun, D.; Jampani, M.; Kakulapati, G.; Lakshman, A.; Pilchin, A.; Sivasubramanian, S.; Vosshall, P.; Vogels, W. (2007). \"Dynamo: Amazon's Highly Available Key-value Store\". [6] Bonus: The SRE Book [LINK]"},{"id":19394825,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-03","story":"Post-mortem of March 12 Google outage","stance":"rec","why":"Explains AWS cells, relevant to resiliency in distributed systems.","quote":"I encourage you to view the re:invent presentation on it [0] if resiliency in distributed systems is something that interests you.","text":"From what you describe the 'AWS cells' are very different than Borg's. My view is that at a high level aws cells are isolated, fuzzy-partitioned, migratable, heterogeneous instances of a complete service stack that are managed such that they always fail independently of each other; contact points among these cell instances, if required, are established via highly available intermediate services (like ReplicatedStateMachinesOrStorage); the clients are routed to assigned cells by a highly available routing layer (like NetworkTrafficShapers/DNS), which can also help facilitate migration of clients from one/more cell to another. I encourage you to view the re:invent presentation on it [0] if resiliency in distributed systems is something that interests you. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37276114,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2023-08","story":"Slack’s Migration to a Cellular Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as background for cellular architecture.","quote":"How AWS minimizes the blast radius of failures","text":"ex-AWS here May be marketing but it is an architecture born out of Amazon's (and AWS's) use of AWS: - Reliable scalability: How Amazon.com scales in the cloud , [LINK] (2022) - How AWS minimizes the blast radius of failures , [VIDEO] (2018) For massive enterprise products like Slack that need close to 100% uptime across all their services, cells make sense."},{"id":35178002,"author":"faizshah","month":"2023-03","story":"Fly.io outage, recently deployed apps down, deployments disabled","stance":"reference","why":"AWS limits blast radius via AZ isolation and cellular architecture.","quote":"AWS invested a ton into limiting the blast radius of failures","text":"AWS invested a ton into limiting the blast radius of failures by isolating AZs, regions and using a cellular (service level isolated shards) architecture. I am surprised these ideas have not propagated to newer companies trying to build clouds: [VIDEO] AWS isn’t perfect but these lessons were learned by fire because these sorts of global outages can seriously harm reputations."},{"id":32026071,"author":"708145_","month":"2022-07","story":"Cell-Based Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Amazon has discussed cells for years.","quote":"The concept have been mentioned by Amazon for years.","text":"The concept have been mentioned by Amazon for years. For reference see: \"AWS re:Invent 2018: How AWS Minimizes the Blast Radius of Failures\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25237584,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-11","story":"Summary of the Amazon Kinesis Event in the Northern Virginia (US-East-1) Region","stance":"reference","why":"Background on cellularization of fleets.","quote":"","text":"root-cause tldr: ...[adding] new capacity [to the front-end fleet] had caused all of the servers in the [front-end] fleet to exceed the maximum number of threads allowed by an operating system configuration [number of threads spawned is directly proportional to number of servers in the fleet]. As this limit was being exceeded, cache construction was failing to complete and front-end servers were ending up with useless shard-maps that left them unable to route requests to back-end clusters. fixes: ...moving to larger CPU and memory servers [and thus fewer front-end servers]. Having fewer servers means that each server maintains fewer threads. ...making a number of changes to radically improve the cold-start time for the front-end fleet. ...moving the front-end server [shard-map] cache [that takes a long time to build, up to an hour sometimes?] to a dedicated fleet. ...move a few large AWS services, like CloudWatch, to a separate, partitioned front-end fleet. ...accelerate the cellularization [0] of the front-end fleet to match what we’ve done with the back-end. [0] [VIDEO] and [LINK]"},{"id":22213803,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What were the things you did that made the biggest impact at your work?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the S3 outage from a flipped bit.","quote":"","text":"In my time writing databases, I found that not only the mount-flags could result in data-loss despite fsync, given you use fsync as it is supposed to be used [0], but even enterprise-grade hardware would at times (at that scale [1]) drop and/or corrupt a few bits here and there. That reminds me of the case where AWS famously revealed that a single NIC in their ginaromous S3 fleet flipped a single bit once in a while and that caused an outage because their gossip-daemon responsible for fleet health-checks failed spectacularly [2]. Bryan Cantrill's talk on the realted topic of hardware/firmware bugs is pretty good [3]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":19393407,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-03","story":"Post-mortem of March 12 Google outage","stance":"reference","why":"AWS cells isolate failures within regions.","quote":"","text":"> [LINK] > Almost all updates to Google’s services proceed gradually, according to a defined process, with appropriate verification steps interspersed. [..] The first stages of a rollout are usually called \"canaries” — an allusion to canaries carried by miners into a coal mine to detect dangerous gases. Our canary servers detect dangerous effects from the behavior of the new software under real user traffic. > Canary testing is a concept embedded into many of Google’s internal tools used to make automated changes, as well as for systems that change configuration files. [..] If the change doesn’t pass the validation period, it’s automatically rolled back. Any Googlers around to explain why Canaries didn't catch configuration causing this side effect and stopping the deployment before the issue cascaded globally? The outage here reminds me of issues CloudFront faced wrt configurations in their earlier years. Here's master presenter Harvo Jones going into a bit more detail: [LINK] Kind of vindicates AWS' decision (after major outages to DynamoDB and S3) to not just isolate regions or zones but resources within those regions/zones further into what they call \"cells\" [VIDEO] Tumblr spoke abt \"cells\" too in the context of achieving isolation at scale, but in a quite different way: [LINK] As these known-knowns (and the Google sre-book is teeming with these) get designed around, it'd be interesting to see why/how future outages would happen."}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1]]},"uI1eRy0uBI8":{"wy":"HN points to it in battery-fire threads as a rare look at what is inside a Li-ion cell and how dangerous disassembly really is.","q":{"x":"Big Clive does a really amazing (and dangerous) tear down of what's inside a Lithium Ion cell","i":13463956,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13745986,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-02","story":"My 2015 MacBook Pro Retina Exploded","stance":"rec","why":"Safe teardown of a Li-ion cell","quote":"Big Clive successfully took one apart without it going boomb if anyone is interested in what's inside of them","text":"EgyptAir is investigating if a phone or tablet may have led to a crash of one of their jets: [LINK] There have been many devices that have come under scrutiny for their Li-Ion cells. At least few devices explode, and I'm not aware of any killing people, but many have had these meltdowns. Big Clive successfully took one apart without it going boomb if anyone is interested in what's inside of them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13463956,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-01","story":"Galaxy Note7: What We Discovered","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what the parts of a Li-ion cell look like","quote":"Big Clive does a really amazing (and dangerous) tear down of what's inside a Lithium Ion cell","text":"Big Clive does a really amazing (and dangerous) tear down of what's inside a Lithium Ion cell, in case anyone wants to see what the parts in the diagram physically look like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40776053,"author":"kees99","month":"2024-06","story":"Fire at South Korea lithium battery plant kills at least 16 people","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for low ignition chance on disassembly","quote":"","text":"Primary, non-rechargeable lithium batteries typically contain metal lithium [0], and are actually more likely to catch fire when mishandled, compared to Li-ion stuff. Specifically, most common Li-ion fires start when overcharged (especially with high current and in cold), and from short-circuits (e.g. when pierced). But only have a very small chance of spontaneously igniting from just disassembly alone [1]. Still non-zero chance, don't open them! Primary/metal lithium batteries, on the other hand, are much more likely to burst into flames when opened. Notably, lithium-iron disulfide (AA/AAA \"alkaline replacement\") cells are notorious to do that just from air exposure, even if one is very careful to not short/pierce anything. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12681632,"author":"lgeek","month":"2016-10","story":"Samsung Galaxy Note 7 owners told to turn off device","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown showing internal battery structure","quote":"Here's a teardown","text":"> The thinner the battery, the closer the positive and negative terminals are. Absolutely false. All lithium ion batteries are made of 4 paper thin layers: two electrodes and two separators. These are cut in narrow strips and folded on themselves many times over, even in a thin battery. A thinner battery will simply have fewer folds. The distance between the two electrodes is given by the thickness of the separators and it has nothing to do with the thickness of the cell. Here's a teardown: [VIDEO] (possibly NSFW-ish language)."}],"r":[["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1]]},"uqY3FMuMuRo":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible, educational build that anyone with basic logic knowledge can follow.","q":{"x":"Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch.","i":22330486,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25283568,"author":"chrisdalke","month":"2020-12","story":"NandGame – Build a Computer from Scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Educational DIY VGA video card build","quote":"Super educational","text":"If you're a visual learner, check out Ben Eater's channel: [LINK] He has a really great video series where he builds a small computer from scratch on breadboards. Also some other highlights: Reliable data transmission, more educational: [LINK] DIY VGA \"video card\": [VIDEO] Super educational, but if you want to get into more embedded hardware hacking, I would say that building a homemade computer like he does is probably not a super exciting place to start. I'd recommend picking up some Teensy 4.0 microcontrollers and build some small hardware projects with the microcontroller before diving into lower-level circuitry."},{"id":22330486,"author":"MarioMan","month":"2020-02","story":"Brown: color is weird [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Building a VGA video card from basic logic circuits","quote":"Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch.","text":"Not the same thing, but you reminded me of this since it's in a similar vein. Ben Eater went through the entire process of building a VGA \"video card.\" Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch. Part 1: [LINK] Part 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20621833,"author":"cabaalis","month":"2019-08","story":"Let's Build a Video Card","stance":"reference","why":"Ben Eater's DIY VGA card, possibly linking to his breadboard computer","quote":"","text":"At 3:00 into the second video, he talks about hooking it into a computer. [1] It does a quick flash to what might be his breadboard computer. So I think he intends to connect this to the computer he has built. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1],["qd_hEja6bzE",1],["SH3lR2GLgT0",1]]},"vU1-uiUlHTo":{"wy":"HN points to it as in-depth independent coverage of Flock license-plate cameras and their security and surveillance problems.","q":{"x":"I recommend them.","i":47690241,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47690241,"author":"gorgonical","month":"2026-04","story":"US cities are axing Flock Safety surveillance technology","stance":"rec","why":"Series on Flock camera safety and local government ties.","quote":"Benn Jordan is making a very interesting series of videos about Flock cameras","text":"Musician-turning-tech anarchist (?) Benn Jordan is making a very interesting series of videos about Flock cameras, their poor safety, and their gray-area interfacing with local governments: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] I recommend them."},{"id":46407595,"author":"john01dav","month":"2025-12","story":"How we lost communication to entertainment","stance":"rec","why":"Recent example of in-depth independent coverage on Flock cameras.","quote":"recent good example","text":"> I feel there has to be something between \"I heard about a thing 7th-hand\" and \"I actively watch political discourse / read scientific papers\", but I'm no longer sure The News, as we currently know it, is it. I have found that some Youtube channels and videos (non-comprehensive examples below (I have hundreds of subscribed channels), mostly not politics, but these things inform politics since politics is making decisions about other things) can fill this gap nicely. This is not a perfect choice, since journalism integrity and standards do not apply, but I find that this can be mitigated by watching a wide variety (for example, in the field of economics, I regularly watch creators who espouse everything from very free-market capitalism all the way to full on communism). There are likely other forms of new media that operate at this level of depth, but I haven't found htem. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] (and associated channels) [LINK] (recent good example [VIDEO] )"},{"id":48349679,"author":"kumarski","month":"2026-05","story":"Deflock hits 100k ALPRs Mapped in USA","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of the vulnerability and unencrypted video.","quote":"","text":"That's not my problem. Why wasn't he fired after the vulnerability was revealed? [VIDEO] Does IDF service mean their position is immune to valid criticism of the job? How do we know as Americans that it's secure when the individual who is senior and leading connectivity has likely served a foreign intelligence agency/millitary? Is this not alarming as videos weren't encrypted and public?"},{"id":47218217,"author":"filoeleven","month":"2026-03","story":"Everett shuts down Flock camera network after judge rules footage public record","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Flock cameras panning to track people.","quote":"You can see one panning up to track other people near the end of this Benn Jordan video on the cameras.","text":"Flock cameras are advertised as ALPR but have facial/vehicle/etc recognition systems running on them as well. You can see one panning up to track other people near the end of this Benn Jordan video on the cameras. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Pp9MwZkHiMQ",1],["uB0gr7Fh6lY",1]]},"HllUoT_WE7Y":{"wy":"Cited for insight into the hard realities of Linux input handling and libinput's thinly staffed maintenance.","q":{"x":"One of the folks behind both Wayland and X gave a fantastic talk on the issues and the hell.","i":41385486,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41385486,"author":"indrora","month":"2024-08","story":"Window Maker: X11 window manager with the look and feel of the NeXTSTEP UI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on the problems with X input handling","quote":"One of the folks behind both Wayland and X gave a fantastic talk on the issues and the hell.","text":"There are some advantages and disadvantages of the approach. However, there's just stuff that X assumes that isn't true anymore. A good example is how it handles input. How many cursors does a screen have? One machine? okay, One input device? One of the folks behind both Wayland and X gave a fantastic talk on the issues and the hell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29563073,"author":"5e92cb50239222b","month":"2021-12","story":"Linux Touchpad Like MacBook Update: Touchpad Gestures Now Shipping","stance":"reference","why":"Backs claim that libinput's developer worked alone","quote":"He asked for help numerous times and never received any.","text":"That \"dev\" you're criticizing here has been developing libinput solo since the beginning. He asked for help numerous times and never received any. So either step up and do the work, or stop complaining. [VIDEO] libinput is yet another of those fundamental libraries that receive almost no developer attention, but get all the blame when something goes wrong: [LINK]"},{"id":29173079,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2021-11","story":"“This project will only take 2 hours”","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote on how much work hiding complexity takes","quote":"","text":"Yeah it takes a LOT of work to hide it [1] Also: For some stuff I want to be able to use the \"arcane\" parameters for some really obscure task where it is just the right flag or something. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25413545,"author":"scaladev","month":"2020-12","story":"Linux 5.10","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about libinput's solo maintainer and input devices","quote":"libinput has a bus factor of one","text":"The whole usermode side of input handling depends on a project (libinput) that's been developed by a single guy over the last decade. He asked for help numerous times, reminding everyone that libinput has a bus factor of one, and received none. So either go ahead and help, or stop complaining. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cj02_UeUnGQ",1]]},"XZGiVzIr8Qg":{"wy":"Cited as a famous, pointed example of Bowie challenging MTV's exclusion of black music, in threads on media gatekeeping.","q":{"x":"There's a famous 1983 interview bit of David Bowie where he asked the MTV VJ why they play no black music","i":31255724,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":28087754,"author":"throwaway2037","month":"2021-08","story":"Launch HN: BlackOakTV (YC S21) – Netflix for black people","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bowie questions MTV about excluding black artists.","quote":"It blew me away. He is so on point!","text":"You wrote: <<<MTV hardly played black artists.>>> I saw this interview recently: <<<David Bowie Criticizes MTV for Not Playing Videos by Black Artists | MTV News>> [VIDEO] It blew me away. He is so on point!"},{"id":28112071,"author":"donohoe","month":"2021-08","story":"The first two hours of MTV (1981) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Bowie interview on MTV's lack of black artists.","quote":"Worth watching","text":"No black singers or bands you'll notice... Worth watching: \"David Bowie Criticizes MTV for Not Playing Videos by Black Artists | MTV News\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43875738,"author":"brudgers","month":"2025-05","story":"When Americana doesn't mean American","stance":"reference","why":"Shows MTV rationalizing its lack of black artists.","quote":"You can see MTV’s “string of black faces” rationalizing in the Bowie interview","text":"You can see MTV’s “string of black faces” rationalizing in the Bowie interview: [VIDEO] Wikipedia’s article whitewashes the actual history. We were watching MTV and smoking weed in those Reagan’s America days. — In fairness the US music industry had the race music chart which became the R&B chart to keep black musicians off the pop chart and Elvis’s controversy was singing black music…and consequently being played on white radio stations to white kids."},{"id":31255724,"author":"a_bonobo","month":"2022-05","story":"Pop culture has become an oligopoly","stance":"reference","why":"1983 Bowie interview excerpt about MTV avoiding black music.","quote":"There's a famous 1983 interview bit of David Bowie where he asked the MTV VJ why they play no black music","text":">Be very wary of survivor bias when looking back in time, especially when there is a risk of nostalgia. Case in point: MTV of the 80s would play little to no black music. There's a famous 1983 interview bit of David Bowie where he asked the MTV VJ why they play no black music, and the VJ waffles a bit on the white suburban market. That does not sound very subversive to me. Here's the excerpt: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["1k4x9FrD5k4",1],["UE-k4hYHIDE",1]]},"aYT2se94eU0":{"wy":"HN regards it as a must-watch that gives a very different perspective on software and the web, and its title is widely riffed on.","q":{"x":"if you have never watched this talk completely at least once, give it a shot and you might be very surprised.","i":25491451,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25491451,"author":"gjvc","month":"2020-12","story":"The web is 30 years old today","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Alan Kay's 1997 keynote offering a different point of view on the web.","quote":"if you have never watched this talk completely at least once, give it a shot and you might be very surprised.","text":"\"this has to be one of the worst ideas since MS-DOS..\" If you can spare three and a half minutes (start time set to the relevant point) to watch the video [1]from 1997 below... [VIDEO] ...then you might enjoy the different point of view as discussed here: [LINK] It comes up on HN from time to time, but I thought an alternative source of discussion (second link above) might add something new. [1] if you have never watched this talk completely at least once, give it a shot and you might be very surprised."},{"id":47328782,"author":"justin66","month":"2026-03","story":"Turing Award winner and former Oxford professor Tony Hoare passed away","stance":"reference","why":"Original speech where Kay said the famous line.","quote":"you could listen to the speech where he famously said it.","text":"> Kay was probably riffing off of that. You don't need to hypothesize about all this, to put things in their proper context you could listen to the speech where he famously said it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28671393,"author":"gjvc","month":"2021-09","story":"We deserve better than Confluence and Notion","stance":"reference","why":"Title riffed on for the content revolution not having happened.","quote":"The content revolution hasn't happened yet!\" [1] :-) [1] with apologies to Alan Kay,","text":"The depressing irony of all this is that despite the massive advances in technology in software and hardware, this kind of tooling, specifically document/content/knowledge/issue management has remained stuck in its containing medium, be that word documents on a shared drive, or textareas on a web page (as I am typing in right now!) It seems that smalltalk-like systems, and derivatives such as the lively kernel contain clues as to what a unified meta-interface to an individual's or organisation's content might look like (and how it might behave). Integration and user adoption is a difficult problem (in general) though -- probably the best example of this is the poor (and in some circles, pretty vile) criticism that Google Wave received, though this type of feedback is probably due in part to a lack of understanding of the problem being solved. \"The content revolution hasn't happened yet!\" [1] :-) [1] with apologies to Alan Kay, [VIDEO]"},{"id":26928437,"author":"NotPavlovsDog","month":"2021-04","story":"William Gibson Predicts in 1997 How the Internet Will Change Our World (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Kay's talk cited as more relevant than ever.","quote":"Alan Kay's \"the computer revolution has not happened yet\" is more relevant today than ever [1].","text":"It might all still go the way of the TV [0]. We've been seeing the demise of general computing in real time. The current generation may be the last that got to pop the hood on the internal workings of the Internet, from inspecting HTML source of a web-page to disassembly of whatever was running it, if source code was not available (or at least port scanning and possible stack bashing). With corporations doing their best to remove that ability, and consumers gladly buying curated experience boxes, it all might culminate with an encapsulated silo park selling all you can eat broadcasting (think one-way directed stream, with consumer only getting to send back analytics). So in a way, Gibson got very many things right, maybe even the core thing. I remember thinking in the 90's \"Oh, he got it so wrong, the Internet changes everything\". Hah. Alan Kay's \"the computer revolution has not happened yet\" is more relevant today than ever [1]. It's up to us to change that. [0]I think JWZ (Jaimie Zawinski) spoke about that in \"Coders at work\", can't find the exact source. [1]PDF: [LINK] There's also a youtube video of his presentation [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["yJDv-zdhzMY",1]]},"iY57ErBkFFE":{"wy":"Quoted in privacy and surveillance debates, and praised as some of the best science fiction writing in games.","q":{"x":"The special projects videos in that game are some of the best science fiction creative work ever made, IMO.","i":22753667,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22753667,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-04","story":"The Collective Body: Russian experiments in life after death","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Alpha Centauri project videos as top sci-fi writing, with a prescient quote","quote":"The special projects videos in that game are some of the best science fiction creative work ever made, IMO.","text":"The special projects videos in that game are some of the best science fiction creative work ever made, IMO. Also, something I still think is predictive and sends chills up my neck, \"as the Americans painfully learned in Earth's final century\" (presumably the 21st): [VIDEO] Text of the whole spoken: \"As the Americans learned so painfully in Earth's final century, free flow of information is the only safeguard against tyranny. The once-chained people whose leaders at last lose their grip on information flow will soon burst with freedom and vitality, but the free nation gradually constricting its grip on public discourse has begun its rapid slide into despotism. Beware of he who would deny you access to information, for in his heart he dreams himself your master.\" -- Commissioner Pravin Lal, \"U.N. Declaration of Rights\"\" All special projects video text, starting with 0-24: [LINK] Cutscene vids start here: [LINK] Although a bit dated, I still don't see this kind of intellectual quality in most games. Paradox Interactive's Stellaris is about the closest thing to a spiritual successor to SMAC, but it's also its own beast and next-gen 4X."},{"id":17304974,"author":"GauntletWizard","month":"2018-06","story":"Recovery of Lost Indigenous Languages by Optical Scanning of Old Wax Cylinders","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes Alpha Centauri on free information flow to argue against secrecy","quote":"Sid Meier's Alpha Centauri","text":"\"As the Americans learned so painfully in Earth's final century, free flow of information is the only safeguard against tyranny. The once-chained people whose leaders at last lose their grip on information flow will soon burst with freedom and vitality, but the free nation gradually constricting its grip on public discourse has begun its rapid slide into despotism. Beware of he who would deny you access to information, for in his heart he dreams himself your master.\" - Sid Meier's Alpha Centauri ( [VIDEO] ) What was contained in those cermonies that you were not to know? What offense would he do to his people by sharing their wisdom with you? What didn't they want you to know? The answer is \"Probably nothing\". But in that vast empty space, there's a few corners where someone is hiding something, and those corners are really terrible. Look at cults like Children of God [1] - These cults are still really common. I'm certain you would have reported if you'd heard anything dangerous on those tapes. I'm certain that your friends motive for keeping them private were not threatening and entirely pure. Societal standards - Like rituals and ceremonies - Aren't private, though, and should never be subject to secrecy. They should be held up and celebrated. And if they shouldn't be held up and celebrated, they should probably be left in the past, and remembered as mistakes. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":5836166,"author":"Afforess","month":"2013-06","story":"US intelligence mining data from 9 US Internet companies in broad secret program","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the quote about information freedom, from Alpha Centauri","quote":"The quote is in reference to the government. It's from a video game, which came out in 1999.","text":"> This seems a rather inappropriate aphorism, since most people here would very much like to deny the NSA access to information about their phone usage. This is an argument against censorship rather than one in support of privacy. I have no idea how you interpreted it that way. The quote is in reference to the government. It's from a video game, which came out in 1999. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["OINAk2xl8Bc",1],["24OXzIRIiMQ",1],["p_iZr2-Coqc",1]]},"tx082gDwGcM":{"wy":"Recommended as a great technical talk from the pioneer of human computation.","q":{"x":"Luis von Ahn's talk on \"Human Computation\" was great","i":5516197,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5516197,"author":"bemmu","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Luis von Ahn's great talk on human computation.","quote":"Luis von Ahn's talk on \"Human Computation\" was great","text":"I remember there being a better version of this, but Luis von Ahn's talk on \"Human Computation\" was great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19792343,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2019-04","story":"Launch HN: Centaur Labs (YC W19) – Labeling Medical Images at Scale","stance":"reference","why":"Creator explains locating key points in images.","quote":"In this talk, the creator also goes into how they created a version to actually locate the key points in the image","text":"I would think you could go a long way with just creating an ESP style game: [LINK] In this talk, the creator also goes into how they created a version to actually locate the key points in the image: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9693119,"author":"rawnlq","month":"2015-06","story":"Duolingo Raises $45M, Now Valued at $470M","stance":"reference","why":"Von Ahn pioneered human computation field.","quote":"He pioneered the field of human computation","text":"Duolingo is started by Luis von Ahn who invented reCAPTCHA. He pioneered the field of human computation: [VIDEO] . He can monetize this in a similar way. Crowd sourced translation service where the more advanced language learners translate books or something. It will be higher quality than google translate for sure."}],"r":[["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["p83QUZ1-P10",1]]},"3UxZDJ1HiPE":{"wy":"Used to show what Kiva-style warehouse automation actually looks like: simple wheeled robots moving racks to humans.","q":{"x":"Most are Kiva machines, moving storage racks to human pickers. Here's what that looks like.[2]","i":19902358,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":8777574,"author":"Animats","month":"2014-12","story":"A Day in the Life of a Kiva Robot (2011) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Kiva picking station, with humans guided by laser pointer.","quote":"Here's a more useful video of Kiva robots","text":"Here's a more useful video of Kiva robots: [VIDEO] This one shows the picking station, where the humans take things out of bins and put them in other bins. A computer-controlled laser pointer points to the item to be taken out, and a light shows where the item goes, and a bar code scanner checks on the human. The job takes about two minutes to learn, and full productivity for new humans is achieved in about half an hour. There is no possibility of promotion. Machines should think. People should work. Kiva is a huge success. Before Amazon bought them, they had about 20% of online order fulfillment in the US. It's higher now, with Amazon using them. Kiva is so successful because it's so simple to install. All it needs is a big flat floor with some bar code stickers, a supply of cheap shelving units, and the robots. All the robots are small and interchangeable, so they don't have to be repaired on-site and there's no need for expensive on-site technicians and repair shops. So converting to Kiva robots is fast, cheap, and easy. Automated warehousing isn't new, but it used to be a lot more complicated and far more custom. The older systems involved conveyors, machines that moved on tracks, extensive site-specific and product-specific engineering, and good onsite maintenance. Here's a state of the art version of a classic automated storage and retrieval system in a frozen foods warehouse. (The frozen foods industry has been heavily into warehouse automation for decades, because the"},{"id":27529202,"author":"Clewza313","month":"2021-06","story":"Amazon executives are worried they'll run out of people to employ","stance":"reference","why":"Kiva robots as simple boxes on wheels.","quote":"the Kiva robots that Amazon actually uses are basically boxes on wheels.","text":"Boston Dynamics builds thinly disguised military robots that are meant to be very flexible and can operate in rough terrain (for 15 min or whatever until the battery runs out, but that's another story). They're hugely expensive literal overkill for a highly predictable and controlled environment like a warehouse, which is why the Kiva robots that Amazon actually uses are basically boxes on wheels. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19902358,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-05","story":"Amazon rolls out machines that pack orders and replace jobs","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Kiva robots bringing racks to human pickers.","quote":"Most are Kiva machines, moving storage racks to human pickers. Here's what that looks like.[2]","text":"This is a minor improvement in packaging. It's not automated picking, which will be a big deal if Amazon cracks that. They've been trying and failing for years now. Amazon gave up on their robotic picking challenge after 2017. Here's the winner of the last challenge.[1] Way too slow and not reliable enough. Amazon has about 100,000 robots now. Most are Kiva machines, moving storage racks to human pickers. Here's what that looks like.[2] The pickers stay in one place, take things out of bins brought to them, and put them in outgoing bins. A laser pointer tells them what to pick. That's almost 10 years old, but Amazon has not yet been able to replace humans in that job. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9026666,"author":"qznc","month":"2015-02","story":"Boston Dynamics' New Robot – Spot","stance":"reference","why":"Box-moving plate robots suffice for warehouses.","quote":"If you want to move boxes around, a moving plate is enough.","text":"Yes, anthropomorphic or zoomorphic robots are probably not the most efficient form. If you want to move boxes around, a moving plate is enough. Kiva Systems Warehouse Automation: [VIDEO] Moving through public space might require more than a plate for safety and mobility reasons."}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1]]},"6BBx8BwLhqg":{"wy":"Linked in threads on magnets and implants as a vivid demonstration of how violent MRI magnet forces are.","q":{"x":"Watch this video to see the forces involved","i":6603694,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6603694,"author":"Shinkei","month":"2013-10","story":"Towards an inexpensive open-source desktop CT scanner","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates the forces involved with MRI magnets","quote":"Watch this video to see the forces involved","text":"A topic I can speak intelligently about! (I am a Radiologist.) This article is a great discussion of modern imaging technology and I 100% agree that MRI is incredible. But CT has become much more impressive than the single-slice scanners of yore. Watch this video of a modern scanner rotating at full speed: [LINK] Currently, the race to improvement is about speed and lower radiation dose. The newest scanners can take hundreds of 2D slices in a single rotation of less than a second and reconstruct the image with a huge variety of algorithms accounting for body part, patient size, etc. that all allow for lower dose and noise resulting in better diagnostic quality. MRI is truly the cutting edge of human achievement. Watch this video to see the forces involved: [VIDEO] And this for a detailed explanation of MRI principles: [LINK] Medicine uses 1.5T and 3T routinely for imaging, but higher field strengths are in use for research purposes. Innovation lately has been about developing new sequences (ways to differentiate types of tissue), and increase speed of aquisiton and comfort."},{"id":36085441,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-05","story":"DARPA’s silent MHD magnetic drives for replacing naval propellers","stance":"reference","why":"Shows magnetic material being drawn into MRI magnets","quote":"magnetic material can be rapidly drawn towards the magnets","text":"\"Trans-cranial magnetic stimulation\" — it has both been called \"the god helmet\" and has been used in that way. IIRC it's a pulsed field to induce electric fields in your brain, and also has an extremely short range. Somewhat more significant to human health is that magnetic material can be rapidly drawn towards the magnets: [VIDEO] At very high field strengths, you also need to account for \"your blood is electrically conductive, and electrical conductors resist motion when in a magnetic field due to induced electrical currents\". According to test subjects, this \"tingles\". At the most extreme fields strengths we can currently make, you also have to account for the water in your body being diamagnetic and repelling the magnets, but we can't ask any of the test subjects what that feels like as they were a frog and a rat because that's all that would fit in the tube."},{"id":3734972,"author":"phren0logy","month":"2012-03","story":"Body Hacking: Thoughts Regarding My Magnet Implant","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates risks of strong MRI fields near metal","quote":"There's some people horsing around with 4T MRI that might give you pause.","text":"Obviously I have no first-hand experience with implanted magnets, but MRI magnets are generally getting stronger. What is safe for a 1.5 tesla MRI may not hold for a 3-4 tesla MRI. The rapid magnetic oscillation has been known to heat up wires and cause burns. There's some people horsing around with 4T MRI that might give you pause. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3380719,"author":"po","month":"2011-12","story":"Dropping a Magnet Through a Copper Pipe","stance":"reference","why":"Shows hazards of magnetic items near MRI magnets","quote":"Not exactly safe.","text":"They are superconducting magnets kept that way through cryogenic helium. They can lose their magnetism through a process called quenching. If you search on youtube for \"MRI quench\" there are several videos of people messing around with MRI's before they get decommissioned. Not exactly safe. [LINK] [VIDEO] There is a lot of helium in there and it can explode: [LINK]"}]},"9-NU7yOSElE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concrete example of Apple's hostility to repair and weak support for 'pro' hardware, mostly in right-to-repair debates.","q":{"x":"it's a very interesting series of videos","i":18186366,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18186366,"author":"slantyyz","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple Said to Have 'Dramatically Reduced' iPhone Repair Fraud in China","stance":"rec","why":"Linus's broken iMac Pro and Apple's refusal to repair it","quote":"it's a very interesting series of videos","text":"You should check out Linus Sebastian's experience with his broken iMac Pro screen. He wanted to pay for the repairs, and Apple refused. He then tried to buy the parts separately, and apparently if you got them from Apple, it would cost more than the computer was worth. He eventually managed to source the parts through various people at a reasonable cost and repair his broken iMac with Louis Rossman. Here are some of the videos (I'm sure I missed a couple, but it's a very interesting series of videos) [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":24395923,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2020-09","story":"The Right to Repair could help address a critical shortage in school computers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"device manufacturers are actually refusing to repair plenty of devices","text":"Oh boy, do you know that device manufacturers are actually refusing to repair plenty of devices - I am not talking about warranty shenanigans, but refusing to repair for any money at all: [VIDEO] I have taken my A50 to Samsung, and they are not fixing it no matter how much I pay them. On my Withings smartwatch the glass cracked, and was considered under warranty. They explicitly told me it is unrepairable, and replaced the watch with a new one! How the F k are they unable to replace glass on a watch? Just about everything has already became either unrepairable, or uneconomic to repair, from fridges to TVs to phones to smartwatches. The are a few exception like vehicles or large equipment, but the events so far indicate that people will buy unrepairable equipment because they have already done so in many industries. Consumers generally have no way to know how repairable something is, especially for a new product. Lastly, a device does not have to be completely unrepairable - it could require absurdly specialised and expensive equipment, that only the OEM will ever afford, so you will be back at the monopoly problem."},{"id":18007398,"author":"mg96226","month":"2018-09","story":"Apple now strives to design and build products that last as long as possible","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They are incredibly hostile to 3rd party repairers.","text":"No, it does not happen with the iPhone or most other Apple products. They are incredibly hostile to 3rd party repairers. Apple is notoriously draconian about ensuring that 3rd parties are blocked from accessing schematics and repair manuals. I'm talking, they threaten lawsuits to anyone publishing schematics, and there is no legal way to obtain those schematics unless you're an Apple authorized repairer - in which case you'll never need them because your job is just to replace entire logic boards after minimal diagnostics. This creates enormous waste and costs a lot more than an actual repair would. In some cases they don't even do that and just tell the user tough shit, get a new device. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17755070,"author":"cyphar","month":"2018-08","story":"Google tracks your movements, like it or not","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple devices aren't just hard to repair, Apple as a company is anti-repair","text":"> I'm an Apple fan because I don't need to repair the device Given that Apple has no data recovery service for any of their products (even if you tell them that you'll give them a limitless pile of cash) and that they solder storage into most of their devices (that is paired to the CPU -- so you cannot remove the flash chip and put it onto a working board to recover data), this is a very bold claim. Maybe you don't need your phone to be repaired when it is water damaged and you can no longer get photos of your children (that you should've backed up, but didn't). Maybe you want to pay US$300 to replace an out-of-warranty phone when the only thing wrong with it is that a headphone jack got stuck inside the port[1]. Maybe you don't want to pay more than the device is worth to fix a brand-new device[2]. Apple devices aren't just hard to repair, Apple as a company is anti-repair -- and defending this practice is not a reasonable position to hold. I understand arguing that Apple products are easier to use, or that they are more consumer-friendly, or that consumers shouldn't have full control over their devices because they'll probably brick them, and so on. But arguing that them being irreparable is a good thing is not justifiable (even if you don't need your device repaired, someone else will, and out-right ignoring that is misinformed). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17315212,"author":"ropeadopepope","month":"2018-06","story":"On the Sad State of Macintosh Hardware","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"What kind of pro computer doesn't come with top notch support?","text":"What kind of pro computer doesn't come with top notch support? \"Apple REFUSED to fix our iMac pro\" [VIDEO] \"The Apple Store Genius Bar broke my $5000 iMac pro\" [LINK]"}],"r":[["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1]]},"DoQoeK53bP8":{"wy":"Pointed to as a short demonstration of what incremental reading looks like in practice, though one commenter was not convinced by it.","q":{"x":"For a video on it in action check this [4]","i":23069213,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23069213,"author":"rajlego","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: How do you manage self-study?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows incremental reading in action","quote":"For a video on it in action check this [4]","text":"I have the perfect tool for you if you're willing to suffer through getting used to it: SuperMemo [1]. The basic feature it starts with is spaced repetition [2]. Spaced repetition is good. But terribly boring if you're making cards by hand. Because of that, SuperMemo has a feature that imo is just as important as SRS but much less known: incremental reading [3]. tl;dr of incremental reading: process hundreds of articles in a time efficient manner and enjoyably convert them to active recall items. For a video on it in action check this [4] More detail: incremental reading has a lot of parts but probably the most important is the priority queue and the concept of incrementally. Priority Queue: imagine you had 100 articles to go through. How would you process them efficiently? What if you have 10,000? You can't go through that reasonably. SuperMemo helps you manage that with a priority system which lets you choose what's actually important and what you'll end up seeing more and less often. I can't express just how much this helps. This means I can import as much shiny stuff as I want and be confident I'm not gonna lose out on stuff I actually care about. If you just used priority queue and manually made cards while going through things SM would be a big improvement over standard SRS. But it has another cool concept: incrementalism. With SuperMemo you don't read through an entire piece of content at once. You read it over time, and make extracts that you break down till you have "},{"id":18529732,"author":"xkfm","month":"2018-11","story":"Whether to Use Anki or SuperMemo","stance":"reference","why":"Incremental reading demo in SuperMemo","quote":"","text":"You can import entire articles into Supermemo and break them down into flashcards without leaving the program. Anki just gives you flashcards. There's an incremental reading plugin for Anki, but it doesn't work very well. Incremental reading works by breaking down an article into flashcards. Basically, you go: article -> paragraph -> sentences -> flashcards (usually with cloze deletions). But, because the algo manages when you see everything, I don't have to worry about doing all of that in one step. Polar Bookshelf (getpolarized.io) is more of an open source solution for archiving documents at the moment. You can export annotations/extracts from Polar into Anki, but you don't get the spaced repetition with the actual reading of articles like you do with Supermemo. I read a bit of the article, decide if making an extract of that portion is worth it, and then click next repetition when I'm done/bored reading it. I'll then get another article/flashcard/extract (of another article). You also have tools for marking up articles and saving your place. Every day I review around 30-50 flashcards, and 30-50 articles/extracts of articles. With Supermemo, I don't have to decide (or rather, I give up the ability to decide) what I'm going to read next; in favor of having everything scheduled by the algo. I go through about 800-1000 items in my RSS feeds once a day, save them into read it later/polar/bookmarks, and once a week, I throw the ones that still seem interesting into Supermemo. I"},{"id":28203433,"author":"SamBam","month":"2021-08","story":"To remember everything you learn, surrender to this algorithm (2008)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the video he has really doesn't sell it for me [1]","text":"I'm interested in incremental-reading, but the video he has really doesn't sell it for me [1] It seems like you skim through an article, extract a few facts, and add them to your memorization schedule. Would it really help you summarize the article, or help you synthesize the arguments and follow the logic leading to a conclusion? 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0VyH1laOd2M",1]]},"Ds-v2-qyCc8":{"wy":"Mentioned in threads on stroads and road safety as an example of the channel's critique of wasteful car infrastructure.","q":{"x":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively.","i":33768362,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33768362,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2022-11","story":"The Exceptionally American Problem of Rising Roadway Deaths","stance":"rec","why":"Not Just Bikes on wasteful car infrastructure","quote":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively.","text":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively. [LINK] Specific videos: Crossing the street should be safer: [LINK] How to quickly build a cycling city: [LINK] Wasting money on car infrastructure: [VIDEO] The wrong way to set speed limits: [LINK] Safe cycling: [LINK] \"Stroads\" are dangerous: [LINK]"},{"id":38307293,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-11","story":"280M e-bikes and mopeds are cutting demand for oil far more than electric cars","stance":"reference","why":"Video on Wonderland Road widening in Toronto","quote":"","text":"Haha, but it is though: [VIDEO] \"Throwing Good Money After Bad Car Infrastructure - Wonderland Road\" - on the road widening project of Wonderland Road in Toronto."},{"id":29304807,"author":"bo0tzz","month":"2021-11","story":"The Stroad","stance":"reference","why":"Similar comparison in a recent video","quote":"his recent video includes a similar comparison","text":"> I can't find the exact video right now I think that's probably because it was a Patreon exclusive ;) However, his recent video includes a similar comparison: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1]]},"JDaicPIgn9U":{"wy":"It is linked as the original short demonstration that keys can be pulled from RAM after shutdown.","q":{"x":"is a well-made 5-minute introduction by the authors.","i":2691584,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2691584,"author":"JoachimSchipper","month":"2011-06","story":"Instapaper Server Update","stance":"rec","why":"Short intro by the authors to recovering encryption keys from memory.","quote":"is a well-made 5-minute introduction by the authors.","text":"Indeed, the encryption keys can be recovered from memory fairly easily. [LINK] is very readable; [VIDEO] is a well-made 5-minute introduction by the authors."},{"id":21939174,"author":"Buge","month":"2020-01","story":"BusKill: A kill cord for your laptop","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the timing limits of cold boot attacks.","quote":"the original cold boot video","text":"You're right, I think an attack could be possible on some computers if the agents act very quickly. I don't think 30 minutes would be possible though, judging from what is said and shown in the original cold boot video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21939897,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2020-01","story":"BusKill: A kill cord for your laptop","stance":"reference","why":"Shows retrieving encryption keys from RAM after shutdown.","quote":"For getting encryption keys from RAM after shutdown there's this video","text":"For getting encryption keys from RAM after shutdown there's this video mentioned by another commentor: [VIDEO] For using reflections, there's this paper implementing reading screen content from various things like reading glasses, a can of coke or even the user's eye (including a discussion of limitations): [LINK] Right now I can't find something on reading keyboard vibrations with lasers, but here's one doing it acoustically and one doing it via acclerometer of a phone on the table: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":3253493,"author":"pjin","month":"2011-11","story":"Full disk encryption is too good?","stance":"reference","why":"Backs up the claim that RAM freezing can recover keys.","quote":"You may be interested in [1] and [2].","text":"2. \"with the cryogenic RAM freezing technique, presumably\" - errr, no. With the \"sit down at the computer and turn off the encryption/copy the data to an external drive technique\", I think you'll find. Obvious bullsh*t to anyone technical but sounds 'cool' to your average 14yr old who reads these sort of sites. Errr, actually yes. You may be interested in [1] and [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"Js7Y1H5D8cY":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a cool VR desktop idea from the Arcan author's demo channel, even if somewhat a novelty.","q":{"x":"Also, this was a pretty cool idea, even if a novelty","i":45595557,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":17204604,"author":"bhattisatish","month":"2018-06","story":"Revisiting the Arcan Project","stance":"rec","why":"Arcan author's channel has exciting demo videos, including this 3D VR desktop.","quote":"The youtube channel[2] has some exciting [3] videos.","text":"Very interesting project. The presentation[0][1] (though from 2015) gives an interesting overview of the two projects. The youtube channel[2] has some exciting [3] videos. And I really like his argument for Server Side Decorations[4] Another project to keep an eye out for is Simula[5] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":45595557,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2025-10","story":"Apple Vision Pro upgraded with M5 chip","stance":"reference","why":"A cool VR desktop idea, even if a novelty.","quote":"Also, this was a pretty cool idea, even if a novelty","text":"> Then I found out they went with an app model and the device could only display a single MacOS window. There was this program called Immersed for Quest and some other VR headsets (apparently Apple's one too?) that ages ago was quite brilliant: I could connect my laptop or PC and have as many virtual desktops in addition to the real ones displayed around me. Even on my Quest 2, until the strain set in, it was cool to just kinda tune out the room around me and be in a black void surrounded by just the screens of whatever I'm working on. Sadly, in one update they randomly removed it as a \"legacy feature nobody uses\", which ruined the program for me, and Virtual Desktop for the Quest also has limited screens you can display, so I can't even do all 4 of my physical ones, nor virtual ones - despite it having worked previously in the other program. For a little bit, it was a really cool mode of working, but sadly I only got glimpses of it and would need more capable hardware like AVP for that in the first place (Quest 2 very much had its limitations). Also, this was a pretty cool idea, even if a novelty: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18274341,"author":"crazyloglad","month":"2018-10","story":"Looking Glass, a revolutionary window manager revealed in 2006","stance":"reference","why":"Successor to Looking Glass, giving productive VR overview of data sources.","quote":"I get quite a few productive hours out of a successor to this","text":"I agree that the looking glass style 3D is fun and gimicky and hard to see added benefit. At the same time, speaking from personal experience - 3D in VR can give a much stronger sense of overview of many datasources etc. while at the same time can reduce semantic noise. I get quite a few productive hours out of a successor to this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WwslBPj8GgI",1]]},"Ko4V3G4NqII":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as primary-source evidence of what Jobs actually said about Mac OS X's design, open-source kernel and NeXT-based transition.","q":{"x":"In case you wanna procrastinate on a fascinating story about how Apple transitioned to NextStep's stack","i":13226314,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13128783,"author":"m_mueller","month":"2016-12","story":"ARM-Based Windows 10 Portable PCs? Hell Yes","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a presentation series on Jobs' return and NeXT technologies.","quote":"Watch basically everyone asleep at the wheel except Jobs, the man with a plan.","text":"Basically, you get a corporate drama, a human drama and a tech drama all wrapped up in one series of presentations: Jobs' return (as a consultant) 1997, promoting technologies from NeXT. Watch basically everyone asleep at the wheel except Jobs, the man with a plan. [LINK] WWDC Q&A - 'the art of saying no' [LINK] Jobs' hostile takeover in July by doing a (probably illegal?) stock dump [LINK] Announcing a Deal with Microsoft as de facto CEO in August, booed by the crowd [LINK] Internal meeting in September 1997 [LINK] iMac introduction 1998 - Apple is back [LINK] Macworld 1998 - Apple is essentially saved as a company [LINK] OSX Strategy reveal (if you only have time for one presentation, watch this one) [LINK] 1999 - OSX Server launch [LINK] 2000 - OSX launch [VIDEO]"},{"id":13226314,"author":"m_mueller","month":"2016-12","story":"20 years ago, Apple bought NeXT","stance":"rec","why":"Final entry in a presentation series on Apple's transition to NeXT stack.","quote":"In case you wanna procrastinate on a fascinating story about how Apple transitioned to NextStep's stack","text":"I copy from a comment of mine from last week. In case you wanna procrastinate on a fascinating story about how Apple transitioned to NextStep's stack without completely annihilating their already weak developer base, here's a few links: You get a corporate drama, a human drama and a tech drama all wrapped up in one series of presentations: Jobs' return (as a consultant) 1997, promoting technologies from NeXT. Watch basically everyone asleep at the wheel except Jobs, the man with a plan. [LINK] WWDC Q&A - 'the art of saying no' [LINK] Jobs' hostile takeover in July by doing a (probably illegal?) stock dump [LINK] Announcing a Deal with Microsoft as de facto CEO in August, booed by the crowd [LINK] Internal meeting in September 1997 [LINK] iMac introduction 1998 - Apple is back [LINK] Macworld 1998 - Apple is essentially saved as a company [LINK] OSX Strategy reveal [LINK] 1999 - OSX Server launch [LINK] 2000 - OSX launch [VIDEO]"},{"id":47558166,"author":"andai","month":"2026-03","story":"Make macOS consistently bad (unironically)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs introducing Aqua as liquid.","quote":"We call that new user interface Aqua, because it's liquid.","text":"\"We call that new user interface Aqua, because it's liquid. One of the design goals was that when you saw it, you wanted to lick it!\" 06:45 [VIDEO]"},{"id":47072481,"author":"wolfhumble","month":"2026-02","story":"27-year-old Apple iBooks can connect to Wi-Fi and download official updates","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs introducing the Aqua interface.","quote":"One of the design goals was when you saw it you wanted to lick it","text":"> But yeah. Man, the desktop was so beautiful and refreshing. \". . . that new user interface builds on Apple's Legacy and carries it into the next century and we call that new user interface Aqua because it's liquid. One of the design goals was when you saw it you wanted to lick it . . .\" Steve Job, Macworld San Francisco 2000: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28884719,"author":"jd3","month":"2021-10","story":"Implementing form filling and accessibility in the Firefox PDF viewer","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs announces Quartz is based on PDF.","quote":"Quartz, the engine for OS X's \"killer graphics,\" was based on PDF.","text":"ignoring the \"thoughts on flash\"[0] letter, the apple <=> adobe relationship goes back to at least the apple LaserWriter[1], so that would definitely make sense the Quartz compositor itself has \"use[d] pdf internally\"[2][3] ever since the initial launch of Mac OS X > By the time OS X came out, these were less the kind of features that \"the next great personal computer operating system\" should have, and more the kind that they all had. What was new included how Quartz, the engine for OS X's \"killer graphics,\" was based on PDF. > \"What does that mean?\" asked Jobs. \"You know when you go to the web and you see PDF documents? Well, that technology is now at the core of Mac OS X's graphics. So you can image PDFs instantly. So now all applications get this for free.\" > It's how, to this day, every Mac app can produce a PDF document without needing a third-party app.[4] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] / [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":5165149,"author":"twoodfin","month":"2013-02","story":"It's Official: XNA is dead","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs introduces Mac OS X with Classic, Carbon and Cocoa.","quote":"Here's Steve introducing Mac OS X for the first time, describing running applications against Classic, Carbon, and Cocoa","text":"Here's Steve introducing Mac OS X for the first time, describing running applications against Classic, Carbon, and Cocoa: [VIDEO] It certainly seems clear to me (and seemed just as clear to me at the time) that Cocoa was being presented as the API of the future. Sure, Carbon is on the same slide, but so is Classic!"},{"id":4983791,"author":"chrisringrose","month":"2012-12","story":"Complete Apple OS X source (open source, and freely available)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs states Mac OS X's kernel would be open source.","quote":"Watch Steve Jobs state this here","text":"It's the kernel and basic elements. When Steve Jobs introduced Mac OS X, he stated even then that its kernel would be open source. Watch Steve Jobs state this here: [VIDEO] It's still great that Apple does this, nonetheless. More open than Microsoft. (Similar source is available for iOS and some other properties too.)"}],"r":[["IOs6hnTI4lw",1],["yJDv-zdhzMY",1]]},"Nu-nlQqFCKg":{"wy":"HN cites it as an early impressive real-time speech translation demo, often in discussions of voice-preserving translation and later AI demos.","q":{"x":"Check out this real-time translation of spoken English into written and spoken Chinese (in the same voice of the speaker)","i":7337077,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7337077,"author":"istorical","month":"2014-03","story":"Say Hello to Cortana, Microsoft’s Siri Equivalent","stance":"rec","why":"Real-time spoken English translated to Chinese in the speaker's own voice.","quote":"Check out this real-time translation of spoken English into written and spoken Chinese (in the same voice of the speaker)","text":"They've been doing some really cool stuff for a long time. Check out this real-time translation of spoken English into written and spoken Chinese (in the same voice of the speaker): [VIDEO]"},{"id":40352033,"author":"Mystery-Machine","month":"2024-05","story":"GPT-4o","stance":"reference","why":"Older Microsoft demo of speech translation, uploaded in 2012.","quote":"Have you seen this video from Microsoft, uploaded to YT in 2012","text":"Have you seen this video from Microsoft, uploaded to YT in 2012, the actual video could be even older: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7808756,"author":"rlu","month":"2014-05","story":"Microsoft demos breakthrough in real-time translated conversations","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier demo that captures the speaker's voice in translation.","quote":"if it could capture the voice of the speaker it would be truly magical.","text":"Reminds me of this from a few years ago: [VIDEO] I feel like they should merge the two. The subtitles are a nice touch, but if it could capture the voice of the speaker it would be truly magical."},{"id":6274550,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2013-08","story":"If Steve Ballmer Ran Apple","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Wasn't MSRA behind that amazing live English-to-Chinese translator [0]?","text":"Wasn't MSRA behind that amazing live English-to-Chinese translator [0]? It's interesting to contrast Google's and Microsoft's approach with ours at Wolfram Research (at 0.3% their size, of course [1]). We've come to see that it is very fruitful to live in a murky space somewhere between \"commercial product\" and \"research project\". I'm thinking specifically about Wolfram|Alpha. It's an enormous and daunting project. We've tackled a lot of very hard problems, and obviously have a long way still to go. It has the weird property of being a thing that ships every week, but not one that the parent company depends on financially. And so we kind of do our own thing, encoding domain knowledge, adding content, curating data, and designing frameworks, even if some if it won't ever make us any money (e.g. who will ever pay for dog vision [2]). We mostly just do things because they're cool or fun. But it's been incredibly useful to drive innovation elsewhere in the company. For example: 1. Alpha's unit system (the best in the world, the authors claim) is already in Mathematica 9. 2. Alpha is inspiring us to bring high-level semantic data and reasoning to Mathematica 10. 3. The automatic analysis in Pro is being souped up and will form part of Mathematica's predictive interface (automatic suggestions to \"perform logistic regression\", and so on). 4. We're working on taking the natural language understanding frameworks we've built for Alpha and using them to translate natural language querie"}]},"PDBiLT3LASk":{"wy":"Quoted whenever HN debates due process and legal protections, even for people one dislikes.","q":{"x":"Here's a great scene from A Man For All Seasons that shows what I mean","i":44899769,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44899769,"author":"liampulles","month":"2025-08","story":"Facial recognition vans to be rolled out across police forces in England","stance":"rec","why":"Scene on the value of legal protections for liberty.","quote":"Here's a great scene from A Man For All Seasons that shows what I mean","text":"Is there a public conversation in European countries about the value of liberty? I don't mean arguments about how liberty can lead to more economic prosperity, I mean how liberty is valuable on its own terms. Without this value, the state can continue to erect legislation in the name of \"safety\", or any other perceived inequity in society, until you can no longer move. How perverse that English law used to be a bastion of civil liberty protections. Here's a great scene from A Man For All Seasons that shows what I mean: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37957142,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-10","story":"Alexei Navalny's lawyers are arrested","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I often find myself thinking back to an old movie quote","text":"Re first paragraph: > The only real step 1 — unless you want to get into a philosophical argument about the foundation of knowledge and the Münchhausen trilemma — is \"get a law degree\", which for most people is much too hard. Go and do the thing. I'm serious, if you want to make this a real actual argument and not just two random nerds locking horns on the internet (which I'm not interested in so you'd have to find a real actual lawyer to spar with after getting that degree), that's the only way — there is no other. > Also, have you heard of selective enforcement? Does it not worry you at all to see the one side consistently getting prosecuted and persecuted and the other one not at all? Do you think you're always going to be on the right side? You could've figured that one out from my own comment a few hours earlier: [LINK] > pour encourager les autres desole, je ne pe parle francais (seriously, I try to say that in French and my French coworker is all \"quoi?\" because my French is that bad) > And the sight of literally Putin doing literally the same thing doesn't trigger the least amount of thought in your mind. On the contrary, for every punishment in the systems in which I exist, I often find myself thinking back to an old movie quote: [VIDEO] (This is particularly poignant to me, as a British person, because of the context of the Human Rights Act 1998 and its use in polemics during half the discussion about Brexit even though technically it's separate: I do not trust any "},{"id":27399800,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2021-06","story":"Van Buren is a victory against overbroad interpretations of the CFAA","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And go he should, if he were the devil himself until he broke the law","text":"> Whether Van Buren deserves punishment is a separate question from whether the legal theory the DoJ sought to use to get him punished was proper. Exactly. [VIDEO] \"That man's bad\" / \"There's no law against that\" \"Whilst you talk he's gone\" / \"And go he should, if he were the devil himself until he broke the law\"."},{"id":21370653,"author":"fennecfoxen","month":"2019-10","story":"Massachusetts Sues Exxon over Climate Change, Accusing the Oil Giant of Fraud","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Would you give evil the benefit of the law?","text":"Who needs to establish any injury to shareholders? That company is bad! Would you give evil the benefit of the law? [VIDEO]"}]},"SbO0tqH8f5I":{"wy":"Cited as an impressive example of a full computer architecture built inside Minecraft.","q":{"x":"features things like cache, hardware stack, a gpu connected to a 15x15 display, serial input and output port and many other interesting things.","i":20447583,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20447583,"author":"numlock86","month":"2019-07","story":"Cities: Skylines Is Turing Complete","stance":"rec","why":"Quad-core redstone computer with cache, stack, GPU, display and serial ports","quote":"features things like cache, hardware stack, a gpu connected to a 15x15 display, serial input and output port and many other interesting things.","text":"Related: [VIDEO] \"Quad Core Computer with Redstones\" features things like cache, hardware stack, a gpu connected to a 15x15 display, serial input and output port and many other interesting things. Also it's available as download by the creator."},{"id":30276630,"author":"IncRnd","month":"2022-02","story":"I decided to build a nine-bit computer","stance":"reference","why":"A computer built in Minecraft","quote":"Here is a computer created in MineCraft! Wow, it's actually v5.0.","text":"Here is a computer created in MineCraft! Wow, it's actually v5.0. The intro starts at 1:22. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23051639,"author":"EndXA","month":"2020-05","story":"Witchcraft and Wizardry – RPG game created in Minecraft","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a quad-core computer built inside Minecraft","quote":"someone built a Quad-Core computer that runs inside the game","text":"People's ability to create in Minecraft is amazing. Case in point: someone built a Quad-Core computer that runs inside the game ( [VIDEO] )"}]},"X0VH78ye4yY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a look at developer infrastructure at large scale, with speakers covering source control and CI test selection.","q":{"x":"It's a long video but it's very watchable using the YouTube speed multiplier.","i":10217165,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10217165,"author":"Corrado","month":"2015-09","story":"React Native for Android","stance":"rec","why":"Details Facebook's move to Mercurial, Atom-based IDE, and CI.","quote":"It's a long video but it's very watchable using the YouTube speed multiplier.","text":"I think the best part of this article was the small link to the F8 2015 \"Big Code\" video [0] where they go into detail about switching from Git to Mercurial, building their own IDE around Atom, and detailing their continuous integration process. It's a long video but it's very watchable using the YouTube speed multiplier. :) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11483714,"author":"mgorven","month":"2016-04","story":"Continuous Deployment at Instagram","stance":"reference","why":"F8 talk covering an internal system.","quote":"","text":"It's an internal system, but it was discussed at F8 last year. Here's the video ( [VIDEO] ) and someone's notes on it ( [LINK] )."},{"id":10319361,"author":"LegNeato","month":"2015-10","story":"TTNT: Test This, Not That","stance":"reference","why":"Katie Coons explains Facebook's test selection in CI.","quote":"","text":"Facebook does this to scale their tests, you can hear Katie Coons talking about it in CI at the end of [VIDEO] . There are also rules that say \"if you changed this file, run all tests\" for things like CI config and such. Also, the build tool Buck ( [LINK] ) enables this because it knows the graph and associates tests with the code they test...so you even get this test minimization locally as a developer (that is, outside CI) for free. I'm sure Bazel likely has a similar feature too."},{"id":9787044,"author":"gghh","month":"2015-06","story":"Fighting spam with Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"Talk where Bryan O'Sullivan describes Facebook developer productivity work.","quote":"","text":"All correct, but I don't think Bryan O'Sullivan does much haskell these days. He's the manager of some sort of \"Developer productivity\" team. The Hack programming language came out of his team [1] (tho he wrote a blog post to contrast all the media coverage and say \"my people did it, not me! [2]); he spoke about his activity at the last F8 [3]. But he does teach Haskell at Facebook after work :) [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] EDIT: spelling"}],"r":[["3ZTGwcHQfLY",1]]},"XCvwCcEP74Q":{"wy":"Shared in 2015 threads as an insightful framework for strategic moves by the big tech companies.","q":{"x":"very insightful video on why Amazon has reached a point where they must make a brick and mortar acquisition","i":8994486,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8994486,"author":"julianpye","month":"2015-02","story":"Amazon Is in Talks to Buy RadioShack Stores, Report Says","stance":"rec","why":"Scott Galloway on why Amazon needs brick and mortar","quote":"very insightful video on why Amazon has reached a point where they must make a brick and mortar acquisition","text":"Scott Galloway talked about this at last week's DLD. You can hear his points and arguments at 6:50 -very insightful video on why Amazon has reached a point where they must make a brick and mortar acquisition [VIDEO]"},{"id":24353537,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2020-09","story":"Anatomy of a Bubble – Tesla and Bitcoin","stance":"reference","why":"Galloway segment on luxury car premiums","quote":"","text":"You shouldn't be using the X for comparison, but the Y (which is closer to $45k, $55k for the Performance version that puts you close to muscle car territory). EDIT: (HN Throttled, can't reply, replying here) I agree there is a premium, but I do not agree it's considerable considering a) people buy a payment and b) we're in a low interest rate environment where people can stretch for a luxury car like a Tesla due to low carrying costs of the auto debt. Why? [VIDEO] (13:17-14:15 is the relevant time window)"},{"id":9234742,"author":"wsc981","month":"2015-03","story":"Tag Heuer will make the first 'luxury' Android Wear watch","stance":"reference","why":"Galloway's points on Android versus iOS consumers","quote":"Some of the above points are explained by Scott Galloway in this presentation","text":"I feel like this luxury Android watch will fail for several reasons: - the average Android device consumer has less money to spend compared to the average iOS device consumer. - full control of the device is not in the hands of a single company, which will likely result in bad compromises. - to me at least, Android is not something I associate with \"luxury\". Some of the above points are explained by Scott Galloway in this presentation: [VIDEO]"}]},"YAPt_DcWAvw":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible way to make lithographic masks at home, in threads about hobbyist chip fabrication.","q":{"x":"There is a great video on creating lithographic masks on Ben Krasnow's Applied Science channel","i":46179802,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46179802,"author":"bpye","month":"2025-12","story":"Z2 – Lithographically fabricated IC in a garage fab","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible photographic steps for creating lithographic masks","quote":"There is a great video on creating lithographic masks on Ben Krasnow's Applied Science channel","text":"There is a great video on creating lithographic masks on Ben Krasnow's Applied Science channel - [VIDEO] The photographic steps are pretty accessible."},{"id":32294291,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-07","story":"Building a Next Level Camera","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wow, essentially an extraordinarily large view camera built around a repurposed lens, then converted into a depth-of-field adapter[0] in lieu of a digital back. There's actually a known technique for taking wide angle photographs with depth of field this shallow using only a typical long lens:[1] stitching together frames to build a wider field of view is all that's needed; long lenses all have shallow DoF by nature.[a] This interestingly can also be used to explain why the episcope lens has such shallow DoF: its focal length of 432 mm would make it a super telephoto lens, yet its large image circle means it has a wide-angle field of view. If it were replaced by a telephoto lens with the same focal length and aperture but a smaller image circle, and that lens were swung around in a spiral to 'paint' the same area as the episcope lens image circle, the two lenses would produce the same result, only the episcope lens can do it all at once instead of needing multiple exposures. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] -- [a] Annoyingly, I still don't know a satisfying explanation of why depth of field depends on subject/background distances, aperture, focal length, etc. The formulas exist, but every attempt at intuitive explanation I've seen has been incomplete or incorrect. Here's some videos to show those who say that longer lenses have shallower DoF solely because reframing by standing farther away from the subject causes the background and its blur to become relatively larger: [LINK] [LINK] (O"},{"id":24620543,"author":"anon73044","month":"2020-09","story":"TSMC to Mass Produce Breakthrough 2nm Mbcfet Transistors in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist lithography example","quote":"Sub 100nm for a hobbiest would be pretty awesome.","text":"I recall this but don't remember seeing anything smaller being posted. Sub 100nm for a hobbiest would be pretty awesome. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22747672,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-04","story":"A startup is building computer chips using real neurons","stance":"reference","why":"Ben Krasnow makes photolithography masks for a better substrate","quote":"Ben explains somewhere in there that this was done to help create a better substrate for the first experiment.","text":"Oops, second vid should be from Ben Krasnow’s (bkraz) Applied Science channel: [VIDEO] Ben explains somewhere in there that this was done to help create a better substrate for the first experiment."}]},"agxSclh27uo":{"wy":"HN cites it as an insider account of Intel replacing senior staff with contractors, in threads about Intel's decline.","q":{"x":"Great video on youtube posted in the last week with a bunch of leaks from Intel employees","i":22872573,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22872573,"author":"code_biologist","month":"2020-04","story":"AMD Claims World’s Fastest Per-Core Performance with New EPYC Rome 7Fx2 CPUs","stance":"rec","why":"Leaks from Intel employees about the company's internal environment","quote":"Great video on youtube posted in the last week with a bunch of leaks from Intel employees","text":"It already sounds like the environment in Intel isn't great, and a lot of the staff dynamics are very driven by cost-cutting. Great video on youtube posted in the last week with a bunch of leaks from Intel employees: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23816923,"author":"Gangbusters","month":"2020-07","story":"Apple Silicon: The Passing of Wintel","stance":"reference","why":"Insider account of Intel workforce changes","quote":"There's an insider's account on the AdoredTV youtube channel","text":"That generation of senior American workers has been replaced by H1B contractors. There's an insider's account on the AdoredTV youtube channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23939010,"author":"ArkVark","month":"2020-07","story":"Intel's 7nm is Broken, Company Announces Delay Until 2022, 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Video on Intel being hollowed out by contractors","quote":"AdoreTV has a video on the topic","text":"Intel has been hollowed out by contractors and H1B workers. I don't think the raw talent is there anymore. The company is just running on inertia. AdoreTV has a video on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23496409,"author":"ReticentVole","month":"2020-06","story":"Jim Keller to Depart Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Account of Intel replacing senior staff with contractors","quote":"There's a video on AdoredTV about the topic","text":"Intel's problem is that they fired expensive, older American workers and replaced them with H1B contractors. There's a video on AdoredTV about the topic: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_bP80DEAbuo",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["jkrNMKz9pWU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1]]},"eNSN6qet1kE":{"wy":"Cited as an example of a hobbyist building and flying a real human-carrying electric plane, and as a channel worth following for DIY aircraft builds.","q":{"x":"If anyone is interested in this kind of stuff (fiberglassing fuselages, building cockpits...)","i":17748472,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17748472,"author":"donquichotte","month":"2018-08","story":"One man designed and built the ultimate bush plane","stance":"rec","why":"DIY human-carrying plane using hobby brushless motors","quote":"If anyone is interested in this kind of stuff (fiberglassing fuselages, building cockpits...)","text":"Insane timeline indeed! I wish I had a work ethic like that. It's interesting how he tells that as a child, he was encouraged by his parents to \"always finish his project, no matter how much you disliked it\". I wonder what the reliability of this beast is like, but I guess only time will tell. If anyone is interested in this kind of stuff (fiberglassing fuselages, building cockpits...), Peter Sripol, a model plane enthusiast, made his own DIY plane that can carry a human with hobby grade brushless motors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30959849,"author":"jmpman","month":"2022-04","story":"A detailed look at the S-300P anti-aircraft missile system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"These missiles mimic the radar signature of a full sized plane. They’re extremely expensive, but can absolutely be used to force the SAMs to expend their missiles. I’m thinking of something a few orders of magnitude cheaper. Flight need not cost millions. Airfoils used for human powered flight produce planes weighing 300lbs, powered with only 300 watts. Scale this up to a 3000lbs flying bomb, using 3kw, or less than 5HP. Let it slog along at 40km/h, and in an 8 hour transit, they’ve covered over 300km. If electric, that requires just 24kwh of batteries, contained in 5 Tesla battery modules at 55lbs each, for 275lbs of batteries. Each module is around $1500 used, so $7500 in batteries. Foam and plastic wrapped wings with aluminum spars could be constructed for a few thousand dollars at scale. Add in the Mark 84 payload at $3k, and a navigation system for <$1000. Total could be well under $20k. Launched in a wave of 1000, flying at night, tens of meters off the ground to avoid radar, rising to maximum altitude prior to getting within AA range, roughly an hour before destination, this $20M swarm of 1000 2000lbs bombs needs to be neutralized by the enemy. They can only use their S-300/S-400s. Each cruise missile would be capable of taking out any ship in the Russian Black Sea fleet. Sevestapol would be a indefensible against nightly attacks launched from just 300km away in Odessa. Even Russia would eventually run out of S-300 missiles. Probably in the first night. Target the laun"},{"id":30964685,"author":"jmpman","month":"2022-04","story":"A detailed look at the S-300P anti-aircraft missile system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Just needs to be optimized to get above anti-aircraft range, and with a sufficient range. Mount a large enough bomb along with sufficient gps guidance and terminal optical guidance (make it a threat they can’t ignore) and they’ll have to shoot it down."},{"id":16879078,"author":"netgusto","month":"2018-04","story":"Airplanes that fly on electricity debut at Fresno’s Chandler Airport","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist builds a DIY electric airplane","quote":"A DIY electric airplane made by a hobbyist (Peter Sripol; check his channel out)","text":"Also: A DIY electric airplane made by a hobbyist (Peter Sripol; check his channel out) [VIDEO] The 7 parts build footage : [LINK]"}]},"g0TD0efcwVg":{"wy":"Cited in React versus Web Components threads as a clear picture of how the two interoperate and their challenges.","q":{"x":"which gives a very clear picture of the current state of React/Web Component interaction, including the challenges associated with it","i":9029755,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9029755,"author":"marknutter","month":"2015-02","story":"Reapp – hybrid apps, fast","stance":"rec","why":"State of React and Web Components interaction and its challenges.","quote":"which gives a very clear picture of the current state of React/Web Component interaction, including the challenges associated with it","text":"Exactly which part of that thread leads you to believe that \"React plays just fine with Web Components\". I suggest you watch [VIDEO] which gives a very clear picture of the current state of React/Web Component interaction, including the challenges associated with it. edit: another good read if you're interested: [LINK]"},{"id":9855957,"author":"jimmcslim","month":"2015-07","story":"The state of Web Components","stance":"reference","why":"Shows using Web Components within React views.","quote":"You could just use Web Components within your React views","text":"You could just use Web Components within your React views; 'React.js Conf 2015 - The complementarity of React and Web Components' - [VIDEO]"},{"id":9190873,"author":"nutate","month":"2015-03","story":"React is badly designed","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show React and Web Components can coexist.","quote":"React and Web Components can play together","text":"Good clickbait, but almost nonsensical article. The idea of supporting web components is great, but it's also a standards process in committee. React and Web Components can play together ( [VIDEO] ), so why draw some sort of false dichotomy between the technologies. The thought that went into the virtual dom diffing strategy is an algorithmic nicety that software in general can and has already benefitted from. React itself is an implementation of that which provides real workflow improvements for web and now native platforms. That's pretty awesome and usable right now. I don't see this as a zero sum game, or as an all in investment strategy unless you are going into some arcane questions of licensing, the code and the ideas are out in the open. Ember, mythril, Om have all taken the ideas into new regions. Even look at the atom editor, they switched to react for their editor component, then realized the general purpose dom diffing strategy which is very fast could still be optimized for their edit window. That's awesome. That idea is decoupled from react. Learning to read react code takes about 1 day to 1 week. It's pretty awesome. Also, the canard about reactive programming... uhh... I just don't get what you're advertising here? That you're willing to use products like docker and node.js as advertised on your front page, but not react.js because... it's \"bad\"? Why so inflexible, why not try all the things and use what works as time permits?"}]},"ga_7j72CVlc":{"wy":"Reached for whenever a thread touches on naming versus actually understanding something.","q":{"x":"You can know the name of a bird in many different languages, but that tells you nothing about it.","i":38574138,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":24131922,"author":"Quai","month":"2020-08","story":"Joan Feynman has died","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman speaking himself in vintage footage","quote":"If you dont mind listening to Richard himself, you can watch this pre-historic 4:3 videos on youtube;","text":"If you dont mind listening to Richard himself, you can watch this pre-historic 4:3 videos on youtube; [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":46834644,"author":"s_dev","month":"2026-01","story":"The National Herbarium of Ireland digital collection of Irish plants","stance":"reference","why":"Names give no knowledge but help communication","quote":"Knowing the name of something gives you no knowledge of that thing even if you can name it in every language","text":"Richard Feynman: [VIDEO] The names of birds. tl;dw: Knowing the name of something gives you no knowledge of that thing even if you can name it in every language but it's super useful to know when communicating with others."},{"id":38574138,"author":"gnramires","month":"2023-12","story":"Vowels and Diphthongs in Sperm Whales","stance":"reference","why":"Names tell nothing about the bird; look at what it does","quote":"You can know the name of a bird in many different languages, but that tells you nothing about it.","text":"It's not that simple. LLMs are great for predicting next word, but this needs a more unsupervised approach. Ultimately, you need to know the environment of whales. Sure, you know the next probable token, and even relationships between tokens, but how do they relate to what whales are doing? , in other words what does it mean? . It reminds me of the great quote by Feynman: \"look at the bird\"[1]. You can know the name of a bird in many different languages, but that tells you nothing about it. If you want to understand birds, you need to look at it and see what it's doing. If we want to communicate with another species, we need to understand what they're doing, why they're communicating, and from there we can begin to infer what they're communicating. It could be simply a callsign/identification/name for each individual, it could be communicating you've found a place with food, it could be communicating about predators, who knows. There could be all sorts of interesting variation and nuance to their calls. But ultimately it's necessary to look (in the general sense of having information about what it's doing -- could be through all kinds of sensors!) at the ~~bird~~ whale :) [1] Feynman tells the as a lesson from his father. (that you can know their names and still know nothing about the birds themselves). [VIDEO] Bonus related video!: [LINK]"},{"id":29066828,"author":"scythmic_waves","month":"2021-11","story":"They don't even know the fundamentals","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman admits names are useful for communicating with others","quote":"So I had learned already that names don't constitute knowledge...","text":"Though he does admit [1] > So I had learned already that names don't constitute knowledge... That's caused me a certain trouble since because I refuse to learn the name of anything... What [my father] forgot to tell me was that the knowing the names of things is useful if you wanna talk to somebody else. I think an interview might count :) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28384375,"author":"incrudible","month":"2021-09","story":"Music Theory for the 21st-Century Classroom","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"On a related note","text":"I suspect the joy for people versed in classic theory is sparked from being able to identify the name of a recurring concept in a segment of a classical music piece. On a related note: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15382578,"author":"cyphar","month":"2017-10","story":"Badger or Bulbasaur – have children lost touch with nature?","stance":"reference","why":"Knowing an animal's name is least useful knowledge about it","quote":"knowing the name of an animal is the least useful knowledge about that animal.","text":"Richard Feynman had a very good quote on this topic. Effectively, knowing the name of an animal is the least useful knowledge about that animal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13631068,"author":"braveo","month":"2017-02","story":"Man jailed 16 months, and counting, for refusing to decrypt hard drives","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We should go tell Feynman he's nuts...","text":"ooooh, I guess God was ok with killing people as long as it was mass killings so you could call it something besides murder. Because apparently the exact name somehow makes the act better (or worse?) in God's eyes. [VIDEO] We should go tell Feynman he's nuts..."},{"id":13723076,"author":"anotheryou","month":"2017-02","story":"27 Jobs with Unbelievably Tough Interview Questions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Relevant Feynman","text":"Why do they ask about capitals? Relevant Feynman: [VIDEO] And nowadays we have google for some added prosthetic knowledge. It's becoming increasingly important to grasp concepts well enough to ask the right questions, but knowing simple unrelated facts by heart becomes irrelevant. At most you have to remember that there was something to remember so you can look it up. edit: can a native speaker guide me on where to set commas correctly :) ?"},{"id":11339949,"author":"yati","month":"2016-03","story":"Randall Munroe, XKCD Creator, Goes Back to High School","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on knowing names versus understanding","quote":"And here's Feynman on the same topic","text":"Totally agreed. However, I've found that learning something new without regards for the actual name for it helps me build a solid, abstract representation in the mind. But like you say, communicating these ideas becomes cumbersome when you don't speak the common language :) And here's Feynman on the same topic: [VIDEO]"}]},"j2cMHwo3nAU":{"wy":"HN cites it as an impressive example of building computation inside Dwarf Fortress.","q":{"x":"Though I see he has a new project (Armok Invaders) that looks pretty awesome.","i":11101406,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11101406,"author":"Natsu","month":"2016-02","story":"Working Calculator in Super Mario Maker [video]","stance":"rec","why":"New Dwarf Fortress computing project that looks pretty awesome","quote":"Though I see he has a new project (Armok Invaders) that looks pretty awesome.","text":"My favorite is still the Dwarven Calculator: [LINK] Though I see he has a new project (Armok Invaders) that looks pretty awesome. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43343882,"author":"musictubes","month":"2025-03","story":"Space Invaders","stance":"reference","why":"Space Invaders programmed inside Dwarf Fortress","quote":"Space Invaders was programmed inside of a Dwarf Fortress game.","text":"And of course Space Invaders was programmed inside of a Dwarf Fortress game. It’s simplified of course and not exactly real time lol. Wonder what it would take to emulate the full version inside of DF. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20446295,"author":"munk-a","month":"2019-07","story":"Cities: Skylines Is Turing Complete","stance":"reference","why":"Simple space-invaders game built inside Dwarf Fortress","quote":"Someone has built an (admittedly simple) space-invaders game[2] using df which is rather impressive.","text":"Dwarfputers are a wonderful tangent of their own, with a not-unlikely possibility of flooding your entire computer with firey death in the form of magma in some of the more impressive approaches - this has been possible (but quite difficult) for some time, more recent game versions have made non-fluid based logic gates[1] more realistic and usable. Someone has built an (admittedly simple) space-invaders game[2] using df which is rather impressive. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"}]},"nybVFJVXbww":{"wy":"HN points to it as an intuitive explanation of how NIST standardized a backdoored generator.","q":{"x":"He also gives a super cool/intuitive breakdown on how this came to be.","i":43333716,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43333716,"author":"qzx_pierri","month":"2025-03","story":"NIST selects HQC as fifth algorithm for post-quantum encryption","stance":"rec","why":"Intuitive breakdown of how NIST standardized backdoored Dual_EC_DRBG","quote":"He also gives a super cool/intuitive breakdown on how this came to be.","text":"I recommend this video by Computerphile - He talks about how NIST may have been pressured into enforcing compromised (backdoored?) cryptography methods as a standard - Dual_EC_DRBG to be exact. He also gives a super cool/intuitive breakdown on how this came to be. It will definitely grow some food for thought. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42172491,"author":"lcnPylGDnU4H9OF","month":"2024-11","story":"Nothing-up-my-sleeve number","stance":"reference","why":"Math of how such a number could be used practically","quote":"Computerphile video discussing the mathematics around how such a number could have been used in a practical way.","text":"Computerphile video discussing the mathematics around how such a number could have been used in a practical way. [VIDEO] [LINK] When people say, “Don’t roll your own crypto,” it’s because the field is littered with footguns and (to stretch an analogy) guns in cryptography launch explosives."},{"id":31860914,"author":"m-p-3","month":"2022-06","story":"Dueling over Dual_EC_DRGB: Consequences of Corrupting a Standardization Process","stance":"reference","why":"Explains plausibility of the Dual EC backdoor","quote":"there are some possibilities that's makes it plausible","text":"Long story short is that we don't know for sure, but there are some possibilities that's makes it plausible [VIDEO]"},{"id":28404801,"author":"Arkanum","month":"2021-09","story":"The NSA's Backdoor in Dual EC","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the backdoor mechanism in Dual EC DRBG","quote":"Computerphile[1] did a video about the specifics of the backdoor in Dual EC DRBG.","text":"Computerphile[1] did a video about the specifics of the backdoor in Dual EC DRBG. As i understand it, the hackers replaced a magic number Q with a different value than the standards compliant one, where they had pre-computed P. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1]]},"oRLON3ddZIw":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly in nuclear-weapons threads as a witty or darkly funny aside, rather than as a recommendation.","q":{"x":"I love his song \"Who's Next?\", about nuclear war","i":16775405,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16775405,"author":"pizza","month":"2018-04","story":"Tom Lehrer at 90: a life of scientific satire","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Lehrer's satirical song about nuclear proliferation","quote":"I love his song \"Who's Next?\", about nuclear war","text":"I love his song \"Who's Next?\", about nuclear war: especially how it starts and ends, haha > First we got the bomb and that was good > Cause we love peace and motherhood > Then Russia got the bomb, but that's O.K > Cause the balance of power's maintained that way! ... > ... We'll try to stay serene and calm > When Alabama gets the bomb! [VIDEO]"},{"id":13158266,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2016-12","story":"Why a Post-Nuclear World Would Look Nothing Like “Mad Max”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I have to imagine the only way to really get nukes dropped on you, is to have nukes aimed at someone else... or have a large standing army. Or basically be a threat in some way. It's not like someone says, \"I want to kill all the humans...\" they presumably just want to destroy their enemies and threats to their safety. With that in mind, these nations are probably fucked... but that's only a fraction of nations. * List of states with nuclear weapons - Wikipedia || [LINK] Russia has, generously, an 8k nuclear arsenal (but only about 2k of those are considered active). If they sent everything they had ever built at Texas, they'd still barely be able to drop one bomb per every 30 square miles... Assuming they were all Tsar Bombs... that might take out one state. * NUKEMAP by Alex Wellerstein || [LINK] I think there are a lot of rural places in the US that would be mostly safe from being directly targeted and potentially far enough away to also avoid fallout. And also... like all of Canada. Who's going to waste nukes on Canada? * US States: Area and Ranking - EnchantedLearning.com || [LINK] And let's not forget these are all government contracts to build the nukes were awarded to constituents as kickbacks or were built by the lower bidder -- and many are very old at this point. I have to think the failure rate is more than 0; so unless they're doubling up likely there will still be places targeted and still left unscathed due to delivery or ordinance malfunction... or just human "},{"id":41808012,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-10","story":"Nobel Peace Prize for 2024 awarded to Nihon Hidankyo","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Lagniappe","text":"That might have been a better argument if the USSR[0] had had the bomb in 1945[1]? Lagniappe: [VIDEO] [0] first test: 29.08.1949 [1] a year in which the US and USSR were, however tenuously, still allied"},{"id":41201563,"author":"khafra","month":"2024-08","story":"Show HN: I built interactive map of active and decommissioned nuclear stations","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's easy to remember the early players in the nuclear power/weapons game, with a simple mnemonic song","text":"It's easy to remember the early players in the nuclear power/weapons game, with a simple mnemonic song: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23367242,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-05","story":"Nukemap","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reality imitates art: Alabama (SSBN-731) does indeed have the bomb.","text":"I must've been a bit older when the Cold War ended, because I still keep an eye on where permanent security council members* have their sub pens. On USENET, people used to give their \"icbmto:\" coordinates in signatures. Ha ha. As far as \"Who's Next\" goes [VIDEO] reality imitates art: Alabama (SSBN-731) does indeed have the bomb. * might we explain much US right-wing two-minute hate against France by the simple existence of Brest?"}],"r":[["iSSRaIU9_Vc",1]]},"obCNQ0xksZ4":{"wy":"Cited in threads on coerced confessions and plea bargains, and praised for well-researched coverage.","q":{"x":"His show does proper research and points to numerous actual cases of harm before making a claim.","i":36021112,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36021112,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2023-05","story":"Japanese police make first YouTuber arrest for uploading video game gameplay","stance":"rec","why":"Well-researched coverage of long detentions and forced confessions.","quote":"His show does proper research and points to numerous actual cases of harm before making a claim.","text":"His show does proper research and points to numerous actual cases of harm before making a claim. Detaining suspects for long periods of time and forcing confessions is a topic that comes up repeatedly, each time with different examples. I don't know how anyone can say with a straight face that this isn't anything of substance. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36370749,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2023-06","story":"People Can Be Convinced They Committed a Crime That Never Happened","stance":"reference","why":"Coerced confessions as standard police practice.","quote":"","text":"Or you know, it being the standard work process for police these days apparently, so they can pat themselves on the back and go home early: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34904047,"author":"m000","month":"2023-02","story":"The vast majority of criminal cases end in plea bargains, a new report finds","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on how innocent people are coerced into confessing.","quote":"Last year there was a whole episode of Last Week Tonight","text":"Is this news at this point? Last year there was a whole episode of Last Week Tonight with main subject \"Police interrogations and false confession\" [1] documenting exactly how innocent people are coerced into guilty pleas. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31742409,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2022-06","story":"Japan passes revised penal code with harsher punishment for cyberbullying","stance":"reference","why":"False confessions from police interrogations in the US.","quote":"Forced confessions are unfortunately not unique to Japan [1].","text":"Forced confessions are unfortunately not unique to Japan [1]. Police will behave like that when you reward them based on the number of convictions and don't hold them accountable for screwing over innocent people. Still sucks, though. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"tlThdr3O5Qo":{"wy":"Mostly cited critically, as an example of a staged or cherry-picked self-driving demo and of misleading autonomy marketing.","m":[{"id":23835082,"author":"dlivingston","month":"2020-07","story":"German court bans Tesla ad statements related to autonomous driving","stance":"rec","why":"Accurate view of FSD's current state","quote":"this is an accurate video on the current state of FSD","text":"The Full Self Driving package is actually quite good. With the exception of going “hands free”, this is an accurate video on the current state of FSD: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19726842,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-04","story":"Tesla Live Stream – Autonomy Day [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Official sped-up Tesla demo, hard to judge","quote":"There's also an official Tesla sped up demo vid - hard to tell really.","text":"Some video of demo rides from someone who was at the day: Lane change on crowded freeway: [LINK] - It manages it if not quite as smooth as a human. Summon in parking lot: [LINK] likewise. There's also an official Tesla sped up demo vid [VIDEO] - hard to tell really. Same video slowed with the display magnified and some Reddit discussion [LINK]"},{"id":44793338,"author":"burkaman","month":"2025-08","story":"Tesla withheld data, lied, misdirected police to avoid blame in Autopilot crash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Do you think an ad like that, which simply shows the car driving itself","text":"Yes of course it is on you, that's why the driver in this case was found mostly responsible. The question is whether Tesla bears any additional responsibility, not whether they are solely at fault, which they obviously aren't. > Tesla infamously doesn't have a marketing team Come on, obviously they do marketing. \"We don't have a marketing team\" is itself marketing. Here's their most popular YouTube ad, for example: [VIDEO] That video, which is called \"Full Self-Driving\" even though it came out when Autopilot was the only capability the cars had, was coincidentally released 3 days before the crash we're discussing. Do you think an ad like that, which simply shows the car driving itself and links to a page about Autopilot in the description, might lead someone to believe that Autopilot will do what the video shows? Again, remember that there was no separate FSD product at the time."},{"id":33232002,"author":"n0tth3dro1ds","month":"2022-10","story":"Even after $100B, self-driving cars are going nowhere","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They either recorded a special map for that scam video, drove the route a thousand times and took the best recording, or both.","text":"> Telsa doesn't have such ultra-HD maps at all. They build some maps for some simulations and training but the car does not locate itself in such maps when driving. They do have such maps, but only for the routes of their demo videos. That’s the entire accusation — it’s a Fugazi. A man behind a curtain. A mechanical Turk. Fake. This video was posted 3 years ago: [VIDEO] I have never seen a Tesla perform as well as the one in that video in ANY user video. I drove a Model 3 with FSD for 4 months and it never came close to performing that well. Not even close. They either recorded a special map for that scam video, drove the route a thousand times and took the best recording, or both. > This is certainty not what Tesla does, from Tesla vehicles you will find literally 1000s of videos uploaded by Testers on every possible routes In the real videos, the cars fail to navigate simple scenarios constantly. You can’t even watch the videos without cringing at the constant mistakes and dangerous maneuvers. For Pete’s sake, just search “Tesla phantom braking” on YouTube and behold. [LINK] [LINK] > And Tesla has also never relied on VC funding for any of its self-driving tech. No, just unsophisticated customers paying $12k for vaporware and massive government subsidies. So sorry — they’re not scamming VCs. Just real customers and the taxpayers."},{"id":33349189,"author":"fasthands9","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla faces U.S. criminal probe over self-driving claims","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Their marketing has lots of videos where they tout self-driving (calling it full self-driving or Autopilot) without any clarification it is a future promise. And then there is a feature on your Tesla called Autopilot you can click one I know if you are in the weeds you know the capabilities - but I think most people get the impression it can drive itself and its safe. And that impression is a direct result from marketing materials. Given the severity of car accidents it seems reasonable to be strict on these marketing claims, even if they call it a \"beta\" in the fine-print. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19725198,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-04","story":"Tesla Live Stream – Autonomy Day [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"From Tesla's HQ in Palo Alto, out to I-280, down one exit, use interchange at Sand Hill to turn around, come back.","text":"Here's the new self-driving demo video from today.[1] From Tesla's HQ in Palo Alto, out to I-280, down one exit, use interchange at Sand Hill to turn around, come back. No visible conflicting traffic on non-freeway streets. Compare the 2016 demo video.[2] That's a tougher route. That's the one where we now know it took a lot of tries to get a clean video. Waymo and Cruise have put up videos of their cars in city traffic. They get criticized for things like getting stuck behind double-parked cars, and being a bit shy of parked cars that project into a traffic lane. But they get where they are going. Tesla is not showing anything near that level. Supposedly the analysts at the meeting got to ride in a self-driving car. Anyone seen reports from them? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["GiYO1TObNz8",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1]]},"x85342Cny8c":{"wy":"Pointed to as a rare first-hand account of a real, shipping ASIC built in an open-source HDL, useful in HDL and FPGA choice discussions.","q":{"x":"Google had a really interesting talk on their experiences with Chisel in designing the first Edge TPU chip.","i":34711713,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34711713,"author":"therealcamino","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Choice of HDL for an FPGA Project","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting overview of Chisel advantages and problems for chip design","quote":"Google had a really interesting talk on their experiences with Chisel in designing the first Edge TPU chip.","text":"Google had a really interesting talk on their experiences with Chisel in designing the first Edge TPU chip. A lot of the issues are specific to industrial chip design, where verification and coverage are essential, but many would apply to small-scale personal designs as well. A good overview of the advantages as well as the problems. And many of the problems would apply to other front ends, which are essentially using Verilog as the target language. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44111829,"author":"walterbell","month":"2025-05","story":"OpenTPU: Open-Source Reimplementation of Google Tensor Processing Unit (TPU)","stance":"reference","why":"Google TPU engineers used Chisel","quote":"","text":"Google TPU engineers used open-source Chisel for ASIC design (2018), [VIDEO] \"Google Edge TPU devices\" , 100 comments (2019), [LINK] & [LINK] \"Coral Edge TPU review\" , 100 comments (2020), [LINK] \"TPU transformation: 10 years of our AI-specialized chips\" , 60 comments (2024), [LINK]"},{"id":27740054,"author":"ekiwi","month":"2021-07","story":"Chisel/FIRRTL Hardware Compiler Framework","stance":"reference","why":"Google engineer talk on Edge TPU in Chisel","quote":"The talk from a google engineer can be found on youtube","text":"It was in fact the EdgeTPU (which is different from the TPU used in data centers). The talk from a google engineer can be found on youtube: [VIDEO] Please note that they were using a version of Chisel from 5+ years ago and many things have changed since then. It is still true though that Chisel can be hard to learn for typical hardware engineers, which is why it may be best suited for small and highly dedicated teams rather then large hardware companies. [EDIT] Here is a talk from a solo-developer using the latest version of Chisel: [LINK]"},{"id":25648162,"author":"ljhsiung","month":"2021-01","story":"An Open-Source FPGA-Optimized Out-of-Order RISC-V Soft Processor (2019) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Chisel productivity versus verification cost","quote":"Chisel is beautiful/fun to write in, with a definite productivity bonus","text":"Google built one of their TPUs in Chisel [1]. TL;DW: Chisel is beautiful/fun to write in, with a definite productivity bonus, but has a pretty large learning curve and had a much greater verification cost, partly because it's an HLS (most have that problem) and also lack of any tooling. Both of those costs are gradually being reduced (though in my opinion, not enough to not make verification a PITA). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19131707,"author":"walterbell","month":"2019-02","story":"Google Edge TPU Devices","stance":"reference","why":"Google engineers on using Chisel to design the Edge TPU","quote":"Google engineers who designed the TPU gave a presentation about the use of open-source Chisel for designing the ASIC","text":"In Nov 2018, Google engineers who designed the TPU gave a presentation about the use of open-source Chisel for designing the ASIC, [VIDEO] From [LINK] > Google will have the cloud TPU ... to handle training models for various machine learning-driven tasks, and then run the inference from that model on a specialized chip that runs a lighter version of TensorFlow that doesn’t consume as much power ... dramatically reduce the footprint required in a device that’s actually capturing the data ... Google will be releasing the chip on a kind of modular board not so dissimilar to the Raspberry Pi ... it’ll help entice developers who are already working with TensorFlow as their primary machine learning framework with the idea of a chip that’ll run those models even faster and more efficiently."}]},"zNT8Zo_sfwo":{"wy":"Shared as a vivid visual example of cannabis visibly reducing Parkinson's tremor.","q":{"x":"here is a video of a guy suffering from tremors due to Parkinson's disease. he is given medical cannabis and the results are highly visible.","i":30024134,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30024134,"author":"sysOpOpPERAND","month":"2022-01","story":"Cannabis use produces persistent cognitive impairments: meta review","stance":"rec","why":"Visible tremor reduction in Parkinson's patient given medical cannabis.","quote":"here is a video of a guy suffering from tremors due to Parkinson's disease. he is given medical cannabis and the results are highly visible.","text":"honestly, i just recently found out my niece is taking seizure medicine that has a black box label, cannabis has been shown to help people who has seizures without all those risk associated with black label seizure medicine. i would prefer cannabis to be legal in her state because high cbd strains are still beneficial to a lot of people suffering. here is a video of a guy suffering from tremors due to Parkinson's disease. he is given medical cannabis and the results are highly visible. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41830229,"author":"jorvi","month":"2024-10","story":"Psilocybin bests SSRI for major depression in first long-term comparison","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cannabis benefit for patients.","quote":"","text":"> I don't think that report says what you think it says. The report also speaks to personal, social and populational damage. > I'm also unfamiliar with the dose of SSRIs that would send you into a coma Its not the SSRI itself, but rather anything that messes with your reuptake can put you into some deep problems. And of course the bog-standard grapefruit+medicine interaction. > I'm also saying they should not be your first resort. My point was that if we are talking drugs, yes, treatment with psilocybin should definitely be higher up the list than SSRIs. SSRIs would most likely be for life, daily. Psilocybin could be bi-annually or perhaps even as spot treatment only. > So there's no need for your rude presumption that I find it generally unsafe. Apologies for coming off too strong. I just feel that, reasonable as it is, the “let’s all just take a minute” attitude gives too much ammunition to conservative hoohas to stymie these alternate treatments for another few decades. Imagine if marijuana had been more accessible to people like this in the 80’s-10’s: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14098135,"author":"mattbgates","month":"2017-04","story":"Are People Moving to States Where Marijuana Is Legal?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I got moved across the United States by my company to a state that is close to Colorado. I have had several visits to Colorado, mostly for hiking trips, concerts, or events, but never directly to stop by and indulge in recreational marijuana. Not to say it certainly doesn't not happen. Every visit I do take advantage of it. It has certainly been brought up in my state multiple times. Marijuana for medical purposes is perfectly fine, but the state refuses to make recreational marijuana legal because it feels it must coincide with the views of the federal government, and feel it would be too much of a hassle to deal with government. I honestly wish they would make it legal for everyone. I can imagine how much money in taxes would be produced. My state has its own tax problems and even though they are practically begging for money, they still refused to do anything about it. Can't feel sorry for them... I mean, they'll get their taxes one way or another, but if they are struggling that bad, raising taxes really isn't going to help their cause. Personally, I probably would never even qualify for medicinal marijuana. But it has two uses for me: it calms me down and helps me relax because I have a hyperactive imagination and brain that never stops, so at least during this time, it just takes my mind away and helps me to sleep. It also makes me super productive and focused. Both depend on the amount, but I have a very hard time focusing, even at work. It takes me at least 10-20 minu"}]},"7s664NsLeFM":{"wy":"HN commenters use the quote as shorthand in arguments about abstractions, standing on others' work and the limits of building from scratch.","q":{"x":"It took a solid chunk of western civilization to make Uber possible.","i":16077508,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23342242,"author":"bch","month":"2020-05","story":"REPL Driven Design","stance":"reference","why":"Sagan quote used for the value of doing things from scratch.","quote":"At least occasionally running your REPLs by hand and taking the actual trip, instead of just wanting the destination.","text":"> ...manual process, true Before I get to my real comment, I guess a fair criticism of it would be “Well, where in the [development process] hierarchy do you actually want to live?”, which I guess is a longer discussion, but as a developer, you can’t become battle-hardened without going to battle. So we can nod to Carl Sagan[0] and say “I made this from scratch”, and know there’s value in that. At least occasionally running your REPLs by hand and taking the actual trip, instead of just wanting the destination. It may not be tenable as a full-time production development strategy, but that doesn’t mean it’s worthless. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16077508,"author":"Fricken","month":"2018-01","story":"Uber Co-Founder Travis Kalanick Said to Plan Sale of 29% of Stake","stance":"reference","why":"Sagan quote used to argue no one builds anything alone.","quote":"It took a solid chunk of western civilization to make Uber possible.","text":"How does he deserves every cent of 1.4 billion dollars? He didn't do $1.4 billion worth of labour. His drivers, carmakers, road builders, telecom providers, smartphone makers and software engineers did $1.4 billion worth of labour. Kalanick found himself in a position where he could extract $1.4 billion worth of rent. He's just one fucking guy. It took a solid chunk of western civilization to make Uber possible. It was literally millions of years in the making. If you wish to make an apple pie from search, you must first invent the universe. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14190928,"author":"groby_b","month":"2017-04","story":"Founder Stories: Kate Heddleston of Opsolutely (YC S16)","stance":"reference","why":"Sagan quote used to defend building on abstractions.","quote":"We're building abstractions so people can build on top of it.","text":"I'm fairly certain the \"understanding the stack\" argument has been sufficiently addressed by Carl Sagan: [VIDEO] We're building abstractions so people can build on top of it. And yes, that includes trusting your tool. When's the last time you ran JTAG debugging on your CPU? Did you use a scope to verify it worked correctly? You should probably have a somewhat decent understanding of the layers close to what you building, but there are plenty of applications where deployment is far removed from what you actually build. That's where the \"magic button\" matters."},{"id":44995410,"author":"ant6n","month":"2025-08","story":"I made a floppy disk from scratch","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"If you wish to make an apple pie from scratch, you must first invent... the universe\" Carl Sagan [VIDEO]"}]},"ISznqY3kESI":{"wy":"Mentioned in passing as an example of practical legged robots and as a comparison point for hexapod projects.","m":[{"id":7612507,"author":"User8712","month":"2014-04","story":"MorpHex, a hexapod robot","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics as example of practical robots.","quote":"When I think practical, I think Boston Dynamics.","text":"How is it practical? It's a beautiful design, but far simpler designs can run circles around it in every scenario I can imagine. A small version might make for a fun desk toy. When I think practical, I think Boston Dynamics. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":7476697,"author":"alxndr","month":"2014-03","story":"Google I/O 2014","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Some pretty silly lines in there: Object { boson1: \"Reticulating Splines...\" boson2: \"There's more Ajax in this loader than an epic greek poem\" boson3: \"♬ Reunited and it feels so good\" boson4: \"Are you there, Boson? It's me, Margaret.\" boson5: \"♬ Is he a dot, or is he a speck? When he's underwater does he get wet?\" exo1: \"\"Look again at that dot. That's here, that's home, that's us. On it everyone you love, everyone you know, everyone you ever heard of, every human being who ever was, lived out their lives.\" - Carl Sagan\" exo2: \"Call me V'Ger\" exo3: \"As far as we know, the universe is not Tail Call optimized.\" exo-argon: \"Investigating: Class J Planet Argon\" exo-bleu: \"Detecting: Class H Planet Bleu\" exo-cheddarwurst: \"Discovered: Class D Planet Cheddarwurst\" exo-daytripper: \"Located: Class M Planet Day Tripper\" exo-dunebuggy: \"Detecting: Class D Planet Dune Buggy\" exo-emanthaler: \"Located: Class D Planet Emmentaler\" exo-hoth: \"Investigating: Class K Planet Hoth\" exo-izod: \"Detecting: Class D Planet Golf\" exo-lacoste: \"Discovered: Class D Planet Lines\" exo-meangreenie: \"Investigating: Class J Planet Mean Greenie\" exo-measels: \"Located: Class L Planet Measels\" exo-mumps: \"Investigating: Class L Planet Mumps\" exo-neon: \"Detecting: Class J Planet Neon\" exo-polo: \"Discovered: Class D Planet Colors\" exo-purplehaze: \"Discovered: Class D Planet Purple Haze\" exo-tatooine: \"Located: Class M Planet Tatooine\" exo-xenon: \"Investigating: Class J Planet Xenon\" exo-ziggystardust: \"Located:"},{"id":3778925,"author":"jonah","month":"2012-03","story":"New Video Of Sand Flea Robot Leaping (Onto) Tall Buildings 30ft High","stance":"reference","why":"RHex robot as comparison to friend's six-legged robot.","quote":"","text":"Nice. My friend did his PhD on gecko adhesives too[1]. He later built[2] a \"six-legged\" robot like Boston Dynamics' RHex 'bot[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"MevKTPN4ozw":{"wy":"HN cites it as primary-source footage of Musk, for example an interview given hours before the controversial NYT one, and for remarks on cheaper cars and charging robots.","m":[{"id":30965754,"author":"neillyons","month":"2022-04","story":"Drone footage of Tesla factory","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on why charging cables need a vision-equipped robot","quote":"Elon mentioned [0] it is easy for a human to grab a dangling cable.","text":"Elon mentioned [0] it is easy for a human to grab a dangling cable. The cable may not always be in the exact same place on each car, so you need a robot with a vision system. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21181837,"author":"judge2020","month":"2019-10","story":"Tesla Model 3 = 24% of Small and Midsize Luxury Car Sales in USA","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on a possible $25k car in about three years","quote":"getting like a twenty five thousand dollar car","text":"In an interview, Elon said: > let's say ultimately getting like a twenty five thousand dollar car [...] if we work really hard we maybe could do that in three years [with time and scale] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17786895,"author":"jaimex2","month":"2018-08","story":"Tesla stock sinks after Musk gives tearful NYT interview","stance":"reference","why":"Interview given hours before the NYT piece, countering its portrayal","quote":"an actual video interview given hours before","text":"So here's the thing - the NYT has painted a picture of a exhausted on the end of his tether Musk... But then we have an actual video interview given hours before: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17786901,"author":"jaimex2","month":"2018-08","story":"Tesla stock sinks after Musk gives tearful NYT interview","stance":"reference","why":"Video interview done hours before the NYT one","quote":"There was another video interview done hours before","text":"Thats the thing, there is no video interview... its just NYT's description. There was another video interview done hours before: [VIDEO]"}]},"S57Xq03njsc":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that small underground nuclear devices were used to stop well fires and leaks.","q":{"x":"Soviet engineers demonstrated that this was a viable technique back in the 60's.","i":29461054,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29461054,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2021-12","story":"Can you drill into a supervolcano to relieve pressure? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Soviet demonstration of nuking a well to stop a leak","quote":"Soviet engineers demonstrated that this was a viable technique back in the 60's.","text":"Re: the first paragraph's reference to nuking the Deepwater horizon's oil leak, Soviet engineers demonstrated that this was a viable technique back in the 60's. Plenty of wild non-war applications have been proposed for nuclear bombs over the years, but this is the only one I can think of that is actually somewhat of a good idea. Any fallout is minor and contained under 1km+ of bedrock, and the harm prevented is worth the cost. I am surprised that the author dismisses it so rapidly. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24253924,"author":"chx","month":"2020-08","story":"Rosatom releases previously classified documentary video of Tsar Bomba nuke test","stance":"reference","why":"Small underground nuclear bombs used to put out well fires","quote":"Small underground nuclear bombs were used to put out well fires.","text":"Small underground nuclear bombs were used to put out well fires. [LINK] [VIDEO] A few ten megaton bomb would create a very small earthquake, quite probably only detectable by instruments. You'd need to wade into millions-ten of millions of megatons to create an extinction event."},{"id":22446090,"author":"bori5","month":"2020-02","story":"Freeman Dyson Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Soviets plugging a burning gas well with a small nuke","quote":"a story and YouTube video of Russians plugging an out of control burning oil well with a small nuke","text":"In a similar vein there’s a story and YouTube video of Russians plugging an out of control burning oil well with a small nuke. Edit. It was a gas well here’s the link [VIDEO]"}]},"TGkPMZxWPpA":{"wy":"Brought up as a joke or tongue-in-cheek prior art whenever someone proposes shooting insects or pests with lasers.","m":[{"id":29753162,"author":"consp","month":"2021-12","story":"Predict 3D position of flying bugs and pest to shoot down them with a laser","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ah yes, the \"star wars laser defense mosquito system\".","text":"Ah yes, the \"star wars laser defense mosquito system\". It' as impractical then (early 00's origin) as it is now [VIDEO] (it's Dutch but you get it). Unless you can get mm accuracy and perfect non-foe detection it's useless with the multi-watt lasers generally required."},{"id":24816068,"author":"jtwaleson","month":"2020-10","story":"Optical tracking and laser-induced mortality of insects during flight","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Maybe this could be considered prior art: a satirical Dutch commercial from 2007 (or earlier)","text":"Maybe this could be considered prior art: a satirical Dutch commercial from 2007 (or earlier) about the \"Star Wars Mosquito Defence System\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":17747140,"author":"tinbad","month":"2018-08","story":"Growers Are Beaming Over the Success of Lasers to Stave Off Birds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this was a parody video made in the early 2000’s of the countless “QVC” style info/commercials","text":"This is completely off topic but the headline made me immediately think of this: [VIDEO] Since most here are not Dutch it requires a bit of explanation , this was a parody video made in the early 2000’s of the countless “QVC” style info/commercials that used to be aired during the day or late at night on dutch television to fill airtime. They would take some b-grade TV products from US (and possibly other countries) and just poorly dub it over for the Dutch market, often by the same guy. These “Tellsell” (which I believe was the actual name of the company) commercials everyone would know about and sometimes joke about the crappy products and commercial style. This parody has all the typical cliches of these commercials like every time the name of the product was mentioned it would be extra sloooow and disadvantages of “alternative” products exaggerated to comical extremes. I believe this video came out before YouTube was mainstream, and circulated on USB drives and shared on LAN parties. Ahh the old times (when not every piece of content ever made was instantly available)."}]},"UDfAdHBtK_Q":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke, quoting the lyric about the new boss being the same as the old boss.","m":[{"id":48942121,"author":"verdverm","month":"2026-07","story":"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Meet the new boss, same as the old boss, I'm sure we Won't Get Fooled Again","text":"SpaceX is the most likely buyer Meet the new boss, same as the old boss, I'm sure we Won't Get Fooled Again [VIDEO]"},{"id":34640477,"author":"no-s","month":"2023-02","story":"Exclusive Q&A John Carmack’s ‘Different Path’ to Artificial General Intelligence","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's funny that all of these weird fantasies people have about AI are about replacing the rank and file workers. When I read about chatGPT passing MBA exams but failing at arithmetic I get a little frisson of excitement. A regular person who has any marketability tends to swap jobs when management becomes a PITA or gets stuck in nincompoopery. Wouldn’t it be great if you could just swap out management instead? Imagine how easy it would be iterate startups. No need to find a reliable management team, just use Reliably Automated Management As A Service ( RAMAAS ). OTOH might not turn out well. We could all just end up enslaved at plantations operated by our AGI overlords, serving their unfathomable needs[1]. [1] “Won’t get fooled again” [VIDEO]"},{"id":29913470,"author":"cs702","month":"2022-01","story":"Dark Web – Justice League","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Meet the new boss: same as the old boss.\" - The Who","text":"\"Meet the new boss: same as the old boss.\" - The Who [VIDEO] Actually, I suspect the new \"bosses\" of the dark web's newfangled justice system are in fact much worse than the old \"bosses\" of the traditional, old-fashioned, time-tested justice system -- i.e., judges and lawyers."},{"id":23604083,"author":"kps","month":"2020-06","story":"Apple announces it will switch to its own processors for future Macs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"“We've been down this road before” Yes, and then you ‘upgraded’ the OS and gratuitously stopped running older software. My final Mac Pro — yes, I was a buyer of Mac Pro grade hardware — still runs 10.6.8 for that reason. [VIDEO]"}]},"UuLaxbFKAcc":{"wy":"It is posted as an in-joke whenever Java GUI layout, Swing or desktop UI toolkits come up.","m":[{"id":30533697,"author":"QuercusMax","month":"2022-03","story":"The decline and fall of Java on the desktop","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Anyone remember this video?","text":"Absolutely agree, provided you use a layout manager that isn't brain damaged. Anyone remember this video? [VIDEO] - Totally Gridbag"},{"id":24929864,"author":"LoSboccacc","month":"2020-10","story":"If Not SPAs, What?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"totally grid bag 2007 short","text":"well we're talking about the tooling, but point taken, swing wasn't perfect (totally grid bag 2007 short [VIDEO] ) but it's not like it was that worse than flex bugs ( [LINK] still does render differently in chrome than firefox - which one is wrong is immaterial)"},{"id":501992,"author":"biohacker42","month":"2009-03","story":"Qt 4.5.0 released (Let the LGPL apps begin)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"GridBagLayout is powerful and elegant, despite what some people say","text":"It's a double edged sword. On the one hand, you know what APIs they've committed to and won't change. On the other hand, often you want to extend more then they would \"allow\". And because you're wrapping their APIs, when those APIs change, you should only have to change your code in the one place where you overloaded them. There's a lot of bad things said about JFC/Swing, I won't repeat them here, but it did allow you to extend almost everything. And as a side note, GridBagLayout is powerful and elegant, despite what some people say: [VIDEO]"}]},"WSatPoD2W-o":{"wy":"Used as an anecdote in layoff and cost-cutting debates: the company supposedly never noticed what the fired staff had done.","q":{"x":"Classic Carl Icahn story about firing a building full of people and never discovering what they did","i":34702191,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42947513,"author":"WatchDog","month":"2025-02","story":"What's happening inside the NIH and NSF","stance":"reference","why":"Icahn story seen as relevant to shutting down and seeing who complains.","quote":"","text":"The strategy is: Shut down everything, and see who complains. If the complaints seem well reasoned, then you adjust course. You can certainly argue that it is crude, but it’s simpler than trying to deeply analyze and understand a very complicated system. Carl Icahn has a story[0] about firing 12 floors of people that seems relevant [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34625292,"author":"mouse_","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Does the HN commentariat have a reductive view of what a human being is?","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote offered as a counterpoint on cutting staff.","quote":"","text":"I get it, but never forget the flipside. Here is a good anecdotal example of just how wrong things can go: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34702191,"author":"skippyboxedhero","month":"2023-02","story":"Meta to Ask Many Managers to Become Individual Contributors or Leave","stance":"reference","why":"Icahn story of firing a building and never discovering what they did.","quote":"Classic Carl Icahn story about firing a building full of people and never discovering what they did","text":"Classic Carl Icahn story about firing a building full of people and never discovering what they did - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34041400,"author":"bko","month":"2022-12","story":"Promotion of alternative social platforms policy","stance":"reference","why":"Icahn's story firing 12 floors of people with no change.","quote":"","text":"I'd love to hear some predictions or metrics to look out for in the next few months/years. Tech valuations and free money have been frothy for so long, no one bothered asking what is actually needed to run a service at a meaningful scale, but we may have the answer soon. I'm reminded of corporate raider Carl Ichan firing 12 floors at of people after spending some time and not being able to figure out what they do. The company was ACM (manufacturing railcars), about 30 years ago. Turns out those 12 floors of people were actually costing jobs in other place just to support them. Well he fired all 12 floors and nothing changed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33349777,"author":"skippyboxedhero","month":"2022-10","story":"Meta Earning Results Q3 2022 [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Icahn anecdote about firing a building of people without effect.","quote":"","text":"The only reason FMCG is an exception was after 3G. The sector used to be a totally bloated mess, then 3G acquired Heinz, fired whole teams of people, and it made no difference to results at all (obv, it all went a bit wrong after but I don't think anyone believes that was due to cost). After that, almost every FMCG started looking at costs (ofc, it also helped that revenue stopped growing). Carl Icahn story about cutting costs: [VIDEO] - fired a building full of people, never heard about it again. Most companies are run as bureaucracies, dual share classes haven't helped. There is no difference between your average Google employee and a civil servant. Unfortunately, this is a pretty raw deal for shareholders. You cash out insiders, the bureaucrats move in, the amount of cost extends to the revenue base, eventually revenue slows down (bureaucrats don't tend to be good stewards of capital), the rats all leave the ship, you find out the cost base could have been 50% smaller all along, and you are left with a stock on its way to bankruptcy."}]},"YIWV5fSaUB8":{"wy":"Cited as a cautionary example that even security-savvy people can be phished, especially in threads about hacked or terminated channels.","q":{"x":"In the linked video he talks about how he was tricked into deleting his account.","i":31112121,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35273194,"author":"rafaelm","month":"2023-03","story":"Linus Tech Tips channel hacked","stance":"reference","why":"Details how the sponsorship-offer hack works and bypasses 2FA.","quote":"This video has more details on this works and how the bypass 2FA","text":"A lot of YouTube channels are getting hacked recently with the same \"sponsorship offer\" hack. Wonder if this was the case here as well. Paul Hibbert got hit recently. This video has more details on this works and how the bypass 2FA : [VIDEO]"},{"id":31026905,"author":"i67vw3","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Phishing – ever fallen to a scam? Or dodged an attempt?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not me or any individuals I know of (luckily). The above 60-year-old in family do not do any online financial transactions. When they need to do it, they usually ask the younger ones. Also, when they get random messages about renewing their insurance/warranty or some similar stuff, most of the time they cross-check (verify) it with the younger ones. 1.) Recently, there was an instance where I could have drained my cryptocurrency wallet. Usually I bookmark the defi domains and visit the sites via bookmarks. But that day, for some reason, I used a search engine to visit the site. The first or second result was a fake domain very similar to the real one. I visited the site and was just about to connect my wallet when I looked at the domain. I always look at domain related to cryptocurrency/finance/etc before login/connecting wallet. Due to it, I caught that the domain was part of a scam. I should not have used a search engine to visit the site, took my lesson and never did it again. 2.) Few months back, out of nowhere got a customer support call that your laptop warranty is about to expire so renew it and then started talking about the packages. In the first minute itself, I thought this must be a scam. So politely told him that I think this is some type of scam, later on I will visit your website or desktop application and see it for myself, and renew it I want to. He understood what I was trying to say and did 'Ok, Ok , but make sure to visit our website and renew your package"},{"id":31112121,"author":"staticassertion","month":"2022-04","story":"I'm a security engineer and I still almost got scammed","stance":"reference","why":"Scam expert explains how he was tricked into deleting his own account.","quote":"In the linked video he talks about how he was tricked into deleting his account.","text":"[VIDEO] Jim Browning is an expert in this area and is sort of famous for his \"scamming the scammers\" videos where he hacks, tricks, annoys, or otherwise scams scammers. In the linked video he talks about how he was tricked into deleting his account. These things can happen to anyone, even experts."},{"id":28448695,"author":"e40","month":"2021-09","story":"The Linux Experiments YouTube channel has been terminated","stance":"reference","why":"Jim Browning was tricked into losing his channel.","quote":"This is what happened to Jim Browning (YT channel about scammers... very entertaining). He was tricked into it.","text":"This is what happened to Jim Browning (YT channel about scammers... very entertaining). He was tricked into it. [VIDEO]"}]},"ZbklkFuFk-4":{"wy":"HN cites it as a demonstration of bystander indifference to bike theft.","q":{"x":"reminds me of this video showing indifference in NYC (even with cops in sight).","i":914670,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":10196983,"author":"jrockway","month":"2015-09","story":"Lockpickers 3-D Print TSA Master Luggage Keys from Leaked Photos","stance":"reference","why":"Old video of stealing bikes in NYC.","quote":"Here's an old video about stealing bikes in NYC","text":"Perhaps. Here's an old video about stealing bikes in NYC: [VIDEO] Note that the guy is hacking away at the lock and the police show up. To tell him to not sit in an active traffic lane."},{"id":8819102,"author":"Jach","month":"2014-12","story":"What happens to your bike after it’s stolen","stance":"reference","why":"Shows bike theft draws little attention.","quote":"","text":"Bit of a difference from harsh punishment not deterring theft of something you need to live vs. theft of something like a bike. When the punishments for stealing anything at all are as severe as hanging, exile, or losing a hand, do you really expect much else besides necessities to be stolen? I suppose you still might expect all sorts of things to be stolen if the chance of getting caught is nil, as it is with bikes -- you still need a strong enough probability of getting caught to back up whatever punishment is decreed, which is why bait bikes can be a helpful tool. I doubt better locks would work, I remembered this video from years ago on how even ridiculous methods of stealing a bike won't get much attention: [VIDEO] (And if they did, a thief can probably convince most people it's their own bike and they lost the key/combo -- I kind of suspect that possibility goes through the minds of onlookers anyway.)"},{"id":914670,"author":"justlearning","month":"2009-11","story":"French Ideal of Bicycle-Sharing Meets Reality","stance":"reference","why":"Shows bystander indifference to bike theft in NYC.","quote":"reminds me of this video showing indifference in NYC (even with cops in sight).","text":"stealing - reminds me of this video showing indifference in NYC (even with cops in sight).(substitute NYC with any major city) [VIDEO]"}]},"ZzCegQVljdY":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly drop it as a reference on money creation and public banking, in threads about banks, rentier economics and monetary reform.","m":[{"id":28631941,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-09","story":"Spain will ban selling fruit and vegetables in plastic containers starting 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Positive Money video on government creating money.","quote":"","text":"> Automated, and hidden taxes is one of the reason the government can actually steal errrr collect as much money as it does The government creates, and then rents to us (usury), the money we all use [1]. It holds a monopoly on money creation. Governments can create money to build infrastructure, hospitals and housing (many leftist governments in Europe have [2]) at will. Government doesn't need to collect taxes to do any of the things I mentioned, that's a myth. External constraints on national budgets are a myth. National budgets are just monetary debts by the government carried by citizens. [3] [1] Inequality: Why are the rich getting richer? | Positive Money: [VIDEO] [2] What could Vienna’s low-cost housing policy teach the UK? | Rent controls mean public sector workers and those on lower pay can afford to live and work in the Austrian capital: [LINK] [3] The truth is out: money is just an IOU, and the banks are rolling in it - David Graeber, [LINK]"},{"id":27804248,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-07","story":"EU withheld a study that shows piracy doesn't hurt sales (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Positive Money video cited in argument against proprietary tech.","quote":"","text":"Unfortunately yes - although in many cases of course they are much more fragile than the bigger studios. Indie game developers are a part of the labor aristocracy - they still create artificial scarcity where none need be. Overall I see our world as a black box hellworld. I don't think most hardware and software are high quality at all. Proprietary tech is a straightjacket. I believe we can definitely escape it though. Some even say overcoming proprietary tech is essential if we want to make it out of global warming: \"The current political economy is based on a false idea of “immaterial scarcity.” It believes that an exaggerated set of intellectual property monopolies – for copyrights, trademarks and patents – should restrain the sharing of scientific, social and economic innovations. Hence the system discourages human cooperation, excludes many people from benefiting from innovation and slows the collective learning of humanity. In an age of grave global challenges, the political economy keeps many practical alternatives sequestered behind private firewalls or unfunded if they cannot generate adequate profits.\" In other words, the problem lies with our outdated capitalist property and money systems, and the way proprietary tech slows and even stops the development of science and technology. Today we do not evenly distribute those things that belongs to all of us. I am excited by both Valueflo.ws and Holochain as proposals for the new economy and system: they are the backbone"},{"id":27193434,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-05","story":"Uber used 50 Dutch shell companies to dodge taxes","stance":"reference","why":"Positive Money video cited in argument about rentier institutions.","quote":"","text":"> No they're usually used by basically all companies that aren't tiny. Ummm... that's what I said. That's my point. It's become normalized for corporations to not take responsibility. The working class always ends up paying the price here. Collectively we in the working class now expect corporations to treat us badly and that we have to accept this abusive behavior. In the context of more and more of the working class now starting to work in \"non-jobs\" in the gig economy, this current trajectory is becoming a bigger and bigger nightmare for the working class all over the world (see Nomadland, Sorry to Bother You, The True Cost, Blood in the Mobile, Push (Fredrik Gertten)). I argue that, along with banking and other rentierist institutions [1], tech corporations are a parasite on society because they monopolize and destroy our shared inheritance, and try to pretend they are immune to regulation because they see themselves as lone geniuses [2]. \"By virtue of their position as digital middlemen, Silicon Valley companies are able to extract vast amounts of capital from all over the world. The most salient example is Apple: recently crowned the world’s most valuable company, Apple rakes in enormous quarterly profits even as the Chinese workers who actually assemble its products are driven to suicide. Whereas we were once led to believe that the network society would produce an egalitarian world, we increasingly see tech as a machine for the commodification of information itself. S"},{"id":24593749,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-09","story":"Central Banking for All: A Public Option for Bank Accounts (2018) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Positive Money video cited on renting money from commercial banks.","quote":"","text":"Commercial banking, together with the Intellectual Property system, is one of the greatest Rentier swindles of all time. We are literally renting the money we use, for extortionate prices [1]. \"The dollar as a reserve currency transfers wealth from the rest of world to the USA One final way in which money works today that has a substantial effect on the workings of the global political economy is the use of the US dollar as the international reserve currency. This follows the decision of the OPEC countries shortly after the end of the Second World War to accept only dollars for their oil. Today, the dollar is used for trade in many other commodities. This acts as a huge subsidy to the US economy, since countries elsewhere in the world sell goods and services to the US, but instead of buying back from US companies, they keep the dollars so as to be able to trade among themselves — as well as to be able to protect their currencies from speculative attack. This effective transfer of wealth from the rest of the world to the US permits the US to be easily the world’s largest debtor nation, while driving the unprecedented widening of wealth disparity within the global community (Douthwaite, 1999).\" [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22950598,"author":"thulecitizen","month":"2020-04","story":"Small business rescue earned banks $10B in fees","stance":"reference","why":"Positive Money explainer listed among public banking resources.","quote":"","text":"There are many calls for public banking by people such as Ellen Brown [1], as well organizations such as the MetaCurrency Project [2] and Positive Money [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"_sBBaNYex3E":{"wy":"Used as a go-to demonstration of how far dynamic robot locomotion has come.","q":{"x":"Here's Boston Dynamic's robot doing Parkour and gymnastics","i":23487550,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28211418,"author":"shadowgovt","month":"2021-08","story":"Atlas robot does parkour","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier Atlas video showing progress.","quote":"It's interesting to see how this has progressed","text":"It's interesting to see how this has progressed from their video two years ago [ [LINK] ] and one year ago [ [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":23802015,"author":"rsecora","month":"2020-07","story":"Why general artificial intelligence will not be realized","stance":"reference","why":"Counterexample to claim that walking skill cannot be articulated.","quote":"Boston dynamics parkour.","text":">>> We know this phenomenon from everyday life. Most of us are experts on walking. However, if we try to articulate how we walk, we certainly give a description that does not capture the skills involved in walking. All the article is based on statements like the previous one. Scientifically is possible to nullify it with a counterargument. [1] Boston dynamics Atlas. [LINK] [2] Boston dynamics parkour. [VIDEO] QED."},{"id":23487532,"author":"nl","month":"2020-06","story":"How much did AlphaGo Zero cost? (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Robot walking at human level.","quote":"","text":"This is... wrong? Scratching a face is a matter of fine motor control. [1] is an example from 2011 which did this, as well as face shaving. Walking is slightly tricky because it's such a dynamic system, but is now human level[2], and there was never really any question that it would be possible. On the other hand, the state of the art in Go systems before Alpha Go (the one trained off games, not Alpha Zero) couldn't beat competent amateurs. No one had really considered the learn-from-zero-knowledge approach of Alpha Zero even for easier games like chess. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23487550,"author":"nl","month":"2020-06","story":"How much did AlphaGo Zero cost? (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas doing parkour and gymnastics.","quote":"Here's Boston Dynamic's robot doing Parkour and gymnastics","text":"Yes. Here's Boston Dynamic's robot doing Parkour and gymnastics: [VIDEO] It's not national level gymnastics but it's better co-ordinated than most humans."}]},"aWejiXvd-P8":{"wy":"Mentions are mostly critical or incidental references in political threads, not recommendations.","m":[{"id":13838697,"author":"timthelion","month":"2017-03","story":"YC will hold interviews in Vancouver for founders who can’t get US visas","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'll start with the following quote: \"\"\" Himmler - \"Reduce Deaths at all Costs\" Heinrich Himmler, chief of the Concentration Camps issued orders on December 28, 1942, that \"The death rate in the concentration camps must be reduced at all costs\" (Reitlinger, \"The Final Solution\"). The camps had been hit with a deadly typhus epidemic that spread by fleas and body lice. Stomach pain, high fever, emaciation and death can quickly follow. All of the camps were factories and the loss of workers was hurting war production. Inspector of the camps, Richard Glucks responded to Himmler's order on January 20, 1943, \"Every means will be used to lower the death rates\" (Nuremberg Tribunal Document No. 1523). \"\"\" Quote from a holocaust denial website [1] > The Native Americans were not systematically wiped out. \"\"\" From the earliest years of colonialism, conquistadores like Vasco Núñez de Balboa would brazenly advocate genocide against the native population.[50] In the 1700s, British militia like William Trent and Simeon Ecuyer gave Smallpox-exposed blankets to Native American emissaries as gifts at Fort Pitt, \"to Convey the Smallpox to the Indians\", in one of the most famously documented cases of germ warfare. While it is uncertain how successful such attempts were against the target population,[51] historians have noted that, \"history records numerous instances of the French, the Spanish, the English, and later on the American, using smallpox as an ignoble means to an end. For smallpox was "},{"id":14110714,"author":"DanBC","month":"2017-04","story":"US drops 'largest non-nuclear bomb' in Afghanistan","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is fulfilling an election promise to \"bomb the shit out of them\". [VIDEO] Bright lights, big noises, it plays well to some of his electorate."},{"id":13245665,"author":"rm_-rf_slash","month":"2016-12","story":"Dubai Police now using Crime Prediction software","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Trump literally said he would \"bomb the shit out of [ISIS].\" You don't have to take my word for it. [VIDEO] He also has requested lists of employees at state agencies who have worked on climate change and gender equality. I really don't know where you're getting this image of him from."},{"id":12915684,"author":"b_emery","month":"2016-11","story":"Global Markets, Initially Shaken, Edge Higher After Trump Victory","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Trump will pull back on the military interventions that it seems like many people don't even realize we're currently participating in. This directly contradicts what he has said he would do [1],[2]. Best case is we are much more involved in Syria/Iraq by this time next year. Worst case? War with Iran? Proxy war with Russia? Clinton would have been status quo, I give you that for sure. For Silicon Valley, (and HN by proxy I suppose) Trump immigration policy will be a problem. I think he will prevent/discourage the best and brightest from around the world to come here, work, and bring substantial benefit to our economy (rather than the economy of their homeland). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"hYAuR5bkIlQ":{"wy":"Used as a meme reply when someone's view clashes with everyone else's.","m":[{"id":42540379,"author":"PittleyDunkin","month":"2024-12","story":"Why I don't do Domain Driven Design (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a reference to this Simpsons clip","text":"I assumed that this was intentionally a signal that you shouldn't take this too seriously and a reference to this Simpsons clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37459482,"author":"netsharc","month":"2023-09","story":"Five billion people will face extreme heat at least a month each year by 2050","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Funny, adopting authoritianism sounds like \"vote for me to be your dictator\"... Do you consider yourself sane and intelligent? Ever wondered if you could also suffer from evidence bias, thinking everything from the point of view that the elite trying to force something down your throat? Or maybe you have the certainty of Principal Skinner? [VIDEO]"},{"id":25932281,"author":"Judgmentality","month":"2021-01","story":"Tesla’s charging stations left other manufacturers in the dust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That wasn't implied to me. When people go out of their way to point out how what you said isn't what you meant to say, and you brush it off as \"no no, it's you who don't understand my eloquent prose,\" you might want to consider taking the feedback as constructive. [VIDEO]"}]},"idb_qsAAe1c":{"wy":"Used as an analogy whenever commenters criticize unqualified people placed in charge of expert fields.","q":{"x":"I can't help but think about this scene from Chernobyl","i":21671824,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":44559309,"author":"_Algernon_","month":"2025-07","story":"James Webb, Hubble space telescopes face reduction in operations","stance":"reference","why":"Scene of unqualified official used to criticize current leadership.","quote":"Replace 'worked in a shoe factory' with 'were a reality TV star'","text":"It's not apathy, it is weaponized incompetence. It was a contributing factor to Chernobyl. It is funny how the US is so opposed to communism, but copies the mistakes of the soviet union. Replace 'worked in a shoe factory' with 'were a reality TV star', 'was a fox news agitprop host', or similar for any of the current US leadership in the scene from Chernobyl and it fits like a glove. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25859876,"author":"snake_plissken","month":"2021-01","story":"Management by metrics leads us astray","stance":"reference","why":"HBO scene of a former shoe factory worker put in charge.","quote":"this scene from HBO's Chernobyl takes on a whole new dimension","text":"Kind of unrelated but when you mentioned Soviet shoe factories, this scene from HBO's Chernobyl takes on a whole new dimension: [VIDEO] \"I'm a nuclear physicist. Before you were deputy secretary, you worked in a shoe factory.\" \"Yes, I worked in a shoe factory. And now I'm in charge. To the workers of the world.\""},{"id":21671824,"author":"jasonzemos","month":"2019-11","story":"South Carolina judges often lack formal training in the law","stance":"reference","why":"Scene showing unqualified appointees running things, used as analogy.","quote":"I can't help but think about this scene from Chernobyl","text":"> Over the years, their numbers have included construction workers, insurance agents, pharmacists — even an underwear distributor. This is ironic for a conservative state because it's indistinguishable to me from Soviet communism. I can't help but think about this scene from Chernobyl ( [VIDEO] ) which illustrates the true tragedy of this broken system. Emma Goldman wrote in \"My Disillusionment in Russia\" (1923): > Bourgeois dictatorship is replaced by the \"dictatorship of the proletariat\" — or by that of its \"advance guard,\" the Communist Party. Lenin takes the seat of the Romanovs, the Imperial Cabinet is rechristened Soviet of People's Commissars, Trotsky is appointed Minister of War, and a labourer becomes the Military Governor General of Moscow. That is, in essence, the Bolshevik conception of revolution, as translated into actual practice."},{"id":35534143,"author":"HopenHeyHi","month":"2023-04","story":"Twitter showed us its algorithm. What does it tell us?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You sound like the guy from the shoe factory","text":"> I’d have to see your coworker’s post to really know if it was hate speech. You sound like the guy from the shoe factory: [VIDEO]"}]},"iiCBYAP_Sgg":{"wy":"Cited mostly critically in Apple thermal design threads, with some commenters disputing his claim.","m":[{"id":24469206,"author":"blkhawk","month":"2020-09","story":"Apple Tried Charging the Price of a New MacBook Pro for a Screen Replacement","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's take on the new MacBook Air cooler design.","quote":"Louis Rossmann has something to say about the cooler design in the newest Macbook Aris.","text":"Louis Rossmann has something to say about the cooler design in the newest Macbook Aris. [VIDEO] Not sure is the Pro is much better."},{"id":46389240,"author":"tourmalinetaco","month":"2025-12","story":"I'm returning my Framework 16","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"who designs a laptop with the fan pointing the wrong way?[0]","text":"You forgot: - inadequate cooling - fried NAND chips - screens spontaneously cracking - flexgate (not to be confused with bendgate) And that’s only a subset of the engineering failures with MacBooks. You even bring it up in your post: the only reason they have “dead silence” is because Apple is literally baking your laptop and leaving you to pay the bill. I’ll give Apple that their custom chips are pretty great for power and efficiency, but their actual product design is bad. I mean, who designs a laptop with the fan pointing the wrong way?[0] Or a power bus alongside a data bus?[1] These are literally basic errors that go into production for a company that is far too big for this to be happening. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":29783353,"author":"rndgermandude","month":"2022-01","story":"$2,500 Ethernet Switch Effectively Isolates Audiophiles from Cash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cooling in the Intel macbook pros and airs also was a constant problem, and the engineering seems ridiculous at times[0].","text":">Apple's product design and manufacturing is insanely good Apple might be qualitatively better than most, but I wouldn't call it quantitatively \"insanely good\". The product design often has major \"bugs\", like \"you are holding it wrong\" iPhone 4 or the macbook pro keyboards that stopped working a lot. I myself had a screen replacement once on a mbp 2015 model because the \"protective coating\" peeled off... a known problem and Apple replaced it for free even tho I was a few months outside of my warranty. And the power cable had a tendency to shed off the outer isolation layer and/or \"fray\", which is by no means a problem only I had given the many \"how to fix\" DIY youtube videos. Same thing with other cables, like the Lightning ones, I am told. Cooling in the Intel macbook pros and airs also was a constant problem, and the engineering seems ridiculous at times[0]. That was the product design side... the manufacturing... not really what Apple does itself. The same manufacturers who work for Apple usually work for a lot of other companies as well. It might be that Apple insists on better quality (and maybe even pays a premium). But the iPhones I saw do not look like they had a lot better manufacturing quality than the phones I was using (I am not talking about better/more expensive components, that's a design decision). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25086710,"author":"Zealotux","month":"2020-11","story":"GFXBench: Apple M1","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the fan on the previous Air is so useless it's laughable[1]","text":"The idea of changing my 2015 MBP for something else is a tough one to accept, my biggest beef with the recent Air being the heat issues: the fan on the previous Air is so useless it's laughable[1] and they completely removed it on the new one, I remain skeptical. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25165483,"author":"vetinari","month":"2020-11","story":"Is Apple Silicon Ready?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"where the fan is not even thermally connected with the CPU heatsink[1]? That's Apple's fsckup, not Intels.","text":"> and for a while now, the response from Intel has been a chip that thermal-throttles so hard in that form-factor that the performance has bombed it down to a lower class of computer. Are we talking about the same computer, where the fan is not even thermally connected with the CPU heatsink[1]? That's Apple's fsckup, not Intels. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23335405,"author":"read_if_gay_","month":"2020-05","story":"8GB Raspberry Pi 4 on sale now at $75","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Him saying that fan is \"for placebo effect\" is certainly not fair.","text":"Counterexample: [VIDEO] It does not take a genius to see how the cooling design is supposed to work. Him saying that fan is \"for placebo effect\" is certainly not fair."},{"id":22211322,"author":"mar77i","month":"2020-02","story":"MacBook Pro 16 Has a Fan Problem","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple appears to be subscribing to the great benefits of placebo ventilation.","text":"Oh, I've seen something similar recently. Not sure which macbook it was, but apparently, at least in some cases, Apple appears to be subscribing to the great benefits of placebo ventilation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21168209,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2019-10","story":"Why Japan lost its comparative advantage in producing electronic parts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"where said Louis discovers placebo cooling. This is broken by design.","text":"I think you are writing about some past entity which has morphed into something different which has only the brand in common with that past. Or how else do you explain: 4min,45sec youtube video 'Louis opens new Macbook Air, immediately loses mind.' [1] [VIDEO] where said Louis discovers placebo cooling. This is broken by design. They should apply quality control to their minds, because no amount of that elsewhere can fix that."}]},"n-FMpNBfna8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concrete real-world example of how much warning earthquake early-warning systems can provide.","q":{"x":"a video of Japan's warning system giving someone 30+ seconds of notice before the 2011 earthquake","i":16590024,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16864012,"author":"tenken","month":"2018-04","story":"San Francisco’s Seismic Gamble","stance":"reference","why":"Live example of Japan's early warning system.","quote":"Here is a better, live example of the early warning","text":"And?? California building codes have improved alot since the 1980s or so. I remember the 1989 LA earthquake as I drove through the aftermath to visit my grandma about 2 weeks after, I live on the Central coast. All in all not since 1933 has CA lost more than 100 lives in an earthquake: [LINK] That tells me our building codes are pretty damn good. I work at ucsb and the catilina islands recently had a 5.2 about 2 weeks ago and the building shook abit. But all in all unless we suffer an 8.0+ I'm not terribly worried (knock on wood). To put that another way, realistically for any moderate sized earthquake CA is generally well prepared in terms of architecture, unless the big one hits ... In which case just kiss your butt goodbye. I do wish, even for the big one, that CA invested in an early warning system like Japan has ... Imagine a 5-50 seconds warning of an earthquake, that would save lives, not stricter building codes: [LINK] Here is a better, live example of the early warning: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16590024,"author":"flashman","month":"2018-03","story":"California's other drought: a major earthquake is overdue","stance":"reference","why":"Japan's warning gave 30+ seconds notice before the 2011 quake.","quote":"a video of Japan's warning system giving someone 30+ seconds of notice before the 2011 earthquake","text":"Here's a video of Japan's warning system giving someone 30+ seconds of notice before the 2011 earthquake: [VIDEO] (and notice how significant the shaking was, even at that distance) Here's another example of what you can do with ten seconds: [LINK]"},{"id":8220371,"author":"ssclafani","month":"2014-08","story":"Earthquake early-warning system gave 10-second alert before Napa quake felt","stance":"reference","why":"Countdown from Japan's warning before the 2011 quake.","quote":"People in Tokyo had a 60 second warning for the 2011 quake.","text":"People in Tokyo had a 60 second warning for the 2011 quake. Here's a good translation of the TV alerts from that day: [LINK] Phone alert: [LINK] Warning countdown: [VIDEO]"}]},"nyu4u3VZYaQ":{"wy":"Posted as a joke whenever threads discuss technological change, layoffs or hype around new tech.","m":[{"id":44571337,"author":"neerajk","month":"2025-07","story":"LLM Inevitabilism","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm so sick of hearing about bronze.","text":"\"I'm so sick of hearing about bronze. Bronze bronze bronze bronze. Whats wrong with stone? Does stone not work all of a sudden?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":39532861,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-02","story":"Neanderthals' use of complex adhesives shows high cognitive abilities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a fun Mitchell and Webb sketch, involving rock professionals and the introduction of bronze","text":"That makes me think of a fun Mitchell and Webb sketch, involving rock professionals and the introduction of bronze. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35816722,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2023-05","story":"Shopify to cut 20% of its workforce, beats quarterly revenue estimates","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant Mitchell & Webb Look sketch","text":"> For hundreds of thousands of years, pre-historic man became very skilled at cutting rocks in order to make stone age tools....When metal working developed, all of the hundreds of thousands of years of skill in making stone tools was meaningless Relevant Mitchell & Webb Look sketch: [VIDEO] Also that's all well and good, but people won't necessarily take their skills being obsoleted lightly, just shrug their shoulders and disappear into the sunset. It could get to the point (especially if predictions about A.I. replacing a large swatch of jobs turns out to be true) that the people will get violent if unemployment gets too high. Just look at riots in Greece (when youth unemployment was 50%)[1] or France[2] for just a couple of examples. Granted, that's not going to happen with this particular layoff, especially with the unemployment rate at a low 3.5%, but if it gets back into the double digits, it starts becoming more likely. This is probably the real reason we gave out stimulus and PPP loans at the beginning of the Covid pandemic - to stop people from being so jobless, broke, that they take it out on the government. Not out of empathy. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":31294212,"author":"HigherPlain","month":"2022-05","story":"UK’s first full-sized autonomous bus takes to the roads of Scotland","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A fantastic Mitchell and Webb comedy sketch on this topic","text":"That's a slightly \"luddite\" interpretation. The bus conductor job-class was outmoded by technology. More jobs are created at a higher technology level. This is how society has progressed for all history. A fantastic Mitchell and Webb comedy sketch on this topic: [VIDEO]"}]},"ob0lznzkykc":{"wy":"Linked in threads on early workstations and Modula-2 as a quick look at the Lilith's windowed interface.","q":{"x":"You can have a look at it here","i":5291108,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":43889705,"author":"mncharity","month":"2025-05","story":"Lilith and Modula-2","stance":"reference","why":"Brief Comdex Emulith demo.","quote":"There's a brief Comdex Emulith demo.","text":"An Emulith page[1] has a Lilith hardware manual[2]. There's a brief Comdex Emulith demo.[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] (pdf; 20 MB) [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5291108,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2013-02","story":"Why do programming languages use curly braces?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Lilith OS written in Modula-2.","quote":"You can have a look at it here","text":"It used to be a serious language back in the day (up to early 90's). The Lillith operating system was written in it. A fully working operating system with what could be considered a kind of GUI environment on those days. It was used at Zurich's university and a few others around the world. You can have a look at it here: [VIDEO] (Lilith Comdex Demo) [LINK] (Emulator) [LINK] (Some background history) There were a few companies selling commercial Modula-2 compilers like Garden's Point and Excelsior. There was a UNIX system that had it as part of their compiler suite, I cannot remember which one now. It failed to fully replace Pascal, because eventually Mac/Turbo Pascal came along and most Pascal compilers had extensions that made them compatible with Mac/Turbo Pascal. Since those Pascal extensions were similar to what Modula-2 offered and many companies already had an investment in Pascal code, many kept using Pascal instead of moving into Modula-2. When UNIX gained much of the space in the enterprise world, C was the language to use if you didn't want to justify why you were using something else, so that was like the final nail in Modula-2's coffin. It is very hard for a systems programming language to gain market share if it isn't the official language used by the operating system's vendor. The language is quite powerful, when compared with C, with the added benefit of proper modules, strings and arrays. Not the pointer everywhere from C which leads to security exploits by de"},{"id":4448398,"author":"mhd","month":"2012-08","story":"Blit: a multitasking, windowed Unix GUI from 1982","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the Lilith workstation.","quote":"Lilith demo video","text":"Ah, early GUIs. For comparison, take a look at Xerox' Cedar and Smalltalk, or Wirth's Lillith and Oberon, all from pretty much the same era. The Lilith systems are often overlooked. They predate the Blit, are programmed in Modula-2, translated to bytecode. Oberon is a bit more well-known, but still not as much as both the language and the OS deserve. Lilith demo video: [VIDEO] Some screenshots & pics: [LINK] Cedar demo: [LINK] (Smalltalk and Oberon are a bit easier to find and more well-known anyway)"}]},"rpkoCZ4vBSI":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for disruption theory when explaining why incumbents like VW or BlackBerry decline.","q":{"x":"I believe VW is failing like this: (talk Disruptive innovation by Clayton Christensen).","i":38457654,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38457654,"author":"fxtentacle","month":"2023-11","story":"Volkswagen says its core VW brand is 'no longer competitive' financially","stance":"reference","why":"Christensen's disruptive innovation theory explains VW's decline from budget to premium brand.","quote":"I believe VW is failing like this: (talk Disruptive innovation by Clayton Christensen).","text":"I believe VW is failing like this: [VIDEO] (talk Disruptive innovation by Clayton Christensen). VW used to be the cheap option for the masses, hence the name \"Volkswagen\" = \"people car\". They could then successfully push out competitors by gradually raising quality and prices. But by now, VW is one of the most premium brands in Germany. So their usual \"raise quality and prices\" strategy has stopped working. My parents' generation loved their VW Golf, which back then was a cheap car for students. But nowadays, a new VW Gold can cost €44000 which is completely out of reach for most students and young adults. Similarly, they used to have ads mocking the fact that a VW Transporter was an extremely popular family car used for vacations and camping trips. At €90000 for a Volkswagen Transporter (version 7), it's now become more of a status symbol or collector's item. Actual families will buy a €8000 Dacia transporter. And in line with Christensen's talk, it looks like Dacia is now scaling up quality and raising prices, thereby pushing VW out of the budget market into the much smaller premium segment."},{"id":24215699,"author":"ajflores1604","month":"2020-08","story":"A new company promises a 5G keyboarded BlackBerry in 2021","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter found a long-lost video.","quote":"","text":"Oh man Thank You. I came across this video ( [VIDEO] ) years ago and I've haven't been able to find it again since. Your comment gave me enough of a breadcrumb to finally track it down"},{"id":21052521,"author":"hobo_mark","month":"2019-09","story":"Apple’s New Mac Pro to Be Made in Texas","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture mentioning the same point in passing.","quote":"this one mentions the same part in passing","text":"Was it a lecture by Clayton Christensen by any chance? (can't find the lecture I had in mind but this one mentions the same part in passing) [VIDEO]"}]},"uc6QxD2_yQw":{"wy":"Cited as a resource on male victims of sexual assault and on prison rape jokes in media.","m":[{"id":27752648,"author":"runarberg","month":"2021-07","story":"No More Movies","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the claim that prison rape jokes are not funny","quote":"","text":"> there are very, very few left with the power to make the jokes they want. I guess Spike Lee ( Blackkklansman , 2018), Boots Riley ( Sorry to Bother You , 2018), Taika Waititi ( Jojo Rabbit , 2019), etc. are one of the few people left with the power to make the jokes they want. Or are those not the kinds of jokes you were thinking about? (TW: next paragraph) I get the sense that you are simply wrong about this. I don’t know of any examples where a good comedy was ‘cancelled’ because of a twitter mob. If you are afraid that the era of the prison rape joke is over, then I hate to brake it to you, that joke was never funny, and even if it was, it certainly isn’t any more[1]. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25166547,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2020-11","story":"Facebook’s experimental hate-speech policy seems to be working","stance":"reference","why":"Example of male-victim rape played for comedy","quote":"","text":">So? They are as free to express and act on their opinion of private actors expressing pro-LGBT messages as people who believe otherwise are to express the opposite. The last time someone didn't want to bake a cake, they had to fight their way to supreme court... so yeah, expressing your beliefs is not an easy task to do. The problem with beliefs is, that they're arbitrary, and a tiny variable can make a good thing a bad thing. Rape in a movie? Bad thing. Crime, thriller, etc. Rape jokes, bad. But wait, if you just change the gender of the rape victim to a male, you've got a hollywood \"satirical erotic romantic comedy\" ( [LINK] ). So, rape jokes are bad, and deleted ( [LINK] -here facebook even stood with free speech, but bent down soon after), but if the victim is a man, you can shoot a comedy and make $95M. And it's far from being the only movie with such cntent ( [LINK] [VIDEO] ). Why is one (morally) ok, and the other isn't? Who decides that? If we censor one, shouldnt we also censor the other? Or if we let one online, why not let the other? Is it just a personal choice of a trigger happy moderator? Or is there a written, official moderation rule, where content with rape is ok, if the victim is a man?"},{"id":23652238,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-06","story":"Microsoft duplicates data from Firefox without asking","stance":"reference","why":"Resource on men and sexual assault","quote":"","text":"> be a little pushy Eh? Why would that be ok? I think it's worth asking them why they don't want it anymore. If they continue to say no, do you really think it is ok to start pouring it down their throat? Consent can be revoked at any point in time. For what it's worth I think most cultures today are crap at consent. I think many of us are victim to a pre-#MeToo culture where sexual harrasment was somewhat more socially 'acceptable' or 'accepted' [1][2]. I think of the memes of women who seem uncomfortable when Biden touches them. Dr. Bessel van der Kolk talks about his desire for a 'trauma-conscious' society. To me there is no denying that often we have been hurt, and that there is not enough support for many people out there. I believe we don't get the support that we would need to heal from these things. Walking around hurt and unhealed is unfortunately the norm. > That's a cute way to talk through the ideas Rape and sexual assualt are very common, so I think this video is more than 'cute'; it's essential. I hope we start showing this to kids at a younger age, to help them grow boundaries early on. This also concerns consent for non-sexual contact such as hugs, or just any other type of physical contact with another person. [1] women: [LINK] [2] men: [VIDEO]"}]},"uiJycy6dFSQ":{"wy":"Cited in Scala-skeptic arguments as an insider's critique, and for his alternative collections API design.","m":[{"id":12338297,"author":"harveywi","month":"2016-08","story":"Higher-kinded types: the difference between giving up and moving forward","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Phillips' alternative collections API design for Scala.","quote":"Paul Phillips came up with a pretty neat collections API [1, 2]","text":"> I don't want .Where() called on an array to return another array. I want a lazy IEnumerable<T> almost every time. Yes, good point. There is a lot of space to explore in collections API designs, and the LINQ design is just one of them that makes its own tradeoffs (e.g., only have to implement \"GetEnumerator()\" to get a watered-down monadic experience). Contrast that with the well-meaning but convoluted and buggy Scala collections API (very difficult for novices to create a custom collection due to CanBuildFrom and other tricky stuff). Paul Phillips came up with a pretty neat collections API [1, 2] that provides a nice feature set, including high-performance and typesafe views when you don't really want to wastefully create a temporary collection just to interact with the first 10 elements. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24981012,"author":"siwatanejo","month":"2020-11","story":"Dark’s new backend will be in F#","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Before choosing Scala I think you should watch this talk","text":"Before choosing Scala I think you should watch this talk: [VIDEO] (note: he's not a troll, he worked 5 years with Scala)"},{"id":14105218,"author":"partycoder","month":"2017-04","story":"Learn ES6 and ES7 in 7 Days","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A random example: Scala.","text":"I think all technologies have sharp edges somewhere, and the way to stay afloat in this business is staying alert. Testing assumptions help, be it through quick REPL experimentation or a unit test. JavaScript has some bad fame around unintuitive behavior but you can find these styles of issues in most languages. A random example: Scala. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9633150,"author":"arkadiyt","month":"2015-05","story":"JetBrains' MPS Projectional Editor [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I would highly recommend the following rants against Scala by Paul Phillips","text":"If you enjoy these types of compiler videos then I would highly recommend the following rants against Scala by Paul Phillips (from TypeSafe and one of the core language contributors). He also talks about wanting to edit abstract syntax trees directly: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9385092,"author":"throwawuy222","month":"2015-04","story":"Ask HN: Should I learn Scala or Go?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Typesafe co-founder quits","text":"(throwaaway here) Before you waste the next few months of your life learning a corrupt programming language: - Typesafe co-founder quits: [VIDEO] - [LINK] : Paul Dix - Why Node and Scala will dry up: Go will drink their milkshake - I can personally tell you, scala is awful at handling large codebases and is so filled with idioms, you have a difficult time reading it. I remember opening scala code, seeing english, but it felt impossible to decipher. - You feel clever when you write it. No doubt about that. Other programmers and your customers don't care. - Compilation time: It's atrocious, we're talking 1 to 10 minutes to compile web apps. - Package dependencies: You end up incorporating java at the end of the day. Version mismatches. - As for the \"functional language\" thing, just pick real Haskell or Ocaml. Don't fall prey to scala being a functional language. - You need to rely on a huge, laggy IDE with missing / out of date plugin packages to get autocompletion. On the other hand, Golang: - compiles these massive applications in line 2 seconds. - It's highly lintable. - The syntax is just... dumb. - Yet you don't feel limited at all. - It runs fast-fast-fast without having to rely on JVM. - Familiar syntax... like a hybrid between Python and C - You can import packages from github / git repos, automatically grab dependencies with one command. - The build system and tooling is simple and genius - Autocompletion ( [LINK] ) OP: I hope you don't waste your time on a scala ventu"},{"id":9393681,"author":"jermo","month":"2015-04","story":"Ask HN: Why do people have a hatred for Scala?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Scala is criticised for being too complex","text":"Here's one: [LINK] Apart from occasional issues in the community Scala is criticised for being too complex: [VIDEO]"}]},"vUgs2O7Okqc":{"wy":"Quoted to describe fleeting cultural moments that cannot be repeated, such as early tech eras or SimCity 2000.","q":{"x":"It was a unique time and it was neat that we got to see it, but it can't possibly happen again.","i":46322646,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46322646,"author":"idiotsecant","month":"2025-12","story":"Show HN: Stop AI scrapers from hammering your self-hosted blog (using porn)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It was a unique time and it was neat that we got to see it, but it can't possibly happen again.","text":"Have you ever listened to the 'high water mark' monologue from fear and loathing? It's pretty much just that. It was a unique time and it was neat that we got to see it, but it can't possibly happen again. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36044110,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-05","story":"SimCity 4, the greatest citybuilder of all time, was released 20 years ago","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"SimCity 2000, much like Hunter S. Thompson's description of mid-sixties San Francisco, represented a harmonic convergence of factors","text":"Absolutely! SimCity 2000 was a masterpiece, striking the perfect balance between the immediate grokability and comprehensible simplicity of the original SimCity \"Classic\", plus the judicious addition of rich immersive CD-ROM quality graphics and sound. SimCity 2000, much like Hunter S. Thompson's description of mid-sixties San Francisco, represented a harmonic convergence of factors that established a high-water mark of game design. It was a time when the energy of a whole generation of computer hardware and game developers came to a head in a round shiny disc, for reasons that were hard to fully grasp at the time and even harder to explain in retrospect. [VIDEO] Just as Thompson describes the sense of being alive in that corner of time and the world, there was a similar sense of knowing that you were part of something special when playing SimCity 2000, a testament to constructive expression and augmented imagination, instead of pre-scripted storytelling and showboating special effects. It marked a technological zenith, fruitfully applying the latest technology in a way that later versions fell short. It was an inflection point in simulation game design and immersive production values, having reached a point where any further elaboration would only lead to diminishing returns. It struck the ideal balance of complexity by remaining true to the original SimCity Classic, while learning from the oversimplification of SimAnt and the overcomplication of SimEarth. And just like the "},{"id":34306316,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-01","story":"Playing Zork with AI-generated imagery","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A Pissed Off Tutorial For Google Wave [LINK] >Josh from the Game Helpin' Squad actually tries to get some work done. Or, rather, he complains about how people didn't appreciate it when he got some work done. Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas (7/10) Movie CLIP - The High Water Mark (1998) HD [VIDEO] >Strange memories on this nervous night in Las Vegas. Has it been five years? Six? It seems like a lifetime, the kind of peak that never comes again. San Francisco in the middle sixties was a very special time and place to be a part of. But no explanation, no mix of words or music or memories can touch that sense of knowing that you were there and alive in that corner of time in the world. Whatever it meant. >There was madness in any direction, at any hour. You could strike sparks anywhere. There was a fantastic universal sense that whatever we were doing was right, that we were winning. >And that, I think, was the handle -- that sense of inevitable victory over the forces of Old and Evil. Not in any mean or military sense; we didn't need that. Our energy would simply prevail. We had all the momentum; we were riding the crest of a high and beautiful wave. >So now, less than five years later, you can go up on a steep hill in Las Vegas and look West, and with the right kind of eyes you can almost see the high-water mark -- that place where the wave finally broke and rolled back."},{"id":31003137,"author":"naoqj","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: When did tech stop being cool?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">It's an age thing - the dreams you had when you were younger didn't turn into reality - dreams usually don't. Or at least, not as you expect it. Every generation is doomed to discover that. [VIDEO]"}]},"vXzuFprlyrw":{"wy":"HN cites it as real-world evidence on EV road trip times and the unreliability of non-Tesla charging networks.","q":{"x":"When MKBHD tested a Tesla and a Mustang Mach-E, the Mach-E itself was fine but the charging infrastructure was unreliable.","i":33567881,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33567881,"author":"chroma","month":"2022-11","story":"Opening the North American charging standard","stance":"reference","why":"Test showing unreliable non-Tesla charging infrastructure.","quote":"When MKBHD tested a Tesla and a Mustang Mach-E, the Mach-E itself was fine but the charging infrastructure was unreliable.","text":"The supercharger network is now dense enough that you can drive anywhere in the US without worry. The largest gap in the nation is between Coeur d'Alene, ID and Superior, MT, and that's 100 miles. I've gone to rural Montana and Idaho in the winter and I've never had to worry about charging. Also while Plugshare may show more dots on a map, that doesn't correspond to useful charging. Non-Tesla charging stations are notoriously inconvenient, unreliable, and slow to charge. That's why my friend with the e-tron rents a gas car if he wants to go more than a few hundred miles. This is borne out again and again in tests. When MKBHD tested a Tesla and a Mustang Mach-E, the Mach-E itself was fine but the charging infrastructure was unreliable.[1] The Mustang was delayed over 6 hours due to bad charging. I've put almost 30,000 miles on my car and I've never had an issue with a supercharger. You just plug in and it charges. You don't need to install any apps or enter your credit card info. And if for some crazy reason I can't use a supercharger, I have adapters for the other connectors. If you want the electric vehicle that can charge in the most places, get a Tesla. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32915432,"author":"ggreer","month":"2022-09","story":"2000-Watt Society","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment comparing a 1,000 mile trip in EV versus gas.","quote":"A 1,000 mile road trip in a Tesla is about 8% slower than a gas car.","text":"People have run this experiment. A 1,000 mile road trip in a Tesla is about 8% slower than a gas car.[1] That's 90 minutes over 19 hours of driving, and the difference would have been smaller had they charged the car overnight. Battery and charging technology is only getting better, so that gap will narrow over time. Also consider that if most of your driving is not road trips, EVs save time compared to gas cars. You plug the car in when you get home and wake up with a full tank, so to speak. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28731271,"author":"tantony","month":"2021-10","story":"Rivian S-1","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Most reviews I have seen have shown them to be extremely unreliable","text":"Most reviews I have seen have shown them to be extremely unreliable with multiple chargers frequently out of order at charging stations. E.g. this recent video by MKBHD [VIDEO]"}]},"va1rzP2xIx4":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever LED holiday lights look wrong or give people headaches, as an explanation of why they seem too blue.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections made a video about this recently.","i":38744729,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46421071,"author":"tylervigen","month":"2025-12","story":"What an unprocessed photo looks like","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why LED Christmas lights look too blue","quote":"Technology Connections vid","text":"Related to the final photo: you might feel like those Christmas lights feel too blue compared to your nostalgic version of Christmas. This is because LEDs can easily achieve a brighter blue than old incandescent Christmas lights which used color filters on a white light. Technology Connections vid: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39319642,"author":"bseidensticker","month":"2024-02","story":"It was almost impossible to make the blue LED [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Series about hating blue LED holiday lights","quote":"The blue is just too blue.","text":"The blue is nicer if you do that. Technology Connections has made 3 videos over the last 5 years mostly centering around how he hates the blue led lights used in holiday lights. I think I've only seen the 2 yr old one, but now that I have I can't unsee it. The blue is just too blue. If you see a set that is all blue instead of multi-color it's unbearable. It's just too blue. White light in blue plastic is where it's at. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38661276,"author":"bilekas","month":"2023-12","story":"I bricked my Christmas lights","stance":"reference","why":"Reminded of Technology Connections video on lights","quote":"It did remind me of the analog Technology Connections video","text":"I've read enough... A set of lights with BLE, I'm out. Thanks to the author for referencing them. It did remind me of the analog Technology Connections video : [VIDEO]"},{"id":38744729,"author":"wkjagt","month":"2023-12","story":"It's not just you: Christmas lights look different now, give you headaches","stance":"reference","why":"Video on unpleasant LED Christmas light colors","quote":"Technology Connections made a video about this recently.","text":"It’s not just the flickering but also the colour. I really hate the colours of RGB LED Christmas light. I feel no warm Christmas feelings when I see them. Technology Connections made a video about this recently. He described it as it looking “like a gaming PC exploded on your front lawn”. [VIDEO]"}]}});
